            'meta_line' => 'Eight frames · about seven minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'intro',
                'photo' => 'biskupin',
                'label' => 'Before the state',
                'year' => 'to the 900s',
                'title' => 'The country is younger than the things people show you',
                'body' => 'This is Biskupin, the wooden settlement every Polish schoolchild is taken to see, and it has nothing to do with the Polish state. Tree rings date its timber to between 747 and 722 BC, some seventeen centuries before anyone wrote the word Poland, and the people who built it were not Slavs but the Lusatian culture. It is the first thing worth knowing here: the land is old, the country is not.',
                'caption' => 'Biskupin, a reconstructed Iron Age settlement',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Chrumps, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'tribes',
                'photo' => 'giecz',
                'label' => 'The tribes',
                'year' => '800s - 900s',
                'title' => 'The Polans, and the neighbours whose names we still use',
                'body' => 'Before there was a state there were tribes: the Polans around Gniezno, the Vistulans near Krakow, the Masovians on the middle Vistula, the Silesians in the south-west. The country takes its name from the first of them, although the Polans themselves only appear in writing around 998, three decades after the baptism. What they left behind are earth ramparts like this one at Giecz, one of their main forts.',
                'caption' => 'The earth ramparts at Giecz, 10th to 11th century',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Gorofil, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'legend',
                'photo' => 'gniezno',
                'label' => 'The legend',
                'year' => 'told later',
                'title' => 'Lech, the white eagle, and a word that means nest',
                'body' => 'Poland\'s founding legend has Lech, one of three brothers, stop where a white eagle sat over its nest and call the place Gniezno, from gniazdo, the Polish word for nest. A second story, about Piast the wheelwright who replaced Popiel, a ruler the mice are said to have eaten, gave the dynasty its name. Both were written down centuries later, and the article on Lech and the eagle traces where they came from. They get one thing right: Gniezno mattered early, and the next frame shows why.',
                'caption' => 'Gniezno Cathedral, on the hill where the legend puts the nest',
                'credit' => 'Photo: MOs810, CC BY 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'rise',
                'photo' => 'warriors',
                'label' => 'How they took over',
                'year' => '900 - 960',
                'title' => 'Two generations that turned one tribe into a state',
                'body' => 'The tribes did not merge peacefully. Through the first half of the 900s the Polans took their neighbours over and threw up a ring of enormous forts of earth and timber at Gniezno, Poznan, Giecz and Ostrow Lednicki, several of them raised on top of settlements that had been burned down first. Gallus Anonymus names the rulers who did it, Siemowit, Lestek and Siemomysl, the last of them Mieszko’s father, but no other source confirms they existed, so the ramparts are better evidence than the chronicle. What is certain is that Mieszko inherited rather than built: when a Jewish traveller, Ibrahim ibn Yaqub, described the country around 965, he counted three thousand armoured men whom the duke fed, armed and married off at his own expense.',
                'caption' => 'Reenactors at the Slavic warriors’ gathering in Grzybowo, one of the Polan forts',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Rafal M. Socha, CC BY 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'mieszko',
                'photo' => 'baptism',
                'label' => 'The decision',
                'year' => '966',
                'title' => 'A baptised duke pushes his borders to the sea',
                'body' => 'That duke is the first ruler we can actually document, Mieszko I. In 965 he married Dobrawa, a Christian princess from Bohemia, and in 966 he was baptised. The usual reading is that it bought him allies, and the article on the baptism of 966 takes the question step by step. What came after belongs here. He pushed his borders north to the Baltic, beat a Saxon margrave, Hodo, at Cedynia on the Oder in 972, and around 990 took Silesia from Bohemia. He died in 992 and left the state split between his sons. The eldest, Boleslaw, drove out his stepmother and his half-brothers, and within a few years the whole of it was back in one pair of hands.',
                'caption' => 'Jan Matejko, The Christianisation of Poland, 1889',
                'credit' => 'Painting: Jan Matejko, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'adalbert',
                'photo' => 'doors',
                'label' => 'The saint',
                'year' => '997 - 1000',
                'title' => 'A missionary is killed, and Otto III comes to the grave',
                'body' => 'Adalbert, a bishop from Prague, went north to convert the Prussians and was killed in 997. Boleslaw bought the body back for its weight in gold and buried it in Gniezno. Three years later the Holy Roman Emperor, Otto III, came to that grave on pilgrimage, recognised an archbishopric at Gniezno under Radzim Gaudentius, Adalbert’s own brother, and, according to the chronicler Gallus Anonymus, put his own crown on Boleslaw’s head. Historians call that meeting the Congress of Gniezno.',
                'caption' => 'The bronze doors of Gniezno Cathedral, cast around 1175',
                'credit' => 'Photo: public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'crown',
                'photo' => 'chrobry',
                'label' => 'The kingdom',
                'year' => '1000 - 1025',
                'title' => 'The first crown, and it took a lifetime to get',
                'body' => 'Otto III died two years after Gniezno, and his successor on the German throne, Henry II, had no intention of keeping his promises: he demanded the border lands back. They fought three wars over them, and the peace of Bautzen in 1018 left them with Boleslaw. He went for a real crown, rather than the gesture at Gniezno, once Henry died in 1024 and the new German king, Conrad II, was barely installed and not yet emperor. At Easter 1025 the archbishop of Gniezno crowned him, probably with the pope’s consent. Conrad protested, but never questioned that it was legal. Boleslaw died two months later, and Poland was a kingdom recognised as one, rather than a duchy at somebody else’s edge.',
                'caption' => 'Boleslaw the Brave, painted by Jan Matejko',
                'credit' => 'Painting: Jan Matejko, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'lednica',
                'label' => 'Where to stand in it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'Biskupin, Giecz, and a page for the rest',
                'body' => 'Two places in this story appear nowhere else on the site. At Biskupin, from the first frame, houses and a gate have been reconstructed on the site of the original settlement. Giecz, one of the Polan forts, has been an archaeological reserve since 1963, with its ramparts and the foundations of an early 11th-century church, and it is run by the Museum of the First Piasts at Lednica. The rest of the beginning, the cathedral island in Poznan, the island in lake Lednica shown here and Gniezno, is set out site by site on the page Where to stand in it, with what is original and how to get there.',
                'caption' => 'Ostrow Lednicki, with a modern sculpture of Mieszko and Dobrawa',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Szydzio, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A map of the Polish lands and the Slavic tribes under Mieszko I, drawn in 1918',
            'caption' => 'Poland and the Slavic tribes under Mieszko I, drawn by Jozef Michal Bazewicz, Warsaw 1918. The names are in Polish: Polanie are the Polans, Wislanie the Vistulans, Mazowszanie the Masovians, Slezanie the Silesians, Pomorzanie the Pomeranians.',
            'credit' => 'Map: Jozef Michal Bazewicz, 1918, public domain',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The earliest written list of tribes in this region is a 9th-century Frankish document known as the Bavarian Geographer. It names the Vistulans, the Silesians, the Opolans and the Goplans, but not the Polans, the Masovians or the Pomeranians. The Polans are first named in writing around 998, in the life of Saint Adalbert. Dagome iudex, a document from about 991, is the first to describe the realm and its borders. The chronicle of Gallus Anonymus, written around 1113, is the earliest narrative of the Piast beginnings, and the legend of Lech is later still. Everything dated before 966 here rests on archaeology rather than writing, which is why this article hedges more often than a school textbook does.',
