                'body' => 'Three brothers, Lech, Czech and Rus, part ways and found Poland, Bohemia and Rus. That story was first written down in Latin around 1222, in a chronicle compiled in Hungary. It kept changing afterwards: a Czech chronicle of the 1300s knows only two brothers, and in the 1400s the chronicler Jan Dlugosz demoted Rus to a descendant of Lech. The scene here was drawn in 1893. Almost everything a Pole pictures on hearing the story was settled in the 19th century, not in the 10th.',
                'caption' => 'Lech and the white eagle in an engraving from 1893',
                'credit' => 'Engraving, public domain',
            ],
