                'body' => 'The bird has its own history, and it is heraldry rather than legend. The oldest one sits on the seal of Kazimierz, duke of Opole, from the 1220s, and it has no crown. Within fifteen years the dukes of Wroclaw, Greater Poland and Kuyavia had put an eagle on their shields too, which is what ruling families across Europe were doing at the time. Kazimierz of Bytom added a crown to his around 1282. Then in 1295 Przemysl II was crowned king and took the crowned white eagle on a red field as the arms of a kingdom that had had no king for more than two hundred years: the object beside this text.',
                'caption' => 'The seal of Przemysl II, 1295, in a 19th-century reproduction',
                'credit' => 'Reproduction: Franciszek Piekosinski, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'crown',
                'photo' => 'evolution',
                'label' => 'The crown',
                'year' => '1927 - 1990',
