                'photo' => 'hill',
                'label' => 'Where to stand in it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'A bird on every document, and how to date it',
                'body' => 'Lech Hill in Gniezno, where the story puts the nest, has an article of its own. The eagle is easier to meet: it is on the entry stamp in your passport, on every coin, on police and border-guard uniforms and on the national football shirt, always in the drawing of 1927 with the crown voted back in 1990. Older eagles are everywhere, over gates, on tombs and on town halls, and the crown lets you date them. A bare-headed one was made under communism, between the late 1940s and 1990. A crowned one on anything official comes from before the Second World War or after 1990.',
                'caption' => 'Gniezno cathedral on Lech Hill, seen across lake Jelonek',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Mateusz Gielczynski, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],

        'emblem_alt' => 'The coat of arms of Poland in the version in force today',
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The legend of the three brothers was first written in Latin around 1222, in the chronicle known as the Hungarian-Polish Chronicle. The nest and the name come from the Greater Poland Chronicle, written before 1296. Gallus Anonymus, around 1113, does not mention Lech at all, which is why this article treats the legend as literature rather than record. The heraldry is dated from the seals themselves: Kazimierz of Opole in the 1220s, a crown from about 1282, Przemysl II in 1295. The hill is dated by excavation and tree rings, and the modern dates come from the regulation of 1927 and the act of 9 February 1990.',
