                'year' => '963',
                'title' => 'Beaten twice, and made to answer to the emperor',
                'body' => 'The Saxon monk Widukind of Corvey records that around 963 Wichmann, a Saxon count in revolt against his own king, Otto I, led the Veleti, a pagan Slavic federation between the Elbe and the Oder, against Mieszko and beat him twice, killing his brother. In the same years, writes Thietmar, bishop of Merseburg, the margrave Gero, who held the Empire’s eastern frontier, brought Mieszko under the emperor’s authority. The emperor was that same Otto I, crowned in Rome in 962 and planning an archbishopric at Magdeburg for the Slavs beyond the Elbe. The ivory beside this text, carved in those years, shows Otto handing the cathedral there to Christ.',
                'caption' => 'Otto I offers Magdeburg Cathedral to Christ, ivory, about 962-968',
                'credit' => 'The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'dobrawa',
                'photo' => 'dobrawa',
                'label' => 'Dobrawa',
                'year' => '965',
                'title' => 'A Czech princess, and a bishop who explained her name',
                'body' => 'Beaten in the west, Mieszko turned south, to Boleslav I, duke of Bohemia, a country Christian since the late 800s. The alliance was sealed with a marriage: Boleslav’s daughter Dobrawa arrived, probably at the end of 965. Thietmar, writing half a century later, explains that her Slavic name means good, and tells how she won her husband over. During Lent Mieszko coaxed her into eating meat, and she gave in on purpose, so that he would listen to her on bigger things. In the end, Thietmar says, she brought him to baptism.',
                'caption' => 'Dobrawa as Jan Matejko imagined her around 1890. No likeness of her survives',
                'credit' => 'Drawing: Jan Matejko, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'baptism',
                'photo' => 'annal',
                'label' => 'The baptism',
                'year' => '966',
                'title' => 'One line in an annal, and no place or day',
                'body' => 'The event the whole country dates itself from is recorded in one line. The annals of the Krakow cathedral chapter, written up in the 13th century, say under 966: Mesco dux Poloniae baptizatur, Mieszko, duke of Poland, is baptised. The older annal on this page, written in the early 1100s, puts both Dobrawa’s arrival and the baptism a year late, and a later reader wrote 966 in the margin. No source says where it happened or on which day. 14 April, Holy Saturday, is a modern guess based on the custom of baptising at Easter, and most historians look to Poznan or to the island in lake Lednica.',
                'caption' => 'The Old Holy Cross Annal, early 12th century: Dobrawa arrives, Mieszko is baptised',
                'credit' => 'Manuscript, National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'why',
                'photo' => 'thietmar',
                'label' => 'Why he did it',
                'year' => '966 - 967',
                'title' => 'Why he did it, and why historians still argue',
                'body' => 'No source gives Mieszko’s reasons, so every answer is a reconstruction. An older reading had him taking baptism through Bohemia to escape a forced German mission. Most historians now see it as a way into the alliances he needed: a Christian duke married into Bohemia had an ally against the Veleti and could deal with the emperor as a fellow ruler. It paid off at once. In 967 Mieszko, with two units of Czech horsemen, beat Wichmann, who died in the fight. About twenty years later Rus, to the east, took its baptism from Constantinople, and the line between Latin and Greek Christianity has run through this part of Europe ever since.',
                'caption' => 'A page of Thietmar’s chronicle on the Liutici, as the Veleti came to be called',
                'credit' => 'Facsimile of the Dresden manuscript, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'poznan',
                'photo' => 'basin',
                'label' => 'A bishop',
                'year' => '968',
                'title' => 'A bishop named Jordan, and a basin under Poznan',
                'body' => 'A Christian duke needed a church. In 968 Jordan became the first bishop in Mieszko’s lands, most likely answering to the pope rather than to Magdeburg, though historians still argue over his status. His church stood in Poznan, and under today’s cathedral archaeologists found the walls of the first one and a basin of lime mortar: a font to some, a pit for mixing mortar to others. A few steps away, a team led by Hanna Kocka-Krenz uncovered the duke’s stone hall in 1999 and, in 2009, a chapel just over two metres wide beside it, perhaps built for Dobrawa. The crypt also holds what are probably the graves of Mieszko and of his eldest son, Boleslaw.',
                'caption' => 'The mortar basin found under Poznan Cathedral',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Mariochom, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'slowly',
                'photo' => 'restorer',
                'label' => 'A slow conversion',
                'year' => '968 - 1030s',
                'title' => 'The duke converted in a day, the country took a century',
                'body' => 'Baptism went from the top down: first the duke and his warriors, then everyone else, by order. Thietmar describes the realm of Mieszko’s son, Boleslaw the Brave, around 1018: anyone caught eating meat in the weeks before Easter had his teeth knocked out. Thietmar disliked Boleslaw and told the story with relish, but the new faith was plainly enforced rather than chosen. When Boleslaw’s son, Mieszko II, died in 1034, the state itself fell apart, and in places people turned back to the old gods. The raid of Bretislav, duke of Bohemia, and the rebuilding that began in 1039 are told in the article on Gniezno.',
                'caption' => 'Casimir the Restorer comes home to a ruined country, Wojciech Gerson, 1880s',
                'credit' => 'Painting: Wojciech Gerson, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'millennium',
                'photo' => 'millennium',
                'label' => 'The Millennium',
                'year' => '1957 - 1966',
                'title' => 'A thousand years on, two rival birthdays',
                'body' => 'In 1966 Poland was a communist state, and the anniversary became a contest. The Church, led by the primate, Cardinal Stefan Wyszynski, spent nine years preparing and sent a copy of the icon of Our Lady of Czestochowa from parish to parish. The state answered with the Millennium of the Polish State: new schools and a military parade in Warsaw. In 1965 the Polish bishops wrote to the German bishops, inviting them to the celebrations with the words "we grant forgiveness and ask for it". The regime attacked the letter, kept Pope Paul VI out of the country, and in 1966 stopped the icon on the road. The pilgrimage went on with an empty frame.',
                'caption' => 'Bishop Stanislaw Adamski at the Millennium in Piekary Slaskie, 22 May 1966',
                'credit' => 'Photo: National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'tumski',
                'label' => 'Where to stand in it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'A holiday since 2019, and two places an hour apart',
                'body' => 'Since 2019, 14 April has been the Feast of the Baptism of Poland, a state holiday, though not a day off work. The date is the modern guess from the fourth frame, Holy Saturday of 966. The two places the story points to are under an hour apart by car: the cathedral island in Poznan, with the walls of the first church, the basin and the probable graves underneath, and the ruined chapel on lake Lednica with its two basins. The page Where to stand in it says what is original at each and how to get there.',
                'caption' => 'Ostrow Tumski in Poznan, the island where the first cathedral stood',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Salo Tchelidze, Unsplash',
            ],
        ],

        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A schematic map of Mieszko’s realm and its neighbours around 970',
            'caption' => 'Schematic, with today’s Poland drawn in for orientation. Frontiers in the 960s were not lines, and the areas marked are approximate.',
            'labels' => [
                'realm' => 'Mieszko',
                'empire' => 'The Empire',
                'veleti' => 'Veleti',
                'bohemia' => 'Bohemia',
                'rus' => 'Rus',
                'latin' => '966, via Bohemia',
                'greek' => '988, from Constantinople',
            ],
        ],

        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'Mieszko’s wars of the 960s are told by Widukind of Corvey, writing around 967 to 973, and by Thietmar of Merseburg, writing between 1012 and 1018, who is also the source for Dobrawa, her name and Boleslaw’s punishments. The year 966 comes from the annals of the Krakow cathedral chapter. The older Holy Cross annal gives 967. Ibrahim ibn Yaqub survives only in an 11th-century summary by the geographer al-Bakri. The basins in Poznan and on Lednica, the stone hall and the chapel are dated by excavation, and whether the basins were fonts is still debated. The Millennium is documented by the Institute of National Remembrance, and the holiday by the act of 22 February 2019.',
        ],

        'next' => 'Back to the history section',
