    'gniezno' => [

        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Gniezno, the First Capital',
            'description' => 'Poland began as a timber stronghold on a hill: the saint buried there, the first archbishopric, and the Czech raid of 1038 that ended it.',
        ],

        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Middle Ages · Topic 04',
            'title' => 'Gniezno,',
            'title_accent' => 'the first capital',
            'lede' => 'Nine frames, from a stronghold of earth and oak to a cathedral roof set alight in 1945. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about eight minutes · sources at the end',
        ],

        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'seats',
                'photo' => 'seats',
                'label' => 'A court on the move',
                'year' => '960s - 1030s',
                'title' => 'The first capital was a court that never stayed put',
                'body' => 'In the 900s Poland had no capital in the sense a visitor means one. Its ruler, Mieszko I, the first we can document, moved with his armed retinue from stronghold to stronghold, and historians speak of several principal seats rather than one. Gniezno was the sacred seat, where a saint would be buried and kings crowned. Poznan, fifty kilometres to the west, had the first bishop and probably the graves of the first two rulers, as the article on the baptism explains, and for the 1000s and 1100s historians add Krakow, Wroclaw and Sandomierz to the list. Both towns still claim the title, and both have a case.',
                'caption' => 'Lech Hill and the cathedral across the lake, drawn by Napoleon Orda',
                'credit' => 'Lithograph: Maksymilian Fajans after Napoleon Orda, 1880, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'name',
                'photo' => 'name',
                'label' => 'The oldest name',
                'year' => 'about 991 - 1000',
                'title' => 'The oldest name of the country is the name of this town',
                'body' => 'Around 991 Mieszko I placed his whole realm under the protection of the pope. The document is known from its first words as Dagome iudex, and it survives only as a summary copied about 1080. It traces the borders of the country and calls it civitas Schinesghe, which most historians read as the state of Gniezno. Before anyone wrote the word Poland, the country was named after this hill. Mieszko died in 992, and around 1000 his son and successor Boleslaw had silver pennies struck with the words GNEZDVN CIVITAS, the city of Gniezno, like the coin beside this text.',
                'caption' => 'A silver penny of Boleslaw, about 1000, reading GNEZDVN CIVITAS',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Gabinet Numizmatyczny D. Marciniak, CC BY 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'stronghold',
                'photo' => 'stronghold',
                'label' => 'The stronghold',
                'year' => '940 - 1030s',
                'title' => 'Ramparts ten metres high around a hill of churches',
                'body' => 'From 940 this hill carried not a stone castle but a stronghold of earth and oak. Its ramparts were timber boxes packed with earth and stone, up to thirty metres thick at the base and eight to twelve metres high with the palisade on top, and they closed off about four and a half hectares in three linked enclosures. Inside, probably in the 970s, Mieszko built a small round church of stone, about nine metres across. Its foundations lie under the nave of today’s cathedral. The photograph shows the rampart timbers being dug out in 1938.',
                'caption' => 'Excavating the timber frame of the rampart on Lech Hill, 1938',
                'credit' => 'Photo: National Digital Archive, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'grave',
                'photo' => 'church',
                'label' => 'The grave',
                'year' => '997 - 1034',
                'title' => 'One grave made the town the capital of the Church',
                'body' => 'What set Gniezno apart from Poznan was a grave: that of Adalbert, the missionary killed by the pagan Prussians in 997, whose body Boleslaw bought back, a story told in the article on how Poland came to be. When the Holy Roman Emperor, Otto III, came to pray at it in 1000, he walked the last stretch barefoot, and the bishop who met him was Unger of Poznan, because Gniezno had none of its own yet. The visit changed that: Gniezno became the seat of an archbishop, the head of the Church in Poland. At Christmas 1025 Boleslaw’s son, Mieszko II, was crowned here, the first coronation we can place in Gniezno for certain.',
                'caption' => 'Adalbert’s silver coffin, made by a Gdansk goldsmith in 1662',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'raid',
                'photo' => 'raid',
                'label' => 'The raid',
                'year' => '1038 or 1039',
                'title' => 'A Czech duke carries the saint off to Prague',
                'body' => 'Mieszko II died in 1034 and the state fell apart: a revolt, a return to the old gods in places, and his son Casimir driven abroad. The duke of Bohemia, Bretislav I, used the moment. In 1038 or 1039, depending on whose chronicle you trust, he took Gniezno without a fight and carried the bodies of Adalbert and of his half-brother Radzim, the first archbishop, off to Prague. Standing at Adalbert’s grave, he proclaimed the oldest Czech law code, the Decrees of Bretislav. The chronicler Gallus Anonymus wrote that wild animals later made their lairs in the empty cathedral.',
                'caption' => 'The relics carried into Prague, as Josef Trenkwald painted it in the 19th century',
                'credit' => 'Painting: Josef Trenkwald, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'krakow',
                'photo' => 'krakow',
                'label' => 'Krakow takes over',
                'year' => '1039 - 1320',
                'title' => 'The court moves to Krakow, the crowns stay in Gniezno',
                'body' => 'Casimir came back around 1039, found Greater Poland in ruins and made Krakow his main seat. Poles call him Casimir the Restorer, because he put the state back together. Gniezno was not given up: the cathedral was reconsecrated in 1064, the archbishopric restored in 1075, and Casimir’s son, Boleslaw II, was crowned here in 1076. Poland then went over two centuries without a king, split between dukes from 1138, until Przemysl II, duke of Greater Poland, was crowned here in 1295, and in 1300 the Czech king Wenceslaus II, who had taken over the Polish lands after him. From 1320 kings were crowned on Wawel, the royal hill in Krakow, but still by the archbishop of Gniezno.',
                'caption' => 'The death of Adalbert on the cathedral’s bronze doors, cast around 1175',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Maciej Szczepanczyk, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'primate',
                'photo' => 'primate',
                'label' => 'The primate',
                'year' => '1417 - 1764',
                'title' => 'The archbishop who stood in for the king',
                'body' => 'Gniezno lost the court but kept the Church, and from 1417 its archbishop carried the title of primate, the first man in the kingdom after the king. In 1572 Sigismund Augustus, the last male of the Jagiellonian dynasty, died without a son, and from then on the nobles elected every king. Between one king’s death and the next election the primate acted as interrex, standing in for the head of state: he announced the death and called the election. The first was Jakub Uchanski, whose seal hangs from this document of May 1573, and the last Wladyslaw Lubienski, in 1763-1764.',
                'caption' => 'Poznan’s vote in the first royal election, sealed by the interrex, May 1573',
                'credit' => 'Document: unknown scribe, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'fire',
                'photo' => 'fire',
                'label' => 'Stolen and burned',
                'year' => '1923 - 1989',
                'title' => 'Silver stolen twice, and a roof set alight in 1945',
                'body' => 'After Poland was partitioned, Gniezno spent most of the 19th century as a Prussian town and came back to Poland after the First World War. In 1923 thieves took the gold reliquary of a head that Gniezno has held to be Adalbert’s since 1127, when it said the head turned up inside the cathedral walls. It was never recovered. On 23 January 1945, two days after the Red Army entered a town the Germans had already left, Soviet tanks set the towers alight with incendiary shells, and the roofs, the organ and part of the vault were lost. The rebuilding stripped out the baroque in this 1938 photograph and brought back the Gothic. In 1986 thieves broke the silver figure off the coffin and melted most of it.',
                'caption' => 'The baroque altar and the silver coffin in 1938, before the fire',
                'credit' => 'Photo: unknown photographer, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'Where to stand in it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'Doors, a silver coffin and a view from the tower',
                'body' => 'Start at the bronze doors, then walk the Gothic nave, begun in 1342, to the silver coffin, rebuilt after 1986 around the silver torso found buried near the cathedral. From April to October you can climb the south tower for this view down the old town. Across lake Jelonek, the Museum of the Origins of the Polish State shows what decades of digging took out of the hill. Two visits tie the place to modern Europe. In June 1979 John Paul II, the Polish-born pope, said Mass on this hill and spoke to all the Slavic nations. In June 1997 he came back with seven presidents, from Germany to Ukraine, to mark a thousand years since Adalbert died.',
                'caption' => 'The old town of Gniezno seen from the cathedral tower',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Guillaume Speurt, CC BY-SA 2.0',
            ],
        ],

        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'Dagome iudex, the summary of Mieszko’s gift of his realm to the pope around 991, survives in a copy made about 1080. The raid of Bretislav is told by the Czech chronicler Cosmas of Prague, writing in the 1120s, who dates it 1039. Polish historians usually give 1038. Gallus Anonymus, writing around 1113, describes the ruined cathedral. The stronghold is dated by excavation and tree rings, and its size comes from the monograph edited by Tomasz Sawicki in 2018. Archaeologists still argue over which town was the first capital, Gniezno or Poznan. This page follows the view that there were several seats and that Gniezno was the sacred one. The fire of 1945 is described by the archdiocese of Gniezno from eyewitness accounts.',
        ],

        'next' => 'Back to the history section',
    ],

    'grunwald' => [

        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The Battle of Grunwald, 1410',
            'description' => 'Who the Teutonic Knights were, what happened on 15 July 1410, and what one of the largest battles of medieval Europe actually settled.',
        ],

        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The nobles · Topic 01',
            'title' => 'Grunwald,',
            'title_accent' => '1410',
            'lede' => 'Nine frames, from crusaders invited in by a Polish duke to a field where reenactors refight the battle every July. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about eight minutes · sources at the end',
        ],

        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'order',
                'photo' => 'malbork',
                'label' => 'Knights on the Baltic',
                'year' => '1226 - 1343',
                'title' => 'Crusaders invited in, who built a state of their own',
                'body' => 'In the 1200s Poland was split between dukes of the Piast dynasty, and one of them, Konrad, duke of Masovia, could not hold off the Prussians, a pagan Baltic people on his northern border. In 1226 he called in the Teutonic Order, German knight-monks founded in the Holy Land during the crusades. They conquered the Prussians and kept the land as a state of their own, owing nothing to any Polish duke. In 1308 they seized Gdansk and the Pomeranian coast, and in 1309 their Grand Master moved his seat to Malbork. In 1343 Casimir the Great, king of a Poland his father had reunited in 1320, gave up Pomerania in return for peace and two border lands, Kuyavia and Dobrzyn.',
                'caption' => 'Malbork, the seat of the Order’s Grand Masters from 1309',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Marcin Czerniawski, Unsplash',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'krewo',
                'photo' => 'krewo',
                'label' => 'A pagan grand duke',
                'year' => '1370 - 1385',
                'title' => 'A ten-year-old king and a pagan bridegroom',
                'body' => 'East of the Order lay Lithuania, the last pagan state in Europe, reaching across today’s Belarus and much of Ukraine. The Order raided it almost every year with volunteer knights from all over Europe: Chaucer’s Knight in The Canterbury Tales has fought "in Lettow". In Poland Casimir the Great died in 1370 without a son. The crown went to his nephew, Louis of Hungary, then to Louis’s daughter Jadwiga, crowned in Krakow in 1384, aged about ten, as king rather than queen. The Polish lords chose her husband: Jogaila, grand duke of Lithuania. At his castle of Krewo, in August 1385, he promised to be baptised, to convert his people and to join his lands to Poland.',
                'caption' => 'The act of Krewo, 14 August 1385, in which Jogaila set out his promises',
                'credit' => 'Document, 1385, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'vytautas',
                'photo' => 'seal',
                'label' => 'Vytautas and Samogitia',
                'year' => '1386 - 1409',
                'title' => 'Samogitia, the land both sides wanted',
                'body' => 'In February 1386 Jogaila was baptised in Krakow as Wladyslaw, married Jadwiga and was crowned. Poles call him Wladyslaw Jagiello. His cousin Vytautas fought him for Lithuania, at times with the Order: in 1390 they besieged Vilnius together, with the future Henry IV of England in their camp. In 1392 the cousins made peace, and Vytautas ruled Lithuania until 1430. The sore point was Samogitia, the lowland between the Order’s Prussia and its branch in Livonia to the north. Vytautas ceded it in 1398 to free his hands in the east, then backed its revolt in 1409. When Jagiello’s envoy said Poland would stand by Lithuania, the Grand Master, Ulrich von Jungingen, declared war on Poland in August.',
                'caption' => 'The seal of Vytautas, grand duke of Lithuania, on a document of 1404',
                'credit' => 'Central Archives of Historical Records, Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'march',
                'photo' => 'vistula',
                'label' => 'The bridge',
                'year' => '1409 - 1410',
                'title' => 'A bridge of boats across the Vistula',
                'body' => 'The Order struck first, in August 1409, taking the Dobrzyn land on the border. Wenceslaus IV, king of Bohemia, arranged a truce until midsummer, and both sides spent the winter getting ready. In the forest near Kozienice, Jagiello’s carpenters, under a master named Jaroslaw, built a bridge of boats and floated it down the Vistula to Czerwinsk. From 30 June 1410 the Polish army crossed it for three days, met Vytautas with his Lithuanians, Rus from his eastern lands and Tatar horsemen, and marched north into Prussia. No reliable count survives. Historians’ estimates for the allies run from under 30,000 to 40,000, servants included, and put the Order at about 20,000.',
                'caption' => 'The Vistula at Czerwinsk, where the army crossed on the bridge of boats',
                'credit' => 'Photo: VVerka5, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'battle',
                'photo' => 'banner',
                'label' => '15 July 1410',
                'year' => '1410',
                'title' => 'Two swords, a long day and a Grand Master dead',
                'body' => 'The armies met on 15 July between the villages of Grunwald, Stebark (Tannenberg to the Germans) and Lodwigowo. The chronicler Jan Dlugosz, whose father fought there, says the Grand Master sent two heralds with two naked swords, a taunt to come and fight. Early on the Lithuanian wing fell back, and historians still argue whether it fled or pulled back on purpose. The Polish royal banner went down and was raised again. Then Ulrich von Jungingen led his reserve in and was killed, with most of the Order’s high officers and about 200 of the roughly 250 knight-brothers in the field. The captured banners went to Wawel cathedral in Krakow, and in 1448 Dlugosz had them painted in a book.',
                'caption' => 'The Grand Master’s own lesser banner, as painted for Jan Dlugosz in 1448',
                'credit' => 'Banderia Prutenorum, Jagiellonian Library, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'peace',
                'photo' => 'treaty',
                'label' => 'The Peace of Torun',
                'year' => '1410 - 1411',
                'title' => 'The battle was won. The war was not',
                'body' => 'Jagiello reached Malbork ten days later and found it held. Heinrich von Plauen, an officer of the Order who had missed the battle, had shut himself in with the survivors, and after eight weeks of siege and sickness the allies went home. That autumn the Order made Plauen its Grand Master. On 1 February 1411, at Torun, the two sides made peace. Lithuania got Samogitia back, but only for the lifetimes of Jagiello and Vytautas. Poland got back the Dobrzyn land. The Order kept its state, Malbork and the coast, and agreed to pay 100,000 kopas of Prague groschen, six million silver coins, as ransom for prisoners. Above all, the treaty handed the Order a bill it could not pay.',
                'caption' => 'The Peace of Torun, 1 February 1411, with its seals',
                'credit' => 'Document, 1411, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'thirteen',
                'photo' => 'torun',
                'label' => 'What it settled',
                'year' => '1454 - 1525',
                'title' => 'The Order’s own towns rise, and the coast comes back',
                'body' => 'Paying meant taxing the Order’s towns and knights, and in 1440 they banded together in the Prussian Confederation. In February 1454 they broke with the Order and asked Jagiello’s son, King Casimir IV, to take them under his crown. In Torun the townspeople stormed the Order’s castle and pulled it down. Thirteen years of war followed. In 1457 Casimir bought Malbork from the Order’s unpaid mercenaries, and in 1466 the Second Peace of Torun gave Poland Gdansk, Malbork and the lower Vistula. The rest became a Polish vassal, and in 1525 the last Grand Master to rule it, Albrecht Hohenzollern, turned Lutheran and made it a duchy: the seed of the Prussia that later helped partition Poland.',
                'caption' => 'The ruins of the Order’s castle in Torun, pulled down by the townspeople in 1454',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Ola Zaparucha, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'memory',
                'photo' => 'matejko',
                'label' => 'The battle remembered',
                'year' => '1878 - 1945',
                'title' => 'Why every Polish child can date it',
                'body' => 'Grunwald became famous a second time, when there was no Poland to be proud of. In 1878, with the country partitioned, the painter Jan Matejko finished the Battle of Grunwald, a canvas almost ten metres wide, and in 1900 the novelist Henryk Sienkiewicz made the battle the climax of The Teutonic Knights. On 15 July 1910, the 500th anniversary, a huge crowd in Krakow saw the unveiling of a monument paid for by the pianist Ignacy Jan Paderewski. In 1914 the German army beat the Russians nearby and named the victory Tannenberg, as revenge for 1410. In 1939 the Germans pulled the Krakow monument down and hunted for the painting, which survived the war buried in Lublin.',
                'caption' => 'Vytautas as Jan Matejko imagined him in 1878, a detail of the Battle of Grunwald',
                'credit' => 'Painting: Jan Matejko, National Museum in Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'field',
                'label' => 'Where to stand in it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'An open field, a chapel over the dead, and Malbork',
                'body' => 'The battlefield is still open farmland, about 40 kilometres south-west of Olsztyn, with a museum that moved into a new building in 2022. The steel masts of the monument went up in 1960, when the communist state turned Grunwald into a story about Germany. Walk to the ruins of the chapel that Heinrich von Plauen had built on the field for the dead: archaeologists found more than 130 bodies in mass graves beside it. Every July, on the weekend nearest the 15th, over a thousand reenactors fight the battle again. Then go to Malbork, a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1997, to the rebuilt monument on Matejko Square in Krakow, and to the National Museum in Warsaw for the painting.',
                'caption' => 'The battlefield seen from the ruined chapel, with the 1960 monument on the skyline',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Lukasz Niemiec, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
