                'body' => 'Around 1300 Poland was still split between dukes of the Piast dynasty, and the king of Bohemia, Wenceslaus II, had himself crowned king of Poland. One of those dukes, Wladyslaw of Kuyavia, called Lokietek, elbow-high, because he was so short, went into exile. He came back after Wenceslaus died in 1305, took Krakow, then Greater Poland in 1314, and on 20 January 1320 became the first king crowned on Wawel. On the way he lost the coast: in 1308 the Teutonic Knights, a German crusading order ruling Prussia, seized Gdansk. His war with them ended in 1332 with Kuyavia, his own land, occupied.',
