                'title' => 'What he gave up in the west, he took in the east',
                'body' => 'In 1340 the boyars poisoned the ruler of Halych-Volhynia, Boleslaw Jerzy II, a Piast by birth who had promised Casimir his inheritance. Within weeks Casimir marched on Lviv, lost it, came back, and by 1349 held Lviv, Halych and Przemysl. The war over the rest, against Lithuania, then still a pagan grand duchy to the north-east, ran until 1366. For the first time the kingdom ruled large numbers of Orthodox Ruthenians, the ancestors of the Ukrainians of today, along with Armenians and Jews. By 1370 its area had roughly doubled. Lviv belonged to Poland for four centuries and again between the world wars. Today it is in Ukraine.',
