                'body' => 'Every Polish child learns that line. It comes from Jan Dlugosz, a Krakow chronicler writing a century later, who said Casimir found a Poland of clay, wood and dirt and left it of brick. Dlugosz and the king’s own vice-chancellor, Jan of Czarnkow, credit him with about fifty castles and 27 walled towns. Historians count anywhere from thirty to sixty, and some attributions are wishful. Two certainly go back to him: Ojcow near Krakow, a genuine ruin, and Bedzin, which guarded the new border with Silesia, although what stands there now is mostly a 1950s reconstruction. The castles along the hills north of Krakow are called the Eagles’ Nests, a name coined only in 1857.',
