                'body' => 'Greater and Lesser Poland had kept their own customs through two centuries apart, so Casimir had their laws written down separately, at Piotrkow for the north-west and at Wislica for the south, between about 1356 and 1362. He paid for this and for his castles largely with salt: the mines at Wieliczka and Bochnia belonged to the crown, his ordinance of 1368 regulated them, and salt brought in a quarter to a third of royal income. In the same years the Black Death killed a third or more of Europe’s people. Poland appears to have escaped the worst of it, though historians still argue over how far, and why.',
