                'body' => 'On 12 May 1364 Casimir founded a university in Krakow, the second in Central Europe after Prague. It was built to train the lawyers a kingdom needed: eight of its eleven planned chairs taught law, two medicine, one the liberal arts, and none theology. That September Krakow hosted the Emperor, Charles IV, the kings of Hungary and Cyprus and a string of dukes. The feast said to have been given for them by a merchant, Wierzynek, still gives its name to a restaurant on the Main Square, but it is known only from Dlugosz and may be a story. The university barely outlived its founder and was refounded in 1400.',
                'caption' => 'Collegium Maius, built in the 15th century for the refounded university',
