                'body' => 'In 1264 a duke of Greater Poland, Boleslaw the Pious, had granted the Jews of his duchy a charter protecting their synagogues and cemeteries and forbidding the blood libel, the accusation of ritual murder. Casimir confirmed it in 1334 and in 1364, and in 1367 extended it to his whole kingdom. It became the legal ground of Jewish life in Poland for centuries. Beside Krakow, in 1335, he founded a town named after himself, Kazimierz, which later became the city’s Jewish quarter. Dlugosz adds a Jewish mistress, Esterka. No document of the time mentions her, and the first charter came more than twenty years before her supposed affair.',
                'caption' => 'Jews received by Casimir, as Wojciech Gerson imagined it in 1874',
