            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The reign is known from the chronicle of Jan of Czarnkow, Casimir’s own vice-chancellor, and from his charters, several of which survive, among them the charter of Kazimierz of 1335 in the National Archive in Krakow. Jan Dlugosz, writing in the 15th century, is the source of the wood-and-brick line, of the Wierzynek feast and of Esterka. The last two are treated here as stories. The treaties of 1335 and 1343, the university charter of 1364 and the salt ordinance of 1368 are dated from the documents. The Black Death is a live debate: a 2022 study of pollen from 261 sites found farming in Poland carried on through the plague years, and other historians reply that pollen does not count the dead.',
