                'body' => 'The Peace of Torun of 1466 gave Jagiello\'s son, King Casimir IV, the lower Vistula and Gdansk. After he died in 1492, three of his sons reigned in turn: John Albert, Alexander and Sigismund. In 1505, at the Sejm in Radom, King Alexander accepted a law called, from its first words, Nihil novi: nothing new without common consent. From then on the king could make no new law without the Sejm, the parliament of the senators and the envoys of the nobles. In 1506 the chancellor, Jan Laski, printed the kingdom\'s laws with this woodcut. Alexander died that year, and the nobles elected his younger brother, Sigismund, later called the Old.',
