                'title' => 'A tomb chapel, and a bell that takes twelve men',
                'body' => 'In 1519 Sigismund ordered a burial chapel for himself and his family on the side of Wawel cathedral. Berrecci designed it and his workshop carved it, and it was consecrated in 1533: a square room under a dome in the Florentine style, one of the first buildings in Poland that is Renaissance through and through. The king lies there in red marble, with his son and heir, Sigismund Augustus, below him. In 1520 Sigismund also had a bell cast in Krakow, weighing 9,650 kilograms. The Sigismund bell still hangs in the cathedral tower, and twelve ringers swing it on the great church and national holidays.',
