                'title' => 'A canon from Torun puts the Sun in the middle',
                'body' => 'Nicolaus Copernicus was born in 1473 in Torun, one of the Prussian towns that had broken with the Teutonic Order and come under the Polish crown. He studied at the university in Krakow from 1491, then in Italy, and spent his adult life as a canon of Warmia, a territory near the Baltic ruled by its bishop. In the Order\'s last war on Poland, in 1521, he organised the defence of the canons\' castle at Olsztyn. All the while he was writing, in Latin, a book arguing that the Earth and the planets circle the Sun. De revolutionibus was printed in Nuremberg in 1543, the year he died. His manuscript, with this diagram in his own hand, is in the Jagiellonian Library in Krakow.',
                'caption' => 'The Sun at the centre, drawn by Copernicus in his manuscript of De revolutionibus',
