                'title' => 'The wealth came from peasants who could not leave',
                'body' => 'The grain did not come from free farmers. To grow more of it for sale, nobles ran home farms of their own, called folwarks, worked by the peasants who lived in their villages. In 1496 the Sejm at Piotrkow allowed only one peasant a year to leave a village. In 1520 King Sigismund\'s privilege of Torun obliged peasants to work for their lord, unpaid, at least one day a week for each lan, a standard farm, and by the end of the century the lords demanded more. The same privilege let nobles send their grain to Gdansk past the merchants of Torun. For most people in Poland the golden age meant this, not Wawel. A later topic on this site, on serfdom, follows that half of the story.',
                'caption' => 'The steward and the ploughmen, from a farming manual printed in Krakow in 1549',
