                'body' => 'Start on Wawel. The arcaded courtyard is free to walk into, and the castle\'s state rooms still hang with Sigismund Augustus\'s tapestries, which sat out the Second World War in Canada and came home in 1961. The Sigismund Chapel is inside the cathedral, and the tower climb takes you up to the bell. On the Main Square, the Cloth Hall got its Renaissance parapet after a fire in 1555. In Kazimierz, Krakow\'s old Jewish quarter, the Remuh synagogue of 1557 is still used for prayer. In Torun you can see the house where Copernicus is said to have been born, and in Frombork the cathedral where he was buried. In Gdansk the Crane belongs to the National Maritime Museum.',
                'caption' => 'The Cloth Hall on the Main Square in Krakow, with its parapet of the 1550s',
