        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The laws of 1496, 1505 and 1520 survive as texts, and the Laski Statute of 1506 survives in printed copies. The dates for Wawel, the Sigismund Chapel and the bell follow the Wawel cathedral and published histories of the Wawel buildings. The grain figures come from the customs registers of Wloclawek and Gdansk. The share of Europe\'s grain is one modern estimate among several. The Copernicus manuscript has been in the Jagiellonian Library since 1956 and on UNESCO\'s Memory of the World register since 1999. That soup vegetables are named after Bona\'s Italians is tradition, not record. Title photograph: Zygmunt Put, CC BY-SA 4.0.',
