                'title' => 'Half an hour on by train, the doors and the saint',
                'body' => 'From Poznan the train to Gniezno takes 25 to 50 minutes, and Lednica lies on the way for anyone driving. The cathedral stands on the hill of the first stronghold. Its bronze doors, cast around 1175, tell the life of Saint Adalbert, the missionary buried here, in eighteen scenes, and the nave ends at his silver coffin. From April to October you can climb the south tower for the view over the town and the lakes. Across lake Jelonek, the Museum of the Origins of the Polish State shows what the excavations found. The story behind all of it is told in the article on Gniezno.',
