                'label' => 'Kruszwica',
                'year' => '12th c. - 1350',
                'title' => 'A tower from a legend, built by a real king',
                'body' => 'Kruszwica, on Lake Goplo about two hours east of Poznan by car, is where the chronicler Gallus Anonymus set the legend of Popiel, the ruler said to have been eaten by mice. The Mouse Tower named after it is real but centuries younger: an octagonal brick tower 32 metres high, all that stands of a castle Casimir the Great built around 1350, and you can climb it. The Romanesque collegiate church in town is 12th-century, though its clean look owes a lot to a restoration in 1954-1956. Twenty minutes away, the church in Strzelno has stone columns carved with the Virtues and the Vices, hidden under plaster until 1946.',
