                'photo' => 'salt',
                'label' => 'Wieliczka',
                'year' => 'from the 1280s',
                'title' => 'The castle is medieval, most of the mine is not',
                'body' => 'Wieliczka is 25 minutes from Krakow by train, and its salt paid for much of what Casimir built. Before you go down, know that the famous chambers and chapels on the tourist route were carved from the 17th century on, and the route itself was laid out around 1800. The Middle Ages are above ground, in the Saltworks Castle, the seat of the royal mines, with walls and a tower from the late 1200s and 1300s and a museum inside. The oldest known shaft was sunk around 1288. The mine went on the UNESCO list in 1978, and in 2013 the listing grew to take in the castle and the mine at Bochnia.',
                'caption' => 'The Saltworks Castle in Wieliczka, seat of the royal salt mines',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'castles',
                'photo' => 'castles',
                'label' => 'Castles and Wislica',
                'year' => '1170s - 1464',
                'title' => 'One real ruin, a Renaissance castle and a rebuilt one',
                'body' => 'North of Krakow the limestone hills carry a line of castles on the Trail of the Eagles’ Nests, a hiking route marked in 1950. Ojcow, 25 kilometres out, is a genuine ruin of the castle Casimir built in about 1354-1370: this gate tower, an octagonal tower and low walls. Pieskowa Skala nearby is older in origin, but what you see is a Renaissance residence of 1542-1580, and Bedzin, an hour west, is mostly a reconstruction of 1952-1956. Wislica, a car day to the north-east, has Casimir’s Gothic church over a floor engraved with praying figures around 1175. The crypt was recently closed for renovation, so ask before you go.',
                'caption' => 'The gate tower of Casimir’s castle at Ojcow',
