                'photo' => 'further',
                'label' => 'Further out',
                'year' => '1140 - 1161',
                'title' => 'A bronze door that is a copy, and a church in a field',
                'body' => 'Two places sit off both routes. Plock, about two hours by bus from Warsaw, was the seat of the dukes of Poland in the late 1000s and early 1100s, and its cathedral keeps remains identified by tradition as two of them, Wladyslaw Herman and his son Boleslaw Wrymouth. Its bronze door, cast in Magdeburg in 1152-1154, is a copy hung in 1981: the original has hung for centuries in St Sophia’s Cathedral in Novgorod, in Russia, and nobody is sure how it got there. Tum, near Leczyca and fifty minutes by car from Lodz, has the Romanesque church in this photograph, begun around 1140 and consecrated in 1161.',
                'caption' => 'The Romanesque collegiate church in Tum, near Leczyca',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Chrumps, CC BY-SA 4.0',
