                'title' => 'A king with no son and a war in the east',
                'body' => 'Since the Union of Krewo in 1385 Poland and Lithuania had shared a ruler but stayed two states, and their king, Sigismund Augustus, had no son to hold them together. From 1558 Lithuania fought Muscovy, the rising Russian state, for Livonia, today\'s Latvia and Estonia, and in 1563 Tsar Ivan IV, the Terrible, took the Lithuanian fortress of Polotsk. Lithuania needed Polish troops, but its great lords feared losing power. In 1569 the king called both parliaments to Lublin, and on 1 March the Lithuanian lords rode home. By June he had moved Podlasie, Volhynia and the lands of Kyiv and Braclaw, over a third of Lithuania, to the Polish crown, and the Lithuanians came back.',
