                'body' => 'Every noble could vote for the next king, and in May 1573 some 40,000 came to a field outside Warsaw and chose Henry of Valois, brother of Charles IX of France, whom many blamed for the Paris massacre. First he had to swear the Henrician Articles: the king is elected and names no heir, calls the Sejm regularly, makes no war and raises no tax without it, and keeps the religious peace. If he broke them, the nobles could refuse to obey. Crowned on Wawel in February 1574, he heard in June that his brother had died and fled by night to take the French throne. The nobles then elected Stefan Batory of Transylvania, and in 1587 Sigismund III Vasa, a devout Catholic prince of Sweden.',
                'caption' => 'A French franc of 1576 that still calls Henry king of France and Poland',
