                'body' => 'In the east most people were Ruthenians, ancestors of today\'s Ukrainians and Belarusians, and Orthodox. Their greatest lord was Prince Konstanty Ostrogski. In 1596, at a synod in Brest, a Lithuanian town now in Belarus, the Metropolitan of Kyiv, Michael Rahoza, and most Orthodox bishops accepted the Pope\'s authority but kept their Eastern rite, hoping for reform and wary of Moscow. This Union of Brest founded the Greek Catholic Church. Ostrogski and two bishops refused, and the king recognised only the union. Only in 1620 did the Patriarch of Jerusalem, Theophanes, consecrate new Orthodox bishops, and only in 1632 did Sigismund III\'s son, Wladyslaw IV, recognise them.',
                'caption' => 'The church of St Paraskeva at Radruz, built in the 1580s, later Greek Catholic',
