                'year' => '1563 - 1658',
                'title' => 'Protestant lords, Jesuit schools and an expulsion',
                'body' => 'By the 1560s many great nobles were Protestant. Mikolaj Radziwill the Black, the most powerful lord in Lithuania, was a Calvinist and paid for a Polish Bible printed at Brest in 1563. The German-speaking towns on the lower Vistula, Gdansk among them, were Lutheran. The Polish Brethren, whom their enemies called Arians, rejected the Trinity. But from 1564 the Jesuits, a Catholic teaching order brought in by Stanislaw Hozjusz, bishop of Warmia, did much through their schools to win the nobles back to Rome. In 1658 the Sejm, blaming the Brethren for siding with the Swedes who invaded in 1655, told them to convert or leave.',
                'caption' => 'The title page of the Brest Bible, printed in 1563 at Radziwill\'s expense',
                'credit' => 'Print, Brest 1563, National Museum in Krakow, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'neighbours',
                'photo' => 'mosque',
                'label' => 'Tatars and Armenians',
                'year' => '1363 - 1679',
                'title' => 'Muslims, Armenians and Karaites',
                'body' => 'Around 1400 Vytautas, grand duke of Lithuania and cousin of King Wladyslaw Jagiello, settled Muslim Tatars from the steppe in his lands. They served as light cavalry and kept their mosques. The Karaites, a Jewish community that follows the Bible but not the Talmud, say he also brought them to his castle town of Trakai. Armenian merchants, Christians with a church of their own, built a cathedral in Lviv from 1363 and in 1585 the chancellor Jan Zamoyski invited them to his new town of Zamosc. In 1679 King Jan III Sobieski paid Tatar officers with villages near Bialystok, Kruszyniany among them. Lviv is in Ukraine today, Trakai in Lithuania.',
                'caption' => 'The wooden mosque at Kruszyniany, probably built in the second half of the 1700s',
                'credit' => 'Photo: AnnaBanasiak, CC BY-SA 4.0',
