                'body' => 'The Ottoman army left Edirne, near today\'s Greek border, at the end of March and reached Vienna on 14 July 1683. How many men it had is uncertain. Estimates start at about 90,000. Leopold and his court had left a week earlier. The city\'s commander, Count Ernst Rudiger von Starhemberg, held the walls with some 11,000 soldiers and 5,000 armed citizens. Kara Mustafa camped west of the city and set his sappers digging: from late July mines blew holes in the bastions, and men fought hand to hand in the ditch. By September the city was close to falling. About 5,000 of its defenders died in the siege.',
