                'year' => '1683',
                'title' => '12 September: the hussars ride down the hill',
                'body' => 'The battle began at dawn on 12 September on the allied left, by the Danube, where imperial and Saxon troops fought all morning through vineyards and villages. The Poles, on the right, came out of the woods around midday. A first probing charge was beaten back, and one of its officers, Stanislaw Potocki, was killed. Late in the afternoon the Polish cavalry, the winged hussars in front, charged down into the Ottoman camp as the Germans pushed in from the left. Hussars were heavy cavalry with lances over four metres long, many with a feathered frame on the back: the wings of their name. The Ottoman army broke and fled, leaving its camp and guns behind.',
                'caption' => 'A winged hussar in full armour on a mounted figure, Polish Army Museum, Warsaw',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Thomas Quine, CC BY 2.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'letter',
                'photo' => 'tent',
                'label' => 'In the vizier\'s tent',
                'year' => '1683',
                'title' => 'Letters from the vizier\'s tent',
                'body' => 'That night Sobieski wrote to Marysienka from Kara Mustafa\'s own tents: God had given "our nation victory and glory such as past ages never heard of", and the vizier had fled "barely on one horse and in one robe". She had the letter printed as news. To Pope Innocent XI he sent a captured banner with his secretary, Tommaso Talenti, and a letter that began Venimus, vidimus, Deus vicit: we came, we saw, God conquered. On 15 September, at Schwechat outside Vienna, Leopold received him coldly, and Sobieski complained of it to Marysienka. Kara Mustafa fell back to Belgrade, where the sultan had him strangled on 25 December.',
                'caption' => 'An Ottoman two-masted tent of the 1600s in the collection of Wawel Castle',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Mach240390, CC BY 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'after',
                'photo' => 'kamianets',
                'label' => 'What it brought',
                'year' => '1683 - 1699',
                'title' => 'Glory for the king, little land for the Commonwealth',
                'body' => 'Catholic Europe hailed the victory as the rescue of Christendom, and Innocent XI gave the whole Church a feast on 12 September, the Holy Name of Mary. The war went on, from 1684 as a Holy League of the emperor, the Commonwealth and Venice. The Habsburgs took Hungary. Sobieski\'s campaigns into Moldavia won nothing lasting, and at home, of six Sejms called from 1688 to 1695, only one reached a conclusion, because one deputy could stop a session. He died at Wilanow on 17 June 1696. At Karlowitz, in today\'s Serbia, the Commonwealth got back in 1699 only what it had lost in 1672, Kamianets and Podolia. Historians add that the Ottoman Empire stayed a great power well into the 1700s.',
                'caption' => 'The castle of Kamianets in Podolia, returned to the Commonwealth in 1699',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Rbrechko, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'wilanow',
                'label' => 'Where to see it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'A palace, a tent, a tomb and a hill above Vienna',
                'body' => 'In Warsaw, Wilanow Palace, built by Sobieski on an estate he bought in 1677, is now the museum of his reign. In Krakow, Wawel Castle shows hussar armour and the largest collection of Ottoman tents in any European museum. Tradition ties some of them to Vienna. In the cathedral crypt below, Sobieski and Marysienka were buried together in 1734. In Vienna, the church on the Kahlenberg has been kept by Polish priests since 1906 and has a Sobieski chapel. But the hill the armies called the Kahlenberg in 1683 is the one next to it, known today as the Leopoldsberg.',
