                'body' => 'The golden age ran on grain floated down the Vistula to Gdansk, and nobles grew it on home farms of their own, called folwarks: 60 to 80 hectares of fields, with barns, granaries and a manor house. Peasants from the lord\'s villages did the work. The laws of 1496 and 1520 had already tied them to the village and to unpaid labour. In the 16th century peasants were just under 70 per cent of the population. The nobles, the szlachta, were between about 6 and 10 per cent, depending on the estimate.',
