                'body' => 'The unpaid work was called panszczyzna and counted in days a week from each lan, the standard farm. The one day of 1520 had grown to two or three by the late 16th century. In the 17th and 18th centuries many estates demanded four to six, and historians give ranges, because it varied by estate and by how a farm was counted. A farmer with oxen came with his team, a smallholder on foot. Many peasants simply ran away east, to the steppe, where the Cossacks lived free, and joined Bohdan Khmelnytsky\'s Cossack rising of 1648.',
