                'year' => '1518 - 1768',
                'title' => 'The lord was also his peasants\' judge',
                'body' => 'In 1518 King Sigismund the Old stopped the royal courts from hearing complaints by peasants against the lords of noble and Church estates. From then on the lord or his steward was the only court, with no appeal. Even a killing was priced by rank: from 1493 a noble who killed a noble paid 120 grzywna, a weight of silver, and went to prison. For a peasant he paid 10. In 1543 the writer Andrzej Frycz Modrzewski attacked that law in print. The price of a peasant rose to 30 grzywna in 1581 and 100 in 1631, but it stayed a fine until 1768, when the Sejm, the nobles\' parliament, made the deliberate killing of a peasant by a noble a capital crime.',
