                'year' => '1600 - 1800',
                'title' => 'A smoky hut, a church and the lord\'s tavern',
                'body' => 'Peasants lived in wooden huts under thatch. Around 1800 a large share of them, perhaps most, had no chimney: smoke from the stove seeped out through the roof, and the door stood open to let it go, even in winter. Poles call such a house a kurna chata, a smoke hut. Besides the church, the village had the lord\'s tavern, the karczma. By a right called propinacja only the lord could brew beer and distil vodka on his land, and his peasants had to buy it from him, in amounts he set. Lords usually leased the tavern out, often to a Jewish tenant, and drink could bring in half an estate\'s income.',
