                'title' => 'Kosciuszko promises the peasants their freedom',
                'body' => 'The Constitution of 3 May 1791, a later topic here, put the peasants under "the protection of the law" but kept serfdom. After the partitions of 1772 and 1793 had handed much of the country to Russia, Prussia and Austria, Tadeusz Kosciuszko, a veteran of the American War of Independence, led a rising against Russia and Prussia in 1794. Peasants with scythes fought for him at Raclawice on 4 April. On 7 May, near Polaniec, he declared them personally free and safe from eviction, owing a third to half less labour while the rising lasted. Most nobles resisted it, and by 1795 the state was gone.',
                'caption' => 'The Polaniec proclamation, reissued in print in Warsaw on 30 May 1794',
