                'body' => 'After 1795 Russia, Prussia and Austria divided what was left. In 1807 the French emperor Napoleon, having beaten Prussia, made the Duchy of Warsaw out of its Polish lands, with a constitution whose Article 4 reads: slavery is abolished. A decree of 21 December let peasants move freely but left the farms to the lords, who could now evict them. Poles say the chains came off together with the boots. Prussia began abolishing serfdom in 1807 too, and from 1823 the larger farmers of the Grand Duchy of Posen, its share of Poland after 1815, could buy their farms with money, land or labour.',
                'caption' => 'Article 4 of the constitution of the Duchy of Warsaw: slavery is abolished, 1807',
