                'body' => 'In Galicia, the Austrian share, serfdom went on. In February 1846 Polish conspirators, most of them nobles, planned a rising against Austria. Around Tarnow the Austrian district chief, Joseph Breinl, told the villages to seize the insurgents and paid for those brought in, alive or dead. The peasants turned on the manors: estimates of the dead run from about 1,000 to 3,000, mostly landowners, their families and stewards, and some 500 manors were looted. The best-known leader was a peasant, Jakub Szela. On 22 April 1848 the governor, Count Franz Stadion, ended labour dues from 15 May, and villages thanked the emperor, Ferdinand I.',
                'caption' => 'Cross at Rudka "in memory of the freedom given to us" under Emperor Ferdinand, 1848',
