                'title' => 'The tsar hands the land to the villages',
                'body' => 'Last came the Kingdom of Poland, the part of the Russian share that the tsar ruled as a separate kingdom from 1815. Tsar Alexander II freed the serfs of Russia itself in 1861. In January 1863 Poles rose against Russian rule, and the insurgents\' National Government promised peasants their land without payment. To win the villages over, Alexander II decreed the same on 2 March 1864: each family got its farm outright, dues ended, and the state paid off the lords. Officials drew up a liquidation table for every village. Serfdom on Polish land was over, and the story moves on to the partitions era.',
                'caption' => 'Liquidation table for the village of Zazolkiew, Lublin province, under the 1864 decree',
