                'body' => 'The laws were made by the Sejm, the parliament, and it had three parts: the king, the Senate of bishops and high officials, and a chamber of some 170 envoys. Each envoy was chosen at a sejmik, the assembly of the nobles of his district, which sent him to Warsaw with written instructions. Only the Sejm could vote taxes and decide how many soldiers to pay. Its decisions were supposed to be unanimous, but in the 1500s that meant long arguments, after which a doubting minority usually gave way.',
