                'photo' => 'radziwill',
                'label' => 'Sicinski, 1652',
                'year' => '1652 - 1669',
                'title' => 'One envoy says no, and walks out',
                'body' => 'On 9 March 1652 the Sejm in Warsaw had run out of time and needed to agree to sit longer. Wladyslaw Sicinski, an envoy from Upita in Lithuania, objected and walked out. Historians have long suspected he was acting for Janusz Radziwill, Lithuania\'s field commander and an enemy of King John Casimir. The marshal, Andrzej Maksymilian Fredro, found no way round the protest and closed the Sejm on 11 March without a single law. In 1669 an envoy from Volhynia, Adam Olizar, broke a Sejm before its time was up. The liberum veto, the free "I do not allow", had become a weapon.',
                'caption' => 'Janusz Radziwill, the Lithuanian commander Sicinski is said to have served',
