                'title' => 'A king from Saxony and a Sejm that kept silent',
                'body' => 'One way round the veto was a confederation, a sworn league of nobles that decided by majority. In 1697 the nobles elected the Elector of Saxony, in Germany, as Augustus II. Charles XII of Sweden, at war with Augustus and Russia in the Great Northern War, had him deposed, and a Polish great lord, Stanislaw Leszczynski, reigned until 1709. Augustus came back with Saxon troops, and in 1715 nobles formed a confederation at Tarnogrod against them. Russia\'s envoy, Grigory Dolgorukov, brokered peace, and on 1 February 1717 a Sejm ringed by Russian soldiers passed it in one day. No envoy was allowed to speak, so none could break it: the Silent Sejm.',
                'caption' => 'Augustus II, Elector of Saxony and King of Poland, from Louis de Silvestre\'s workshop',
