            ],
            [
                'key' => 'double',
                'photo' => 'danzig',
                'label' => 'Two kings, 1733',
                'year' => '1720 - 1736',
                'title' => 'One king for the nobles, one for Russia',
                'body' => 'The Silent Sejm fixed the army at 24,000 men, and from then on Russia acted as the guardian of the settlement. In 1720, at Potsdam, Russia and Prussia agreed to defend the Commonwealth\'s free election and noble liberties, which kept it weak. When Augustus II died in 1733, most nobles wanted a Pole, and on 12 September they elected Leszczynski again. On 5 October his opponents, shielded by Russian troops across the Vistula, elected the dead king\'s son as Augustus III at Kamien, the field of 1573. Leszczynski held out in Gdansk until a Russian and Saxon siege took it in 1734. A Sejm in 1736 made the peace and accepted Augustus III.',
                'caption' => 'Homann\'s map of the siege of Gdansk, 1734, with the Russian fleet off the coast',
                'credit' => 'Engraving, Nuremberg, about 1739, Polona, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'saxon',
                'photo' => 'augustus3',
                'label' => 'The Saxon king',
                'year' => '1736 - 1763',
                'title' => 'Thirty years, and one Sejm that passed a law',
                'body' => 'That Sejm of 1736 was the only one in Augustus III\'s thirty-year reign to pass any law. Every other Sejm was broken or ended without agreement. Russia and Prussia, and at times France, paid envoys to do it, and the great families, the magnates, broke Sejms to block their rivals. Older histories blamed the nobles\' "anarchy" for the fall of the state. Historians today point as much to the magnates and to the neighbours who paid them. Nobles remembered a peaceful time: za krola Sasa jedz, pij i popuszczaj pasa, under the Saxon king, eat, drink and loosen your belt.',
                'caption' => 'Augustus III, King of Poland from 1733, painted by Louis de Silvestre',
                'credit' => 'Painting, National Museum in Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'poniatowski',
                'photo' => 'bellotto',
                'label' => 'Poniatowski, 1764',
                'year' => '1761 - 1764',
                'title' => 'Elected with Russian troops nearby',
                'body' => 'From 1761 Stanislaw Konarski, a Piarist priest and teacher, argued in four volumes that the veto must go, and the Czartoryski family, the Familia, wanted reform. When Augustus III died in 1763, they asked Russia for troops. For the throne they put forward their nephew, Stanislaw Antoni Poniatowski, once the lover of Russia\'s empress, Catherine II. In April 1764 Russia and Prussia agreed to keep the Commonwealth\'s laws unchanged. On 7 September a thin crowd of nobles at Wola, with Russian soldiers present, elected Poniatowski. Crowned in Warsaw as Stanislaw August, he began with Sejms held as a confederation, which no veto could stop.',
                'caption' => 'Bernardo Bellotto\'s Election of Stanislaw August, 1778, painted from reports of 1764',
                'credit' => 'Painting, Royal Castle in Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'repnin',
                'photo' => 'cake',
                'label' => 'Repnin and Bar',
                'year' => '1767 - 1772',
                'title' => 'Russia\'s ambassador has bishops seized',
                'body' => 'Catherine II wanted reform stopped, and she backed equal rights for the Protestants and Orthodox. Her ambassador, Prince Nikolai Repnin, had nobles form a confederation at Radom against the king. When its leaders, among them Bishop Kajetan Soltyk of Krakow, opposed those rights, Russian soldiers seized them one night in October 1767 and took them to Kaluga, in Russia. In February 1768 the Sejm passed "cardinal laws", the veto and free election among them, under Catherine\'s guarantee. On 29 February nobles formed the Bar Confederation against Russia and the king. Four years of war ended in the First Partition, where the next era begins.',
                'caption' => 'The Troelfth Cake, 1773: Catherine II, Stanislaw August and two neighbours over a map',
                'credit' => 'Engraving by Noel Le Mire, London 1773, British Museum, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'senate',
                'label' => 'Where to see it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'A castle hall and a field of kings in Warsaw',
                'body' => 'Start at the Royal Castle in Warsaw, where the Sejm met. German bombs and shells wrecked it in 1939, German troops blew up the ruins in 1944, and it was rebuilt from 1971. In its Senators\' Hall the king, senators and envoys sat together, and there the Constitution of 3 May 1791, the next topic here, was passed. Next door, in the Canaletto Room, hangs Bellotto\'s election of 1764. Altomonte\'s election of 1697 belongs to the castle too. Out in Wola, on a small square, a column with a crown, put up in 1997, marks the field where the kings were chosen.',
                'caption' => 'The Senators\' Hall of the Royal Castle in Warsaw, rebuilt after 1971',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
        ],

        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The Sejms follow Dzieje Sejmu, the history site of the Chancellery of the Sejm, which records each session and how it ended, and the Museum of Polish History. That only the Sejm of 1736 passed laws under Augustus III follows the historians Zofia Zielinska, Adam Perlakowski and Jerzy Lukowski. That Russia and Prussia used the veto and paid envoys follows Urszula Kosinska. That Sicinski acted for Radziwill is an old suspicion, not a proof. The 1764 election follows the Royal Lazienki Museum and the Royal Castle, whose paintings by Altomonte and Bellotto are depictions by an eyewitness and by a painter working from reports. Title picture: a detail of Bellotto\'s Election of Stanislaw August, 1778, public domain.',
