                'label' => 'National schools',
                'year' => '1773 - 1788',
                'title' => 'Out of the defeat, a ministry for schools',
                'body' => 'The same Sejm did one more thing. Pope Clement XIV had just dissolved the Jesuits, who ran many of the country\'s schools, and on 14 October 1773 the Sejm gave their property to a Commission of National Education, often called Europe\'s first ministry of education. Its schools taught in Polish rather than Latin and added physics and natural history. Stanislaw Konarski, the priest whose books had attacked the veto, died that August, but the authors of 1791 drew on his ideas. In Warsaw the king gave Thursday dinners for writers and scholars.',
                'caption' => 'The Commission\'s grammar for the third class of its schools, 1783',
                'credit' => 'Title page, Society for Elementary Books, 1783, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'sejm',
                'photo' => 'diary',
                'label' => 'The Great Sejm',
                'year' => '1788 - 1790',
                'title' => 'A Sejm that no single envoy could break',
                'body' => 'In 1787 Russia went to war with the Ottoman Empire, and its armies were busy in the south. On 6 October 1788 a Sejm opened in Warsaw and at once formed a confederation, so it voted by majority and no veto could break it. It voted an army of 100,000 men, against some 18,500 then, and in January 1789 abolished the Permanent Council, set up under Russian protection in 1775. On 29 March 1790 it signed an alliance with Prussia, which promised help if Russia attacked. The Sejm never went home. In late 1790 newly elected envoys joined the old ones, and it sat on in double strength until 1792, hence its name, the Four-Year Sejm.',
                'caption' => 'The Sejm\'s official diary, opened in 1788 under the bond of a confederation',
                'credit' => 'Printed title page, Warsaw 1788, Central Archives of Historical Records, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'patriots',
                'photo' => 'kollataj',
                'label' => 'Towns and patriots',
                'year' => '1789 - 1791',
                'title' => 'Burghers in black, and a draft kept secret',
                'body' => 'On 2 December 1789 delegates of 141 towns, dressed in black and led by the mayor of Warsaw, Jan Dekert, walked to the Royal Castle with a petition: the Black Procession. A Law on Towns of 18 April 1791 gave burghers protection from arrest without trial and the right to buy country estates. Meanwhile a small circle wrote a constitution in secret: the king, Ignacy Potocki (a Lithuanian magnate who led the reformers, the Patriotic Party), Hugo Kollataj (a priest and school reformer) and Scipione Piattoli, the Italian secretary of the king, who made the fair copies.',
                'caption' => 'Hugo Kollataj in 1791, the year of the constitution, painted by Jozef Peszka',
                'credit' => 'Painting, 1791, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'day',
                'photo' => 'wojniakowski',
                'label' => '3 May 1791',
                'year' => '2 - 3 May 1791',
                'title' => 'Voted in one day, with many envoys still away',
                'body' => 'The vote was set for 5 May, but the Patriots brought it forward while many opponents were still away after Easter. On 3 May, a Tuesday, 182 members met in the Senators\' Hall of the Royal Castle. Jan Suchorzewski, an envoy from Kalisz, dragged his small son into the middle of the hall and threatened to kill him, so that the boy would not live to see slavery. After some seven hours the act passed by acclamation. Historians count 110 for and 72 against. The king swore it on the spot and led the envoys to St John\'s church nearby to swear it again.',
                'caption' => 'The vote in the Senators\' Hall, painted by Kazimierz Wojniakowski in 1806',
                'credit' => 'Painting, 1806, National Museum in Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'act',
                'photo' => 'print',
                'label' => 'The Government Act',
                'year' => '1791',
                'title' => 'The veto is gone and the crown is hereditary',
                'body' => 'The Government Act has eleven articles. All power, it says, comes from the will of the nation, and it splits that power between a Sejm that makes the laws, a king who governs with a council called the Guardians of the Laws, and the courts. The veto and confederations are abolished, and the majority decides. Free election goes too: after Stanislaw August the crown passes to the Elector of Saxony, Frederick Augustus, and his heirs. It is usually called Europe\'s first modern written constitution and the world\'s second, after the American one of 1787. In October 1791 the Mutual Guarantee of the Two Nations, Poland and Lithuania, kept Lithuania an equal partner.',
                'caption' => 'The act as printed in 1791: Prawo uchwalone dnia 3 Maia, the law passed on 3 May',
                'credit' => 'Printed pamphlet, 1791, Polona, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'peasants',
                'photo' => 'norblin',
                'label' => 'The peasants',
                'year' => '1791',
                'title' => 'Most of the country got one sentence',
                'body' => 'Peasants were, in the act\'s own words, the most numerous part of the nation, and Article IV took them "under the protection of the law and the national government". What it protected were agreements a lord chose to make with his village, and nothing made him sign one. Serfdom stayed, as the serfdom page tells. Real freedom went to newcomers: anyone arriving from abroad was free the moment he set foot on Polish soil, because the state wanted settlers. Serfs were first promised personal freedom in 1794, during a rising against Russia.',
                'caption' => 'A peasant with a scythe, after Jean-Pierre Norblin, a French artist in Poland',
                'credit' => 'Print, Paris 1817, Bibliotheque nationale de France, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'targowica',
                'photo' => 'poniatowski',
                'label' => 'Targowica, 1792',
                'year' => '1792 - 1793',
                'title' => 'Russia invades, and the king gives in',
                'body' => 'Russia made peace with the Ottomans and turned west. On 27 April 1792, in St Petersburg, magnates who opposed the constitution, led by Stanislaw Szczesny Potocki, signed a confederation against it, proclaimed at Targowica, in Ukraine. On 18 May nearly 100,000 Russian troops crossed the border, and Prussia refused to help. The king\'s nephew, Prince Jozef Poniatowski, won at Zielence on 18 June, a victory the king marked by founding the Virtuti Militari order. Tadeusz Kosciuszko, a veteran of the American War of Independence, held off a larger force at Dubienka. On 24 July the king gave up and joined Targowica. In January 1793 Russia and Prussia agreed a Second Partition.',
                'caption' => 'Prince Jozef Poniatowski, the king\'s nephew, painted by Josef Grassi in 1792',
                'credit' => 'Painting, 1792, Royal Castle in Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'garden',
                'label' => 'Where to see it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'A holiday, a painting and a stone in Warsaw',
                'body' => 'In 1793 a Sejm at Grodno, under Russian pressure, annulled the constitution after fourteen months. Independent Poland made 3 May a state holiday in 1919. The communists banned marches on 3 May 1946, the security police broke up students who marched in Krakow anyway, and in 1951 it was struck off the list of holidays. It came back in 1990. At the Royal Castle, next to the Senators\' Hall, hangs Jan Matejko\'s huge painting of 1891, a centenary homage rather than a record: it even shows Jan Dekert, who died in 1790. The Temple of Divine Providence, a church the Sejm vowed in 1791, opened in Wilanow only in 2016.',
                'caption' => 'The spot in the University of Warsaw Botanic Garden where the 1792 stone was laid',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
