    'partition1' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The First Partition, 1772',
            'description' => 'Why Russia, Prussia and Austria took almost a third of the Commonwealth in 1772, what each of them took, and how the Sejm was made to sign it.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The partitions · Topic 01',
            'title' => 'The First Partition',
            'title_accent' => 'of 1772',
            'lede' => 'Nine frames, from a Russian war with the Ottomans to a Sejm that signed away almost a third of the country with foreign troops inside it. Why three rival monarchs agreed, what each of them took, who tried to stop it, and where you can still see it. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about eight minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'war',
                'photo' => 'chesma',
                'label' => 'Russia\'s Turkish war',
                'year' => '1768 - 1771',
                'title' => 'A Polish revolt pulls Russia into a Turkish war',
                'body' => 'This era opens where the veto page ended, with the Bar Confederation of 1768, a league of nobles fighting both Russia\'s hold on the Commonwealth and Stanislaw August, the king Russia had helped to elect. They held out until 1772 and lost, but their war spread. Chasing them, a troop of Cossacks crossed the southern border and sacked Balta, a town on the Ottoman side, and in October 1768 the Ottoman Empire declared war on Russia. Catherine II, the Russian empress, won on land and at sea: by 1770 her armies held Moldavia and Wallachia, in today\'s Romania, close to Austria\'s lands on the Danube. In Vienna there was talk of war with Russia.',
                'caption' => 'The Ottoman fleet burning at Chesma in 1770, painted for Catherine II by Jakob Hackert',
                'credit' => 'Painting, 1771, Peterhof Museum, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'zips',
                'photo' => 'lubowla',
                'label' => 'The Spisz towns',
                'year' => '1769 - 1770',
                'title' => 'Austria moves first, in the mountains',
                'body' => 'Austria had an excuse of its own. In 1412 Sigismund of Luxembourg, king of Hungary, had pawned Lubowla castle and a string of towns in Spisz, Zips in German, below the Tatra mountains, to the Polish king, Wladyslaw Jagiello. The loan was never repaid, and for more than 350 years Polish starosts, royal governors, ran the district. In 1769 Austrian troops marched in on behalf of the Hungarian crown, which Maria Theresa, the Habsburg empress, also wore. In 1770 they moved their posts north, around Nowy Targ, Nowy Sacz and Czorsztyn. Her chancellor, Wenzel Anton von Kaunitz, had warned that others would copy it.',
                'caption' => 'Lubowla castle, seat of the Polish starosts of Spisz, now Stara Lubovna in Slovakia',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Miro Svorc, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'henry',
                'photo' => 'frederick',
                'label' => 'Prince Henry\'s visit',
                'year' => '1770 - 1772',
                'title' => 'A joke in St Petersburg becomes a plan',
                'body' => 'Frederick II, king of Prussia, ruled a state in pieces: Brandenburg and Pomerania in the west, East Prussia around Konigsberg in the east, and between them Royal Prussia, a Polish province on the lower Vistula. In his political testaments of 1752 and 1768 he named taking it as a task for the future. His brother, Prince Henry, spent the winter of 1770-1771 in St Petersburg. There, in January 1771, Catherine suggested half in jest that if Austria could take Spisz, others might take something too. Henry brought the idea home and Frederick pressed it: Russia would be paid for its war in Polish land, not Ottoman. Early in 1772 Russia and Prussia agreed who would take what.',
                'caption' => 'Frederick II of Prussia, painted by Anton Graff in the early 1780s',
                'credit' => 'Painting by Anton Graff, about 1781-1786, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'treaty',
                'photo' => 'theresa',
                'label' => 'St Petersburg, 1772',
                'year' => '1771 - 1772',
                'title' => 'Signed in the name of the Holy Trinity',
                'body' => 'Maria Theresa was against any partition. Kaunitz argued that Austria could not let Russia and Prussia grow without it. Her son and co-ruler, Joseph II, wanted even more. She gave way, reluctantly and bitterly. On 5 August 1772, in St Petersburg, envoys of the three powers signed treaties that opened in the name of the Most Holy Trinity, and their armies marched in. Their manifestos blamed Polish anarchy and dug up old rights: Austria\'s rested on medieval Hungary\'s rule over Galicia-Volhynia, so it named its share the Kingdom of Galicia and Lodomeria. Catherine said she had never ratified anything with greater satisfaction.',
                'caption' => 'Maria Theresa in a widow\'s black, painted by Franz Messmer around 1770',
                'credit' => 'Painting, Belvedere, Vienna, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'shares',
                'photo' => 'zannoni',
                'label' => 'Who took what',
                'year' => '1772',
                'title' => 'Nearly a third of the country is gone',
                'body' => 'Historians\' figures differ, so treat these as rough. Russia took the largest and poorest area, some 84,000 to 92,000 square kilometres with about 1.3 million people: Polish Livonia and the lands of Polotsk, Vitebsk and Mstislavl, beyond the Dvina and Dnieper rivers. Austria took some 83,000 square kilometres and the most people, well over two and a half million: the south, with Lwow and the salt mines of Wieliczka and Bochnia, though not Krakow. Kaunitz admitted that Lwow and the salt were Austria\'s only claims of real value. Prussia got the smallest share, some 35,000 square kilometres, and the richest: Royal Prussia and the bishopric of Warmia, without Gdansk and Torun.',
                'caption' => 'A sheet of Rizzi Zannoni\'s map of the Commonwealth, printed in Paris in 1772',
                'credit' => 'Map, Bibliotheque nationale de France, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'vistula',
                'photo' => 'gdansk',
                'label' => 'Gdansk cut off',
                'year' => '1772 - 1793',
                'title' => 'Gdansk stays Polish, but Prussia holds the river',
                'body' => 'For Frederick the prize was the lower Vistula, the river that carried grain from deep inside the Commonwealth to the Baltic. Gdansk, the port at its mouth, and Torun, upstream, stayed Polish, but both were now islands in Prussian land. Prussia held the banks between them and taxed whatever passed, with what the American historian Robert H. Lord called merciless transit duties. Frederick\'s kingdom now ran without a break from Brandenburg to East Prussia. Gdansk and Torun remained Polish for twenty-one more years, until Prussia took them in the Second Partition of 1793.',
                'caption' => 'Gdansk from the south-west, engraved by Matthaus Deisch in 1765',
                'credit' => 'Engraving, National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'sejm',
                'photo' => 'rejtan',
                'label' => 'Rejtan\'s protest',
                'year' => 'April 1773',
                'title' => 'An envoy lies down in the doorway',
                'body' => 'The three powers demanded that a Sejm give its consent, and their troops stayed in the country while the envoys were chosen. On 16 April 1773, before the Sejm opened in Warsaw, a group of envoys formed it into a confederation, so that a majority decided and no veto could stop it. Their marshal was Adam Poninski, a magnate protected by the three ambassadors. Tadeusz Rejtan, an envoy from Nowogrodek in Lithuania, refused to accept him, and with two colleagues, Samuel Korsak and Stanislaw Bohuszewicz, he held the chamber for three days. On 21 April, as the envoys filed out, Rejtan lay down across the doorway and told them to walk over him. They did.',
                'caption' => 'Jan Matejko\'s Rejtan of 1866: a painted verdict on 1773, not a record of it',
                'credit' => 'Painting, Royal Castle in Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'council',
                'photo' => 'council',
                'label' => 'The Permanent Council',
                'year' => '1773 - 1775',
                'title' => 'Ratified, then governed on Russia\'s terms',
                'body' => 'No help came. The king protested to the courts of Europe, but Britain and France, busy with their own rivalry, did nothing, and the French writer Voltaire sent Frederick his congratulations. On 18 September 1773 the treaties of cession were signed in Warsaw. The Sejm sat on until 1775 and set up a Permanent Council, 36 members governing with the king, drawn up by the Russian ambassador, Otto Magnus von Stackelberg, and guaranteed by Russia. Poles saw it as Russia\'s tool, yet it gave the state its first real administration, and revenue and the army grew. The same Sejm set up a Commission of National Education to run the schools. The constitution page tells its story.',
                'caption' => 'The Permanent Council in session, from a print of 1775',
                'credit' => 'Engraving, National Library of Poland, Polona, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'kinga',
                'label' => 'Where to see it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'Where Austria took the salt and Prussia the river',
                'body' => 'Start at Wieliczka, 25 minutes from Krakow by train. With nearby Bochnia, its salt mines had belonged to the crown as the Cracow saltworks for close to five hundred years when Austria took them in 1772. Austria kept them until 1918, brought in steam engines and laid out the first route for visitors. Both mines are now on UNESCO\'s World Heritage List. Lwow, the main city of Austrian Galicia, is Lviv in Ukraine today. In Gdansk you stand in a city that stayed Polish in 1772 while the fields outside its walls became Prussian. Prussia took the city itself in 1793, in the Second Partition, the next topic here.',
                'caption' => 'St Kinga\'s Chapel, carved from rock salt deep inside the Wieliczka mine',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Arian Zwegers, CC BY 2.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A schematic map of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth in 1771 and the three shares taken in 1772: Prussia\'s in the north-west, Russia\'s in the north-east and Austria\'s in the south',
            'caption' => 'Schematic, with today\'s Poland drawn in for orientation. Towns are in their places. The frontiers are approximate.',
            'labels' => [
