        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The diplomacy follows two historians who worked in the Russian, Prussian and Austrian archives: Robert H. Lord, The Second Partition of Poland (Harvard, 1915), and Saul K. Padover, Prince Kaunitz and the First Partition of Poland (Chicago, 1932), checked against the Encyclopedia of the Early Modern World. The shares are the estimates of the German historian Hans Roos and of the Encyclopedia of Ukraine. They differ, hence the ranges. The Partition Sejm and Rejtan follow Dzieje Sejmu, the history site of the Chancellery of the Sejm, and Rejtan\'s entry in the Polish Biographical Dictionary. The Russian guarantee is Article VII of the treaty of 1773. Wieliczka follows the Cracow Saltworks Museum and UNESCO, Spisz the museum at Lubovna castle. Title picture: Le Gateau des Rois, a satire of 1773 engraved by Noel Le Mire after Jean-Michel Moreau, Bibliotheque nationale de France, public domain.',
