    'partition2' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The Second Partition, 1793: half what was left',
            'description' => 'How Prussia turned from ally into Russia\'s partner, what the two powers took in 1793, and how the last Sejm at Grodno was forced to agree in silence.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The partitions · Topic 02',
            'title' => 'The Second Partition',
            'title_accent' => 'of 1793',
            'lede' => 'Nine frames, from an ally who stayed at home to a Sejm that signed away half of what was left with cannon trained on its hall. Who took what, which city fought back, why the deputies fell silent, and how it led to the rising of 1794. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about eight minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'ally',
                'photo' => 'frederick',
                'label' => 'Prussia turns away',
                'year' => '1790 - 1792',
                'title' => 'The ally who stayed at home',
                'body' => 'After the First Partition the Commonwealth set about reforming itself, and in 1791 it passed a constitution. The constitution page tells that story. It did not expect to face Russia alone. In March 1790 it had signed an alliance with Prussia, whose king, Frederick William II, nephew and heir of Frederick II, promised troops if anyone attacked it. When Russia invaded in May 1792 to overturn the constitution, Ignacy Potocki, the reform leader behind the alliance, went to Berlin to call it in. Frederick William refused: the constitution, he replied, came after the treaty and changed everything.',
                'caption' => 'Frederick William II of Prussia, painted by Anton Graff around 1792',
                'credit' => 'Painting by Anton Graff, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'deal',
                'photo' => 'medal',
                'label' => 'The January deal',
                'year' => '1792 - 1793',
                'title' => 'Paid in Polish land for a war in France',
                'body' => 'Prussia had its own war. In 1792 it joined Austria against revolutionary France, and after the French stopped its army at Valmy in September it wanted a reward for fighting on: land in Poland. Catherine II, the Russian empress, agreed. On 23 January 1793, in St Petersburg, Russian ministers and the Prussian envoy signed a convention to split the Commonwealth again, leaving Austria out. Its preamble blamed the spirit of the French revolution, which, it claimed, was spreading into Poland.',
                'caption' => 'Catherine II\'s medal for 1793: What was torn away, I have restored',
                'credit' => 'Medal, St Petersburg 1793, Royal Castle in Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'shares',
                'photo' => 'atlas',
                'label' => 'Who took what',
                'year' => '1793',
                'title' => 'Half of what was left is gone',
                'body' => 'The figures come from a count made for the Sejm that same year and from historians\' estimates, so treat them as rough. Russia took the east: most of today\'s Belarus up to a line from Druja to Pinsk, and the Ukrainian lands of Kiev, Braclaw and Podolia, some 230,000 to 250,000 square kilometres with about three million people. Prussia took some 57,000 square kilometres and about a million people: Greater Poland with Poznan and Kalisz and part of Mazovia, which it named South Prussia, and at last Gdansk and Torun.',
                'caption' => 'The lands newly taken by Russia, on a Russian atlas map of 1793',
                'credit' => 'Map, Small Atlas of the Russian Empire, Russian State Library, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'gdansk',
                'photo' => 'crane',
                'label' => 'Gdansk holds out',
                'year' => 'January - May 1793',
                'title' => 'The one city that tried to keep Prussia out',
                'body' => 'On 24 January 1793 Prussian troops under General Wichard von Mollendorf marched into Greater Poland and met almost no resistance. Gdansk, for twenty-one years an island in Prussian land, shut its gates. From 8 March General Karl von Raumer blockaded it. The city council offered to yield if its privileges were kept, and the poorer townspeople were furious. When the Prussians took over the outer forts on 28 March, part of the garrison and a band of sailors fought back. The city gave in on 4 April and swore allegiance to the Prussian king on 7 May.',
                'caption' => 'The harbour crane of Gdansk, engraved by Matthaus Deisch in 1765',
                'credit' => 'Engraving, National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'targowica',
                'photo' => 'potocki',
                'label' => 'Targowica betrayed',
                'year' => '1793',
                'title' => 'The men who invited Russia are pushed aside',
                'body' => 'The country was still ruled by the Targowica Confederation, the magnates who had called in Russia in 1792 to overthrow the constitution. They had sworn to defend its borders. Now they protested on paper, and Russia\'s new ambassador, Jakob Sievers, told them not to resist the Prussians. In March their leader, Stanislaw Szczesny Potocki, left for St Petersburg. He later served Russia as a general. Another leader, Seweryn Rzewuski, wrote to the Russians that they had ruined his country and him.',
                'caption' => 'Stanislaw Szczesny Potocki, the Targowica leader, painted by Johann Baptist Lampi',
                'credit' => 'Painting, about 1788-1791, National Museum in Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'sievers',
                'photo' => 'sievers',
                'label' => 'The ambassador',
                'year' => 'February - June 1793',
                'title' => 'A Sejm chosen for its answer',
                'body' => 'Sievers had one task: a Sejm that would agree to the partition. Catherine ordered it held at Grodno, in Lithuania, far from the crowds of Warsaw. Stanislaw August, the king, deep in debt, asked to be allowed to abdicate and was refused, then took Russian money for the journey. The deputies were elected under Russian guard. Anyone who had backed the constitution was barred, lists of approved names were sent out to the local assemblies, and Russian troops kept watch while the nobles voted.',
                'caption' => 'Jakob Sievers, Russia\'s ambassador, painted by Josef Grassi in the early 1790s',
                'credit' => 'Painting by Josef Grassi, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'silent',
                'photo' => 'treaty',
                'label' => 'The silent session',
                'year' => 'June - September 1793',
                'title' => 'Silence in the hall, taken for a yes',
                'body' => 'The Sejm opened in the royal castle at Grodno on 17 June 1793, with Russian troops around it. Sievers arrested deputies who blocked him and seized their estates, and in July the Sejm gave Russia its share. Prussia it hated more and resisted longer. On 23 September Cossacks dragged four opposition deputies from their beds at dawn, and Russian grenadiers ringed the castle with cannon aimed at the hall. The deputies sat silent into the night. The marshal, Stanislaw Bielinski, asked if they agreed, heard nothing, and declared that silence meant yes.',
                'caption' => 'The treaty with Prussia, signed at Grodno on 25 September 1793',
                'credit' => 'Manuscript, Central Archives of Historical Records, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'army',
                'photo' => 'kosciuszko',
                'label' => 'An army cut down',
                'year' => '1793 - 1794',
                'title' => 'A state that could no longer defend itself',
                'body' => 'Before it closed on 23 November, the Sejm did what Catherine wanted. A treaty of alliance signed on 14 October let Russian troops enter whenever Russia judged it necessary, and one deputy said Poland had become a Russian province. The laws of 1788-1792 were annulled and the army was cut to roughly 15,000 to 18,000 men. General Antoni Madalinski refused to disband his cavalry brigade and on 12 March 1794 marched it from Ostroleka towards Krakow. In Saxony exiles around Tadeusz Kosciuszko, a general of the 1792 war, had been planning a rising. The next topic tells it.',
                'caption' => 'Tadeusz Kosciuszko, drawn by Josef Grassi in 1793',
                'credit' => 'Drawing by Josef Grassi, 1793, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'grodno',
                'label' => 'Where to see it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'A castle in Belarus, two Prussian cities',
                'body' => 'Grodno is Hrodna today, in Belarus, close to the Polish border. The New Castle where the last Sejm met was badly damaged in 1944 and rebuilt. It is now a museum, and its Senators\' Hall survives. Gdansk became Danzig, a Prussian city, and apart from a spell as a free city under Napoleon it stayed so until after the First World War. Poznan became Posen and, but for the Napoleonic years, stayed Prussian until the end of 1918. What was left of the Commonwealth lasted two more years.',
                'caption' => 'The New Castle above the Neman in Hrodna, rebuilt after the Second World War',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Alex Zelenko, CC BY-SA 4.0',
