        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The diplomacy and the Grodno Sejm follow the American historian Robert H. Lord, The Second Partition of Poland (Harvard, 1915), who worked in the Russian, Prussian and Austrian archives, checked against the Museum of Polish History, Dzieje Sejmu, the history site of the Chancellery of the Sejm, and Benon Dymek\'s study of the Grodno Sejm (Rocznik Mazowiecki, 2013). The shares set the Grodno Sejm\'s own figures, given by Lord, against the estimates of ZPE, the Polish education ministry\'s platform, and Polish Radio. They differ, hence the ranges, and so do the counts of the reduced army. Gdansk follows a lecture by Slawomir Koscielak of the University of Gdansk and the Gedanopedia, Madalinski the Museum of Polish History and the museum in Ostroleka, the New Castle the Grodno State Historical and Archaeological Museum. Title picture: A Dance round the Poles, a London satire of 1794, British Museum, public domain.',
