    'kosciuszko' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Kosciuszko, 1794: a rising against two empires',
            'description' => 'Who Tadeusz Kosciuszko was, from West Point to the oath in Krakow, the scythemen of Raclawice, Maciejowice, and the mound that carries his name.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The partitions · Topic 03',
            'title' => 'Kosciuszko',
            'title_accent' => 'and 1794',
            'lede' => 'Nine frames, from an engineer who fortified West Point to a rising against Russia and Prussia. An oath in Krakow, peasants with scythes, two cities that threw out their garrisons, gallows, a siege, a defeat and a massacre, and why his name is on a mound in Krakow and a mountain in Australia. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about eight minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'engineer',
                'photo' => 'westpoint',
                'label' => 'The engineer',
                'year' => '1746 - 1784',
                'title' => 'A Lithuanian noble who fortified West Point',
                'body' => 'General Antoni Madalinski\'s march out of Ostroleka set off a rising that had long been planned around one man. Tadeusz Kosciuszko was born in 1746 into a family of middling nobles in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, the eastern half of the Commonwealth. He trained at the Knights\' School, the cadet academy the king, Stanislaw August, opened in Warsaw in 1765, then spent five years in France on a royal grant, studying engineering. In 1776 he went to America, and the Continental Congress made him a colonel of engineers. He fortified the heights at Saratoga, where a British army surrendered in October 1777, then built the fortress of West Point on the Hudson.',
                'caption' => 'West Point on the Hudson, drawn in watercolour by Pierre L\'Enfant in 1782',
                'credit' => 'Watercolour, Library of Congress, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'dubienka',
                'photo' => 'dubienka',
                'label' => 'War and exile',
                'year' => '1784 - 1794',
                'title' => 'A general who would not serve Targowica',
                'body' => 'Kosciuszko came home in 1784 a brigadier general of the United States, and when the Commonwealth enlarged its army in 1789 he was made a major general. In 1792 Russia invaded to overturn the reform constitution of 3 May 1791. At Dubienka on the Bug, on 18 July, he faced about 25,000 Russians with some 5,000 men and fell back in good order. Within a week the king gave in and joined the Targowica Confederation, the magnates who had invited Russia in. Kosciuszko resigned and left the country. In exile in Saxony he planned a rising with two reform leaders, Hugo Kollataj and Ignacy Potocki, and looked to revolutionary France for help that never came.',
                'caption' => 'A later German plan of Dubienka. Its title gives the wrong year, 1794',
                'credit' => 'Lithograph by Oscar Furstenau, Leipzig, National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'oath',
                'photo' => 'oath',
                'label' => 'The oath in Krakow',
                'year' => '24 March 1794',
                'title' => 'An oath in the Krakow market square',
                'body' => 'The conspirators had meant to wait, but Madalinski\'s march forced their hand. Kosciuszko hurried to Krakow, and on 24 March 1794 the Act of Insurrection was read out in the market square. It gave him full civil and military power as Supreme Commander of the National Armed Force. Poles still call him simply the Naczelnik, the Chief. He swore not to use that power to oppress anyone, only to defend the borders, win back the nation\'s sovereignty and establish general freedom. The enemy was Russia, whose troops were stationed across the country.',
                'caption' => 'Kosciuszko\'s oath in the Krakow market square, painted by Michal Stachowicz in 1797',
                'credit' => 'Gouache, District Museum in Nowy Sacz, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'raclawice',
                'photo' => 'scythemen',
                'label' => 'Scythes at Raclawice',
                'year' => 'April - May 1794',
                'title' => 'Peasants with scythes take the Russian guns',
                'body' => 'Kosciuszko had called on the villages as well as the army, and some 2,000 peasants came armed with war scythes, the blade turned to stand straight up on the shaft. On 4 April, at Raclawice, north-east of Krakow, his 6,000 or so men met a smaller Russian force under General Alexander Tormasov, and the scythemen, led by Kosciuszko himself, charged and took the guns. The story goes that one of them, Wojciech Bartosz, smothered a cannon\'s touch-hole with his cap. He died of wounds two months later. Kosciuszko took to wearing a peasant\'s russet coat, and on 7 May, at Polaniec, he eased serfdom. The serfdom page tells that promise.',
                'caption' => 'Scythemen resting in camp, painted by Michal Stachowicz around 1797',
                'credit' => 'Gouache, District Museum in Nowy Sacz, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'warsaw',
                'photo' => 'miodowa',
                'label' => 'Warsaw and Vilnius',
                'year' => 'April 1794',
                'title' => 'Warsaw and Vilnius throw out their garrisons',
                'body' => 'News of Raclawice reached Warsaw, where the Russian ambassador, General Osip Igelstrom, commanded the garrison. On 17 April, in Holy Week, the city rose. Polish soldiers fought beside townspeople led by Jan Kilinski, a shoemaker and city councillor, and after two days of fighting in streets such as Miodowa the Russians were gone, having lost more than two thousand men. On the night of 22 April Vilnius, the capital of Lithuania, rose too, led by Colonel Jakub Jasinski with a few hundred men. In both cities the crowds turned on the Targowica leaders who had not fled in time.',
                'caption' => 'Fighting in Miodowa Street, Warsaw, drawn by Jean-Pierre Norblin in 1794, detail',
                'credit' => 'Drawing, Polish Army Museum, Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'gallows',
                'photo' => 'gallows',
                'label' => 'The gallows',
                'year' => 'April - June 1794',
                'title' => 'Gallows for the men of Targowica',
                'body' => 'In Vilnius the commander of the Lithuanian army, Hetman Szymon Kossakowski, was caught and hanged on 25 April. In Warsaw an insurgent court sentenced four Targowica leaders on 9 May, two hetmans, a marshal and a bishop, and they were hanged that day. Then the war turned. Prussia joined Russia, and on 6 June their armies beat Kosciuszko at Szczekociny. In mid-June Krakow surrendered to the Prussians. On 28 June, as news of the defeats came in, a Warsaw crowd hanged eight prisoners without trial, among them Bishop Ignacy Massalski. Kosciuszko condemned it, and a handful of those involved were later tried and executed.',
                'caption' => 'The hanging of traitors in Warsaw, painted by Jean-Pierre Norblin in 1794',
                'credit' => 'Painting, National Museum in Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'siege',
                'photo' => 'maciejowice',
                'label' => 'Siege and capture',
                'year' => 'July - October 1794',
                'title' => 'Warsaw holds, and Kosciuszko is taken',
                'body' => 'From 13 July Prussian and Russian armies besieged Warsaw, with the Prussian king, Frederick William II, in the field, and Kosciuszko held the ring of earthworks around the city. Behind the Prussian lines Greater Poland, which Prussia had taken in 1793, rose in its turn, and on 6 September the king lifted the siege. Russia then sent General Alexander Suvorov up from the south. To stop a second Russian corps under General Ivan Fersen from joining him, Kosciuszko attacked it at Maciejowice on 10 October. He lost, and was cut down, badly wounded, and taken prisoner.',
                'caption' => 'Kosciuszko falls from his horse at Maciejowice, painted by Jan Bogumil Plersch',
                'credit' => 'Painting, Polish Army Museum, Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'praga',
                'photo' => 'praga',
                'label' => 'The storm of Praga',
                'year' => 'November 1794',
                'title' => 'The massacre at Praga, and the end of the rising',
                'body' => 'Suvorov came to Praga, the suburb across the Vistula from Warsaw, and at about five in the morning of 4 November stormed its earthworks. Once the defences broke, his troops killed soldiers and civilians alike in the streets, and many drowned in the river as the bridge to Warsaw burned. The Museum of Polish History puts the dead at 13,000 to 20,000. Among the defenders was a regiment of Jewish light horse raised by Berek Joselewicz, and most of its men died there. Warsaw capitulated the next day, and on 16 November the last units of the rising laid down their arms at Radoszyce.',
                'caption' => 'The stone for the defenders and victims of Praga, laid by its people in 1994',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'memory',
                'photo' => 'mound',
                'label' => 'Where he stands',
                'year' => '1794 - today',
                'title' => 'A mound in Krakow, a mountain in Australia',
                'body' => 'Kosciuszko spent two years a prisoner in St Petersburg. When the empress, Catherine II, died in 1796, her son Paul I freed him and some 20,000 Polish prisoners, after Kosciuszko swore loyalty to the tsar. In America in 1798 he wrote a will asking Thomas Jefferson to spend his American pay on freeing and educating enslaved people. Jefferson declined, and no one was freed with it. He died in Solothurn, Switzerland, in 1817, and lies in Wawel Cathedral. Krakow raised his mound in 1820-1823, West Point cadets his monument in 1828, and in 1840 a Polish explorer, Pawel Strzelecki, named the highest peak of mainland Australia after him. What became of his country is the next topic.',
                'caption' => 'The Kosciuszko Mound in Krakow, inside the fort the Austrians later built round it',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Pawelsuder, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A schematic map of what was left of the Commonwealth in 1794, with the battlefields of Raclawice, Szczekociny and Maciejowice marked between Krakow and Warsaw',
            'caption' => 'Schematic, with today\'s Poland drawn in for orientation. Battlefields are in their places. The frontiers are approximate.',
            'labels' => [
                'sea' => 'Baltic Sea',
                'commonwealth' => 'What was left',
                'east_prussia' => 'East Prussia',
