    'partition3' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The Third Partition, 1795: Poland off the map',
            'description' => 'How Russia, Austria and Prussia shared out the rest of the Commonwealth in 1795, why Stanislaw August abdicated, and what 123 years without a state meant.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The partitions · Topic 04',
            'title' => 'The Third Partition',
            'title_accent' => 'of 1795',
            'lede' => 'Nine frames, from the capitulation of Warsaw to a marching song written in Italy. How three empires shared out what was left of the Commonwealth, why the last king signed away his crown on his coronation day, how the very name of Poland was meant to disappear, and how to read the 123 years that followed. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about eight minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'occupied',
                'photo' => 'singleton',
                'label' => 'Under Russian guard',
                'year' => 'November 1794 - January 1795',
                'title' => 'Warsaw occupied, its leaders in prison',
                'body' => 'Warsaw capitulated the day after the massacre at Praga, its suburb across the Vistula, and General Suvorov\'s army marched in. Russian troops held the city for more than a year. The leaders of the rising of 1794 were taken to St Petersburg as prisoners: Kosciuszko, its commander, the reform leader Ignacy Potocki, Jan Kilinski, the shoemaker who had led the Warsaw crowd, and the writer Julian Ursyn Niemcewicz. On 7 January 1795 the king, Stanislaw August, was ordered to leave his capital for Grodno, in Lithuania, where Russian officers kept watch on him. He never saw Warsaw again.',
                'caption' => 'Paul I frees Kosciuszko in 1796, as Henry Singleton imagined it in London in 1797',
                'credit' => 'Painting by Henry Singleton, 1797, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'deal',
                'photo' => 'catherine',
                'label' => 'Two powers agree',
                'year' => '3 January 1795',
                'title' => 'A deal in St Petersburg that left Prussia out',
                'body' => 'With the rising crushed, Russian and Prussian troops stood on what was left of the Commonwealth, and the only question was how to share it. Catherine II, the Russian empress who had signed both earlier partitions, settled it first with Austria, ruled by Emperor Francis II. On 3 January 1795, in St Petersburg, Russia and Austria signed a treaty dividing the rest of the country. Nobody asked Prussia. Its troops had held Krakow since June 1794, and its king, Frederick William II, wanted to keep the city. The treaty gave Krakow to Austria, and Frederick William even weighed a war over it.',
                'caption' => 'Catherine II, painted by Johann Baptist Lampi in 1793',
                'credit' => 'Painting, State Hermitage Museum, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'shares',
                'photo' => 'cruttwell',
                'label' => 'Who took what',
                'year' => '24 October 1795',
                'title' => 'Warsaw goes to Prussia, Krakow to Austria',
                'body' => 'In the end Prussia gave way. On 24 October 1795 it joined the treaty, and the three powers drew the last lines. Russia took the east: Lithuania with Vilnius, the rest of today\'s Belarus and western Volhynia, now in Ukraine. Austria took the lands between the Pilica, Vistula and Bug rivers, with Krakow and Lublin, and called them New Galicia. Prussia got Warsaw and the north-east as far as the Niemen river, with Bialystok. The cities changed hands in the winter: the Austrians marched into Krakow on 5 January 1796, and on 9 January the Russians handed Warsaw to the Prussians.',
                'caption' => 'Poland divided, on an English map by Clement Cruttwell, 1799',
                'credit' => 'Map, Geographicus Rare Antique Maps, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'abdication',
                'photo' => 'coronation',
                'label' => 'Abdication at Grodno',
                'year' => '25 November 1795',
                'title' => 'The last king steps down at Grodno',
                'body' => 'In Grodno the king learned that the country had been divided, and that one more signature was wanted from him. On 25 November 1795 Stanislaw August abdicated. It was the anniversary of his coronation in 1764, held on St Catherine\'s day, the feast of the empress\'s patron saint. The act had been drawn up by Prince Nikolai Repnin, the Russian governor-general of Lithuania, and the king, deep in debt and under guard, had no way to refuse. Thirty-one years earlier Catherine, once his lover, had made him king. Now there was no country left for him to be king of.',
                'caption' => 'Stanislaw August in coronation robes, painted by Marcello Bacciarelli around 1770',
                'credit' => 'Painting, National Museum in Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'petersburg',
                'photo' => 'petersburg',
                'label' => 'The last years',
                'year' => '1796 - 1798',
                'title' => 'The last king and his three graves',
                'body' => 'Catherine II died in November 1796. Her son and successor, Paul I, brought Stanislaw August to St Petersburg and gave him the Marble Palace, a comfortable cage. He died there on 12 February 1798, and Paul buried him with royal honours in the Catholic church of St Catherine. In 1938, when the Soviet authorities planned to pull the church down, the coffin was quietly taken to Wolczyn, the village where he was born, then in Poland and now in Belarus. After the war the grave was looted. What was left of it was laid in St John\'s Cathedral in Warsaw on 14 February 1995.',
                'caption' => 'The first, now empty grave of Stanislaw August in St Catherine\'s Church, St Petersburg',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Ivonna Nowicka, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'name',
                'photo' => 'cary',
                'label' => 'Erasing the name',
                'year' => '26 January 1797',
                'title' => 'Three empires promise to forget the word Poland',
                'body' => 'One more document was needed. On 26 January 1797, in St Petersburg, Russia, Prussia and Austria signed a final convention. It fixed the new borders, settled the old debts, and declared the political existence of Poland ended for all time. The three rulers also agreed that none of them would ever use the name Kingdom of Poland in their titles. Paper could only do so much. Mapmakers in London went on printing maps of Poland, and the people who lived there went on calling their country by its name.',
                'caption' => 'A New Map of Poland, published by John Cary in London in 1799',
                'credit' => 'Map, Geographicus Rare Antique Maps, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'legions',
                'photo' => 'milan',
                'label' => 'Legions in Italy',
                'year' => '1797',
                'title' => 'A marching song for an army with no country',
                'body' => 'The state had lost its army, but many Poles wanted to fight on. Jan Henryk Dabrowski, a general who had led an expedition into Greater Poland in 1794, turned to the French. On 9 January 1797, in Milan, he signed an agreement with the government of Lombardy, which the French general Napoleon Bonaparte had taken from Austria, to raise Polish legions in its pay. Many of the first recruits were Poles drafted into the Austrian army and captured by the French. In July Jozef Wybicki, a nobleman from Pomerania, joined them at Reggio Emilia, in northern Italy, and wrote them a song: Poland has not yet died, while we live.',
                'caption' => 'Dabrowski reads his appeal of 20 January 1797 to the first legionaries in Milan',
                'credit' => 'Engraving by W. A. Le Petit after Elie Tremo, 1839, British Library, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'years',
                'photo' => 'hourglass',
                'label' => 'The 123 years',
                'year' => '1795 - 1918',
                'title' => 'How to read the 123 years without a state',
                'body' => 'Poles count from October 1795 to November 1918: 123 years with no independent Polish state. The number is right, but it does not mean that nothing Polish was left. Poles, and the Lithuanians, Ruthenians, forebears of today\'s Belarusians and Ukrainians, and Jews who had shared the Commonwealth with them, stayed on the same land, spoke their own languages and read their own books, now ruled from three foreign capitals. The name even came back twice, on states that answered to an emperor: in 1807 Napoleon, by then emperor of the French, made the Duchy of Warsaw out of Prussia\'s share, and in 1815 the Russian tsar, Alexander I, took the title King of Poland. The next topics tell how.',
                'caption' => 'Stanislaw August with an hourglass and a crown, painted by Marcello Bacciarelli',
                'credit' => 'Painting, National Museum in Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'isle',
                'label' => 'Where to see it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'A palace on the water, a song in a manor house',
                'body' => 'Stanislaw August\'s Warsaw survives, rebuilt. The Palace on the Isle in Lazienki Park, a bathhouse he turned into his summer residence, was set on fire by the Germans in 1944 and restored. The Royal Castle, which they destroyed, was rebuilt from 1971. His tomb is in the crypt of St John\'s Cathedral, a few steps from the castle. At Bedomin, south-west of Gdansk, the manor where Wybicki was born has housed the Museum of the National Anthem since 1978, and in Reggio Emilia a plaque by the bishop\'s palace marks where he wrote the song. On 26 February 1927 it became Poland\'s national anthem.',
                'caption' => 'The Palace on the Isle in Lazienki Park, Warsaw, across the northern pond',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Krzysztof Poplawski, CC BY 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A schematic map of the Commonwealth in 1795: the shares lost in 1772 and 1793 greyed out, and the last of it divided, Russia in the east, Austria in the south with Krakow, Prussia in the north-west with Warsaw',
