    'uprisings' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The uprisings of 1830 and 1863 against the tsar',
            'description' => 'Why cadets rose in Warsaw in November 1830 and partisans took to the forests in January 1863, what it cost, and why Poles still argue about it.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The partitions · Topic 05',
            'title' => 'The uprisings',
            'title_accent' => 'of 1830 and 1863',
            'lede' => 'Nine frames, from Napoleon\'s short-lived duchy to the gallows below the Warsaw Citadel. How the Russian tsar became King of Poland, why the Poles rose against him twice, how each rising was lost, who went into exile and who went to Siberia, and why Poles still argue about whether the risings were worth it. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about nine minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'duchy',
                'photo' => 'bacciarelli',
                'label' => 'Napoleon\'s duchy',
                'year' => '1807 - 1813',
                'title' => 'Napoleon brings back a small Polish state',
                'body' => 'The legions kept fighting for Napoleon, emperor of the French since 1804, and in 1806 he crushed Prussia. By the peace he made at Tilsit in July 1807 with Russia and Prussia, most of Prussia\'s share of Poland became the Duchy of Warsaw. Napoleon gave it a constitution on 22 July, and his civil code followed. Its duke was his ally, Frederick Augustus, King of Saxony. Its army was led by Prince Jozef Poniatowski, the last king\'s nephew. In 1809, when Austria invaded, it struck back into Galicia and won Krakow and Lublin. In 1812 close to 100,000 Poles marched on Moscow with Napoleon. Few returned, and in October 1813, at Leipzig, the wounded Poniatowski drowned in the river Elster.',
                'caption' => 'Napoleon grants the Duchy of Warsaw its constitution, painted by Bacciarelli in 1811',
                'credit' => 'Painting by Marcello Bacciarelli, Royal Castle in Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'kingdom',
                'photo' => 'schlicht',
                'label' => 'The tsar as king',
                'year' => '1815 - 1830',
                'title' => 'At Vienna, the Russian tsar is made King of Poland',
                'body' => 'After Napoleon\'s defeat Russian troops held the duchy, and the victors, meeting at the Congress of Vienna, divided it again in 1815. Prussia took Poznan and the west as the Grand Duchy of Posen. Krakow became a free city under all three powers. Most of the rest became the Kingdom of Poland, bound to Russia for ever, with Tsar Alexander I as its king. His constitution of 27 November 1815 gave it a Sejm, an army, its own money and Polish in every office and court. It was not kept. Censorship came in 1819, and after 1825 the Sejm could no longer debate in public. The army was run by the tsar\'s brother, Grand Duke Constantine, whose drill and temper the officers came to hate.',
                'caption' => 'The Kingdom of Poland on a German map by Rudolph Schlicht, 1831',
                'credit' => 'Map, Harvard Library, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'night',
                'photo' => 'arsenal',
                'label' => 'The November Night',
                'year' => '29 November 1830',
                'title' => 'Cadets rise in Warsaw on a November night',
                'body' => 'Alexander I died in 1825. His brother, Nicholas I, was crowned King of Poland in Warsaw in 1829, but had no patience with a constitution. In 1830 revolutions broke out in Paris and Brussels, and Nicholas meant to send the Polish army against them. A conspiracy of young officers, fearing arrest, moved first. On the evening of 29 November Piotr Wysocki, an instructor at the infantry cadet school in Lazienki Park, led the cadets out. Some burst into the Belweder palace to seize Constantine, who escaped. Townspeople broke into the Arsenal for weapons, and six Polish generals were killed, most for refusing to join. By morning Warsaw was in Polish hands.',
                'caption' => 'Townspeople take the Warsaw Arsenal, painted by Marcin Zaleski in 1831',
