            ],
            [
                'key' => 'war',
                'photo' => 'ostroleka',
                'label' => 'War with Russia',
                'year' => '1831',
                'title' => 'The Sejm dethrones the tsar, and loses the war',
                'body' => 'Nicholas I wanted surrender, not talks. On 25 January 1831 the Sejm declared that he was no longer King of Poland, and war could not be avoided. On 5 February Field Marshal Ivan Diebitsch crossed into the kingdom with 115,000 men, far more than the Polish army. The Poles stopped him at Olszynka Grochowska, a wood outside Warsaw, on 25 February, but were beaten at Ostroleka on 26 May. That summer cholera killed both Diebitsch and Constantine. Diebitsch\'s successor, Field Marshal Ivan Paskevich, stormed Warsaw on 6 September, and two days later it capitulated. On 5 October some 20,000 soldiers crossed into Prussia and laid down their arms.',
                'caption' => 'The battle of Ostroleka, 26 May 1831, on a print published in Nuremberg',
                'credit' => 'Print by Georg Benedikt Wunder, National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'exile',
                'photo' => 'finis',
                'label' => 'The Great Emigration',
                'year' => 'from 1831',
                'title' => 'Thousands go into exile, most of them to France',
                'body' => 'Rather than wait for the tsar\'s courts, officers, deputies, writers and soldiers went west, some 9,000 to 10,000 of them for good. Crowds in the German states cheered them on the road, and most settled in France. Poles call it the Great Emigration. In Paris Prince Adam Jerzy Czartoryski, once Alexander I\'s foreign minister and head of the rebel government of 1831, led the conservatives, from 1843 out of his house, the Hotel Lambert. The Polish Democratic Society, founded in 1832, wanted the peasants freed. Two exiles are known far beyond Poland: Frederic Chopin, who had left Warsaw a month before the rising, and the poet Adam Mickiewicz, who published Pan Tadeusz in Paris in 1834.',
                'caption' => 'Polish troops leave their country in 1831, in Finis Poloniae by Dietrich Monten',
                'credit' => 'Painting by Dietrich Monten, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'citadel',
                'photo' => 'gate',
                'label' => 'The Citadel',
                'year' => '1832 - 1848',
                'title' => 'Nicholas I builds a fortress over Warsaw',
                'body' => 'Nicholas punished the kingdom in law and in brick. His Organic Statute of 26 February 1832 replaced the constitution: no Sejm and no Polish army. The universities of Warsaw and Vilnius were closed and captured soldiers were sent to serve in the Caucasus. Paskevich ruled as viceroy until he died in 1856. From 1832 a citadel rose on the northern edge of Warsaw, paid for by the city and the kingdom, its guns trained on the streets. Its Tenth Pavilion became a political prison and is now a museum. Elsewhere the lid lifted twice: a rising in Krakow in 1846 ended with Austria annexing the Free City, and one in Poznan in 1848 was crushed by Prussia.',
                'caption' => 'The Execution Gate of the Warsaw Citadel, photographed in 2017',
