                'label' => 'Warsaw in black',
                'year' => '1856 - 1862',
                'title' => 'Warsaw goes into mourning and the patriots split',
                'body' => 'Nicholas I died in 1855, while losing the Crimean War, and his son Alexander II loosened the grip. In 1861 Warsaw filled with patriotic demonstrations. On 27 February troops killed five marchers. On 8 April, on Castle Square, between about 100 and 200. Women went into national mourning, in black dresses and jewellery of iron and wood, crosses and crowns of thorns. The patriots split. The Whites, landowners and bankers, hoped for concessions. The Reds, students, craftsmen and young officers, wanted a rising. Margrave Aleksander Wielopolski, the Pole running the civil government for the tsar, set out to break the Reds by drafting their young men into the Russian army.',
                'caption' => 'The funeral of the five killed on 27 February 1861, painted by Aleksander Lesser',
                'credit' => 'Painting, 1861, National Museum in Krakow, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'forest',
                'photo' => 'insurgents',
                'label' => 'War in the forests',
                'year' => '1863 - 1864',
                'title' => 'A partisan war run by a secret government',
                'body' => 'The draft, called the branka, began in Warsaw on the night of 14 January 1863. Young men fled to the forests, and on 22 January the Reds, as the Provisional National Government, called the nation to arms and promised peasants their land. There was no army, only partisan units, many armed with scythes, fighting over 1,200 mostly small clashes against Russian forces that grew to several hundred thousand. The government stayed hidden. Its seal joined the Polish eagle, the knight of Lithuania and the archangel of Rus. In Lithuania and Belarus the rising was led by Konstanty Kalinowski, who wrote for peasants in Belarusian. The Russians hanged him in Vilnius on 22 March 1864.',
                'caption' => 'Insurgents of 1863 with scythes turned into pikes, photographed in a studio',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, 1863-1864, National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'aftermath',
                'photo' => 'traugutt',
                'label' => 'After 1864',
                'year' => '1864 - 1874',
                'title' => 'The last leader is hanged below the Citadel',
                'body' => 'From October 1863 the rising was led by Romuald Traugutt, a retired lieutenant-colonel of the Russian army. He was arrested in April 1864 and hanged on 5 August with four members of his government, below the Citadel, where a cross now stands. By then the tsar had turned to the peasants: a decree of 2 March 1864 gave them their land, as the serfdom topic tells. Some 38,000 people were deported, many to Siberia. The kingdom lost the last of its institutions. Russian became the language of offices and schools, and officials took to calling the kingdom the Vistula Land. Krakow historians called the risings folly in 1869. Later historians answer that they kept the nation alive.',
                'caption' => 'Romuald Traugutt, the last leader of the January Uprising, in a studio portrait',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, 1860s, public domain',
