    'keeping' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Keeping a country without a country, 1795-1918',
            'description' => 'How a nation with no state kept its language for 123 years: Chopin, Mickiewicz, secret lessons in Warsaw, organic work in Poznan and Marie Curie.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The partitions · Topic 06',
            'title' => 'Keeping a country',
            'title_accent' => 'without a country',
            'lede' => 'The risings of 1830 and 1863 were lost, and the three empires set out to make Poles into Russians, Germans or loyal Austrians. Nine frames on what the Poles kept instead: music and poems, the parish and the synagogue, shops and cooperative banks, lessons taught in secret, two Polish universities, and children who would not pray in German. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about nine minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'chopin',
                'photo' => 'bisson',
                'label' => 'Chopin\'s heart',
                'year' => '1810 - 1880',
                'title' => 'Chopin leaves, and his heart comes home',
                'body' => 'The uprisings topic named two exiles. The first, Fryderyk Chopin, was born near Warsaw in 1810, the son of a Frenchman who taught French there. In 1829 his teacher, Jozef Elsner, called him a musical genius. On 2 November 1830, four weeks before the rising, Chopin left for Vienna and never came back. In Paris, from 1831, he kept writing mazurkas and polonaises, Polish dances, which the German composer Robert Schumann called cannons buried in flowers. Chopin died in 1849. His sister Ludwika carried his heart home, and since 1880, but for a year hidden in 1944-1945, it has rested in a pillar of Holy Cross Church in Warsaw.',
                'caption' => 'Chopin in 1849, in one of only two photographs known of him',
                'credit' => 'Daguerreotype by Louis-Auguste Bisson, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'bards',
                'photo' => 'tadeusz',
                'label' => 'The poets in Paris',
                'year' => '1823 - 1834',
                'title' => 'Poland\'s national epic, printed in Paris',
                'body' => 'The second exile, Adam Mickiewicz, was born in 1798 near Navahrudak, now in Belarus. Arrested in Vilnius in 1823 for a student secret society, the Philomaths, he was exiled deep into Russia. In Paris in 1834 he published Pan Tadeusz, a long poem about gentry life in 1811-1812 that opens with "Lithuania, my fatherland", meaning the old Grand Duchy, half of the Commonwealth. In Forefathers\' Eve and a book for the exiles, both of 1832, he cast Poland as a nation crucified for others that would rise again. Poles call him and two younger poets, Juliusz Slowacki and Zygmunt Krasinski, the bards. Russia banned their books, so they were smuggled in.',
                'caption' => 'The first edition of Pan Tadeusz, printed in Paris in 1834',
                'credit' => 'National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'faiths',
                'photo' => 'meisels',
                'label' => 'Church and synagogue',
                'year' => '1861 - 1875',
                'title' => 'The Church stands for the nation, but not alone',
                'body' => 'A nation also needs institutions, and the largest one that spanned all three partitions was the Catholic Church. After 1864 Tsar Alexander II closed most monasteries in the Kingdom of Poland, and in 1875 he forced its Greek Catholics, who worship in the Eastern rite, into Orthodoxy. Prussia, the core of the German Empire since 1871, jailed the Archbishop of Gniezno, Mieczyslaw Ledochowski, in 1874, during its Chancellor Otto von Bismarck\'s fight with the Church. Yet Polishness was never only Catholic. In 1861 rabbis and Lutheran pastors walked with priests behind the coffins of protesters shot in Warsaw, and the city\'s chief rabbi, Dov Ber Meisels, was expelled for backing them.',
                'caption' => 'Dov Ber Meisels, chief rabbi of Warsaw, photographed in 1861',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Karol Beyer, National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'work',
                'photo' => 'invoice',
                'label' => 'Organic work',
                'year' => '1841 - 1891',
                'title' => 'In Poznan, shops and banks resist',
                'body' => 'In the Prussian partition some Poles drew a lesson early: a nation without a state had to be strong in business. In Poznan the physician Karol Marcinkowski raised the money for the Bazar, a Polish hotel and shopping hall opened in the early 1840s, and in 1841 founded a society that paid for poor boys\' schooling. Hipolit Cegielski, a teacher sacked by the Prussian authorities in 1846 for refusing to search his pupils\' lodgings, opened an ironmonger\'s shop in the Bazar, then a works making ploughs and threshers, the firm that still bears his name. Poles called it organic work. From 1891 a priest, Piotr Wawrzyniak, led a union of Polish cooperative banks that lent to Polish farmers.',
                'caption' => 'An invoice from Cegielski\'s ironmongery in Poznan, 9 March 1853',
                'credit' => 'State Archive in Poznan, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'positivists',
                'photo' => 'orzeszkowa',
                'label' => 'The positivists',
                'year' => '1864 - 1890',
                'title' => 'After 1864, writers trade sword for work',
                'body' => 'After the defeat of 1864 young writers in Warsaw took a similar road. They called themselves positivists, after the French philosopher Auguste Comte, and preached work at the foundations: schools, science, industry, and rights for peasants, women and Jews. Boleslaw Prus, wounded as a 16-year-old insurgent in 1863, put their world into The Doll, a novel of 1890 about a Warsaw merchant. Eliza Orzeszkowa wrote On the Niemen, and in Meir Ezofowicz she portrayed a young Jew who defies his town\'s strict elders. Jewish Warsaw had its own debate. The weekly Izraelita, from 1866, urged Jews to become Poles of the Mosaic faith. Most traditional and Hasidic Jews refused.',
                'caption' => 'Eliza Orzeszkowa in a studio portrait of 1899',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Jan Mieczkowski, National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'schools',
                'photo' => 'dawidowa',
                'label' => 'Secret lessons',
                'year' => '1869 - 1891',
                'title' => 'Russian at school, Polish in secret',
                'body' => 'In the Kingdom of Poland the school became the front line. In 1869 Warsaw\'s Main School was turned into a Russian university, and under Alexander Apukhtin, the tsar\'s curator of schools in Warsaw from 1879 to 1897, only religion was still taught in Polish. Children learned to read Polish at home, in secret classes that risked fines and prison. From 1885 Jadwiga Szczawinska-Dawidowa ran secret courses for women that moved from one private flat to another, so Poles called them the Flying University. An estimated 5,000 women studied there over two decades, among them a young governess, Maria Sklodowska. In 1891 she left for Paris, where a woman could study at the Sorbonne.',
                'caption' => 'Jadwiga Szczawinska-Dawidowa, founder of the Flying University, about 1878',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Jan Mieczkowski, National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'galicia',
                'photo' => 'copernicus',
                'label' => 'Galician autonomy',
                'year' => '1866 - 1873',
                'title' => 'In Galicia, Polish returns to school',
                'body' => 'Austria, beaten by Prussia in 1866, needed its peoples\' loyalty and gave its share of Poland, Galicia, home rule. From 1867 Poles ran its government and schools, and from 1870 Krakow\'s Jagiellonian University taught in Polish again, followed by the university of Lviv. The Ukrainians of eastern Galicia, then called Ruthenians, got far less. Krakow gained an Academy of Learning in 1872 and became the city where Poles from all three partitions came to see their past: the royal tombs on Wawel and the vast history paintings of Jan Matejko. In 1873, the 400th anniversary of Copernicus\'s birth, Krakow people bought his Astronomer Copernicus for the university where Copernicus had studied.',
                'caption' => 'Astronomer Copernicus, or Conversation with God, painted by Jan Matejko in 1873',
                'credit' => 'Jagiellonian University Museum, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'prussia',
                'photo' => 'wrzesnia',
                'label' => 'The school strike',
                'year' => '1873 - 1908',
                'title' => 'Children in Wrzesnia go on strike',
                'body' => 'Prussia went furthest. From 1873 German was the language of its schools, from 1887 Polish was dropped even as a subject, and from 1886 its Settlement Commission bought Polish land for German settlers. Only religion was left in Polish, until that went too. In 1901, in the small town of Wrzesnia, children refused to say their prayers in German. 14 were flogged, and parents who protested were jailed. In 1906-1907 some 75,000 children, by the most common estimate, went on strike across the partition. A law of 1904 barred a peasant, Michal Drzymala, from building a house on his land, so he lived in a circus wagon and moved it a little every day.',
                'caption' => 'Pupils of Wrzesnia who struck in 1901, reproduced in a book of 1932',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, 1901, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'nobel',
                'photo' => 'laboratory',
                'label' => 'Two Nobel prizes',
                'year' => '1884 - 1914',
                'title' => 'Polonium, named for a missing country',
                'body' => 'By 1900 Polish culture was known across a continent on which Poland did not exist. In Warsaw, Henryk Sienkiewicz published a trilogy of novels about the wars of the 1600s, in 1884-1888, and on its last page wrote that he had written it to strengthen hearts. In 1905 he won the Nobel Prize for literature. In Paris, Maria Sklodowska, now Marie Curie, found a new element with her husband Pierre in 1898 and named it polonium, "after the country of origin of one of us". In 1914 the three empires that had divided Poland went to war with one another, and by 1918 all three had fallen. The next topic shows how each had shaped its Poles, city by city.',
                'caption' => 'Marie Curie in her laboratory in Paris, around 1900',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, public domain',
            ],
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The Chopin frame follows the Fryderyk Chopin Institute, its biographical calendar and the parish of Holy Cross Church in Warsaw. Schumann\'s phrase is from his 1836 review in the Neue Zeitschrift fur Musik. Mickiewicz follows Britannica, the Pan Tadeusz Museum of the Ossolineum and the texts themselves. The churches follow the Museum of Polish History, the Primate of Poland\'s site and the Siedlce diocese. Meisels and Izraelita the YIVO Encyclopedia and POLIN\'s Virtual Shtetl. Organic work follows dzieje.pl, the Poznan city site and the Heritage Board. The Flying University culture.pl and the University of Warsaw. Galicia the Polish Academy of Sciences and the Polish Biographical Dictionary. Wrzesnia and Drzymala the Wrzesnia museum and the Institute of National Remembrance. The Nobel prizes nobelprize.org and the Curies\' note of 18 July 1898. Where sources differ on a number, the page gives the common estimate and says so. Title picture: Michal Drzymala moving his wagon through Grodzisk, on a postcard of 1908, National Digital Archive, public domain.',
