                'title' => 'Three empires meet where two rivers join',
                'body' => 'The last topic ended with the empires at war in 1914. This one goes back to what each of them built. Start at a bend of the river near Myslowice, in Prussian Silesia. From 1846, when Austria took the Free City of Krakow, the borders of Prussia, Russia and Austria met here, where the Black and the White Przemsza join. Before 1914 thousands of day-trippers came each week to look across, climb a tower named after the German chancellor Otto von Bismarck and send postcards with the stamps of three empires. Cards sent from the Russian bank carried a date 12 or 13 days behind, because Russia still kept the old Julian calendar.',
                'caption' => 'The Three Emperors\' Corner, with each bank named, on a postcard from before 1917',
                'credit' => 'Postcard, publisher unknown, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'fortress',
                'photo' => 'kernwerk',
                'label' => 'Fortress Posen',
                'year' => '1828 - 1896',
                'title' => 'Prussia turns Poznan into a fortress',
                'body' => 'Poznan came back to Prussia in 1815 as the capital of the Grand Duchy of Posen, less than 100 kilometres from the Russian border. Prussia made it a fortress. From 1828 to 1842 it built Fort Winiary, a huge citadel on a hill north of the old town, and closed the town inside a ring of walls that kept it cramped for decades. Between 1876 and 1896 a second ring of 18 forts went up a few kilometres out. Many of them still stand in the green belt around the city, and several can be visited. In 1939 the German occupiers turned one of them, Fort VII, into a concentration camp. Today it is a museum to the people held and killed there.',
                'caption' => 'Soldiers on parade in the core of Fort Winiary, on a postcard from before 1920',
                'credit' => 'Postcard, J. Themal, Poznan, National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'castle',
                'photo' => 'schloss',
                'label' => 'The Kaiser\'s castle',
                'year' => '1902 - 1910',
                'title' => 'The Kaiser builds himself a castle in Poznan',
                'body' => 'By 1900 the walls were strangling the city. In 1902 Wilhelm II, German Emperor and King of Prussia, decided the western ring could go, and on the freed land the town planner Joseph Stubben laid out a new district. At its heart stood a castle for the emperor, built in 1905-1910 to a design by the architect Franz Schwechten. Around it rose a theatre, a Royal Academy and the offices of the Settlement Commission, which bought land for German settlers, all meant to show the province it was German. Poznan has been Polish since 1919, and the academy is now the university\'s great hall. The Nazis later made the castle a residence for Hitler. Today it is a cultural centre.',
                'caption' => 'The Royal Residence Castle in Poznan on a postcard from 1913-1917',
                'credit' => 'Postcard, National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'planty',
                'photo' => 'stanislawski',
                'label' => 'Krakow\'s green ring',
                'year' => '1810 - 1901',
                'title' => 'In Krakow, a park grows where the walls stood',
                'body' => 'Krakow, the old royal capital, changed hands four times after 1795 and was Austrian for good from 1846. It was not even the capital of its province: Galicia\'s governor and Sejm sat in Lviv, some 300 kilometres east. Its medieval walls had come down early in the century, first on Austrian orders, and in 1822-1830 the Free City laid out the Planty, a ring of gardens about four kilometres long on the line of the walls and the moat. Only the Florian Gate and the Barbican were kept. Walk the Planty and you walk the old walls. Home rule later let Krakow build for itself, and its Palace of Art of 1901 is modelled on Vienna\'s Secession.',
                'caption' => 'The Planty Park in Krakow, painted by Jan Stanislawski in 1903',
                'credit' => 'Painting, National Museum in Krakow, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'wawel',
                'photo' => 'krieger',
                'label' => 'Barracks on Wawel',
                'year' => '1796 - 1905',
                'title' => 'Soldiers move into the royal castle',
                'body' => 'To Austria, Krakow was a frontier post facing Russia. On Wawel, the hill of the kings, it turned the royal castle into barracks: soldiers slept in the royal chambers and the arcades of the courtyard were bricked up. From the middle of the century the hill was fortified as a citadel, and in 1850-1854 a fort was built around the mound Krakow had raised for Kosciuszko. Rings of forts later spread far into the countryside, and more than a hundred of those works survive. Only in 1905, after Poles had raised the money to buy the hill back, did the army begin to leave Wawel, and a restoration began that took decades. The castle now tells that story in an exhibition, Wawel Recovered.',
                'caption' => 'Wawel in 1897, with the Austrian army\'s buildings along the hill',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Krieger studio, Museum of Krakow, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'galicia',
                'photo' => 'szczepanowski',
                'label' => 'Galician poverty',
                'year' => '1888 - 1914',
                'title' => 'Galicia\'s poor leave for America',
                'body' => 'Home rule did not fill stomachs. Galicia had little industry, and its peasants farmed plots split smaller with each generation. In 1888 Stanislaw Szczepanowski, an oil industrialist and member of the Austrian parliament, published The Poverty of Galicia in Figures. By his count a Galician ate 10 kilograms of meat a year to an Englishman\'s 50, and a Galician man lived 27 years on average. His summary is still quoted: the Galician works little because he eats too little. People left. The historian Wlodzimierz Suleja counts some 350,000 emigrants from Galicia in the 1890s and over 500,000 in the next 15 years, most bound for America.',
                'caption' => 'Stanislaw Szczepanowski, oil man and author of The Poverty of Galicia in Figures',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, 1890, National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'cathedral',
                'photo' => 'sobor',
                'label' => 'The lost cathedral',
                'year' => '1894 - 1926',
                'title' => 'Russia builds its cathedral on Warsaw\'s Saxon Square',
                'body' => 'After 1864 the Kingdom of Poland lost what was left of its own government, and Warsaw was ruled by the tsar\'s governor-general. In 1894 work began on the largest church in the city, the Orthodox cathedral of St Alexander Nevsky, in the middle of Saxon Square. Designed by the architect Leonty Benois, it held some 2,500 worshippers, and its bell tower, about 70 metres high, rose over the whole centre. It was consecrated in 1912. To most Poles it was Russian rule in stone. Independent Poland pulled it down in 1924-1926, and some of its columns now frame the entrance to the Wawel crypt of Marshal Jozef Pilsudski, the first head of the reborn state.',
                'caption' => 'The cathedral on Saxon Square, on a postcard from before 1915',
                'credit' => 'Postcard, publisher unknown, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'gauge',
                'photo' => 'station',
                'label' => 'Two railway gauges',
                'year' => '1845 - 1914',
                'title' => 'In Warsaw, two railway gauges meet',
                'body' => 'Warsaw\'s first railway, to the Austrian border, opened in stages from 1845 on the standard gauge used in the West. The line to St Petersburg, finished in 1862, was built on Russia\'s broad gauge, so no train could run straight through. For years there was not even a bridge between them: the iron bridge of the engineer Stanislaw Kierbedz, the first permanent bridge over the Vistula in Warsaw, opened only in 1864, and a railway bridge below the Citadel in 1875. A ring of forts, with a ban on building in front of them, kept the city from spreading, so it grew upwards, in dense tenements. In 1914 some 880,000 people lived on 33 square kilometres.',
                'caption' => 'The station of the Warsaw-Vienna Railway, photographed around 1890',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Konrad Brandel, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'ghost',
                'photo' => 'vote',
                'label' => 'The ghost border',
                'year' => '1918 - today',
                'title' => 'A border that still shows on the map',
                'body' => 'By November 1918 all three empires had fallen and Poland was back, stitched together from their pieces: several legal systems, several currencies, railways that did not meet. A direct line from Warsaw to Poznan opened only in the early 1920s. In 2015 the economists Irena Grosfeld and Ekaterina Zhuravskaya showed that gaps in income and schooling at the old borders have faded, but railway density, church-going, attitudes to democracy and voting still differ. Election maps often show the line. Other things shape them too, not least the resettlement of the west after 1945, when former German lands became Polish, so the old border explains part of the pattern, not all of it.',
                'caption' => 'The 2025 presidential run-off by commune. The old border shows in the colours',
                'credit' => 'Map: Szturnek, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
