        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The Three Emperors\' Corner follows the Silesian and Lesser Poland tourist boards. Poznan\'s fortress follows the Fort VII Museum, the city of Poznan and the Greater Poland Museum of Independence. The Imperial District and the castle Cyryl, the digital archive of the city of Poznan, the castle\'s own cultural centre, the university\'s magazine and reports by TVN24 and Spotkania z Zabytkami. Krakow follows the city of Krakow, the Wawel Royal Castle, the Kosciuszko Mound and the Krakow Fortress trail, and the National Heritage Board. Galicia follows Szczepanowski\'s book as summarised by Nowy Obywatel and WielkaHistoria, and Wlodzimierz Suleja on the Institute of National Remembrance\'s history site. Warsaw follows the Museum of Warsaw, the Warszawa1939 foundation and TVN24. The ghost border follows Grosfeld and Zhuravskaya in the Journal of Comparative Economics (2015), Pawel Bukowski in the same journal (2019) and Magdalena Skorupska of the University of Warsaw. Where sources differ, the page gives the range or the rounder figure. Title picture: the Three Emperors\' Corner with the three emperors and their stamps, on a postcard of 1903-1910, National Library of Poland, public domain.',
