    'republic' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => '1918 and the Second Republic, 1918-1939',
            'description' => 'How Poland came back in 1918 and what it built in twenty years: the zloty, the port at Gdynia, a country where one citizen in three was not a Pole.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · Independence and war · Topic 01',
            'title' => '1918 and the',
            'title_accent' => 'Second Republic',
            'lede' => 'After 123 years off the map, Poland came back in November 1918, pieced together from three empires. Nine frames on how it happened and what the new state made of its twenty years: the wars for its borders, one currency in place of four, a port built from a fishing village, its minorities, and the marshal who took power by force. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about nine minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'legions',
                'photo' => 'carpathians',
                'label' => 'Three armies',
                'year' => '1914 - 1917',
                'title' => 'Poles fight in three armies, and some in their own',
                'body' => 'The last topic ended with Poland back on the map in November 1918. This one tells how it got there. In August 1914 the three empires that had divided Poland went to war with one another, and each called up its Poles: about three million served in the Russian, German and Austro-Hungarian armies, and estimates of the dead run from about 400,000 to over half a million. On 6 August a company of volunteers marched out of Krakow into the Russian partition, led by Jozef Pilsudski, a socialist from near Vilnius who had spent five years in Siberian exile. It grew into the Polish Legions, which fought on the Austrian side against Russia. Some 40,000 men served in them.',
                'caption' => 'Soldiers of the Legions\' Second Brigade with machine guns in the Carpathians, 1914',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'paris',
                'photo' => 'paris',
                'label' => 'Prison and Paris',
                'year' => '1915 - 1918',
                'title' => 'Pilsudski in a cell, Dmowski in Paris',
                'body' => 'In 1915 Germany took Warsaw from Russia, and in November 1916 Germany and Austria-Hungary promised a Kingdom of Poland, with no king or borders, to raise soldiers. In July 1917 most legionnaires refused to swear brotherhood in arms with the German and Austrian armies, and the Germans locked Pilsudski in the fortress of Magdeburg. Others looked west. Roman Dmowski, leader of the nationalist National Democrats, ran a Polish National Committee in Paris from 1917, and France raised a Polish army, nearly 70,000 men by 1919, led by General Jozef Haller. On 8 January 1918 the US president, Woodrow Wilson, made an independent Poland with access to the sea the 13th of his Fourteen Points.',
                'caption' => 'The Polish army raised in France marches through Paris in July 1918',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'november',
                'photo' => 'act',
                'label' => '11 November',
                'year' => '1918',
                'title' => 'The regents hand their power to Pilsudski',
                'body' => 'In the autumn of 1918 the empires fell apart. On 7 October the Regency Council, three Poles the occupiers had put in charge of their Kingdom in 1917, declared Poland independent, and at the end of the month Poles took over Krakow from the Austrians. Revolution in Germany freed Pilsudski, and on 10 November he reached Warsaw by train. On 11 November, the day the armistice ended the fighting in the West, the regents gave him command of the army, and on 14 November, in the letter shown here, all their power. That is why Poland celebrates 11 November: a holiday set by law in 1937, abolished in 1945 and restored in 1989. A decree of 28 November gave women the vote.',
                'caption' => 'The Regency Council hands its power to Pilsudski, Warsaw, 14 November 1918',
                'credit' => 'Central Archives of Historical Records, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'lwow',
                'photo' => 'truce',
                'label' => 'The war for Lviv',
                'year' => '1918 - 1921',
                'title' => 'Poles and Ukrainians fight for the same city',
                'body' => 'In the east, borders were drawn by war. In Lviv, which Poles call Lwow, Poles were the largest group in the city and Ukrainians the majority in the countryside around it, and both claimed eastern Galicia. On 1 November 1918 Ukrainian troops took the city as the capital of a new Ukrainian state. Polish residents, many of them schoolboys, fought back, and by 22 November the city was in Polish hands. In the days that followed Polish soldiers and civilians killed at least 50 Jews, probably 70 to 100, in the Jewish quarter. By July 1919 Poland held eastern Galicia. A war with Soviet Russia came next. Its decisive battle, at Warsaw in August 1920, has a topic of its own.',
                'caption' => 'Polish and Ukrainian soldiers together in Lviv during a truce, 17-18 November 1918',
                'credit' => 'From Ruski miesiac, Lviv, 1919, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'silesia',
                'photo' => 'poster',
                'label' => 'Western borders',
                'year' => '1918 - 1922',
                'title' => 'In the west, a rising, a treaty and a vote',
                'body' => 'Further west the lands had been Prussian. On 27 December 1918, a day after the pianist Ignacy Jan Paderewski, who had lobbied America for Poland, arrived in Poznan, the city rose, and within weeks insurgents held most of the province. The Treaty of Versailles, signed on 28 June 1919, gave Poland most of it and a strip of Pomerania to the sea, while German-speaking Gdansk became the Free City of Danzig under the League of Nations. Upper Silesia, a coal region, voted on 20 March 1921: about 60 per cent for Germany, 40 for Poland. After a third Polish rising the Allies split it that October, and Poland got most of the industry and Katowice, seat of an autonomous Silesian Parliament.',
                'caption' => 'Vote for Poland and you will be free: a plebiscite poster in both languages, 1921',
                'credit' => 'Poster, National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'marks',
                'photo' => 'marks',
                'label' => 'One currency',
                'year' => '1918 - 1924',
                'title' => 'Four currencies in, one zloty out',
                'body' => 'The new state was stitched together from three. It had three legal systems and railways built to serve three foreign capitals, and it began with four currencies: the Russian rouble, the German mark, the Austrian crown and the Polish mark, issued since 1917 by a bank the German occupiers set up. A constitution adopted on 17 March 1921 gave most power to the parliament, the Sejm. Paying for wars with printed money ended in hyperinflation, and by January 1924 a dollar cost more than nine million marks. That year the prime minister, Wladyslaw Grabski, founded a new central bank, the Bank Polski, and brought in the zloty, worth 1,800,000 marks. Poles still pay in zloty today.',
                'caption' => 'A note for ten million Polish marks, dated 20 November 1923',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Gabinet Numizmatyczny Damian Marciniak, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'minorities',
                'photo' => 'corner',
                'label' => 'A third not Polish',
                'year' => '1921 - 1938',
                'title' => 'One citizen in three is not a Pole',
                'body' => 'In the 1921 census 69 per cent of citizens declared Polish nationality. In 1931, asked for their mother tongue, about 14 per cent named Ukrainian or Ruthenian, 9 per cent Yiddish or Hebrew, 3 per cent Belarusian and 2 per cent German, and almost one in ten was Jewish by religion. In December 1922 parliament elected the first president, Gabriel Narutowicz, helped by minority deputies\' votes. Nationalist papers called him the Jews\' president, and a week later a painter, Eligiusz Niewiadomski, shot him dead. In 1930 troops and police punished Ukrainian villages for arson by underground Ukrainian nationalists, and from 1937 most universities made Jewish students sit apart.',
                'caption' => 'A street corner in Warsaw\'s Jewish quarter in 1934, with signs in Polish and Yiddish',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Willem van de Poll, National Archives of the Netherlands, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'coup',
                'photo' => 'bridge',
                'label' => 'The May Coup',
                'year' => '1926 - 1935',
                'title' => 'Pilsudski takes power by force',
                'body' => 'Pilsudski left power in 1922 and the army soon after, scorning the quarrelling Sejm. On 12 May 1926 he marched on Warsaw with loyal troops. President Stanislaw Wojciechowski met him on the Poniatowski Bridge and would not yield. After three days of fighting and about 380 dead, the president and the prime minister, the peasant leader Wincenty Witos, resigned. Pilsudski never became president but ruled through his men, who called their rule Sanacja, healing. In 1930 opposition leaders, Witos among them, were jailed in Brest and tried. Pilsudski died on 12 May 1935 in the Belweder palace, weeks after a constitution strengthened the presidency, and lies in the crypt of Wawel Cathedral.',
                'caption' => 'Pilsudski on the Poniatowski Bridge, going to meet the president, 12 May 1926',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Marian Fuks, National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'gdynia',
                'photo' => 'gdynia',
                'label' => 'Gdynia and the COP',
                'year' => '1922 - 1939',
                'title' => 'A port from a village, a steelworks from a forest',
                'body' => 'The strip of coast had no port Poland controlled, since Danzig was a free city. So in 1922 the Sejm voted to build one at Gdynia, a village of some 1,300 people. Eugeniusz Kwiatkowski, minister of industry and trade from 1926, drove the work, and by 1939 Gdynia had about 127,000 inhabitants and, by cargo, the busiest port on the Baltic. Its white modernist centre went on UNESCO\'s World Heritage List in 2026. From 1936 Kwiatkowski, now deputy prime minister, built a Central Industrial District, the COP, far from both borders, with a steelworks at Stalowa Wola. The republic was twenty years old in 1939. On 1 September Nazi Germany invaded, and on 17 September the Soviet Union.',
                'caption' => 'Gdynia around 1930, the new town above the harbour it was built for',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Henryk Poddebski, National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
