        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The First World War follows the Museum of Polish History, the historian Mieczyslaw Wrzosek via dzieje.pl, the Jozef Pilsudski Museum in Sulejowek and Histmag. Wilson\'s point is quoted from the Avalon Project at Yale and the US National Archives. November 1918, the women\'s vote, the constitutions of 1921 and 1935, the Treaty of Riga and the laws on the holiday and on Gdynia are checked against the Journal of Laws in the Sejm\'s own archive. Lviv follows the Encyclopedia of Ukraine and the YIVO Encyclopedia, whose estimates of the pogrom\'s dead differ, so the page gives the range. Silesia follows the Institute of National Remembrance and dzieje.pl. The currency the IPN and the National Bank\'s Obserwator Finansowy. The censuses Statistics Poland. Narutowicz the IPN. The coup Polska Zbrojna and Polish Radio. Gdynia the IPN, the Gdynia City Museum and the city itself. The COP dzieje.pl and Polish Radio. Title picture: Pilsudski, the prime minister Ignacy Jan Paderewski and the future president Stanislaw Wojciechowski at the opening of the first Sejm, February 1919, National Digital Archives, public domain.',
