    'war1920' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The Battle of Warsaw, 1920',
            'description' => 'Why Poland and Soviet Russia went to war in 1919, how the Red Army reached the edge of Warsaw in August 1920, and where the Miracle on the Vistula began.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · Independence and war · Topic 02',
            'title' => 'The Battle of',
            'title_accent' => 'Warsaw, 1920',
            'lede' => 'Two years after it came back on the map, Poland was fighting for its life against the Red Army on the edge of its own capital. Ten frames on how that war began, how close it came, how it turned in a single week of August, and why the victory is still called a miracle. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Ten frames · about ten minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'vacuum',
                'photo' => 'vacuum',
                'label' => 'The empty east',
                'year' => '1918 - 1919',
                'title' => 'Two armies walk into the same empty land',
                'body' => 'The last topic followed the Second Republic from 1918 to 1939 and left one war untold. This is that war. When Germany lost the First World War in November 1918, its troops began pulling out of the lands they had taken from Russia, today\'s Lithuania, Belarus and Ukraine. Lenin, the Bolshevik leader in Moscow, sent the Red Army west behind them, because his revolution was meant to reach Germany. Polish units moved east into the same ground. Nobody declared war. The first shots are usually dated to February 1919, in what is now Belarus, and by that April Polish troops held Vilnius.',
                'caption' => 'Polish cavalry in the market square at Pinsk, in today\'s Belarus, 1919',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'ideas',
                'photo' => 'ideas',
                'label' => 'Two eastern plans',
                'year' => '1919 - 1920',
                'title' => 'Federation, annexation, or a Soviet Poland',
                'body' => 'Poles disagreed about what to do with that ground. Jozef Pilsudski, the socialist commander who had taken charge of the new state in November 1918, wanted a belt of allied countries east of Poland, above all a Ukraine strong enough to keep Russia at a distance. Roman Dmowski, leader of the nationalist National Democrats, wanted a smaller state that annexed only what it could make Polish. The Lithuanians, who had a country of their own, wanted neither, and Belarusian villages stayed out of it. Moscow had a third plan: in March 1920 Lenin approved an offensive that would go through Poland into Europe.',
                'caption' => 'Jozef Pilsudski, seated in the middle, on the Belarusian front in 1919',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'kyiv',
                'photo' => 'kyiv',
                'label' => 'The Kyiv gamble',
                'year' => 'April - June 1920',
                'title' => 'Pilsudski strikes first and takes Kyiv',
                'body' => 'Rather than wait to be attacked, Pilsudski went east. On 21 April 1920 Poland signed a treaty in Warsaw with Symon Petliura, head of the Ukrainian People\'s Republic, whom the Bolsheviks had already driven out of Kyiv: Poland recognised his state, he gave up his claim to eastern Galicia, and the two armies advanced together. The offensive opened on 25 April, and on 7 May Polish and Ukrainian troops entered a city the Red Army had left. Ukrainians did not join in the numbers Pilsudski had counted on, and Petliura\'s army reached only some 20,000 men. On 5 June the First Cavalry Army of Semyon Budyonny, the best horsemen the Bolsheviks had, broke the front south of Kyiv.',
                'caption' => 'Polish infantry marching into Kyiv on 7 May 1920, watched by a crowd',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'advance',
                'photo' => 'advance',
                'label' => 'The summer rout',
                'year' => 'July 1920',
                'title' => 'Over the corpse of White Poland',
                'body' => 'The Poles fell back across Ukraine, and the north gave way too. On 2 July 1920 Mikhail Tukhachevsky, the 27-year-old commander of the Soviet Western Front, had an order read to his soldiers: over the corpse of White Poland lies the road to a worldwide conflagration, on to Vilnius, Minsk and Warsaw. His offensive opened two days later and did not stop. The Red Army took Vilnius on 14 July and handed it to Lithuania. Britain offered an armistice on a line drawn by its foreign secretary, Lord Curzon, close to Poland\'s border today, and Moscow refused. On 23 July it named a Polish revolutionary committee, under the secret police chief Feliks Dzierzynski, to govern Poland once it fell.',
                'caption' => 'A Soviet poster of 1920 showing Poland as the last dog of the Entente',
                'credit' => 'Poster by Viktor Deni, New York Public Library, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'defence',
                'photo' => 'defence',
                'label' => 'A country called up',
                'year' => 'July - August 1920',
                'title' => 'A farmer takes office, a hundred thousand enlist',
                'body' => 'Warsaw got ready to be besieged. On 1 July parliament created a Council for the Defence of the State under Pilsudski, and two days later it asked the country for men: 105,714 volunteered, among them whole school classes with their teachers and some 9,000 scouts. General Jozef Haller, who had led the Polish army raised in France, was put in charge of them. On 24 July a Government of National Defence took office under Wincenty Witos, a working farmer who led the peasant party, with the socialist Ignacy Daszynski as his deputy. Hungary sent 35 million rifle cartridges through Romania, the only route still open.',
                'caption' => 'Women volunteers of the Polish army at the front, August 1920',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, Agence Rol, National Library of France, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'codes',
                'photo' => 'codes',
                'label' => 'Reading the enemy',
                'year' => '1919 - 1920',
                'title' => 'The Poles are reading the Red Army\'s radio',
                'body' => 'The Polish staff had one advantage the other side could not see. In September 1919 a lieutenant of 27, Jan Kowalewski, who spoke Russian and had trained as an engineer, broke the first Red Army cipher keys. Three mathematicians, Stefan Mazurkiewicz, Waclaw Sierpinski and Stanislaw Lesniewski, were brought in to help, and through 1920 Polish cryptologists read Soviet orders as they were transmitted. On 13 August they read the order of the Soviet 16th Army to take Warsaw. The next day volunteer cavalry captured the radio station of the Soviet Fourth Army at Ciechanow, and that army spent the decisive days with no way to hear from Tukhachevsky.',
                'caption' => 'A staff sketch of where the Bolshevik armies stood, 30 August 1920',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'radzymin',
                'photo' => 'radzymin',
                'label' => 'Radzymin and Ossow',
                'year' => '13 - 15 August 1920',
                'title' => 'The fighting reaches the bus stops of today',
                'body' => 'The battle opened on 13 August at Radzymin, 25 kilometres from the middle of Warsaw, and the town changed hands for three days. On 14 August at Ossow, the next village along, a battalion of the 236th Volunteer Regiment, schoolboys and students with a few weeks of training, held off a Red Army brigade. Their chaplain, Father Ignacy Skorupka, was killed there at 27 and became the face of the whole battle. North of the city, on the river Wkra, the Fifth Army of General Wladyslaw Sikorski attacked rather than wait to be surrounded. Both are on a Warsaw bus route today.',
                'caption' => 'Order number 71, issued on 14 August 1920 and read out to every unit',
                'credit' => 'Central Military Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'wieprz',
                'photo' => 'wieprz',
                'label' => 'The counter-attack',
                'year' => '16 - 25 August 1920',
                'title' => 'Five divisions come up from the south',
                'body' => 'Between Tukhachevsky\'s armies and the Soviet forces marching on Lviv, where Joseph Stalin served as political commissar, a gap had opened north of Lublin. On 16 August Pilsudski attacked into it from the river Wieprz, leading the strike group himself, and covered 45 kilometres on the first day. The Red Army, cut off from its supplies and its orders, broke and ran, and within ten days it was beaten. The usual estimate is some 25,000 Soviet soldiers killed and 66,000 taken prisoner, with nearly 50,000 more crossing into German East Prussia, where they were interned.',
                'caption' => 'Polish cavalry drawn up in line during the war of 1920',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'riga',
                'photo' => 'riga',
                'label' => 'The peace of 1921',
                'year' => 'September 1920 - March 1921',
                'title' => 'A border drawn through three nations',
                'body' => 'The war ran on into the autumn. In the battle of the Niemen, from 20 to 26 September, the Polish army broke Tukhachevsky\'s rebuilt front and took Grodno, and an armistice came into force on 18 October. Nine days before it, General Lucjan Zeligowski staged a mutiny that Pilsudski had quietly ordered, marched into Vilnius, held by the Lithuanians, and set up a statelet there that voted to join Poland in 1922. The peace signed in Riga on 18 March 1921 fixed the border, split Belarusians and Ukrainians between Poland and the Soviet republics, and left Petliura\'s Ukraine on the Soviet side. In the camps of both armies prisoners died in thousands.',
                'caption' => 'The Polish delegation to the peace talks at Riga, 1921',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'memory',
                'photo' => 'memory',
                'label' => 'Miracle and memory',
                'year' => '1920 - today',
                'title' => 'Why they still call it a miracle',
                'body' => 'On 14 August 1920, with the outcome still open, the nationalist writer Stanislaw Stronski asked in print for a miracle on the Vistula. His camp kept the phrase, because a miracle owes nothing to a commander they disliked, and Poles have argued ever since over how much of the plan was Pilsudski\'s and how much belonged to his chief of staff, General Tadeusz Rozwadowski. The British ambassador Edgar d\'Abernon called it the eighteenth decisive battle in world history. Historians differ over how far the revolution would really have travelled. 15 August is now Armed Forces Day and, in church, the Assumption. The graves are at Ossow and Radzymin, and a museum opened at Ossow in 2025.',
                'caption' => 'The war cemetery at Ossow, where volunteers of 1920 are buried',
                'credit' => 'Photo: MOs810, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A schematic map of the Polish-Soviet war in 1920: the Polish offensive on Kyiv in the spring, the Soviet advance on Warsaw and on Lviv in the summer, the Polish counter-attack from the river Wieprz in August, and the border agreed at Riga in 1921',
            'caption' => 'Schematic: the campaign of 1920 on today\'s map. Towns are in their places. The arrows and the borders are approximate.',
            'labels' => [
                'sea' => 'Baltic Sea',
                'germany' => 'Germany',
                'east_prussia' => 'East Prussia',
                'lithuania' => 'Lithuania',
                'soviet' => 'Soviet Russia',
                'czechoslovakia' => 'Czechoslovakia',
                'romania' => 'Romania',
                'border' => 'Border agreed at Riga, 1921',
                'kyiv_thrust' => 'Polish offensive, April 1920',
                'soviet_advance' => 'Red Army, July and August',
                'budyonny' => 'Budyonny towards Lviv',
                'counter' => 'Counter-attack, 16 August',
                'warsaw' => 'Warsaw',
                'radzymin' => 'Radzymin',
                'ossow' => 'Ossow',
                'kyiv' => 'Kyiv',
                'minsk' => 'Minsk',
                'vilnius' => 'Vilnius',
                'lwow' => 'Lviv',
                'grodno' => 'Grodno',
                'brest' => 'Brest',
                'lublin' => 'Lublin',
            ],
        ],
        'periodmap' => [
            'alt' => 'A Polish staff sketch of the counter-attack from the Wieprz, 16-18 August 1920',
            'caption' => 'The counter-attack from the river Wieprz, 16 to 18 August 1920, as the Polish army\'s own historian mapped it ten years later. The lettering is Polish: natarcie means attack, odwrot means retreat.',
            'credit' => 'Map: Major Adam Przybylski, The Polish War 1918-1921, 1930, public domain',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The course of the war follows the historian Miroslaw Szumilo of the Institute of National Remembrance, Janusz Odziemkowski in the same institute\'s bulletin, and the Polish Press Agency\'s history service dzieje.pl. The alliance with Petliura and the Ukrainian army follow the institute\'s Przystanek Historia and the Ukrainian historian Andrij Rukkas. Tukhachevsky\'s order of 2 July is quoted from Odziemkowski and from dzieje.pl. The radio intelligence follows Polska Zbrojna and Andrzej Peplonski via dzieje.pl. The dispute over the plan follows Mariusz Patelski of the University of Opole. The treaties, the armistice and the law on Armed Forces Day are checked against the Journal of Laws in the Sejm archive, and the Riga settlement against dzieje.pl and the Museum of Polish History. Ossow follows the Polish Army Museum, which opened its Museum of the Battle of Warsaw 1920 there in August 2025. Title picture: a Polish machine-gun crew in the field in 1920, National Digital Archives, public domain.',
        ],
