    'sept1939' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'September 1939: two invasions of Poland',
            'description' => 'What happened in Poland in September 1939: Wielun and Westerplatte on the first morning, the siege of Warsaw, and the Soviet invasion of 17 September.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · Independence and war · Topic 03',
            'title' => '1939:',
            'title_accent' => 'two invasions',
            'lede' => 'Nazi Germany attacked on 1 September, the Soviet Union on 17 September, and five weeks later the country had been cut in two along a line the two of them had agreed on in advance. Nine frames on how that was arranged, what each side actually had, where the cavalry legend came from, what it cost and where you can stand in it today. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about ten minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'road',
                'photo' => 'road',
                'label' => 'The road to war',
                'year' => '1938 - 1939',
                'title' => 'Danzig, a motorway, and a pact in Moscow',
                'body' => 'The republic was twenty years old. On 24 October 1938 the German foreign minister Joachim von Ribbentrop asked Poland\'s ambassador in Berlin, Jozef Lipski, for the Free City of Danzig, a German-speaking port under League of Nations rule, now Gdansk, and for a German motorway and railway across the Polish corridor, the strip that gave Poland the sea. Poland refused. On 31 March 1939 the British prime minister Neville Chamberlain promised to stand by Poland. On 28 April Adolf Hitler tore up the 1934 non-aggression declaration with Poland. On 23 August Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union signed a pact in Moscow whose secret protocol divided the country along the Narew, Vistula and San.',
                'caption' => 'Molotov signs the German-Soviet pact in Moscow, with Ribbentrop and Stalin behind',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, 23 August 1939, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'forces',
                'photo' => 'forces',
                'label' => 'Horses and tanks',
                'year' => '1 September 1939',
                'title' => 'The myth that outlived the campaign',
                'body' => 'On paper it was one-sided: the Institute of National Remembrance counts 1.8 million German troops, 2,800 tanks and 3,000 aircraft against a million Poles, 880 tanks and tankettes and 400 aircraft, with Poland two thirds mobilised. The charge everyone remembers, cavalry against tanks, never happened. On the afternoon of 1 September the 18th Pomeranian Uhlan Regiment under Colonel Kazimierz Mastalerz charged German infantry resting in a clearing at Krojanty. Armoured cars in the trees cut it down. The Italian reporter Indro Montanelli was shown the field next day and told by German officers the Poles had charged tanks. Hitler\'s propaganda minister, Joseph Goebbels, filmed it.',
                'caption' => 'A platoon of Polish 7TP tanks on exercise, photographed in 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photo: National Digital Archives, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'first',
                'photo' => 'first',
                'label' => 'The first morning',
                'year' => '1 September 1939',
                'title' => 'Two towns argue over who was hit first',
                'body' => 'It began before dawn in two places at once. German dive-bombers left three quarters of Wielun, a town with no garrison, in ruins. The hour is argued over, because the traditional 4.40 assumes German summer time and Grzegorz Bebnik of the Institute of National Remembrance argues for 5.40. The toll is disputed too, from about a hundred victims named to the traditional twelve hundred, which Mateusz Jan Piatkowski reads as the county\'s figure. At 4.45 the German battleship Schleswig-Holstein shelled the Polish depot at Westerplatte in Danzig, where two hundred men under Major Henryk Sucharski held for seven days. The Polish Post Office there fell the same day, after fourteen hours.',
                'caption' => 'The centre of Wielun after the bombing of 1 September 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'battles',
                'photo' => 'battles',
                'label' => 'Mokra and the Bzura',
                'year' => '1 - 22 September',
                'title' => 'Guns stop tanks, and one counter-attack hits home',
                'body' => 'Where the Polish army did stop armour, it used guns. On 1 September at Mokra the Wolynska Cavalry Brigade under Colonel Julian Filipowicz held off the German 4th Panzer Division all day with anti-tank guns and an armoured train called Smialy. The border line broke anyway, and the largest battle of the campaign was fought behind it. From 9 September General Tadeusz Kutrzeba turned his Poznan Army, joined by the Pomorze Army of General Wladyslaw Bortnowski and about 200,000 men in all, against the German flank on the river Bzura west of Warsaw. It took Leczyca and Piatek, was surrounded, and by 22 September was finished. Some 30,000 men broke through to Warsaw and Modlin.',
                'caption' => 'Wrecked Polish guns and carts on the bank of the Bzura, 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'capital',
                'photo' => 'capital',
                'label' => 'The capital holds',
                'year' => '8 - 28 September',
                'title' => 'Three weeks of a city being taken apart',
                'body' => 'Britain and France declared war on 3 September and then stayed put. That same day General Walerian Czuma took charge of the defence of Warsaw, with the mayor, Stefan Starzynski, as civil commissioner and as the voice on the radio. German armour reached the Ochota gates on 8 September and was thrown back. The city was shelled and bombed for three weeks, worst on Monday 25 September, when the raids took the Old Town, the National Museum and the filter station that supplied the taps. Estimates of civilians killed run from ten to twenty-five thousand. With no water, power or ammunition left, General Tadeusz Kutrzeba, in from the Bzura, signed the surrender at 13.15 on 28 September.',
                'caption' => 'A Warsaw family sheltering by the Opera House during the siege',
                'credit' => 'Photograph by Julien Bryan, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'soviet',
                'photo' => 'soviet',
                'label' => 'The second invasion',
                'year' => '17 September 1939',
                'title' => 'A second army crosses from the east',
                'body' => 'Poland was still fighting in the west when the other half of the August pact arrived. Before dawn on 17 September the deputy foreign commissar Vladimir Potemkin handed the Polish ambassador in Moscow, Waclaw Grzybowski, a note saying the Polish state had ceased to exist and that the Red Army was crossing to protect the people of Western Ukraine and Western Belarus. Grzybowski refused to take it. At least 620,000 Soviet soldiers came over that morning. Marshal Edward Rydz-Smigly, the Polish commander, ordered his men not to fight them unless attacked. That night he, President Ignacy Moscicki and the government crossed into Romania, where they were interned.',
                'caption' => 'German and Soviet troops on the same street in Brest, 22 September 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Gutjahr, Dutch National Archives, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'zones',
                'photo' => 'zones',
                'label' => 'The two zones',
                'year' => '22 September - 6 October',
                'title' => 'The fighting stops and a new border is drawn',
                'body' => 'On 22 September German and Soviet troops paraded together in Brest, and the Germans then pulled back over the river Bug and handed the city over. Resistance ran on. Lwow, today Lviv in Ukraine, had given up to the Red Army that same day, and its officers were deported instead of being sent home as promised. Modlin fortress surrendered on 29 September, the Hel peninsula on 2 October, and the last battle ended at Kock on 6 October, where General Franciszek Kleeberg\'s Polesie group ran out of ammunition and 16,860 men laid down their arms. On 28 September Ribbentrop and Molotov signed a treaty in Moscow that moved the line east to the Bug and put Lithuania in the Soviet sphere.',
                'caption' => 'Polish troops massed after the surrender of the Modlin fortress, 29 September 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Piepen, National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'cost',
                'photo' => 'cost',
                'label' => 'What it cost',
                'year' => '1939 - 1940',
                'title' => 'The bill, and how much of it is still guesswork',
                'body' => 'Nobody can say exactly what those five weeks cost. The usual figure for Polish military dead, 66,000, is an estimate the Polish government\'s War Reparations Bureau made in January 1947, because the army\'s casualty returns are lost. The historian Tadeusz Panecki later reached 95,000 to 97,000. Nazi Germany lost some 16,000 dead, the Soviet Union about 3,000. Civilian dead were never counted nationally. Some 420,000 Polish soldiers went into German captivity, 250,000 into Soviet hands. From February 1940 the Soviet authorities deported 320,000 to 340,000 Polish citizens east, and that spring the Soviet secret police, the NKVD, shot nearly 22,000 prisoners: Katyn, in the next article.',
                'caption' => 'Polish prisoners of war leaving Warsaw after the surrender, 30 September 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photograph HU 5485, Imperial War Museums, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'visiting',
                'photo' => 'visiting',
                'label' => 'Where to stand',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'The places that still carry the dates',
                'body' => 'Most of it is walkable. Westerplatte in Gdansk is a branch of the Museum of the Second World War, with the defenders\' cemetery, the ruins of the barracks and an exhibition in the old power house. A new museum building has been going up there since 2026, so check what is open before you go. In the city the Polish Post Office on plac Obroncow Poczty Polskiej has its monument and its own museum, reopening after rebuilding. Wielun sounds its sirens at 4.40 every 1 September. The Bzura has its museum at Sochaczew, and Kock keeps the war cemetery where Kleeberg lies.',
                'caption' => 'The cemetery of the Westerplatte defenders, with the Coast Defenders monument',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Virtual-Pano, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A printed map of eastern Poland, in German, with a heavy inked line running from East Prussia south past Brest, Zamosc and Rzeszow to the Carpathians, a large signature written across the middle, and signature boxes at the foot dated Moscow, 28 September 1939',
            'caption' => 'The demarcation map attached to the German-Soviet treaty of 28 September 1939, printed in German. The boxes at the foot are signed for the German government by Joachim von Ribbentrop and for the Soviet government by Vyacheslav Molotov, and a further signature runs across the middle of the sheet.',
            'credit' => 'Photographed in 1946 for the Nuremberg trials, public domain',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The road to war follows the Institute of National Remembrance, which dates the German demands over Danzig and the corridor to Ribbentrop\'s conversation with Jozef Lipski on 24 October 1938, and Hitler\'s denunciation of the 1934 declaration to his Reichstag speech of 28 April 1939, in which he also denounced the naval agreement with Britain of 1935. The British guarantee is quoted from the Hansard record of the Commons debate of 31 March 1939, and the pact of 23 August with its secret protocol follows the Polish Press Agency\'s history service dzieje.pl. The strengths are the institute\'s own figures and are not the only ones published: the press agency gives 2,000 German aircraft rather than about 3,000, and 700 Polish tanks rather than 880, the difference being whether tankettes are counted. That two thirds of the Polish mobilisation and under half of the concentration were finished on 1 September is the institute\'s account, drawn from General Waclaw Stachiewicz. The charge at Krojanty, the visit of Indro Montanelli of the Corriere della Sera to the field the following day, the reprinting of his account in Die Wehrmacht on 13 September 1939 and the two German propaganda films follow the Museum of the Second World War in Gdansk, which states plainly that no charge was made against armoured vehicles in the first month of the war, and which names Andrzej Wajda\'s film Lotna of 1959 as carrying the story on. Wielun follows dzieje.pl and the Institute for Western Affairs in Poznan: the hour of 5.40 rather than 4.40 is Grzegorz Bebnik\'s, from the flying unit\'s war diary. About a hundred victims have been identified by name, an investigation by the Institute of National Remembrance established at least 109 inhabitants and 18 people in the hospital, and Mateusz Jan Piatkowski reads the traditional figure of twelve hundred as the whole county\'s. Westerplatte follows dzieje.pl, which gives 4.45 and the ship\'s log time of 4.48, about two hundred defenders and fifteen dead. The Polish Post Office follows the Institute of National Remembrance and the Museum of the Second World War: fifty-seven people inside, more than fourteen hours of fighting, two German courts martial in September, the shootings at Zaspa on 5 October 1939, the grave found by chance in 1991, and the acquittal by a court in Lubeck at the end of the 1990s. Mokra, the Bzura and the siege of Warsaw follow dzieje.pl and the institute\'s Przystanek Historia. The number of civilians killed in the siege is an estimate and published figures differ widely, so a range is given instead of one number. The Soviet invasion, the note handed to Ambassador Waclaw Grzybowski, the order of Marshal Edward Rydz-Smigly and the crossing into Romania follow dzieje.pl and the Museum of Polish History, which records that the Romanian government decided on internment on 18 September. The parade at Brest follows the Museum of the Second World War. Kock follows dzieje.pl, which gives 16,860 men laying down their arms on 6 October, and Hel and Modlin follow the Museum of the Second World War and Przystanek Historia. The estimate of about 66,000 Polish military dead was made by the War Reparations Bureau in January 1947 from indirect evidence and is probably low, which is why Tadeusz Panecki\'s higher figure stands beside it. The numbers of prisoners are dzieje.pl\'s, and the deportation figure of 320,000 to 340,000 is the archival count from NKVD transport records, which replaced an older estimate of about a million. What is open at Westerplatte, at the post office museum, at Wielun, at Sochaczew and at Kock is taken from those institutions\' own pages and it changes: a new museum building on Westerplatte has been under construction since 2026, and the post office museum is reopening after rebuilding. Title picture: defenders of Warsaw in September 1939, Central Photographic Agency, public domain.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the history section',
    ],

    'danzig' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The Free City of Danzig, 1920-1939',
            'description' => 'A League of Nations city that belonged to neither Poland nor Germany, with its own parliament and currency, and the crisis that ended it in 1939.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · Danzig',
            'title' => 'The Free City',
            'title_accent' => 'of Danzig',
            'lede' => 'For nineteen years Gdansk was not in Poland and not in Germany. It was a small state of its own, invented at the peace conference of 1919 because Poland needed a port and the port was German. Nine frames on how that was arranged, what Poland held inside it, why Poland gave up on it and built Gdynia instead, how the Nazis took it from within, and what a visitor finds there now. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'what',
                'photo' => 'what',
                'label' => 'A state of a kind',
                'year' => '1920 - 1939',
                'title' => 'The city that belonged to nobody',
                'body' => 'Gdansk spent nineteen years as neither Polish nor German. The Free City of Danzig was proclaimed on 15 November 1920 under articles 100 to 108 of the Treaty of Versailles, the settlement that ended the First World War, and it was put under the protection of the League of Nations, the body founded that same year to keep the peace. It had a constitution passed on 11 August 1920, a parliament called the Volkstag, a senate that governed, a flag, its own postage stamps and, from 1923, its own money, the Danzig gulden. Its own census of 1923 counted 366,730 people, and 95 per cent of them gave German as their mother tongue.',
                'caption' => 'A 100-pound Free City of Danzig state loan bond, 10 October 1927',
                'credit' => 'Scan: EDHAC collection, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'why',
                'photo' => 'why',
                'label' => 'Why it existed',
                'year' => '1918 - 1920',
                'title' => 'Wilson promised Poland the sea',
                'body' => 'Poland had come back onto the map in November 1918, after 123 years carved up between Russia, Prussia and Austria. On 8 January 1918 the American president, Woodrow Wilson, made the thirteenth of his Fourteen Points a Poland with free access to the sea. The obvious port was Danzig, at the mouth of the Vistula, the river that drains most of Poland. It was also a German city. At the peace conference in Paris the Polish delegate, Roman Dmowski, asked for it, and the British prime minister, David Lloyd George, refused to put a German city under Polish rule. The free city was the compromise. The strip of land that carried Poland to the coast became known as the Polish corridor.',
                'caption' => 'The Hall of Mirrors at Versailles as the peace treaty is signed, 28 June 1919',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Helen Johns Kirtland, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'rights',
                'photo' => 'rights',
                'label' => 'What Poland held',
                'year' => '1920 - 1926',
                'title' => 'Polish rights inside somebody else\'s city',
                'body' => 'A convention signed in Paris on 9 November 1920 set out Poland\'s rights there. Poland spoke for the Free City abroad, the city sat inside the Polish customs area, and Poland ran the railways and a post office of its own, opened on 5 January 1925. The harbour was run by a board of five Polish and five Danzig commissioners under a neutral chairman, and a Polish Commissioner-General, Marian Chodacki from 1936, dealt with the Danzig Senate. In March 1924 the League Council granted Poland a spit of land in the harbour, Westerplatte, as a military depot. Poland took the site over in October 1925, twenty-one soldiers landed on 18 January 1926, and the League capped the guard at 88.',
                'caption' => 'Poland\'s Post Office No. 3 on the Danzig harbour channel, after 1927',
                'credit' => 'Photo: National Library of Poland, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'friction',
                'photo' => 'friction',
                'label' => 'The quarrels',
                'year' => '1920 - 1932',
                'title' => 'A hundred disputes and one destroyer',
                'body' => 'It went wrong at once. On 21 July 1920, with the Red Army marching on Warsaw, the steamer Triton docked with ammunition for the Polish army and Danzig dockers refused to touch it. British and French servicemen unloaded it from 27 July. That week is why Poland wanted a depot of its own. Money was the next quarrel, and when the Polish mark collapsed in 1923 the Free City left it and issued the gulden. One legal study counts 106 Polish-Danzig disputes taken to the League between 1920 and 1933. The most theatrical came on 15 June 1932, when the Polish destroyer ORP Wicher entered the port over Danzig\'s formal protest while a British squadron was visiting. Nothing was fired.',
                'caption' => 'The Polish Navy destroyer ORP Wicher lying off the shore, 1930s',
                'credit' => 'Photo: National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'gdynia',
                'photo' => 'gdynia',
                'label' => 'Poland builds Gdynia',
                'year' => '1922 - 1939',
                'title' => 'A fishing village takes Danzig\'s trade',
                'body' => 'Poland drew the obvious conclusion and built a port it controlled, up the coast. The Sejm, the Polish parliament, voted for it on 23 September 1922, the engineer Tadeusz Wenda picked the site, and Gdynia, a village of about 1,200 people, opened a temporary harbour on 29 April 1923. From June 1925 Germany shut its market to Polish coal, which pushed the coal to the sea, and from 1926 the minister for industry and trade, Eugeniusz Kwiatkowski, drove the building. By 1933 Gdynia was carrying more of Poland\'s trade than Danzig. The Encyklopedia Gdanska puts Danzig\'s best year at 8.6 million tonnes in 1928 and 7.1 million in 1938, the year Gdynia handled about 9.2 million.',
                'caption' => 'A Polish State Railways coal wagon tipped into a ship at Gdynia, 1937',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Annemarie Schwarzenbach, Swiss National Library, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'nazi',
                'photo' => 'nazi',
                'label' => 'The takeover',
                'year' => '1930 - 1938',
                'title' => 'The Nazis take the city from inside',
                'body' => 'In October 1930 the Nazi party sent a 28-year-old Bavarian, Albert Forster, to Danzig as its Gauleiter, its regional chief. On 28 May 1933 the party took just over half the vote and 38 of the 72 seats in the Volkstag, and Arthur Greiser became President of the Senate in November 1934. The election of 7 April 1935 brought just under sixty per cent and about 43 seats, short of the two thirds needed to rewrite the constitution, so the rest was done by arrests and threats. The communists were banned in 1934, the Social Democrats on 14 October 1936, and the Centre party was gone by October 1937. The League\'s man in the city, Sean Lester, protested and was ignored.',
                'caption' => 'A crowd fills Danzig\'s Long Market to demand union with Germany, 1933',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Robert Sennecke, Bibliotheque nationale de France, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'crisis',
                'photo' => 'crisis',
                'label' => 'Danzig or war',
                'year' => '1938 - 1939',
                'title' => 'The last year, and who got out of it',
                'body' => 'On 24 October 1938 the German foreign minister, Joachim von Ribbentrop, asked Poland\'s ambassador in Berlin, Jozef Lipski, for Danzig. Poland refused. The city\'s Jews, about ten thousand, had read the signs: the synagogues of Sopot and Wrzeszcz were burned on 12 and 13 November 1938, race laws followed on 21 November, and on 16 December the community\'s board resolved to get almost everyone out. It sold everything it owned and 1,272 people were gone by the end of July. A former French minister, Marcel Deat, asked in print on 4 May whether anyone should die for Danzig. On 4 August the Senate told Poland its customs inspectors could no longer work, then denied it had ever said so.',
                'caption' => 'A Danzig passport with the British visa that carried 75 children to London, 1939',
                'credit' => 'Scan: Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'end',
                'photo' => 'end',
                'label' => 'The end of it',
                'year' => '1939 - 1947',
                'title' => 'Nine days as head of state, then a province',
                'body' => 'On 23 August 1939 the Senate made Forster head of state, an office the constitution did not have, and on 25 August the German training ship Schleswig-Holstein moored opposite Westerplatte. She opened fire on 1 September, and that day Forster declared the Free City part of the German Reich while Adolf Hitler signed a law of reunification. Fifty-seven people held the Polish Post Office for fifteen hours, and after two courts martial 38 were shot at Zaspa on 5 October. Stutthof camp opened on 2 September with about 150 Poles from the city. On 30 March 1945 the Red Army took the ruins, the Germans fled or were expelled, and Poles from the lands Poland lost in the east replaced them.',
                'caption' => 'Hitler driven through a swastika-hung Danzig street, 19 September 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photo: US Holocaust Memorial Museum, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'What is left',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'Where the Free City is still visible',
                'body' => 'The Polish Post Office still stands on the square now named after its defenders, and the museum inside it reopens on 5 October 2026, the anniversary of the shootings at Zaspa. Westerplatte is a branch of the Museum of the Second World War, open while a new museum building goes up there, with the guardhouses, the barracks ruins and the defenders\' cemetery. The museum itself, on the edge of the old town, opened on 23 March 2017. Around it the Main Town has been rebuilt from photographs and measured drawings after 1945, and the shipyard where the free trade union Solidarnosc began in 1980 is a walk to the north.',
                'caption' => 'The Polish Post Office in Gdansk today, on plac Obroncow Poczty Polskiej',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Elke Wetzig, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A colour atlas map of the Baltic coast in German, with the Bay of Danzig at the top, Danzig and Zoppot on the shore, Gdingen to the north-west, and a corridor of Polish territory hatched in red and green running south past Dirschau and Graudenz between West Prussia and East Prussia',
            'caption' => 'The Free City and the Polish corridor as a German atlas drew them: Meyers Grosser Hausatlas, 1938, in German. Danzig sits inside its own border, with Gdingen, today Gdynia, just outside it to the north-west, and the corridor runs south between West Prussia and East Prussia.',
            'credit' => 'Map from Meyers Grosser Hausatlas, 1938, public domain',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The shape of the Free City follows the Encyklopedia Gdanska, published by the Fundacja Gdanska, and the Polish Institute of National Remembrance. The census of 1 November 1923 counted 366,730 people, of whom 348,493 gave German as their mother tongue, and that is where the familiar 95 per cent comes from. It is worth knowing what it measures. It counts mother tongue rather than declared nationality, and most Poles in the territory were not Danzig citizens, so counting citizens alone pushes the German share higher still. Polish historians put Poles and Kashubians at 35,000 to 40,000, nearer a tenth. The Encyklopedia Gdanska and the historian Kazimierz Piwarski both settle on over 90 per cent, which is the honest form. The area is given as 1,893 square kilometres of land, or 1,952 including inland water. The constitution was passed by the constituent assembly on 11 August 1920 and finally approved by the League of Nations in May 1922, and the Volkstag had 120 seats until an amendment of 1930 cut it to 72. The gulden is a 1923 currency, introduced that October, with the Bank von Danzig following in February 1924. Poland\'s rights follow the Polish-Danzig convention signed in Paris on 9 November 1920. The Polish Post Office opened on 5 January 1925, which the Polish Press Agency\'s history service dzieje.pl dates from the office\'s own records. Westerplatte follows the Gdanski Notatnik Historyczny and the Central Military Library: the League Council granted the depot on 14 March 1924, Poland took the site over on 31 October 1925, the first detachment of twenty-one men landed from the minesweeper ORP Mewa on 18 January 1926, and the League fixed the guard at 88 men, two officers, twenty non-commissioned officers and sixty-six other ranks. By September 1939 there were about 210 men there, smuggled in over years, and the exact roll is still argued over. The refusal to unload the Triton on 21 July 1920 and the unloading by British and French servicemen from 27 July follow a study of Rear Admiral Michal Borowski\'s papers and the city of Gdansk\'s own history service. The count of 106 Polish-Danzig disputes between 1920 and 1933 is one legal study, by Konrad Purzycki, and is given here as that rather than as a settled number. For the Wicher this page uses the version built on her captain\'s own memoir, in which Tadeusz Podjazd-Morgenstern was ordered to answer obstruction by all available means including firearms. The better-known version, that he was to shell the nearest Danzig government building if the Polish flag were insulted, comes from popular history and no archival source for it was found, so it is left out. Gdynia follows the Museum of the City of Gdynia and the Institute of National Remembrance: the act of 23 September 1922, the engineer Tadeusz Wenda, about 1,200 villagers, the temporary harbour of 29 April 1923 and about 127,000 inhabitants by mid-1939. The tonnages are the Encyklopedia Gdanska\'s for Danzig and the Polish education portal zpe.gov.pl for Gdynia, and the years in between are not published as a matching series, so no year-by-year comparison is attempted. That Gdynia was the busiest port on the Baltic is a claim this page does not make, because the comparison with Leningrad, Riga and the Swedish ports was never checked. Forster was born on 26 July 1902 and sent to Danzig as Gauleiter on 15 October 1930, so he was 28. The election figures are the seat counts, which the sources agree on, rather than the decimal percentages, which they do not. That the party fell short of two thirds in 1935 and reached it later by pressure on opposition deputies follows the German and Polish accounts together. It was the Senate, not the Volkstag, that made Forster head of state on 23 August 1939, and the Danzig constitution had no such office. The Jewish community follows Krzysztof Ulanowski\'s study in Studia Historica Gedanensia of 2022, built on the state archive in Gdansk, together with the Encyklopedia Gdanska: 10,448 people in the Free City in 1929, the boycott of April 1933, the burning of the Sopot and Wrzeszcz synagogues on 12 and 13 November 1938, the Danzig race laws of 21 November, the board\'s resolution of 16 December 1938, the sale of the community\'s whole property for 330,000 gulden in January 1939, the last service in the Great Synagogue on 15 April and its demolition from 2 May, and 1,272 people leaving between January and July 1939. How many were left in September 1939 is genuinely unsettled, with figures from a few hundred to about 1,700, so no number is printed. Marcel Deat\'s article ran on the front page of L\'Oeuvre on 4 May 1939 under the heading Faut-il mourir pour Dantzig, and he was a former air minister rather than a sitting deputy, having lost his seat in 1936. The customs inspectors affair follows the British and French diplomatic papers of 1939: the Senate\'s decision of 4 August, the Polish note the same day with a deadline of the following evening, and the Senate\'s reply of 7 August that no such order had ever been issued. The Schleswig-Holstein was an obsolete pre-dreadnought used for training, not a modern battleship, and she carried 225 marines into the harbour on 25 August 1939. The Post Office follows the Institute of National Remembrance: fifty-seven people inside, more than fourteen hours of fighting, two courts martial on 8 and 29 September, 38 men shot at Zaspa on 5 October 1939, the grave found by accident in August 1991 and the sentences quashed by a court in Lubeck at the end of the 1990s. Stutthof follows the museum on the site, which dates the camp from 2 September 1939, counts about 150 people in the first transport and estimates about 110,000 prisoners and about 65,000 dead over the whole war. The taking of Gdansk on 30 March 1945 is the formal date and fighting went on after it. The Encyklopedia Gdanska puts the destruction of the Main Town at 95 per cent, and estimates for the city as a whole run from 80 to 95 per cent. What is open today is taken from the museums\' own pages in September 2026 and it changes: the Polish Post Office museum has been closed since January 2025 and is announced to reopen on 5 October 2026, and a new museum building at Westerplatte has been under construction since April 2026. Title picture: the Motlawa waterfront in Danzig in 1924, National Library of Poland, public domain.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],

    'westerplatte' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Westerplatte: the first shots, 1 September 1939',
            'description' => 'The small Polish depot in Danzig harbour that the German training ship Schleswig-Holstein shelled at 4.45 on 1 September 1939, and the seven days after.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · Westerplatte',
            'title' => 'Westerplatte',
            'title_accent' => 'and the first morning',
            'lede' => 'A sand spit in a harbour, a Polish depot of about two hundred men, and an old German training ship moored 150 metres away. At quarter to five in the morning on 1 September 1939 she opened fire, and the men on the spit held out for seven days. Nine frames on what was really there, what happened on each of those days, which parts of the story the sources support and which ones a poem and a film added later. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'depot',
                'photo' => 'depot',
                'label' => 'What was there',
                'year' => '1924 - 1939',
                'title' => 'A bathing beach turned into a depot',
                'body' => 'Westerplatte is a spit of sand two kilometres long at the mouth of the Martwa Wisla, the dead arm of the Vistula, across the entrance to the harbour of Danzig, today Gdansk. In 1939 that harbour was not in Poland. It belonged to the Free City of Danzig, a small German-speaking state under the League of Nations. From the 1830s it was a bathing resort with a bath house. In March 1924 the League granted Poland a military transit depot there and capped the guard at 88 men. Six guardhouses went up in the 1930s, four in reinforced concrete in 1933 and 1934, the barracks about 1936. The guard had one 75 mm field gun, two 37 mm anti-tank guns, four mortars and over forty machine guns.',
                'caption' => 'Guardhouse No 1 on Westerplatte today, its concrete still pitted by shellfire',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Ainka, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'night',
                'photo' => 'night',
                'label' => 'The ship arrives',
                'year' => '25 - 31 August 1939',
                'title' => 'An old training ship pays a courtesy call',
                'body' => 'On 1 September 1939 about two hundred men were on the spit rather than the permitted 88, rotated in quietly over years, under Major Henryk Sucharski, commander since 3 December 1938, and his deputy Captain Franciszek Dabrowski. They had watched a warship since 25 August. The Schleswig-Holstein was a pre-dreadnought battleship of 1906, turned into a floating school for naval cadets in 1936, and she moored 150 metres from the depot fence to honour the graves of the crew of the cruiser SMS Magdeburg, lost in 1914. She carried an assault company of about 225 marines under Wilhelm Henningsen. The attack was set for 26 August, and Adolf Hitler, the German leader, put it off by a week.',
                'caption' => 'The Schleswig-Holstein in the Danzig harbour channel, 1 September 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photo: photographer unknown, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'dawn',
                'photo' => 'dawn',
                'label' => 'Quarter to five',
                'year' => '1 September 1939',
                'title' => 'The first salvo, and the argument about it',
                'body' => 'At 4.45 on 1 September the ship fired on the depot from a few hundred metres. Her own log says 4.48, and the historian Jaroslaw Tuliszka reads the gap as the distance between the planned hour, the order and the guns. Whether this was the first shot of the war is argued about in Poland: German aircraft bombed the bridges at Tczew minutes earlier, and the raid that destroyed the town of Wielun is dated by some to 4.40 and by others to later, with Grzegorz Bebnik of the Institute of National Remembrance doubting the earlier hour because the witnesses were only questioned in 1961. On the spit, the assault company came over the harbour wall and was stopped in about ninety minutes.',
                'caption' => 'The port workshops burning beside the depot fence, 1 September 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photo: National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'seven',
                'photo' => 'seven',
                'label' => 'The seven days',
                'year' => '1 - 7 September 1939',
                'title' => 'Dive-bombers, flamethrowers and one doctor',
                'body' => 'The garrison beat off a dozen or more attacks over the week, and the count differs from historian to historian. The heaviest blow fell on 2 September, when about sixty Ju 87 Stuka dive-bombers dropped some 26.5 tonnes of bombs in forty minutes. Guardhouse No 5 collapsed with at least six men inside, the mortars were destroyed and the radio with them. German engineers came in with flamethrowers, and on 6 September they twice rolled fuel wagons into the depot to set the wood alight. Both attempts failed. Captain Mieczyslaw Slaby, the only doctor, worked in the barracks cellar after Free City customs men had confiscated his dressings and his operating table.',
                'caption' => 'Ju 87 Stuka dive-bombers over Poland, September 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Heinrich Hoffmann, German Federal Archives, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'dispute',
                'photo' => 'dispute',
                'label' => 'The argument',
                'year' => '1939 - 2013',
                'title' => 'Who was actually in command here',
                'body' => 'What passed between the two officers after the dive-bombers has never been settled. One account has Sucharski breaking down and wanting to surrender on 2 September, and Dabrowski taking over. A German officer, Walter Schug, reported a white flag over the depot at half past seven that evening. No Polish document confirms any of it. The heroic version was set by the reporter Melchior Wankowicz in 1959 and by Stanislaw Rozewicz\'s film Westerplatte in 1967. Surviving officers revised it from the 1990s and Mariusz Borowiak pressed the case in 2001, while Andrzej Drzycimski, Sucharski\'s biographer, defends him. Pawel Chochlew\'s film Tajemnica Westerplatte restarted it in 2013.',
                'caption' => 'The ruined barracks of the Westerplatte depot, where the officers argued',
                'credit' => 'Photo: 1bumer, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'surrender',
                'photo' => 'surrender',
                'label' => 'The white flag',
                'year' => '7 September 1939',
                'title' => 'Ten fifteen, and a sabre worth checking',
                'body' => 'On the morning of 7 September the German guns went over the peninsula again and the engineers came in ahead of the infantry with their flamethrowers. A white flag went up over the barracks at about 9.45 and Sucharski surrendered at about 10.15 to General Friedrich-Georg Eberhardt, who commanded the SS-Heimwehr Danzig, the German force raised in the city. Every Polish schoolchild is told that Eberhardt let him keep his sabre in recognition of the defence. Something of the sort did happen, and it did not last: the sabre was taken from him in his first prison camp, and by one account the weapon he was handed was not his own but Dabrowski\'s.',
                'caption' => 'General Friedrich-Georg Eberhardt salutes Major Henryk Sucharski, 7 September 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photo: photographer unknown, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'cost',
                'photo' => 'cost',
                'label' => 'What it cost',
                'year' => '1 - 12 September 1939',
                'title' => 'Fifteen dead, and a German figure in dispute',
                'body' => 'Fifteen Polish soldiers were killed on Westerplatte. Some historians count sixteen and add Sergeant Kazimierz Rasinski, whom the Germans murdered in captivity on 12 September for refusing to give up his radio codes. The Encyklopedia Gdanska puts the wounded at twenty-six, other accounts at forty or more. German losses are the harder number. Polish accounts written during the war and after it spoke of three to four hundred dead, which no German casualty return supports. The German figures give about fifty killed and between a hundred and twenty and a hundred and fifty wounded, and that is what historians now work from. The rest of the garrison went into captivity.',
                'caption' => 'German infantry in the splintered pine wood of Westerplatte, 7 September 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photo: National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'after',
                'photo' => 'after',
                'label' => 'Afterwards',
                'year' => '1939 - 2022',
                'title' => 'Camps, a grave in Italy, a grave found here',
                'body' => 'The defenders went into German prison camps, the officers to oflags and the men to stalags. Sucharski passed through Stablack, Arnswalde and Gross-Born, was freed in 1945, joined the Polish Second Corps in Italy and died of peritonitis in a British military hospital in Naples on 30 August 1946. He was buried at Casamassima near Bari, exhumed on 21 August 1971 and laid on Westerplatte on 1 September that year. Dabrowski caught tuberculosis in captivity, was pushed out of the navy in the Stalinist purges of the early 1950s and sold newspapers from a kiosk in Krakow until he was rehabilitated. He died there in 1962.',
                'caption' => 'Polish prisoners from the Westerplatte depot under guard, September 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photo: National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'Going there',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'What stands on the peninsula now',
                'body' => 'Archaeologists of the Museum of the Second World War found the defenders\' grave in 2019, and the nine men in it were buried on 4 November 2022 in a new military cemetery on the spit, beside Sucharski. The monument on the mound, a granite obelisk by the team of the sculptor Franciszek Duszenko, was unveiled on 9 October 1966. Guardhouse No 1 came through the battle, was moved in 1967 to save it from demolition and holds a branch of the Museum of Gdansk. The peninsula itself is an open-air branch of the war museum, with a building site on it since February 2026. Buses 106 and 138 go there all year and boats run from the old town in summer. On 1 September the sirens sound at 4.45.',
                'caption' => 'Major Henryk Sucharski\'s uniform and a sabre in Guardhouse No 1',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Fallaner, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A German nautical chart of the mouth of the Vistula at Danzig, with the harbour channel and the settlement of Neufahrwasser on its left bank, Westerplatte named at the top left where the channel meets the sea, depth soundings scattered across the bay, a compass rose to the right of them, and the old town of Danzig at the bottom left',
            'caption' => 'The harbour mouth before the depot existed: Die Weichsel von Neufahrwasser bis Danzig, a chart of the Reichs-Marine-Amt, Berlin 1909, in German, at 1:15000. Westerplatte is named at the top left, on the seaward side of the channel, and it was still a bathing resort when this was printed.',
            'credit' => 'Chart: Reichs-Marine-Amt, Berlin 1909, public domain',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The peninsula, the bathing resort, the garrison and the casualties follow the Encyklopedia Gdanska, published by the Fundacja Gdanska, together with the Museum of the Second World War in Gdansk, the Museum of Gdansk and the Museum of Polish History. The spit is about two kilometres long and between 200 and 500 metres wide, and no source found here gives its area, so none is printed. The resort dates from the 1830s, with 1835 and 1839 both in print for the first bath house, which is why the decade is given rather than the year. The League of Nations Council granted the depot in March 1924 and fixed the guard at 88 men, two officers, twenty non-commissioned officers and sixty-six other ranks, and the first detachment landed on 18 January 1926. How many were actually there on 1 September 1939 is not settled: the Encyklopedia Gdanska says about 210, the Museum of Gdansk about 217, and published estimates run from 182 to 240, so this page says about two hundred and nothing more precise. The armament is one 75 mm field gun, two 37 mm Bofors anti-tank guns, four 81 mm mortars and over forty machine guns, and the popular version with two field guns confuses the field gun with the anti-tank pair. The Museum of Gdansk counts six guardhouses built in the 1930s, four of them in reinforced concrete in 1933 and 1934 and the barracks about two years later, and the numbering that runs from one to five is the wartime fighting numbering. Nothing was found in these sources about the thickness of the concrete or about the guardhouses being disguised as villas, so neither claim appears here. Henryk Sucharski was born on 12 November 1898 at Greboszow and took command on 3 December 1938. Franciszek Dabrowski was born in Budapest on 17 April 1904, because his father served in the Austro-Hungarian army, and died in Krakow on 24 April 1962. Mieczyslaw Slaby was a captain and a doctor, not a junior lieutenant, and he reached Westerplatte on 4 August 1939. He was arrested by the communist security police in 1947 and died in a Krakow prison in March 1948, which is not part of this page but is worth knowing. The Schleswig-Holstein was launched in 1906 and rebuilt as a cadet training ship in 1936, and she entered the harbour on 25 August 1939, the pretext being the graves of the crew of the cruiser SMS Magdeburg, lost in 1914. Her assault company is given as about 225 men and its commander as Wilhelm Henningsen, whose exact rank differs between accounts and is therefore left out. The postponement of the attack from 26 August is recorded, but the reasons usually given for it, the Anglo-Polish alliance of 25 August and a message from Benito Mussolini, could not be confirmed from a source opened for this page, so they are not asserted here. The hour of the first salvo follows Jaroslaw Tuliszka, who reconciles the traditional 4.45 with the ship log entry of 4.48 as the planned hour, the order and the firing. Which place was hit first is deliberately left open: the bridgeheads at Tczew were bombed minutes earlier, and on Wielun this page follows Grzegorz Bebnik of the Institute of National Remembrance, who does not argue for a later hour because of German summer time, since the clocks in Germany and Poland agreed, but doubts the traditional 4.40 because the witness accounts were collected in 1961, after a memorial plaque carrying that hour had already been unveiled. The number of assaults beaten off is often given as thirteen, and no source outside Wikipedia was found for that figure, so this page says a dozen or more. The raid of 2 September is about sixty Ju 87 dive-bombers and some 26.5 tonnes of bombs in about forty minutes, and the hour of 18.05 that circulates could not be confirmed, so it is not given. At least six defenders died in Guardhouse No 5 and the popular figure of eight is not supported by what was checked. The two fuel-wagon attempts to burn the wood are dated to 6 September. The command dispute is presented as a dispute because that is what it is: the account of Sucharski breaking down on 2 September and Dabrowski taking over rests on post-war recollections and on a German report by Walter Schug of a white flag at about 19.30, and no Polish document confirms it. Tomasz Sudol of the Institute of National Remembrance also questions the familiar line that the depot was only meant to hold for twelve hours, which he reads as belonging to a plan for a crisis inside the Free City rather than for a war. Melchior Wankowicz published his Westerplatte in 1959, Stanislaw Rozewicz filmed the story in 1967 with Zygmunt Hubner as Sucharski and Arkadiusz Bazak as Dabrowski, Mariusz Borowiak argued the revision in Westerplatte. W obronie prawdy in 2001, and Andrzej Drzycimski, Sucharski\'s biographer, defends the traditional picture and attacked Pawel Chochlew\'s film Tajemnica Westerplatte of 2013 in those terms. The surrender times, a white flag at about 9.45 and the capitulation at about 10.15, differ slightly between accounts and are given as approximate. The sabre is left as contested on purpose: accounts agree it was taken from Sucharski in his first camp, and one has it that the sabre handed to him was Dabrowski\'s. The Polish dead are fifteen, with Kazimierz Rasinski, murdered in captivity on 12 September 1939, counted by some as the sixteenth. The wounded are twenty-six in the Encyklopedia Gdanska and forty or more elsewhere. The German losses of three to four hundred dead come from Polish wartime and post-war accounts and are not supported by German casualty records, which give about fifty dead and a hundred and twenty to a hundred and fifty wounded, the figure Krzysztof Komorowski and Mariusz Wojtowicz-Podhorski both work with. Sucharski died in Naples on 30 August 1946, was buried at Casamassima, exhumed on 21 August 1971 and laid on Westerplatte on 1 September 1971. The archaeology follows the Museum of the Second World War, which has dug on the peninsula since 2016, located the grave in the fifth season in the autumn of 2019 and buried nine men on 4 November 2022, seven of them identified by name. The monument was unveiled on 9 October 1966 to a design by the team of Franciszek Duszenko, with Adam Haupt and Henryk Kitowski responsible for the setting rather than for the obelisk, which is 24.7 metres tall and stands on a mound of 22.8 metres. Guardhouse No 1 was moved in 1967, by a distance none of these sources gives, and has housed a museum since 1980. The song every Polish child knows, Konstanty Ildefons Galczynski\'s Piesn o zolnierzach z Westerplatte, was written about a fortnight after the battle by a poet who did not yet know what had happened, and it left Poland with the impression that the defenders all died, which they did not. What is open on the peninsula is taken from the museums\' own pages in September 2026 and it changes: a building site has been there since February 2026, the exhibition in the old power house is open, buses 106 and 138 run to it all year, and boats from the old town run in summer under operators and timetables that change from season to season, so nothing is promised here about fares. Title picture: the Monument to the Coast Defenders on Westerplatte, photographed by Andrzej Otrebski, CC BY-SA 4.0.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],
    'pact' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The Molotov-Ribbentrop pact, 23 August 1939',
            'description' => 'The German-Soviet treaty signed in the Kremlin in August 1939 and the secret protocol that drew a line through Poland a week before the first shot.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · The pact',
            'title' => 'The pact',
            'title_accent' => 'that divided the country',
            'lede' => 'Two governments that had spent a decade calling each other the main enemy signed a treaty of non-aggression in Moscow at two in the morning. The part they published was dull. The part they hid was one typed page that handed half of Poland, the three Baltic states and a slice of Romania to the Soviet Union, and the other half of Poland to Nazi Germany. Nine frames on what the page said, what it did, and why it is still argued about. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'talks',
                'photo' => 'talks',
                'label' => 'The summer before',
                'year' => 'August 1939',
                'title' => 'Two offers on the table in Moscow',
                'body' => 'Britain and France had promised on 31 March 1939 to stand by Poland if Nazi Germany attacked. At Munich six months earlier they had handed Germany a piece of Czechoslovakia without asking Moscow. On 12 August a British mission under Admiral Reginald Drax and a French one under General Joseph Doumenc sat down with Marshal Kliment Voroshilov, the Soviet defence commissar. Two days later he asked the question the talks died on. Could the Red Army cross Poland and Romania to reach the Germans. Warsaw said no on 20 August, and Jozef Beck, the Polish foreign minister, called it a fourth partition, after the three that had erased his country from the map between 1772 and 1795.',
                'caption' => 'Marshal Kliment Voroshilov, who led the Soviet side, in a later TASS portrait',
                'credit' => 'Photo: G. Weil for TASS, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'signing',
                'photo' => 'signing',
                'label' => 'The night itself',
                'year' => '23 - 24 August 1939',
                'title' => 'Signed at two in the morning',
                'body' => 'Joseph Stalin, the Soviet leader, took the other offer. Joachim von Ribbentrop, the German foreign minister, flew into Moscow on 23 August, and the treaty of non-aggression was signed in the Kremlin at two the following morning by him and by Vyacheslav Molotov, the Soviet foreign minister, with Stalin standing behind the table. The German record of the evening has Stalin raising his glass and saying that he knew how much the German nation loved Adolf Hitler, the German leader, and would therefore drink his health. No Soviet record of that toast has ever been published.',
                'caption' => 'Adolf Hitler at the Berghof, his house in Bavaria, on 23 August 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Eva Braun album, US National Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'protocol',
                'photo' => 'protocol',
                'label' => 'The secret page',
                'year' => '23 August 1939',
                'title' => 'One page that divided five countries',
                'body' => 'Attached to the treaty was a single typed page nobody was meant to see. It ran to four numbered points. Finland, Estonia and Latvia fell to the Soviet sphere of interest and Lithuania to the German one, the northern border of Lithuania being the line between them. In Poland the two spheres were to be separated roughly along the rivers Narew, Vistula and San, and whether a Polish state should go on existing at all was left for later. The Soviet side declared an interest in Bessarabia, then a province of Romania, and Germany declared complete political disinterest there. The fourth point said both governments would keep the page strictly secret.',
                'caption' => 'The German original of the secret protocol, signed by Ribbentrop and Molotov',
                'credit' => 'Photo: ThoralfSchade, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'poland',
                'photo' => 'poland',
                'label' => 'The line moves',
                'year' => 'September 1939',
                'title' => 'The line was redrawn a month later',
                'body' => 'Nazi Germany invaded Poland on 1 September 1939 and the Red Army crossed the eastern border on 17 September. On 28 September the two ministers signed in Moscow again, this time the German-Soviet Boundary and Friendship Treaty. A fresh secret protocol amended the August bargain. Lithuania passed to the Soviet sphere, and in exchange the province of Lublin and parts of the province of Warsaw passed to Germany. The border now ran on the Bug instead of the Vistula, leaving Warsaw on the German side. The Katyn Museum in Warsaw, which records the Soviet killing of Polish prisoners in 1940, reckons the August line would have left Germany 38 per cent of Poland and the Soviet Union 62.',
                'caption' => 'Molotov signs the treaty of 28 September 1939, Stalin and Ribbentrop behind him',
                'credit' => 'Photo: photographer unknown, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'cost',
                'photo' => 'cost',
                'label' => 'The rest of the map',
                'year' => '1939 - 1940',
                'title' => 'Finland, the Baltic states, Bessarabia',
                'body' => 'Poland was the first item on the page and not the only one. On 30 November 1939 the Soviet Union attacked Finland, which the protocol had put in its sphere. The Winter War ran until March 1940 and cost Finland the Karelian Isthmus, the land bridge north of Leningrad, the city now called Saint Petersburg. In June 1940 Soviet troops occupied Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania, and within weeks all three had been annexed as Soviet republics. That same summer Romania was made to hand over Bessarabia, which the protocol had named, along with northern Bukovina, which it had not.',
                'caption' => 'Finnish civilians being evacuated at Tyrja-Parikkala, 15 March 1940',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Finnish Wartime Photograph Archive, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'trade',
                'photo' => 'trade',
                'label' => 'Grain and oil',
                'year' => '1939 - 1941',
                'title' => 'They traded until the last night',
                'body' => 'The pact had a commercial side, settled four days earlier. On 19 August 1939 Germany granted the Soviet Union a credit of 200 million Reichsmarks, the German currency of the day, to spend on German machinery, against 180 million Reichsmarks of Soviet raw materials over two years. The German foreign office memorandum on the deal lists feed grain, petroleum, cotton, timber, phosphate, platinum and raw furs, and calls it the first decisive step in reshaping political relations. Larger agreements followed in 1940 and 1941. Edward Ericson, who wrote the standard study of this trade, reads the deliveries as a real prop under the German war economy. The trains ran until 22 June 1941.',
                'caption' => 'A Lot Of Fellows Were On That Limb, an American cartoon of 26 August 1939',
                'credit' => 'Drawing: Clifford Berryman, US Senate Collection, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'denial',
                'photo' => 'denial',
                'label' => 'Fifty years of no',
                'year' => '1946 - 1993',
                'title' => 'Moscow denied the protocol for fifty years',
                'body' => 'The secret page became public on 25 March 1946 at the Nuremberg trial of the German leadership, and in January 1948 the United States State Department printed the German copies as Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941. Moscow answered that year with a pamphlet, Falsifiers of History, which the historians Jan Szumski and Bogdan Musial showed in 2022 that Stalin himself had edited and largely written by hand. The denial held until 24 December 1989, when the Congress of People\'s Deputies, the new Soviet parliament, admitted that no original had been found and declared the protocol invalid from the day it was signed. The originals turned up in 1992 in the Communist Party archive in Moscow.',
                'caption' => 'The journal that printed the Soviet originals, first issue of 1993, page 83',
                'credit' => 'Scan: Novaya i Noveyshaya Istoriya, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'memory',
                'photo' => 'memory',
                'label' => 'The chain of 1989',
                'year' => '1989 - today',
                'title' => 'Two million people on one road',
                'body' => 'On 23 August 1989, fifty years to the day, about two million people in Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania held hands in a chain some 600 kilometres long running from Tallinn through Riga to Vilnius. What they were demanding was that Moscow admit the protocol, and four months later it did. The European Parliament proposed on 23 September 2008 that 23 August be kept as a day of remembrance for the victims of Stalinism and Nazism, and it has been marked across the European Union since 2009. In Russia the argument has changed since. Vladimir Putin, then prime minister, called the pact immoral on 31 August 2009, and as president defended it in Saint Petersburg on 20 December 2019.',
                'caption' => 'A banner of Hitler and Stalin at the Riga demonstration of 23 August 1989',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Europeana 1989, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'visiting',
                'photo' => 'visiting',
                'label' => 'Going to see it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'Where to read the documents in Poland',
                'body' => 'The Katyn Museum sits in a casemate of the Warsaw Citadel, the only museum anywhere given over entirely to the Soviet murder of about 22,000 Polish prisoners in the spring of 1940, which is where the protocol led. It opens Tuesday to Sunday. The Museum of the Second World War in Gdansk starts its main exhibition in the Europe of the 1930s and reaches the pact within the first rooms. The Institute of National Remembrance publishes the documents in Polish, and the Avalon Project at Yale Law School has the English texts of the treaty and of both secret protocols, free to read.',
                'caption' => 'The Katyn Museum in the Warsaw Citadel, a prisoner\'s last letter cast on the wall',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Qkiel, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A printed map in Russian of Poland and its neighbours, with East Prussia and Germany on the left, the Soviet Union on the right, Romania at the bottom, and a heavy toothed line running from the East Prussian border down past Warsaw and Lublin to the Carpathians, with a scale bar of 200 kilometres at the bottom left and a legend box beside it',
            'caption' => 'The line as the Soviet public first saw it: a map printed in Russian by the Moscow daily Izvestia on 18 September 1939. The legend calls it the demarcation line between the German and Soviet armies established by the German Government and the Government of the USSR. It still runs on the Vistula and the San, because the 28 September treaty had not been signed yet.',
            'credit' => 'Map: Izvestia, Moscow, 18 September 1939, public domain',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The texts of the treaty and of every protocol attached to it are quoted here from the Avalon Project at Yale Law School, which publishes the German foreign office documents released by the United States State Department in 1948 as Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941, edited by Raymond James Sontag and James Stuart Beddie. The secret additional protocol of 23 August 1939 has four points, and the wording used on this page follows that edition: the northern border of Lithuania as the dividing line in the Baltic, the rivers Narew, Vistula and San in Poland, the question of a Polish state left to the further course of political developments, the Soviet interest in Bessarabia against a German declaration of complete political disinterest, and the final point that both sides would treat the document as strictly secret. The secret supplementary protocol of 28 September 1939 is quoted in the same edition and says plainly that Lithuania falls to the Soviet sphere while the province of Lublin and parts of the province of Warsaw fall to Germany. The Moscow military talks follow the Institute of National Remembrance, where Sebastian Pilarski sets out the Soviet request of 14 August 1939 for a right of passage through the Vilna region and eastern Lesser Poland, the four Polish objections and the Polish answer of 20 August, and records Jozef Beck describing such an agreement as a fourth partition. The heads of the three delegations, Admiral Reginald Drax for Britain, General Joseph Doumenc for France and Marshal Kliment Voroshilov for the Soviet Union, and the date of 12 August for the first session, are taken from a second and popular account rather than a scholarly one, and are given without a claim about who was at fault, because that is argued about and this page does not settle it. The hour of two in the morning on the night of 23 to 24 August comes from the chronology of the Katyn Museum in Warsaw, which is also the source for the division of Polish territory into 38 per cent for the Reich and 62 per cent for the Soviet Union, and for the protocol becoming public at the Nuremberg trial on 25 March 1946. The toast is quoted only as the German record has it, because the memorandum of the conversation held on the night of 23 to 24 August is a German document and no Soviet minute of the evening has been published. The trade agreement is the German foreign office memorandum on the agreement concluded on 19 August 1939: a merchandise credit of 200 million Reichsmarks and 180 million Reichsmarks of Soviet raw materials over two years, with lumber, cotton, feed grain, oil cake, phosphate, platinum, raw furs and petroleum named in the text, and with the memorandum itself calling the credit agreement the first decisive step in the reshaping of political relations. No tonnage of grain or oil actually delivered is printed here, because no source outside Wikipedia was opened for one. The judgement that the deliveries propped up the German war economy is attributed to Edward E. Ericson, whose Feeding the German Eagle of 1999 is the standard study. The occupation of Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania in 1940, the taking of Bessarabia and northern Bukovina from Romania and the attack on Finland follow the Holocaust Encyclopedia of the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum. The Winter War is dated from 30 November 1939 to March 1940 and no casualty figure is given for it, because it is not this page\'s subject. The pamphlet Falsifiers of History of 1948 and Stalin\'s hand in it follow Jan Szumski and Bogdan Musial in the Institute of National Remembrance Review number 4 of 2022, pages 243 to 287. The resolution of the Congress of People\'s Deputies of 24 December 1989 is quoted from its own text: it accepts the conclusions of the commission, states in point three that the original of the protocol has not been found in Soviet or foreign archives while copies and other documents prove that it was signed and existed, and in point seven condemns the protocol as legally unfounded and invalid from the moment of signing. That the Soviet originals were found in 1992 in the Communist Party files was announced by an aide to President Boris Yeltsin and reported on 30 October 1992. They were printed in the Moscow journal Novaya i Noveyshaya Istoriya in its first issue of 1993, whose editorial note names the sealed packet number 34 in the Central Committee archive on Staraya Ploshchad and the instruction on the folder that it was not to be opened without the permission of the head of the general department. The 1993 date is why this page says the originals surfaced in 1992 rather than that they were published then. The Baltic Way of 23 August 1989 is about two million people and a chain of about 600 kilometres from Tallinn through Riga to Vilnius, following the nomination of the three Baltic states to the UNESCO Memory of the World register and the account published by the Latvian government, which gives the hour as seven in the evening and the length as 660 to 670 kilometres. Published figures for the length run from 595 to 675 kilometres, so a round number is used. The European Parliament adopted the written declaration on the proclamation of 23 August as a European Day of Remembrance for Victims of Stalinism and Nazism on 23 September 2008, and the Union has marked the day since 2009. The two remarks by Vladimir Putin are dated and quoted narrowly: the letter to Polish readers published in Gazeta Wyborcza on 31 August 2009, in which he called the pact immoral, and his statement at a meeting of former Soviet states in Saint Petersburg on 20 December 2019, in which he presented it as forced on the Soviet Union by the agreements others had already signed with Germany. This page takes no position on that argument and prints no judgement about present-day politics. The September campaign itself, the Free City of Danzig and Westerplatte have their own articles here and are not retold. Katyn has its own article too, and the figure of about 22,000 prisoners is the one the Katyn Museum uses. Opening days for the museums were read from their own pages in September 2026 and they change, so check before travelling. Title picture: the page of the German Reich law gazette for 1939 that printed the treaty of non-aggression in German and Russian side by side, with the announcement of 25 September 1939 recording that the instruments of ratification were exchanged in Berlin on 24 September, public domain.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],
    'exile' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The Polish government in exile, 1939-1990',
            'description' => 'Poland was occupied from 1939, so who was the government? The state that reassembled in Paris, moved to London and gave the insignia back in 1990.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · The government in exile',
            'title' => 'The government',
            'title_accent' => 'that never surrendered',
            'lede' => 'Poland was beaten in five weeks in 1939 and occupied for six years, and in all that time it never signed a surrender and never had a government that worked for the occupiers. The state simply moved. It spent eight months in France, then forty five years in London, with a president, a cabinet, an army and a budget. Nine frames on how that was done, what it bought, and where it ended.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'romania',
                'photo' => 'romania',
                'label' => 'The state leaves',
                'year' => 'September 1939',
                'title' => 'Poland got a new president, in Paris',
                'body' => 'Nazi Germany invaded Poland on 1 September 1939 and the Soviet Union came in from the east on 17 September 1939. That night the president, Ignacy Moscicki, and his ministers crossed into Romania expecting to be waved through to France. They were interned instead. The constitution of 1935 let a president at war name his own successor, and Moscicki used that article while he still could. Wladyslaw Raczkiewicz, a former interior minister who was already in Paris, was sworn in there on 30 September 1939, and the same day he made General Wladyslaw Sikorski, a soldier the pre-war rulers had pushed aside, prime minister. By 22 November the government had a seat at Angers on the Loire.',
                'caption' => 'The chateau of Pignerolle near Angers, the Polish seat in 1939 and 1940',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Simon Bonaventure, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 2.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'london',
                'photo' => 'london',
                'label' => 'France falls',
                'year' => '1939 - 1940',
                'title' => 'The state moved a second time, to London',
                'body' => 'In France it built two things. One was an army of over eighty thousand men, drawn from Polish emigrants and from soldiers who had walked out through Hungary and Romania. The other was a National Council, an appointed stand-in for the parliament it had no way of electing, chaired by the pianist Ignacy Jan Paderewski, who had been prime minister of Poland in 1919. Nazi Germany attacked France on 10 May 1940 and both were lost inside six weeks. About twenty five thousand soldiers got across to Britain and the government followed them. A plaque on the Rubens Hotel at 43 Buckingham Palace Road in London marks where Sikorski then worked.',
                'caption' => 'A joint session of the government and the National Council in London',
                'credit' => 'Photo: unknown photographer, Narodowe Archiwum Cyfrowe, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'work',
                'photo' => 'work',
                'label' => 'What it did',
                'year' => '1940 - 1944',
                'title' => 'It ran a country it could not set foot in',
                'body' => 'London was not a waiting room. The government kept Poland a recognised ally with an army, a navy and an air force of its own, and it paid for and gave orders to the Polish Underground State, a whole administration at home that judged, taught and printed in secret. Between 1941 and 1944 it parachuted 316 specially trained officers back into occupied Poland, the cichociemni, the silent and unseen. Its couriers carried intelligence the other way. On the night of 25 July 1944 a Dakota landed on a field near Wal-Ruda in southern Poland and flew out parts of a German V-2 rocket.',
                'caption' => 'Officers working a relief map at the staff of the commander in chief in London',
                'credit' => 'Photo: unknown photographer, Narodowe Archiwum Cyfrowe, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'majski',
                'photo' => 'majski',
                'label' => 'Moscow, briefly',
                'year' => '1941 - 1942',
                'title' => 'For a year Stalin was on the same side',
                'body' => 'Nazi Germany attacked the Soviet Union on 22 June 1941, and Moscow suddenly needed the country it had invaded two years earlier. On 30 July 1941 Sikorski and the Soviet ambassador in London, Ivan Maisky, signed an agreement that restored relations, voided the treaties Moscow had made with Germany about Poland, and allowed a Polish army to be raised on Soviet soil. With it came an amnesty for the Poles deported into the Soviet interior. They resented the word, having been convicted of nothing. General Wladyslaw Anders took command of it on 10 August 1941.',
                'caption' => 'Soldiers of the Polish army raised in the Soviet Union, photographed in Iran in 1942',
                'credit' => 'Photo: unknown photographer, Narodowe Archiwum Cyfrowe, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'katyn',
                'photo' => 'katyn',
                'label' => 'The graves',
                'year' => 'April 1943',
                'title' => 'A forest full of graves ended the alliance',
                'body' => 'That army did not stay. By November 1942 more than 115,000 people had left the Soviet Union for Iran with it, about 78,500 of them soldiers, and the officers the Soviet Union had taken prisoner in 1939 were still missing. On 13 April 1943 Berlin radio announced that their graves had been found at Katyn near Smolensk, in ground the German army then held. The London government asked the International Committee of the Red Cross to go and look, and on the night of 25 April 1943 Moscow called that collusion with Germany and broke off relations. The Soviet security police had shot about 22,000 prisoners in 1940, and Moscow denied it for decades.',
                'caption' => 'Steel plates over the mass graves at the Polish war cemetery at Katyn',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Zala, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'gibraltar',
                'photo' => 'gibraltar',
                'label' => 'Gibraltar 1943',
                'year' => '4 July 1943',
                'title' => 'Sikorski was killed seconds after take-off',
                'body' => 'Sikorski was flying home from inspecting his troops in the Middle East when his Liberator bomber went into the sea off Gibraltar on 4 July 1943, seconds after leaving the runway. His chief of staff, General Tadeusz Klimecki, and his daughter Zofia Lesniowska died with him. A British court of inquiry found that the aircraft controls had jammed for reasons it could not establish, and ruled out sabotage. Poles have argued about it ever since. The Polish Institute of National Remembrance exhumed him in 2008, found injuries of the kind a crash makes and no sign of shooting or strangling, and closed its investigation on 30 December 2013.',
                'caption' => 'General Wladyslaw Sikorski at Gibraltar, hours before the crash of 4 July 1943',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Polish war photo service, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'mikolajczyk',
                'photo' => 'mikolajczyk',
                'label' => 'Decided elsewhere',
                'year' => '1943 - 1945',
                'title' => 'The border was fixed in rooms with no Pole in them',
                'body' => 'Sikorski was followed by his deputy, Stanislaw Mikolajczyk, who led the peasant party. At Tehran in November 1943 and at Yalta in February 1945 the British, American and Soviet leaders, Winston Churchill, Franklin Roosevelt and Joseph Stalin, agreed where post-war Poland would lie, and moved it bodily west. No Polish representative sat in either room, and what that did to the map has its own article here. Mikolajczyk resigned on 24 November 1944 rather than sign away the eastern half of the country. Tomasz Arciszewski, the socialist flown out of occupied Poland on that Dakota, took over instead.',
                'caption' => 'Prime Minister Stanislaw Mikolajczyk laying a wreath in the United States in 1944',
                'credit' => 'Photo: unknown photographer, Narodowe Archiwum Cyfrowe, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'end',
                'photo' => 'end',
                'label' => 'Recognition ends',
                'year' => '1945 - 1990',
                'title' => 'Abandoned in 1945, it carried on to 1990',
                'body' => 'A Provisional Government of National Unity, settled in talks in Moscow, was formed in Warsaw on 28 June 1945, and on 5 July Britain and the United States recognised it and stopped recognising the government in London the same day. Mikolajczyk went home and joined the Warsaw cabinet as a deputy prime minister, then left Poland again in 1947. The men who stayed in London kept the offices open and went on appointing presidents, with no state and almost no money, for forty five years. On 22 December 1990 the last of them, Ryszard Kaczorowski, handed the presidential insignia to Lech Walesa.',
                'caption' => 'The travel document Ryszard Kaczorowski carried in London, issued in 1974',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Cezary Piwowarski, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'visiting',
                'photo' => 'visiting',
                'label' => 'Going to see it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'Four places where the London state survives',
                'body' => 'The Polish Institute and Sikorski Museum stands at 20 Princes Gate in London, a terraced house with a Polish eagle over the door, and it keeps the archive of the whole exile period along with Sikorski\'s own things. Sikorski himself lies in the crypt of Saint Leonard under Wawel Cathedral in Krakow, brought back from Newark in England in 1993. The Katyn Museum in the Warsaw Citadel is given over to the killings that broke the alliance. The insignia Kaczorowski handed back are in the Royal Castle in Warsaw, in rooms opened to visitors in 2021.',
                'caption' => 'The door of the Polish Institute and Sikorski Museum at 20 Princes Gate, London',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Kerim44, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0',
            ],
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The crossing into Romania on the night of 17 to 18 September 1939, the internment, the use of article 24 of the constitution of 23 April 1935 to name a successor and the swearing in of Wladyslaw Raczkiewicz in Paris on 30 September 1939 follow the Institute of National Remembrance, whose portal Przystanek Historia carries both a study of the April constitution and the continuity of the Polish state abroad and a life of Raczkiewicz, and the state history portal dzieje.pl, run by the Polish Press Agency and the Ministry of Culture, whose entry on Raczkiewicz adds the Paris agreement he signed with Sikorski the same day. Sikorski was made prime minister on 30 September 1939 and commander in chief on 7 November 1939, which is dzieje.pl. That the seat moved to Angers on 22 November 1939, that the National Council was created by presidential decree on 9 December 1939, that the Polish army in France passed eighty thousand men and that about twenty five thousand soldiers reached Britain in 1940 all come from the Institute of National Remembrance. Ignacy Jan Paderewski was elected to chair the National Council at its opening session on 23 January 1940, so he chaired it from 1940 rather than from the decree, and this page says only that he chaired it. The Rubens Hotel at 43 Buckingham Palace Road is identified by the plaque on the building itself, which names Sikorski as prime minister and commander in chief and calls it his headquarters. The figure of 316 cichociemni parachuted into occupied Poland is from the Institute of National Remembrance magazine Pamiec.pl, number 1 of 2014. Operation Most III, on the night of 25 to 26 July 1944, the landing strip called Motyl near Wal-Ruda in the Tarnow district, the Dakota that sank its wheels in wet ground, the parts of the German V-2 rocket flown out and Tomasz Arciszewski flying out on the same aircraft are from the Krakow branch of the Institute of National Remembrance. No percentage is printed here for the share of Allied intelligence that came from Polish sources, because the figure that circulates traces back to a single report and no source outside Wikipedia was opened for it. The Sikorski-Maisky agreement of 30 July 1941, the amnesty that followed, Wladyslaw Anders taking command on 10 August 1941 and the evacuation figures of more than 115,000 people and about 78,500 soldiers leaving the Soviet Union by November 1942 are given by dzieje.pl and by the Institute of National Remembrance, which publish the same numbers. The announcement by Berlin radio on 13 April 1943 is dated from the Katyn service of the Institute of National Remembrance, which also records a first German agency report two days earlier. The Polish request to the International Committee of the Red Cross on 16 and 17 April 1943, the refusal of the committee for want of Soviet consent and the Soviet note breaking relations in the night of 25 to 26 April 1943 follow the Institute of National Remembrance and the Museum of Polish History. The figure of about 22,000 prisoners shot by the Soviet security police in the spring of 1940 is the one the Katyn Museum in Warsaw uses. On the death of Sikorski this page prints what the investigations established and nothing else. Two communiques of the Institute of National Remembrance are the source: the one of 8 March 2013, which lists the dead, states that the exhumed victims had multi-organ injuries consistent with the crash and that for Sikorski strangling, hanging, gunshot wounds and stab wounds were categorically excluded, and the one of 30 December 2013, which closed the investigation, found no evidence of a crime and recorded that the British inquiry of 1943 had put the crash down to the aircraft controls jamming for reasons it could not determine while excluding sabotage. The theories that circulate about the crash are not repeated here, because the evidence does not support them and a reader has no way to weigh a rebuttal. The Tehran and Yalta decisions and what they did to the Polish map have their own article on this site and are not retold. Stanislaw Mikolajczyk resigned on 24 November 1944, returned to Warsaw as deputy prime minister and minister of agriculture in the Provisional Government of National Unity, and left Poland from October 1947, which is the Institute of National Remembrance. The Provisional Government of National Unity was formed on 28 June 1945 and Britain and the United States recognised it on 5 July 1945, withdrawing recognition from the London government that day, which the Institute of National Remembrance and a study by Tadeusz Kmiecik published in the journal archive of the Museum of Polish History both state. France had already withdrawn recognition a few days earlier, which is why the transfer is sometimes dated 5 and sometimes 6 July, and only the British and American date is printed here. That Ryszard Kaczorowski handed the presidential insignia to Lech Walesa at the Royal Castle in Warsaw on 22 December 1990, the day Walesa swore his oath before the National Assembly, is recorded by the office of the President of Poland and by dzieje.pl, and an official notice of the transfer of the insignia from London to the Royal Castle was published in the Polish journal of laws for 1990. Sikorski\'s body was brought from Newark in England to Krakow in 1993, which is dzieje.pl, and it lies in the crypt of Saint Leonard under Wawel Cathedral. The permanent display on the presidents of the Second Republic and the authorities in exile opened at the Royal Castle in Warsaw on 22 December 2021. The Underground State, Witold Pilecki, Jan Karski, the breaking of the Enigma cipher, the Polish forces abroad and the Warsaw Uprising of 1944 are named here only where the government touches them, because each is getting its own article. Two things a Polish reader will look for are deliberately left out for want of room and are flagged here instead: the agreement Raczkiewicz and Sikorski signed in Paris on 30 September 1939, by which the president gave away part of his own powers, and the trial in Moscow in June 1945 of sixteen leaders of the Underground State who had been invited to talks and arrested. No map is published on this page. This is a story about an institution rather than a territory, the two things here that are spatial belong to other pages, and the articles on the September campaign and on the German-Soviet pact already carry the real published maps of these years. No opening hours are printed on this page, because they change and none were checked here, so look them up on the museums own pages before travelling. Title picture: a meeting of the government of General Wladyslaw Sikorski and the National Council in London, photographer unknown, Narodowe Archiwum Cyfrowe, public domain.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],
    'gg' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The General Government, 1939-1945',
            'description' => 'The part of occupied Poland that Nazi Germany did not annex in 1939, governed from Wawel Castle in Krakow by a Nazi Party lawyer, Hans Frank.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · The General Government',
            'title' => 'A country',
            'title_accent' => 'run as a colony',
            'lede' => 'When the fighting stopped in October 1939, most of what had been Poland disappeared into Nazi Germany and into the Soviet Union. One piece was left over, given a name of its own and no country attached to it, and run from the castle of the Polish kings by a lawyer from the Nazi Party. Nine frames on how the map was cut, what the place was for, how it was governed, what people ate, what was carried away, and where it can still be read on the ground.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'carved',
                'photo' => 'carved',
                'label' => 'Cut into pieces',
                'year' => '1939 - 1941',
                'title' => 'Four annexations and one leftover',
                'body' => 'Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union had agreed in August 1939 to divide Poland between them, and in the autumn they did it. Germany took the western provinces into the Reich outright: the Poznan region became the Reichsgau Wartheland, Pomerania and the former Free City of Danzig became Danzig-West Prussia, the Upper Silesian industrial belt went to the Silesian province and the land around Ciechanow north of Warsaw was tacked onto East Prussia. The Soviet Union held everything east of the river Bug until June 1941. What was left in the middle got a name of its own by a decree Adolf Hitler signed on 12 October 1939, in force from 26 October: the General Government.',
                'caption' => 'The proclamation posted in October 1939, German above and Polish below',
                'credit' => 'Poster of the General Government, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'colony',
                'photo' => 'colony',
                'label' => 'Meant as a colony',
                'year' => '1939 - 1940',
                'title' => 'A reservoir of labour, and not a state',
                'body' => 'Hitler put a Nazi Party lawyer, Hans Frank, in charge as Governor General, and Frank took Wawel Castle in Krakow, the old seat of the Polish kings, as his residence. He wrote in his diary on 3 October 1939 that Poland would be treated like a colony and Poles would become the slaves of the greater German world empire, and the judges at his trial in Nuremberg read that sentence back to him. On 31 July 1940, on a secret order from Hitler, he struck the words for the occupied Polish territories out of the official name, so that the law of occupation would have nothing to attach to. Around 860,000 Poles and Jews were expelled from the annexed provinces into his territory.',
                'caption' => 'Frank\'s ordinance of 31 October 1939, German left, Polish right',
                'credit' => 'Printed ordinance of the General Government, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'rule',
                'photo' => 'rule',
                'label' => 'Rule by notice',
                'year' => '1939 - 1944',
                'title' => 'Germans on top, Poles left at the desks',
                'body' => 'The first German proclamation, posted by the army as it advanced, ordered every Polish official to stay at his post, and that remained the pattern. German officials sat on top of Polish clerks, Polish town halls and the pre-war Polish police, which people called the granatowa policja, the navy blue police, after the colour of its uniform. Justice was German. The first ordinance Frank signed, on 31 October 1939, made death the penalty for violence against a German, for damage to German property and for urging anyone to disobey. On 2 October 1943 a further ordinance handed almost everything to summary courts, and the executions came out into the street.',
                'caption' => 'The first German proclamation in occupied Poland, in German and Polish',
                'credit' => 'Poster of the German army command, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'shortage',
                'photo' => 'shortage',
                'label' => 'Card and market',
                'year' => '1939 - 1944',
                'title' => 'The ration card fed nobody on its own',
                'body' => 'Ration cards came in on 2 December 1939, and what a Pole was allowed on his card was a fraction of what a German in the same street drew. People lived off the black market instead. Food came in on the trains from the villages, carried by smugglers with their own specialities, meat from Karczew and spirits from Jablonna, and a conductor who looked out of the window. The other thing that came off the street was people. A lapanka was a round-up: a lorry at the kerb, a cordon at both ends, and whoever happened to be walking past went to work in Germany. Young men were called up to the Baudienst, the building service, to dig for the administration.',
                'caption' => 'A German round-up in Aleja 3 Maja in Warsaw, photographed in secret',
                'credit' => 'Photograph from the collection of Muzeum Warszawy, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'culture',
                'photo' => 'culture',
                'label' => 'Schools and loot',
                'year' => '1939 - 1944',
                'title' => 'Closed schools, and a church stripped',
                'body' => 'Secondary schools and universities were shut. What was left, the seven year elementary school and the trade school, was forbidden to teach Polish history, Polish geography and Polish literature. Teachers answered in October 1939 with the Tajna Organizacja Nauczycielska, a secret organisation of teachers, under Zygmunt Nowicki, and taught in private flats in groups of five or six. About eight and a half thousand Polish teachers were killed during the war. Meanwhile an SS officer working for the Reich marshal Hermann Goering, Kajetan Muehlmann, catalogued the collections and took what he liked, among it the carved altarpiece Veit Stoss made for St Mary\'s church in Krakow.',
                'caption' => 'The Veit Stoss altarpiece, back in St Mary\'s church in Krakow',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Gerd Eichmann, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'krakow',
                'photo' => 'krakow',
                'label' => 'The capital',
                'year' => '1939 - 1943',
                'title' => 'Krakow was made a capital it did not want',
                'body' => 'Warsaw was bigger and would have been the obvious seat, and Krakow got it instead. The Wawel, the hill above the river that carries the royal castle and the cathedral where Polish kings are buried, was Hans Frank\'s residence and office for more than five years. Across the Vistula, in the working district of Podgorze, the governor of the Krakow district, Otto von Waechter, ordered the Jews of the city into a closed quarter on 3 March 1941. It was about twenty hectares and three hundred and twenty buildings, where some three thousand people had lived before the war, and they had until 20 March to move in.',
                'caption' => 'Inside the Under the Eagle pharmacy on Plac Bohaterow Getta in Podgorze',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'districts',
                'photo' => 'districts',
                'label' => 'Four then five',
                'year' => '1939 - 1944',
                'title' => 'Warsaw, Radom, Lublin, and then Galicia',
                'body' => 'Under Frank sat district governors, one for Krakow and one each for Warsaw, Radom and Lublin, and each ran his district like a small state of his own, down to the price of a passport photograph. Warsaw was the largest city in the territory and never its capital, and the segregation there was visible by the hour. From the spring of 1942 one tram line ran for Germans only, and on ordinary cars a movable wooden beam fenced off the German platform, which the conductor had to slide back when the German end got crowded. In August 1941, after Germany attacked the Soviet Union, eastern Galicia around Lwow, today Lviv in Ukraine, was added as a fifth district.',
                'caption' => 'A police ordinance of January 1942 from the Lublin district, fixing prices',
                'credit' => 'Printed ordinance of the Lublin district, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'end',
                'photo' => 'end',
                'label' => 'Flight and trial',
                'year' => '1945 - 1946',
                'title' => 'The Governor General left with the paintings',
                'body' => 'The Red Army opened its winter offensive on the Vistula on 12 January 1945 and was in Krakow within the week. Frank drove west with looted pictures in the luggage, and American soldiers arrested him in Bavaria that May. He handed over the diary he had dictated all through, thirty eight volumes of it by the count of the Institute of National Remembrance, and it became the evidence against him. At Nuremberg he said that a thousand years would pass and the guilt of Germany would not be erased. The court convicted him of war crimes and crimes against humanity and he was hanged on 16 October 1946.',
                'caption' => 'The defendants in the dock at the Nuremberg trial, 1945 or 1946',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Ray D\'Addario, United States National Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'Where to look',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'Four addresses where this is still legible',
                'body' => 'The Wawel is a museum and a cathedral again, and the castle Frank moved into is open to walk around. The Under the Eagle pharmacy at 18 Plac Bohaterow Getta, kept through the ghetto years by a Polish pharmacist, Tadeusz Pankiewicz, is a branch of the Museum of Krakow. So is the old enamel works at 4 Lipowa Street. People queue for it as the factory of the German owner Oskar Schindler, and the permanent exhibition inside is about occupied Krakow and the people who lived in it rather than about him. North of Warsaw, in the Kampinos forest, the cemetery laid out at Palmiry in 1948 holds the people the Germans shot there.',
                'caption' => 'The old enamel works on Lipowa Street in Krakow, a museum today',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A printed atlas plate headed GERMANY in Russian, the Reich and its neighbours washed in colour, with Warsaw, Lodz, Krakow and Lublin lying inside a pale block lettered as the area of German state interests, and the Soviet Union in solid red along the right-hand edge',
            'caption' => 'Germany in the Soviet Pocket Atlas of the World of 1940, at a scale of 1:7,500,000, in Russian. The land Nazi Germany took from Poland is lettered only as its area of state interests, and the Soviet share is the red block on the right.',
            'credit' => 'Main Directorate of Geodesy and Cartography of the USSR, Pocket Atlas of the World, Moscow 1940, public domain',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'That the General Government was created by a decree Adolf Hitler signed on 12 October 1939 and in force from 26 October 1939, that it had four districts and that Galicia came in as a fifth in 1941, follows the Institute of National Remembrance, whose portal Przystanek Historia carries a study of whether the territory was occupied land in law, and the proclamation Hans Frank had posted in October 1939, which prints both dates in German and in Polish and is reproduced on this page. The exact day in August 1941 on which the Galicia district was formed is given differently in different places, so only the month is printed here. The German names of the annexed units, the Reichsgau Wartheland and Danzig-West Prussia, are the ones the German administration used, and the institute lists the same lands as Wielkopolska, Pomerania, Silesia, the Lodz region, northern Mazovia and the Suwalki region. That Frank struck the words for the occupied Polish territories out of the name on 31 July 1940, on a secret decree of Hitler of 8 July 1940, and that German jurists then argued the Hague Convention no longer applied, is from the same study. The diary entry of 3 October 1939 about Poland as a colony and Poles as slaves, the conviction on the counts of war crimes and crimes against humanity and the sentence carried out on 16 October 1946 are taken from the judgment of the International Military Tribunal at Nuremberg, published by the Avalon Project of the Yale Law School. That Frank kept the diary in thirty eight volumes, that he lived on the Wawel and that he looted art for his own houses is the Institute of National Remembrance. The figure of about 860,000 Poles and Jews expelled from the annexed provinces into the General Government, the closing of secondary schools and universities, the ban on teaching Polish history, geography and literature, and the looting of the Veit Stoss altarpiece and of the Lady with an Ermine from the Krakow collections are from the institute study of German occupation policy on Polish lands. No calorie table is printed on this page. The figures that circulate for the German, Polish and Jewish ration in December 1941 could not be confirmed in an institutional source in this session, and the institute says only that the legal ration ran well below what a person needs, so the page says that instead. The ration card was introduced on 2 December 1939 and the smuggling routes into Warsaw, meat from Karczew and spirits from Jablonna, are from the institute article on the black market in occupied Warsaw. The tram line reserved for Germans from the spring of 1942 and the movable wooden beam on ordinary cars are from its article on city transport in occupied Warsaw. The Tajna Organizacja Nauczycielska, founded in October 1939 under Zygmunt Nowicki, the teaching in groups of five or six in private flats and the figure of about eight and a half thousand teachers killed are from its article on clandestine teaching. No count of underground newspaper titles is printed, because none was confirmed here. Kajetan Muehlmann, his authority from Hermann Goering, his office opened in October 1939 and his catalogue of secured works printed in 1940 are from the institute article on him. The Krakow ghetto decree of Otto von Waechter of 3 March 1941, the deadline of 20 March, the twenty hectares and three hundred and twenty buildings and the roughly three thousand pre-war residents are from the institute article on how the Germans set up the ghetto in Krakow. The Soviet offensive on the Vistula opened on 12 January 1945 and Krakow was taken in the days that followed, and because sources give both 18 and 19 January the page says only that it fell within the week. Frank was arrested by American soldiers in Bavaria in May 1945, and the place of his arrest and the house where the stolen Leonardo was found are two different places, so neither is named here. The Under the Eagle pharmacy at 18 Plac Bohaterow Getta and the enamel works at 4 Lipowa Street are both branches of the Museum of Krakow, which states on its own pages that the permanent exhibition in the factory is about Krakow under occupation between 1939 and 1945 rather than about Oskar Schindler. The Palmiry cemetery in the Kampinos forest was laid out in 1948, which is the institute study of the Palmiry executions. No wartime German street sign in Krakow is described here, because none could be confirmed. The ghettos, the German camps built on occupied Polish soil, the killing of the Polish elites and Katyn belong to the article on the occupation and the Holocaust and are not told again here. Zamojszczyzna, the Warsaw Uprising and the Polish Underground State are each getting their own page. The money of the General Government, a zloty reissued by a German-run bank of issue, is left out for want of room. No opening hours are printed, because they change and none were checked. Title picture: the Wawel and the cathedral seen across the Vistula at dawn, photographed by Paul Colin Hennig in 2025, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],
    'katyn' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Katyn, 1940: the massacre and the lie',
            'description' => 'The Politburo decision of 5 March 1940 that had about 22,000 Polish officers and officials shot, the graves found in 1943, and fifty years of denial.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · Katyn and the deportations',
            'title' => 'Twenty two thousand men',
            'title_accent' => 'and fifty years of denial',
            'lede' => 'Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union invaded Poland sixteen days apart in September 1939, under a pact they had signed that August, and only one of them went on to lose the war, so for half a century one half of the story could not be told inside Poland at all. In the spring of 1940 the Soviet secret police shot about twenty two thousand Polish prisoners, and from February 1940 it loaded hundreds of thousands of civilians into goods wagons going east. Nine frames on what was done, who ordered it, how long it was denied, and where you can stand in it now.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'zone',
                'photo' => 'zone',
                'label' => 'The Soviet half',
                'year' => '1939 - 1941',
                'title' => 'Half of Poland was voted into the Soviet Union',
                'body' => 'The Soviet Union crossed into eastern Poland on 17 September 1939, sixteen days after Nazi Germany invaded from the west, and kept about half the country, with almost fourteen and a half million people. Then it held a vote. On 22 October 1939 two assemblies were elected, for Western Ukraine and Western Belarus, from candidates settled in advance, and both asked to join the Soviet Union. The Presidium of the Supreme Soviet said yes on 1 and 2 November. Everyone there was made a Soviet citizen, with a passport and, for young men, conscription. Before June 1941 some 150,000 people were arrested: officers, policemen, officials, judges, landowners.',
                'caption' => 'Delegates of the People\'s Assembly of Western Ukraine, photographed in Moscow in 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photographer unknown, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'camps',
                'photo' => 'camps',
                'label' => 'Three monasteries',
                'year' => '1939 - 1940',
                'title' => 'The officers were kept in three monasteries',
                'body' => 'The Red Army, the Soviet army, took close to 250,000 prisoners that September and handed about 126,000 of them to the NKVD, the Soviet secret police, which sorted the officers out. Three camps were made in monasteries the state had emptied of monks. Kozelsk, south west of Moscow, held 4,727 men that December, among them four generals and Janina Lewandowska, a pilot and the only woman in the Polish army. Starobelsk in Ukraine held 3,907, and Ostashkov, on an island in Lake Seliger, held 5,963 policemen, frontier guards and prison officers. They were allowed censored postcards home. In the spring of 1940 the letters stopped.',
                'caption' => 'A postcard to Tadeusz Knopp at Starobelsk, returned by the Moscow post in June 1940',
                'credit' => 'Documentary edition, London 1982, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'decision',
                'photo' => 'decision',
                'label' => 'One sheet of paper',
                'year' => 'March - May 1940',
                'title' => 'One note to Stalin, and four signatures',
                'body' => 'In March 1940 Lavrentiy Beria, head of the NKVD, asked the Soviet leader Joseph Stalin to have 14,700 prisoners from the camps and 11,000 from the prisons of western Ukraine and Belarus shot, without charge or trial. The Politburo, the Soviet party leadership, agreed on 5 March. He signed, with Kliment Voroshilov, Vyacheslav Molotov and Anastas Mikoyan, Mikhail Kalinin and Lazar Kaganovich noted as in favour. From 3 April the camps emptied. The men from Kozelsk were shot in the Katyn forest near Smolensk, those from Ostashkov in NKVD cellars in Kalinin and those from Starobelsk in cellars in Kharkiv. About 22,000 died.',
                'caption' => 'The first page of the note Lavrentiy Beria sent to Stalin',
                'credit' => 'Russian State Archive of Socio-Political History, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'waves',
                'photo' => 'waves',
                'label' => 'Four nights',
                'year' => '1940 - 1941',
                'title' => 'Four nights that emptied whole villages',
                'body' => 'Beside the shootings ran the deportations, and those took families rather than soldiers. The first came on 10 February 1940, in a frost around thirty below: about 140,000 people, mostly settlers, forestry workers and railwaymen with their wives and children, given twenty minutes to pack. The second, on 13 April 1940, took more than 61,000, among them the families of the men in the three camps, mostly to Kazakhstan. The third, on 29 June 1940, took some 80,000 refugees who had fled the German zone, most of them Jews. The fourth came in May and June 1941. The wagons were cattle wagons, with bunks, an iron stove and a hole in the floor for a lavatory.',
                'caption' => 'A goods wagon in the Sybir Memorial Museum in Bialystok, lettered Belostok 13 IV 1940',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Rakoon, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'settlements',
                'photo' => 'exile',
                'label' => 'Work or no bread',
                'year' => '1940 - 1946',
                'title' => 'Who does not work does not eat',
                'body' => 'They were set down in settlements in Kazakhstan, Siberia and the far north and put to felling timber with a hand saw and an axe. Everyone between sixteen and sixty had to work, and twelve year olds often did. Rations were paid against a daily quota, on a rule the deportees never forgot: kto nie rabotayet, tot ne kushayet, who does not work does not eat. One of them, Helena Lapinska, remembered the bread: thirty decagrams a day for a working person, which she said was nothing at all. By 1942 about 20,000 Polish children there had no parents. Nazi Germany turned on the Soviet Union in June 1941, and an amnesty then let many of them out with a Polish army raised there.',
                'caption' => 'The saw, the axe and the bread ration in the Sybir Memorial Museum in Bialystok',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Sybir Memorial Museum, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'discovery',
                'photo' => 'discovery',
                'label' => 'Found in 1943',
                'year' => '1943 - 1946',
                'title' => 'The graves found, the alliance broken',
                'body' => 'On 13 April 1943 Berlin radio announced that the graves of Polish officers had been found at Katyn, in forest the German army then held, and made a propaganda campaign of it. On 15 April the Polish government, by then in London, asked the International Committee of the Red Cross to look, and Berlin asked a day later. Moscow called that collusion and broke off relations on 25 April 1943, as the government in exile article tells. When the Red Army came back, a Soviet commission under the surgeon Nikolai Burdenko reported in January 1944 that the Germans had done it. Soviet prosecutors charged Germany with Katyn at Nuremberg, and the judgment never mentioned it.',
                'caption' => 'The Katyn forest near Smolensk, where the graves were opened in 1943',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Kapsuglan, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'lie',
                'photo' => 'lie',
                'label' => 'Fifty years',
                'year' => '1945 - 1989',
                'title' => 'In Poland the year 1940 could not be cut',
                'body' => 'Poland came out of the war with a government Moscow installed, and it kept the Soviet version. An instruction of 1975 told the censors to pass no attempt to blame the Soviet Union for the deaths of the officers, so a family could carve a name on a stone but not the year 1940 beside it, because 1940 said who had done it. From 1977 the underground presses put it out in pamphlets that could get a reader arrested, and a monument raised at the Powazki military cemetery in Warsaw on 31 July 1981 was carried off the next night by the secret police. The composer Andrzej Panufnik, who had left Poland in 1954, wrote his Katyn Epitaph in Britain in 1967, because there it could be said.',
                'caption' => 'The Katyn monument in the Powazki military cemetery in Warsaw, cut with the date 1940',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Lowdown, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'truth',
                'photo' => 'truth',
                'label' => 'The admissions',
                'year' => '1990 - 2013',
                'title' => 'Moscow admitted it, then shut the file',
                'body' => 'On 13 April 1990 the Soviet news agency TASS named Beria and his deputy Vsevolod Merkulov as responsible and called it one of the gravest crimes of Stalinism, and the Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev gave the first copies to the Polish president, Wojciech Jaruzelski. On 14 October 1992 an envoy of the Russian president Boris Yeltsin brought Lech Walesa more, among them a note of 3 March 1959 in which the KGB chairman Aleksandr Shelepin counted 21,857 shot. Russian prosecutors closed the investigation in September 2004, and in 2013 the European Court of Human Rights ruled it had no competence to judge it. Nobody has ever been tried.',
                'caption' => 'The courtroom of the European Court of Human Rights in Strasbourg',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'Where to stand',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'Four cemeteries, a museum and the oaks',
                'body' => 'The Katyn Museum sits in the casemates of the Warsaw Citadel and holds what came out of the pits: identity discs and police badges, bowls and spoons, cigarette papers with writing on them. Among the Katyn finds were a lipstick and a hand mirror that can only have been Janina Lewandowska\'s. Four Polish war cemeteries were built over the graves, at Kharkiv, Katyn and Mednoye in 2000 and at Bykivnia near Kyiv in 2012. Since 2008 people have been planting Katyn oaks, one tree for one name. On 10 April 2010 a Tu-154 carrying a delegation to the commemoration in the Katyn forest crashed near Smolensk, killing all 96 on board, among them President Lech Kaczynski.',
                'caption' => 'The main avenue of the Polish war cemetery at Kharkiv, with a September Campaign cross',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Aleksander Martyniak, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'Almost everything here comes from two Polish state institutions and one European court. The Institute of National Remembrance, which runs a Katyn portal of its own at katyn.ipn.gov.pl, is the source for the half of pre-war Poland the Soviet Union took and the almost fourteen and a half million people on it, for the voting of 22 October 1939 and the answer of the Presidium of the Supreme Soviet on 1 and 2 November, for the imposition of Soviet citizenship at the end of November 1939, and for about 150,000 people arrested between 17 September 1939 and 22 June 1941. What happened to somebody who refused a Soviet passport could not be established here, so the page does not say. The same institute gives close to 250,000 prisoners of war taken in September 1939 and about 126,000 of them handed to the NKVD, and the camp counts of 4,727 at Kozelsk and 5,963 at Ostashkov on 1 December 1939 and 3,907 at Starobelsk on 4 December. The letters are its subject too: the prisoners wrote censored postcards, the exchange broke off in the spring of 1940, and the Soviet Union later had to invent three road-building camps to explain the silence. No day is printed for the last letter, because none was confirmed. The note Beria sent is dated by the institute only to March 1940, because the day is left blank on the typed sheet, and 5 March is the date of the Politburo sitting that took it, so the page says it that way. The four signatures on the paper and the secretary note that Mikhail Kalinin and Lazar Kaganovich were in favour follow two institute articles that agree. The figures of 14,700 and 11,000 are the ones in the note. The round figure of about 22,000 is the one the Katyn Museum in Warsaw uses, and the exact 21,857, of whom 4,421 at Katyn, 6,311 from Ostashkov, 3,820 from Starobelsk and 7,305 in the prisons, comes from the note Aleksandr Shelepin wrote to Nikita Khrushchev on 3 March 1959, which the institute and the European Court of Human Rights both quote. The transports ran from 3 April to 12 May 1940, and 395 men out of the three camps survived. The deportation dates and sizes, 10 February 1940 with about 140,000 people, 13 April 1940 with more than 61,000, 29 June 1940 with about 80,000 and the fourth wave in May and June 1941, are from institute articles on each wave, as are the twenty minutes to pack, the frost, the bunks and the hole in the floor. Two accountings of the grand total exist and this page chooses between them by printing neither. Aleksandr Gurianov of the Russian association Memorial, working from the records of the NKVD convoy troops, put the four waves at 320,000 to 340,000 people. The Polish government in London and the Polish embassy in the Soviet Union in 1941 arrived at about a million. The difference is that the NKVD records count the four mass deportations only, while the wartime Polish estimate takes in the arrests, the prisoners, the conscripts and the forced labourers as well. The director of the Sybir Memorial Museum in Bialystok, Wojciech Sleszynski, gives a minimum of 330,000 for the four waves. The piece rates, the three days off a year, the Russian saying and about 20,000 Polish orphans by 1942 are from the same institute material. The line of Helena Lapinska about thirty decagrams of bread is on the display case in the Sybir Memorial Museum that this page photographs. Scurvy is not mentioned, because no source opened here names it. For 1943 the institute gives a German agency report on 11 April and the Berlin radio announcement on 13 April, and then the sequence that decided everything: the Polish government instructed its representative in Switzerland on 15 April, the German Red Cross telegraphed Geneva on 16 April, and in Geneva on 17 April the German note was lodged half an hour before the Polish one. The International Committee of the Red Cross answered that under its memorandum of 12 September 1939 it could not act without the consent of all parties, and no date is printed for that reply because none was confirmed. The Soviet note breaking relations is dated 25 April 1943. Germany brought in a commission of twelve forensic experts from a dozen European countries, which worked from April to June 1943, and the Polish Red Cross separately sent a technical commission of twelve people under Kazimierz Skarzynski, which reached the same conclusion. Of 4,243 bodies, between 2,730 and 2,805 were identified, the two figures coming from the court and from the Polish Red Cross. No photograph of a body or of an exhumation appears anywhere on this page, and the German pictures of 1943 are not used, because they were made over the victims for a propaganda campaign. The papers taken from the graves were examined in Krakow by Jan Zygmunt Robel, who told the NKVD his materials had been destroyed and kept two copies, and those copies are still the basic source. The Burdenko commission was appointed on 13 January 1944 and gave its communique on 22 January. At Nuremberg the Soviet deputy chief prosecutor charged the Germans with 11,000 Polish officers on 13 February 1946, the tribunal heard three witnesses on each side on 1 and 2 July 1946, and the judgment of 30 September and 1 October 1946 does not mention Katyn at all, nor does the dissent of the Soviet judge. The censorship instruction of 1975 is quoted by the institute from a study by Witold Wasilewski. The monument raised in the Katyn Hollow on 31 July 1981 and carried off the next night, and the underground publishing from 1977, are from the same institute article. The Black Book of Censorship is not cited here, because the institute article that carries its name does not quote it. The Katyn Epitaph of Andrzej Panufnik is described by the Polish Music Information Centre. The TASS communique of 13 April 1990 and the handing of the first copies to Wojciech Jaruzelski the same day are recorded by the institute and by the court, as is the transfer of 14 October 1992, made by the Russian state archivist Rudolf Pikhoya on behalf of Boris Yeltsin. The Russian investigation, case number 159, was discontinued on 21 September 2004, and the court records that thirty six of its 183 volumes were later classified top secret and eight more restricted, and that the existence of the decision to close it was revealed only at a press conference on 11 March 2005. The ruling of 21 October 2013 is Janowiec and Others v. Russia, applications 55508/07 and 29520/09, in which the Grand Chamber held that it had no competence to examine the complaint under article 2, that there had been no violation of article 3, and unanimously that Russia had failed to comply with article 38 by not producing the documents the court asked for. Nothing is said here about present-day Polish and Russian politics. The museum objects named are the ones its own collections page lists. The cemeteries opened at Kharkiv on 17 June 2000, at Katyn on 28 July 2000, at Mednoye on 2 September 2000 and at Bykivnia on 21 September 2012, and they were built by the Council for the Protection of Struggle and Martyrdom Sites under a Polish-Russian agreement of 1994. The oak programme, one oak for one name from the Katyn list, was begun on 13 April 2008 by the Parafiada association and aims at 21,857 trees. No count of trees planted is printed, because it changes, and no opening hours are printed for the same reason. The crash of 10 April 2010 is stated as the state history portal dzieje.pl states it: a Tu-154M carrying a delegation to the ceremonies in the Katyn forest, 96 dead, among them President Lech Kaczynski, his wife Maria and Ryszard Kaczorowski, the last president in exile. Nothing about its causes appears here. No map is published on this page. The published maps of the 1939 division are already on the articles about September 1939 and the German-Soviet pact, and no published map of the deportation destinations or of the three camps could be found for use here, so rather than draw one none is shown. The break in relations of 1943, the Sikorski-Maisky agreement of 1941 and the army General Wladyslaw Anders led out of the Soviet Union belong to the article on the government in exile. The Ukrainian list of 3,435 names, handed over by Ukraine in 1994, the Belarusian list of about 3,870 people that has never been released, and Kurapaty near Minsk, which the institute itself calls a probability rather than an established burial place, are named here rather than in the frames, for want of room. Title picture: the Polish war cemetery at Mednoye, photographed by Natalia Shkurenok in July 2024, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],
    'zamosc' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The Zamosc expulsions, 1942-1943',
            'description' => 'How Nazi Germany emptied close to 300 villages around Zamosc for German settlers, and what happened to the 30,000 children taken with them.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · The Zamosc expulsions',
            'title' => 'A hundred thousand people',
            'title_accent' => 'cleared off the land',
            'lede' => 'Nazi Germany had occupied this part of Poland since 1939 and ruled it as a colony called the General Government. In the autumn of 1942 it began a rehearsal there for something far larger. The country around the town of Zamosc in south-east Poland, which Poles call Zamojszczyzna, was to be emptied of the people living on it and settled with Germans instead, and the emptying began with one village in one night. About 110,000 people were driven out of close to 300 villages, roughly 30,000 of them children, and the children were graded by what German officials judged their blood to be worth. Nine frames on why this place, what was done to it, who stood in the way, and what is left to see.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'plan',
                'photo' => 'plan',
                'label' => 'Land for Germans',
                'year' => '1942',
                'title' => 'Where the remaking of the east was to begin',
                'body' => 'Nazi Germany meant to remake the land it had taken. The General Plan for the East was drawn up in June 1942 by Konrad Meyer, an agronomist in the SS, the armed and police corps of the Nazi party. It was to move some thirty million people off the land over twenty-five years and put Germans on their farms. The country around Zamosc was picked to go first, for its good soil and the roads and railways running east. On 12 November 1942 Heinrich Himmler, who commanded the SS, signed the order making it the first German settlement area, and handed the work to his police commander in Lublin, Odilo Globocnik, who wanted the town renamed Himmlerstadt. That name was never formally adopted.',
                'caption' => 'Fields above Skierbieszow, the first village the Germans emptied',
                'credit' => 'Photo: kruczjak, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'night',
                'photo' => 'night',
                'label' => 'The first village',
                'year' => 'November 1942',
                'title' => 'A cordon round the village, and an hour to pack',
                'body' => 'It began in the night of 27 to 28 November 1942 at Skierbieszow. German police surrounded the village, woke the houses and gave people about an hour to take what they could carry. The land, the buildings and the animals stayed behind. Those taken went to a camp of wooden barracks outside Zamosc, opened ten days earlier, which a German report of the time put at 10,500 places. There they were sorted into four groups: families judged to have German blood, people of working age fit for labour in Germany, people of the same age marked for the German camp at Auschwitz, and everybody else. Mothers kept only babies under six months old.',
                'caption' => 'German gendarmes during a pacification in the Zamosc countryside',
                'credit' => 'Photographer unknown, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'children',
                'photo' => 'monument',
                'label' => 'Thirty thousand',
                'year' => '1942 - 1943',
                'title' => 'The children were counted, graded and sent on',
                'body' => 'Four counties were cleared, Bilgoraj, Hrubieszow, Tomaszow and Zamosc, and by August 1943 about 110,000 people had been taken from close to 300 villages. The Institute of National Remembrance, the Polish state body that researches these years, counts roughly 30,000 children among them and about 10,000 who died or were never traced. Some 4,500 were sent into the Reich to be brought up as Germans. Three transports carried more than 1,300 people from Zamosc to Auschwitz that winter, at least 150 of them children, and the museum there records that of those children 44 girls and five boys came out alive.',
                'caption' => 'The Angel of Death in Zamosc, on the ground where the camp stood',
                'credit' => 'Sculptor: Jerzy Hycki, 1978. Photo: Ziuteknowocz, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'rescue',
                'photo' => 'shelter',
                'label' => 'The winter trains',
                'year' => 'Winter 1942 - 1943',
                'title' => 'Who was waiting when the trains stopped',
                'body' => 'The people the Germans had no use for, mostly children under fourteen and the old, were loaded into goods wagons and sent north into the Warsaw district of the General Government. Six of those transports carried 5,321 people between December 1942 and February 1943, by the count of the historian Beata Kozaczynska. Where they stopped, at Pilawa and Sobolew and Mrozy and Siedlce, people came to the platforms and took children home with them. In Siedlce 465 households took a child in. In Warsaw you could also buy one from a German railway clerk for fifty zloty.',
                'caption' => 'Children from the Zamosc region in a wartime shelter in Warsaw',
                'credit' => 'Photographer unknown, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'settlers',
                'photo' => 'settlers',
                'label' => 'The new owners',
                'year' => '1943',
                'title' => 'German families moved into the emptied farms',
                'body' => 'The point of all of it was to put Germans on the land. Between ten and thirteen thousand colonists were brought in, most of them ethnic Germans who had been moved out of Bessarabia and Bukovina in 1940 under an agreement Nazi Germany made with the Soviet Union, and about seven thousand Ukrainians were settled there too. In January 1943 Globocnik issued instructions on how a settler house was to look. The German character of it, he wrote, had to be obvious at a glance, down to the fence and the garden at the front.',
                'caption' => 'A German printed page on the Bessarabian resettlement, autumn 1940',
                'credit' => 'Printed page, photographer unknown, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'rising',
                'photo' => 'rising',
                'label' => 'Fighting back',
                'year' => '1942 - 1943',
                'title' => 'Two underground armies stood in the way',
                'body' => 'The Home Army answered to the Polish government that had gone into exile in London in 1939. The Peasant Battalions were raised by the underground peasant movement from the village men themselves. On 23 December 1942 the Home Army commander Stefan Rowecki ordered resistance. At Wojda on 30 December a Peasant Battalions company under Jerzy Miller, with 37 Soviet partisans under Vasily Volodin, held off some 350 German police until dusk, then slipped into the forest. The Germans burned the village. At Zaboreczno on 1 February 1943 Peasant Battalions companies under Franciszek Bartlomowicz drove a German expedition off the field, and the mass expulsions stopped for nearly half a year.',
                'caption' => 'The monument on the hill at Zaboreczno, raised by Tomaszow county',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Pftf, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'sochy',
                'photo' => 'sochy',
                'label' => 'The reprisals',
                'year' => '1943',
                'title' => 'What was done to the villages in return',
                'body' => 'The day after Zaboreczno the same partisans were caught resting at Roza, and the Germans burned that village and killed some of its people. Reprisal was the pattern. On 1 June 1943, at five in the morning, German police and an SS unit surrounded Sochy, near Zwierzyniec, killed the people they found there and then had the village bombed from the air. The Institute of National Remembrance gives the dead as 183, or 185, or 194, of whom 180 were identified by name. One house in the whole village was left standing. On 24 June the clearances themselves came back, for the third and bloodiest round, which the Germans codenamed Werwolf and which ran until August 1943.',
                'caption' => 'The war cemetery at Sochy, on the forest edge above the village',
                'credit' => 'Photo: KoronaC, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'after',
                'photo' => 'after',
                'label' => 'The card index',
                'year' => '1944 - 1950',
                'title' => 'After the war came the looking',
                'body' => 'The Red Army drove the Germans out of the region in the summer of 1944. Those who survived came back through 1944 and 1945 to burnt farms, or to farms with other people living in them. The children were the harder problem. The government plenipotentiary for the recovery of Polish children, Roman Hrabar, worked at it from 1947 to 1950, and the Polish Red Cross kept the card index. At the end of 1948 it still held 5,490 children whose families were looking for them. Many had been too small to remember their own names. Of the Zamosc children taken into the Reich, the Institute of National Remembrance reckons about 800 came back.',
                'caption' => 'Survivors of the Zamosc camp in the snow outside the town, forty years on',
                'credit' => 'CIRCE-Archiv, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'Where to stand',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'A perfect square, and the places around it',
                'body' => 'Zamosc itself came through the war intact, which is why its Renaissance market square is on the UNESCO World Heritage list. An architect from Padua, Bernardo Morando, laid it out for the chancellor Jan Zamoyski. Walk south and you reach the Rotunda, a round brick gun emplacement of the old fortress that the Zamosc Museum keeps as a memorial, with the cells inside and the graves around it. Zaboreczno is marked every 1 February in Krynice and Polany, the villages where it was fought. Sochy has its cemetery on the forest edge. In the summer of 1943 about 9,000 people from these four counties were sent to the German camp at Lublin, now the State Museum at Majdanek.',
                'caption' => 'The town hall and the Great Market Square in Zamosc, begun in 1580',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Maciej Ukleja, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A folded map plate of the Zamosc region with every village marked by a symbol, filled circles for villages emptied completely, open circles for villages emptied in part, crosses for sites of mass execution and hatching for the areas where partisans fought',
            'caption' => 'The Zamosc region village by village, in the documentary edition Zamojszczyzna - Sonderlaboratorium SS edited by Czeslaw Madajczyk, Warsaw 1979, in Polish. Filled circles are villages emptied completely, open circles villages emptied in part.',
            'credit' => 'Zamojszczyzna - Sonderlaboratorium SS, ed. Czeslaw Madajczyk, Ludowa Spoldzielnia Wydawnicza, Warsaw 1979, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The backbone of this page is the Institute of National Remembrance, which carries the essay of Ryszard Sodel on the pacification of Zamojszczyzna and a separate article on Aktion Zamosc, together with the Museum of the Second World War in Gdansk, the State Museum at Majdanek, the Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and two academic historians who have worked in the archives. That the General Plan for the East was drawn up in June 1942 by Konrad Meyer, head of the planning and land department in the office of the Reich Commissioner for the Strengthening of Germandom, is stated in the same words by the institute and by the state history portal dzieje.pl. The figure of about thirty million people to be moved over twenty-five years comes from dzieje.pl. The reasons for choosing this region are given by three institutions and agree: good soil, position on the routes running east, and German colonists settled there in the 1780s. The word black earth is not used here, because none of the sources opened uses it. That Heinrich Himmler signed general ordinance number 17c on 12 November 1942 naming Zamosc county the first German settlement area in the General Government is documented by Beata Kozaczynska of the University of Siedlce, citing the documentary edition Zamojszczyzna - Sonderlaboratorium SS, and it is repeated by dzieje.pl. Himmler visited Zamosc in 1941, but the sources give three different days for that visit, so no date for it is printed here. The plan to rename the town Himmlerstadt is the subject of one scholarly article, by Aleksandra Paradowska, whose title says it was a Himmlerstadt that never was, and a German report on the visit of the Warsaw district governor Ludwig Fischer in May 1943 still speaks of the name Pflugstadt, the town of the plough, as something intended for later. One popular account says the town was renamed. This page says the name was proposed and never formally adopted, which is what the scholarship says. Odilo Globocnik is named here as SS and police commander in the Lublin district, which is how the institute names him. Friedrich-Wilhelm Krueger, his superior in Krakow, is left out for want of room. The night of 27 to 28 November 1942 and the first village, Skierbieszow, are given by the institute, by dzieje.pl and by the Museum of the Second World War. The hour to pack and the order to leave everything movable and immovable behind are the institute, which also records that the old, the sick and the disabled were sometimes killed on the spot. That is not printed on this page in those words, and it is true. The camp outside Zamosc was opened on 18 November 1942 and closed on 18 January 1944, and the capacity of 10,500 comes from the German report of 31 December 1942 held in the institute archive, both from Beata Kozaczynska. Estimates of how many people passed through it range from about 41,000 to about 50,000, so no total is printed. The address is not printed either, because the sources give two different streets. The four groups follow Kozaczynska: groups one and two were families held to be of German blood and were sent to Lodz for further examination, group three were people aged 14 to 60 fit for physical work and were sent to labour in the Reich, group four were people of the same ages marked for Auschwitz, and children under 14 and people over 60 formed a separate category. The German term for the groups is not used here, because the Polish sources do not use it. That mothers kept only babies under six months is given by three sources. The totals of about 110,000 people, close to 300 villages, which the Museum of the Second World War puts precisely at 293, and roughly 30,000 children are the figures the institute, the museum and the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum all publish, and lower counts of about 100,000 exist, which is why the page says about. That about 10,000 children died or went missing and about 4,500 were marked for Germanisation is the institute in two places and Kozaczynska. One institute historian gives 13,000 dead children instead, and the lower figure is printed here because three sources carry it. The three transports to Auschwitz in December 1942 and February 1943 carried more than 1,300 people, among them at least 150 children and young people, and it is of those children that 44 girls and five boys survived, not of the 1,300. All of that is the Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial teaching service, which also names the two boys killed on 21 January 1943. Their names are not printed here. Czeslawa Kwoka, whose camp photograph is the best known face of this story, is named in the sources rather than in the frames, because a page that used her picture would be using a photograph the SS took of a fourteen-year-old. No photograph of a dead or dying person appears anywhere on this page. The six transports north with 5,321 people, the stations at Pilawa, Sobolew, Mordy, Mrozy and Siedlce and the fifty zloty a child cost from a German railway clerk in Warsaw are all Beata Kozaczynska, working from the papers of the Central Welfare Council in the Archive of Modern Records. She is also the reason this page does not say that the Home Army freed the trains: she calls that a postwar myth and shows that individual children were bought or handed over instead. The 465 households in Siedlce are from the institute. The funeral of 3 February 1943 in Siedlce, where 23 of the dead were buried in front of some five thousand people, is in two institute articles and is left out of the frames for want of room. The settlers, between ten and thirteen thousand of them from Bessarabia and Bukovina, plus about seven thousand Ukrainians, are dzieje.pl and the Museum of the Second World War, and the figure of 12,000 German colonists is the institute. Transnistria is not mentioned, because no source opened here mentions it. The instruction of January 1943 on the look of a settler house is quoted by dzieje.pl from Globocnik. What became of the settlers in 1944 rests on a single source and is not printed. The order to resist, given by the Home Army commander Stefan Rowecki on 23 December 1942, the fight at Wojda on 30 December 1942 under Jerzy Miller, whose surname some publications give as Mara-Meyer, and the fight at Zaboreczno on 1 February 1943 under Franciszek Bartlomowicz are from two institute articles by different authors. His rank is left off because the institute gives both captain and major. Both fights were the Peasant Battalions rather than the Home Army, which is the institute, quoting Marek Wierzbicki: at Wojda the newly raised 1st Cadre Company of about 130 men from the emptied villages, joined on 28 December by 37 Soviet partisans under Vasily Volodin, against 300 to 350 German policemen and colonists, and at Zaboreczno several Peasant Battalions companies with one Home Army platoon. Wojda was not a victory. The institute writes that the partisans ran short of ammunition, pulled back into the forest at dusk, and that the Germans then took the village, burned it and shot 11 of its people, or 20 by other accounts. The number shot is not printed here because the institute itself gives two. German losses at Zaboreczno are estimated from an order for 103 coffins placed at a sawmill the next day, which is why no figure is printed. Roza is dated 2 February 1943 in the sources, as the sequel to Zaboreczno rather than a second day of it, and no commander is named for it anywhere. The word uprising is avoided in the frames, because the institute author says plainly that it is a debatable label for what was self-defence. Sochy was surrounded at five in the morning on 1 June 1943 by German Schutzpolizei and an SS unit and then bombed from the air, and the dead are given by the institute as 183 or 185 or 194, of whom 180 were identified, which is why all three numbers appear. One regional source dates it 2 June, and 1 June is printed because the institute uses it twice and the monument carries it. That the expulsions stopped after Zaboreczno and came back for a third and bloodiest stage, that it began on 24 June 1943, that it carried the German codename Werwolf, spelled that way and not Wehrwolf, and that it ran until August 1943, is the institute, the State Museum at Majdanek and the Museum of the Second World War. Sochy on 1 June 1943 came three weeks before that operation and is not part of it, which is why the two are dated separately here. The institute article on Zaboreczno is the reason the frames stop counting in August 1943: Himmler ordered Aktion Zamosc closed about two months after the June return, and of the roughly 700 villages the Germans had listed they emptied not quite 300. The figure of about 60,000 people from 171 villages that the Museum of the Second World War gives for Werwolf alone is not printed, because it cannot be added to the totals in the frames without double counting. That the Red Army drove the Germans out of this part of the country in the summer of 1944 is why the last frame begins there, and no day is printed, because the towns of the region fell on different days. No figure for villages burned across the region is printed, because no institution publishes one. Roman Hrabar was the government plenipotentiary for the recovery of Polish children from 1947 to 1950, and the card index figure of 5,490 children still sought at the end of 1948 is from Krzysztof Lesiakowski of the University of Lodz, working from Polish Red Cross records. No closing date for the searches is printed, because the tracing office of the Polish Red Cross still exists. The figure of about 800 Zamosc children returning rests on one institute historian and is attributed here. Zamosc was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage list in 1992 under criterion four, and UNESCO itself notes that the town escaped the destruction other Polish towns suffered, names Jan Zamoyski as its founder and Bernardo Morando of Padua as its architect. The Rotunda was built between 1825 and 1831 as a gun emplacement of the Zamosc fortress, served the German security police as a prison and transit camp from 19 June 1940 to 22 July 1944, and is estimated by the Zamosc Museum to have been where six to eight thousand people died. No opening hours are printed, because they change and none were checked. There is no Museum of the Children of Zamojszczyzna to send anyone to. A centre for their memory works at the Zamosc Academy and a museum is under discussion, and until it exists the places to go are the Rotunda, the State Museum at Majdanek in Lublin and the cemetery at Sochy. No permanent exhibition on the Zamosc deportees is claimed for Majdanek, because the museum lists only two permanent exhibitions and neither is that one. Its exhibition The Deportees from the Zamosc Region in the Camp at Majdanek ran from 28 April to 27 September 2018 and is filed as archived. That about 9,000 people from the four counties were sent to the German camp at Lublin between 30 June and 1 August 1943, most of them mothers with children, by way of the transit camps at Zamosc and Zwierzyniec, is the same museum in two places. The anniversary of Zaboreczno is marked on 1 and 2 February in Krynice and Polany in Tomaszow county, at the Peasant Battalions monument, and not in Zamosc, which is the parish and the district authorities. That the region had a Jewish population and that it was murdered before the Poles were driven out is true and is not told here: the Jews of Zamosc were deported from April 1942, most of them to the German extermination camp at Belzec, the ghetto was emptied on 16 October 1942 and the town declared free of Jews two days later. That belongs to the article on the occupation and the Holocaust, which tells it properly, and it is named here so that no reader closes this page thinking the expulsions were all that happened to this country. The General Government, the Polish government in exile and the camps themselves have their own articles here. They are named on this page only far enough to place the reader, and are not told again. Title picture: the inner courtyard of the Rotunda in Zamosc, photographed by Zala in 2018, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],
    'volhynia' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Volhynia, 1943: the massacres of Poles',
            'description' => 'What happened to the Polish villages of Volhynia between 1943 and 1945, who the UPA were, how many died, and why Poland still argues about one word.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · Volhynia 1943',
            'title' => 'Volhynia, 1943',
            'title_accent' => 'and the name that ends a conversation',
            'lede' => 'Volhynia is a flat country of fields, marsh and pine forest, now in north-western Ukraine and until 1939 a province of Poland. In the summer of 1943 the Ukrainian Insurgent Army set out to clear the Polish population off it, village by village, and then carried the same action south into Eastern Galicia. This is the hardest page on this site. It is written from what Polish and Ukrainian historians have established, it says plainly where they disagree, and it names the people who gave the orders as carefully as it names the ones who saved their neighbours.',
            'meta_line' => 'Thirteen frames · about sixteen minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'together',
                'photo' => 'land',
                'label' => 'Two peoples',
                'year' => '1569 - 1939',
                'title' => 'How Poles and Ukrainians came to share a countryside',
                'body' => 'Volhynia joined the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, the joint state of Poland and Lithuania, at the Union of Lublin in 1569, passed to the Russian empire when that Commonwealth was carved up in the late eighteenth century, and came back to Poland under the Treaty of Riga, the peace signed with Soviet Russia on 18 March 1921. So between the wars it was a Polish province with a Polish governor and Polish courts, and most of the people living in it were not Poles. The census of 1931 counted 2,085,574 inhabitants of the Wolyn voivodeship. Sixty-eight per cent gave Ukrainian as the language of their home, 16.6 per cent Polish and 9.9 per cent Yiddish or Hebrew.',
                'caption' => 'The overgrown road from Wyrka to Tur in Volhynia, photographed in 2011',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Grzegorz Naumowicz, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'republic',
                'photo' => 'torczyn',
                'label' => 'A state presses',
                'year' => '1921 - 1939',
                'title' => 'What that state did with a Ukrainian majority',
                'body' => 'Poland between the wars ran two policies in Volhynia at once. Its governor from 1928 to 1938, Henryk Jozewski, ran what was called the Volhynian experiment: Ukrainian schools, Ukrainian societies, a conciliatory line. It also settled Polish army veterans on the land, and after the death of Jozef Pilsudski, the marshal who had run the state since 1926, the line hardened into Polonisation. In Galicia, the Polish province to the south, the army and police had already run a punitive pacification of Ukrainian villages in 1930. In 1938 the state pulled down between 91 and 127 Orthodox churches in the Chelm region. Those are real grievances. They are not an explanation of what came next.',
                'caption' => 'A rifle association marches past the governor Henryk Jozewski at Torczyn',
                'credit' => 'Photographer unknown, before 1938, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'oun',
                'photo' => 'pieracki',
                'label' => 'Nationalists',
                'year' => '1929 - 1940',
                'title' => 'A movement that wrote murder into its rules',
                'body' => 'The Organisation of Ukrainian Nationalists, the OUN, was founded in Vienna in 1929 to win an independent Ukraine by force. Its decalogue told members not to hesitate at the greatest crime if the cause required it. Stepan Bandera, born on 1 January 1909 in Austrian Galicia, ran its campaign of assassination. His men shot the Polish interior minister Bronislaw Pieracki in Warsaw on 15 June 1934. At his trial Bandera said the idea was so great that not hundreds but millions of victims had to be sacrificed for it. Sentenced to death and reprieved to life, he was freed in September 1939. In 1940 the OUN split, the older wing under Andrii Melnyk, the radicals under Bandera.',
                'caption' => 'The funeral of Bronislaw Pieracki, the Polish minister shot in June 1934',
                'credit' => 'Photographer unknown, 1934, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'occupations',
                'photo' => 'zellenbau',
                'label' => 'Two occupiers',
                'year' => '1939 - 1943',
                'title' => 'Soviets, then Germans, and Bandera in a cell',
                'body' => 'On 17 September 1939 the Soviet Union invaded eastern Poland under the Molotov-Ribbentrop pact with Nazi Germany, took Volhynia and deported tens of thousands of its people east. Germany drove the Soviets out in June 1941. On 30 June 1941 the Bandera wing, the OUN-B, proclaimed a Ukrainian state in Lviv. Germany did not want one and arrested Bandera within days. He was held until 27 September 1944, latterly in the Zellenbau, the special prison block at the German camp Sachsenhausen. German units and the Ukrainian auxiliary police they recruited murdered almost the whole Jewish population of Volhynia in 1942. In the spring of 1943 those policemen deserted, with their weapons.',
                'caption' => 'The Zellenbau at Sachsenhausen, the prison block where Bandera was held',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Tommes73, 2007, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'upa',
                'photo' => 'parosla',
                'label' => 'The UPA rises',
                'year' => 'February 1943',
                'title' => 'An insurgent army, and the first village',
                'body' => 'The Ukrainian Insurgent Army, the UPA, was the armed force of the OUN-B. Ukrainian tradition dates it to 14 October 1942, historians to the winter that followed, when the first units formed and the deserting policemen filled them. On 9 February 1943 one of those units, led by Hryhorii Perehiniak, killed the Polish inhabitants of the colony of Parosla in Sarny county. The Institute of National Remembrance, the Polish state body that investigates these years, puts the dead there at at least 155. The UPA commander in Volhynia was Dmytro Kliachkivskyi, known as Klym Savur, and in June 1943 he passed his commanders an order for the physical liquidation of the entire Polish population.',
                'caption' => 'The mass grave in the forest at Parosla, where the killing began',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Grzegorz Naumowicz, 2011, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'july',
                'photo' => 'kysylyn',
                'label' => '11 July 1943',
                'year' => 'July 1943',
                'title' => 'A Sunday chosen because people would be at mass',
                'body' => 'On Sunday 11 July 1943 UPA units struck dozens of Polish villages in western Volhynia within a few hours, timed for morning service. Pawel Nalezniak, writing for the Institute of National Remembrance, counts 97 villages and colonies and 13 estates that day and more than 3,100 people killed. Another account from the same institute gives 99 villages and about 8,000 dead. The next day 67 more places were attacked and at least 2,020 people killed. At Poryck 222 died, at Chrynow 150, at Kisielin about 90, killed with guns and grenades in the church and the monastery beside it. Through July and August the UPA attacked more than 830 villages and killed about twenty thousand people.',
                'caption' => 'The ruined church and monastery at Kisielin, attacked on 11 July 1943',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Promek, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'neighbours',
                'photo' => 'halinowka',
                'label' => 'Face to face',
                'year' => 'July 1943',
                'title' => 'Why this is remembered as more than a killing',
                'body' => 'What sets this apart from an execution is who did it and how. The Institute of National Remembrance, drawing on the inventory that Ewa and Wladyslaw Siemaszko made village by village, records that most of the victims were killed with farm tools rather than firearms, that families were shut into houses and barns and burned alive, and that churches were set alight with the congregation inside. The attacks came at night on the houses, or on a Sunday at mass. Alongside the UPA units came Ukrainian peasants from the next village, people the dead had known by name. Ewa Siemaszko has documented 580 mixed Polish and Ukrainian families caught up in it.',
                'caption' => 'The field where the Polish colony of Halinowka stood until 1943',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Grzegorz Naumowicz, 2011, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'defence',
                'photo' => 'huta',
                'label' => 'Holding on',
                'year' => '1943 - 1944',
                'title' => 'Who held out, and who warned the neighbours',
                'body' => 'On 22 April 1943 Kazimierz Babinski, who commanded the Home Army in Volhynia for the Polish government in London, ordered self-defence villages set up. About 180 were formed, 142 of them in Volhynia. The largest, Przebraze, sheltered ten thousand people and was never taken. Huta Stepanska, which with the village beside it held five thousand, fell on 16 to 18 July 1943. The other thing that saved lives was Ukrainian neighbours. A register compiled by Romuald Niedzielko for the institute in 2007 documents more than 1,300 Ukrainians in 500 places who warned, hid people or refused orders, and over 2,500 Poles alive because of them. Several hundred were killed for it.',
                'caption' => 'The cross at Huta Stepanska, over a self-defence village that fell',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Grzegorz Naumowicz, 2010, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'revenge',
                'photo' => 'sahryn',
                'label' => 'The reprisals',
                'year' => '1944 - 1945',
                'title' => 'What Polish units did back, and to whom',
                'body' => 'In 1944 the killing moved south into Eastern Galicia, the provinces around Lviv, Ternopil and Stanislaviv, where the UPA more often drove Poles out than destroyed whole villages, and killed tens of thousands all the same. Polish units struck back, and their victims were largely civilians too. At Sahryn on 10 March 1944 Home Army and Peasant Battalion units killed Ukrainian villagers: the institute puts the dead at 150 to 300, the historian Mariusz Zajaczkowski names 234, Ihor Halahida counts more than 600. At Pawlokoma on 3 March 1945 a Home Army company with local self-defence killed 365 Ukrainians. This is not a footnote to the story. It is part of it.',
                'caption' => 'The Orthodox cemetery at Sahryn, with the names of the dead of March 1944',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Loraine, 2016, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'numbers',
                'photo' => 'ostrowki',
                'label' => 'Counting them',
                'year' => '1943 - 1945',
                'title' => 'How many died, and why the counts differ',
                'body' => 'Polish scholarship and the Institute of National Remembrance put the Polish dead at about a hundred thousand: some 40,000 to 60,000 in Volhynia, 30,000 to 40,000 in Eastern Galicia and at least 4,000 in other regions. The same Polish estimates put the Ukrainians killed by Polish hands at about 10,000 to 12,000. Ukrainian historians count differently in both directions, giving roughly 40,000 to 60,000 Polish dead and 17,000 to 24,000 Ukrainian. Nobody has a register of the dead. The villages are gone, most of the graves have never been opened, and every number on this page is an estimate that somebody disputes.',
                'caption' => 'The cross and the figure of the Virgin at Ostrowki, where the church stood',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Glaube, 2014, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'bandera',
                'photo' => 'lviv',
                'label' => 'That one name',
                'year' => '1943 - today',
                'title' => 'Why Bandera is the name Poles say',
                'body' => 'Bandera was in German custody through all of 1943, so he did not give the order for 11 July. In Volhynia the orders came from Klym Savur. At the third OUN congress in August 1943 the movement\'s new leader inside Ukraine, Roman Shukhevych, who also commanded the UPA, backed him and carried the same action into Eastern Galicia. The Institute of National Remembrance puts it plainly. Bandera\'s personal responsibility for the murders was very limited or none, and his moral responsibility is complete, because the killing was done by the organisation he built, within the revolution he had planned, and he never condemned it. Polish took his name for the men who did it: banderowcy.',
                'caption' => 'The monument to Stepan Bandera in Lviv, photographed in 2022',
                'credit' => 'Photo: SERP75DNIPRO, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'memory',
                'photo' => 'search',
                'label' => 'Kept quiet',
                'year' => '1945 - 2003',
                'title' => 'Forty years when nobody was allowed to ask',
                'body' => 'Communist Poland prosecuted none of it. The killings had happened on land that was now Soviet, and the alliance with Moscow did not allow the subject, so survivors talked at home and nowhere else. In 1947 the same government deported 140,575 Ukrainians and Lemkos, a Carpathian people, from the south-east of the new Poland and scattered them across the former German lands, in an operation codenamed Vistula. The surviving Poles had already been moved west into the new Poland. After 1989 the subject came back. Aleksander Kwasniewski and Leonid Kuchma, the presidents of Poland and Ukraine, unveiled a monument together at Pavlivka, the village that had been Poryck, on 11 July 2003.',
                'caption' => 'Searching for the graves of the murdered at Ostrowki, Volhynia, 2014',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Glaube, 2014, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'hero',
                'label' => 'Where to go',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'Two states, one word, and graves still shut',
                'body' => 'The Polish parliament called it ethnic cleansing bearing the marks of genocide in 2013 and genocide in 2016, and made 11 July a day of national remembrance. Ukraine does not use that word and speaks of a wartime tragedy between two peoples. Ukraine also barred Polish searches and exhumations from 2017, lifted the ban in November 2024, and the first dig followed at Puzhnyky in April 2025, where the remains of at least 42 people were found. In Warsaw the memorial stands on Skwer Wolynski below the Citadel. If you hear somebody here say banderowcy, this is what is behind it.',
                'caption' => 'The Volhynia memorial on Skwer Wolynski in Warsaw, on 11 July 2022',
                'credit' => 'Photo: gov.pl, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0 PL',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A colour military map sheet of 1928 covering Luck and the country around it, printed in Polish, showing towns, villages, rivers, marsh and the roads and railways between them',
            'caption' => 'Volhynia before any of this: sheet 77, Luck, of the Operational Map of Poland at 1:300,000, drawn by the Military Geographical Institute in Warsaw in 1928, in Polish. Many of the places named on it are villages that no longer exist.',
            'credit' => 'Wojskowy Instytut Geograficzny, Warsaw 1928, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'This page is built on the Institute of National Remembrance, which runs a separate portal on the Volhynian crime with a database of victims, on the Polish state history service dzieje.pl, and on published academic historians on both sides. It is deliberately explicit about disagreement, because the disagreement is part of the subject. The census figures for the Wolyn voivodeship in 1931 are the published census itself: 2,085,574 inhabitants, 68 per cent giving Ukrainian as the language of the home, 16.6 per cent Polish and 9.9 per cent Yiddish or Hebrew. The 1931 form asked for language and religion rather than nationality, which is why this page says language of the home. The Volhynian experiment of Henryk Jozewski, governor from 1928 to 1938, the military settlement, the hardening after the death of Jozef Pilsudski in 1935 and the demolition of between 91 and 127 Orthodox churches in the Chelm region in 1938 are from dzieje.pl and the historian Jan Jacek Bruski, who also explains why the OUN-B chose Volhynia: the Ukrainian numerical advantage and the ground for partisan war. The pacification of Ukrainian villages in Galicia in 1930 is named here without a casualty figure, because the ones in circulation are not agreed. The OUN was founded in Vienna in 1929, and its decalogue, the trial statement about millions of victims, the assassination of Bronislaw Pieracki on 15 June 1934, the death sentence commuted to life, the release in September 1939 and the split of 1940 into the Melnyk and Bandera wings are all from the study of Bandera written by Miroslaw Szumilo for the institute. The murder of the school director Ivan Babii five weeks after Pieracki is in the same study and is left out of the frames for want of room. That Bandera was arrested in July 1941 after the Lviv proclamation of 30 June and held until 27 September 1944, latterly in the Zellenbau block at Sachsenhausen and in privileged conditions, is the same study and dzieje.pl. The letter he wrote from captivity to the Nazi ideologist Alfred Rosenberg offering cooperation is in Szumilo and is not printed in the frames. The founding date of the UPA is given as 14 October 1942 in Ukrainian tradition and is treated by historians as the date of a first unit rather than of an army, with the real formation in the winter and spring of 1943, which is why both are stated. Parosla on 9 February 1943 and the figure of at least 155 dead are the institute. The commemorative plaque at the site says 173, and the institute figure is printed because it is the institution speaking. The desertion of the Ukrainian auxiliary police to the UPA in March and April 1943 is the institute, which also records that those men had taken part in the murder of the Jews of Volhynia in 1942. No total for the Jews of Volhynia is printed here, because that belongs to the article on the occupation and the Holocaust, which tells it properly. The order of Dmytro Kliachkivskyi, known as Klym Savur, passed to his commanders in June 1943 for the physical liquidation of the Polish population, is quoted by Ewa Siemaszko in the institute\'s own publication. The two counts for 11 July 1943 are both the institute and they do not agree. Pawel Nalezniak gives 97 villages and colonies with 13 estates and more than 3,100 dead, an earlier institute release gives 99 villages and about 8,000 dead. Both are printed rather than one, because choosing between them would be pretending to a precision nobody has. The 67 places and at least 2,020 dead on 12 July, and the village figures for Poryck, Chrynow and Kisielin, are Nalezniak. The total of more than 830 villages attacked and around twenty thousand killed in July and August 1943 is the same article. The order of 22 April 1943 by Kazimierz Babinski, the roughly 180 self-defence centres of which about 142 were in Volhynia, Przebraze under Henryk Cybulski and the fall of Huta Stepanska between 16 and 18 July 1943 are the institute and the academic literature on the self-defence. The register of Ukrainians who saved Poles is Kresowa ksiega sprawiedliwych, compiled by Romuald Niedzielko and published by the institute in 2007: more than 1,300 Ukrainians in 500 localities, over 2,500 Poles saved, several hundred of the rescuers killed for it by the people carrying out the anti-Polish action. That book is on this page because leaving it off would turn a description of what one organisation did into an accusation against a nation. Sahryn on 10 March 1944 is given three different ways by three historians, 150 to 300 by institute historians, 234 named by Mariusz Zajaczkowski and more than 600 by Ihor Halahida, and all three are printed. Pawlokoma on 3 March 1945 and the figure of 365 are the published literature on that crime. Operation Vistula in 1947 and the figure of 140,575 people deported are the institute. The casualty estimates are the hardest part of this page and are set out as two sets rather than one. Polish scholarship, summarised on the institute portal, gives about 100,000 Polish dead, 40,000 to 60,000 in Volhynia, 30,000 to 40,000 in Eastern Galicia and at least 4,000 elsewhere, and 10,000 to 12,000 Ukrainians killed by Polish hands. Ukrainian historiography, summarised on the same portal, gives roughly 40,000 to 60,000 Polish dead and 17,000 to 24,000 Ukrainian. Grzegorz Motyka, the Polish historian who has worked longest on this, gives about 100,000 Polish dead and 10,000 to 15,000 Ukrainian and argues that the action meets the definition of genocide, because it aimed at destroying a group defined by ethnicity. Ihor Iliushyn writes of a Volhynian tragedy, and Volodymyr Viatrovych of a second Polish-Ukrainian war continuing the fighting of 1918 and 1919. Those positions are attributed here rather than reconciled. On Bandera the page follows the institute word for word. His personal responsibility for the murders of Poles was very limited or none, because he was in German hands, and his moral responsibility is complete, because the crimes were committed by the organisation he led within the national revolution he had planned, and he never condemned them. Roman Shukhevych, who became leader of the OUN-B inside Ukraine and commander of the UPA in 1943, backed Kliachkivskyi at the third OUN congress in August 1943 and extended the action to Eastern Galicia, which is Motyka. The title Hero of Ukraine given to Bandera by President Viktor Yushchenko in January 2010 and struck down by a Ukrainian court in 2011 is documented by the Centre for Eastern Studies in Warsaw and is left out of the frames for want of room. The silence in communist Poland, the resolution of the Sejm of 12 July 2013 calling it ethnic cleansing bearing the marks of genocide, and the resolution of 22 July 2016 calling it genocide and making 11 July a national day of remembrance, are the parliament\'s own record. The 2016 resolution does not state a number of victims, which is why no figure is attributed to it anywhere on this site. The meeting of Aleksander Kwasniewski and Leonid Kuchma at Pavlivka, formerly Poryck, on 11 July 2003 is the Polish presidential archive. The Ukrainian ban on Polish searches and exhumations from 2017, its lifting in November 2024 and the first dig at Puzhnyky between 23 April and 10 May 2025, where the remains of at least 42 people were found, are the Polish Press Agency and the foundation that carried out the work. The earlier exhumations at Ostrowki and Wola Ostrowiecka in 1992, 2011 and 2015, which recovered 674 people, are the institute. No photograph of a dead or a dying person appears on this page. The best known photographs of this crime are of murdered families, and they are not used here, because a reader who came to learn should not be handed that. That most of the victims were killed with farm tools rather than firearms, that people were locked into houses and barns and burned alive, that churches were burned with the congregation inside, and that the attacks were made by UPA units together with the local self-defence squads and the peasants of the neighbouring Ukrainian villages, is Ewa Siemaszko writing for the institute on July 1943 in Volhynia, which also names Chrynow, Poryck and Zablocce among the places attacked during the service. The figure of 580 mixed Polish and Ukrainian families is her separate study on the institute portal. No witness account is quoted anywhere on this page, because none could be checked in this session, and nothing about the violence is printed that is not in an institutional source. Title picture: the Volhynia memorial on Skwer Wolynski in Warsaw, photographed on 11 July 2022, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0 PL.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],
    'ghettos' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The ghettos in occupied Poland, 1939-1942',
            'description' => 'How the ghettos were ordered and what they were: Lodz sealed in 1940, over 400,000 people behind a wall in Warsaw, and the trains to Treblinka.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · The ghettos',
            'title' => 'Eight hundred years',
            'title_accent' => 'ended behind a wall',
            'lede' => 'Nazi Germany had occupied Poland since 1939 and ran the middle of the country as a colony called the General Government. Jews had been living in these lands for about eight hundred years, and on the eve of the war they made up about a tenth of the country\'s citizens. From 1939 German authorities shut them into closed districts inside their own towns, more than a thousand such districts across occupied Europe, and from 1942 emptied those districts into killing centres. Nine frames on who gave the orders, what a ghetto actually was from the inside, who wrote it down at the cost of their lives, and where you can stand today.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'before',
                'photo' => 'before',
                'label' => 'Before the walls',
                'year' => 'to 1939',
                'title' => 'One Polish citizen in ten was Jewish',
                'body' => 'Jews had been settling in Polish lands since the twelfth century, invited by rulers who wanted traders and craftsmen and protected from 1264 by a charter that a duke of Greater Poland, Boleslaw the Pious, granted them at Kalisz. They stayed for some eight hundred years. The United States Holocaust Memorial Museum puts the community at about three million people in 1933, around 9.5 per cent of the country\'s citizens and the largest in Europe. In Warsaw the Warsaw Ghetto Museum counts nearly 370,000 Jews in 1939, a little under 30 per cent of the city. In hundreds of country towns they were half the inhabitants or more, and Yiddish was what you heard in the market square.',
                'caption' => 'A crossing in the Jewish business quarter of Warsaw, photographed in 1934',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Willem van de Poll, 1934, Nationaal Archief, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'orders',
                'photo' => 'orders',
                'label' => 'The first orders',
                'year' => '1939',
                'title' => 'The express letter that started it',
                'body' => 'The German invasion of 1 September 1939 was three weeks old when Reinhard Heydrich, the head of the German Security Police, sent an express letter to the commanders of his task forces on 21 September. It ordered Jews cleared out of the countryside into larger towns, by preference towns standing on railway junctions, and a Council of Jewish Elders, a Judenrat, appointed in each one to pass German orders on to their own people. The first ghetto in occupied Poland followed on 8 October 1939 at Piotrkow Trybunalski. On 23 November Hans Frank, the German governor of the General Government, ordered every Jew over ten to wear a white armband with a blue star.',
                'caption' => 'The armband German orders required in the General Government, shown at POLIN',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Simon Burchell, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'lodz',
                'photo' => 'lodz',
                'label' => 'Litzmannstadt',
                'year' => '1940 - 1944',
                'title' => 'The first big ghetto, and a bet on work',
                'body' => 'Lodz, a textile city west of Warsaw, was annexed to Nazi Germany outright and renamed Litzmannstadt after a German general of the First World War, Karl Litzmann. Its ghetto was sealed on 30 April 1940 with about 160,000 people inside, and it was the last ghetto left in German-occupied Poland. Its Jewish council was led by Chaim Rumkowski, who staked everything on making the place too useful to destroy, and by July 1942 it ran 74 workshops sewing uniforms for the German army. It saved nobody: Germany sent about 70,000 Jews and 4,300 Roma to the killing centre at Chelmno in 1942 and the last 67,000 to Auschwitz in August 1944. Historians still argue about Rumkowski.',
                'caption' => 'Ghetto money issued in Litzmannstadt, signed by the Eldest of the Jews',
                'credit' => 'Photo: LGLou, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'warsaw',
                'photo' => 'warsaw',
                'label' => 'Warsaw walled in',
                'year' => '1940 - 1942',
                'title' => 'Four hundred thousand people, three square kilometres',
                'body' => 'The largest ghetto of all was in Warsaw. It was ordered in October 1940 and closed on 16 November, and the wall round it was over three metres high. Inside were more than 400,000 people in about 3.4 square kilometres, an average of 7.2 to a room. The German ration allowed a Jew 181 calories a day, in 1941 people actually ate an average of 1,125, and 83,000 died of hunger and typhus between 1940 and the middle of 1942, on the figures of the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum. The Germans had cut the district in two, and from 26 January 1942 a wooden footbridge carried Jews from the large ghetto to the small one over Chlodna street, which was left to the rest of the city.',
                'caption' => 'A ghetto boundary wall still standing at 11 Walicow street in Warsaw',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'inside',
                'photo' => 'inside',
                'label' => 'How it worked',
                'year' => '1940 - 1942',
                'title' => 'A council, a police force and a hole in the wall',
                'body' => 'The Germans gave the orders and made Jews carry them out. The Judenrat ran the bread, the sanitation and the labour lists, and in Warsaw its chairman, an engineer named Adam Czerniakow, kept a diary of being asked for quotas of his own people. Under it stood the Jewish Order Service, an unarmed ghetto police force of several thousand men. Food came over the wall rather than through the gate, much of it carried by children small enough to squeeze through a gap. Workshops sewed for German firms because a work card was a reason for the Germans to leave you alive, and schools and concerts went on illegally in flats.',
                'caption' => 'An armband of a Jewish council, in the State Museum at Majdanek in Lublin',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adam Jones, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'towns',
                'photo' => 'towns',
                'label' => 'The small towns',
                'year' => '1939 - 1942',
                'title' => 'Most ghettos were nothing like Warsaw',
                'body' => 'Warsaw and Lodz are the picture everyone has, and they were the exception. The United States Holocaust Memorial Museum counts more than 1,300 ghettos in all, about 1,100 of them set up by German authorities, almost all in occupied Poland, the occupied Baltic states and the occupied Soviet Union. Most were small. Many were open ghettos, a few streets a Jew was forbidden to leave rather than a district behind a wall, and plenty of them lasted months rather than years. Piotrkow Trybunalski, where the first one was ordered, is a town of exactly that size, and its Great Synagogue is still standing.',
                'caption' => 'The Great Synagogue in Piotrkow Trybunalski, the town of the first ghetto',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Witia, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'trains',
                'photo' => 'trains',
                'label' => 'The liquidation',
                'year' => '1942',
                'title' => 'From July 1942 the ghettos were emptied',
                'body' => 'Shutting Jews into ghettos and killing them were separate German decisions. Gassing had begun at Chelmno in December 1941. Under Operation Reinhard, the German plan to murder the Jews of the General Government, the ghettos went to the killing centres at Belzec, Sobibor and Treblinka. In Warsaw the emptying ran from 22 July 1942 to 21 September, and some 265,000 people went to Treblinka, a figure the Holocaust Memorial Museum and the Warsaw Ghetto Museum share. Adam Czerniakow refused to sign the deportation notice and killed himself on 23 July. On 5 August the doctor Janusz Korczak and Stefania Wilczynska, who ran his orphanage, walked the children to the loading yard and went too.',
                'caption' => 'The Umschlagplatz monument in Warsaw, on the loading yard at Stawki street',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'archive',
                'photo' => 'archive',
                'label' => 'What survived',
                'year' => '1946 - 1950',
                'title' => 'Ten tin boxes and two milk cans',
                'body' => 'The people shut inside knew and wrote it down. In November 1940 the historian Emanuel Ringelblum started a secret group in the Warsaw ghetto, Oneg Shabbat, the joy of the Sabbath, which gathered some 35,000 documents. Ten tin boxes went under 68 Nowolipki street in the summer of 1942 and two milk cans in February 1943, and they were dug out of the rubble in September 1946 and December 1950. A third cache has never been found. UNESCO put the archive on its Memory of the World register in 1999. The Lodz ghetto kept a chronicle day by day, and photographs were taken in secret. What was left of the Warsaw ghetto rose in April 1943, and that has an article of its own here.',
                'caption' => 'Boxes and milk cans that held the Ringelblum archive under the Warsaw ghetto',
                'credit' => 'Photographer unknown, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'Where to stand',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'The line is still there in the pavement',
                'body' => 'Start at POLIN, the Museum of the History of Polish Jews, which stands on ground the Warsaw ghetto covered and tells a thousand years of Jewish life here, of which the murder is one gallery of eight. From there the boundary is marked in the pavement at more than twenty points, a strip of cast iron reading Mur getta 1940 and Ghetto wall 1943. The Umschlagplatz monument is on Stawki street. In Lodz the goods station at Radegast keeps its wagons and the lists of who was loaded into them, and the Jewish cemetery holds the Ghetto Field. These are burial grounds. Dress as you would for a funeral, and do not photograph yourself there.',
                'caption' => 'The ghetto boundary marked in the pavement on Chlodna street in Warsaw',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A printed street plan of central Warsaw headed PLAN DZIELNICY ZYDOWSKIEJ, with a heavy black line drawn right round the Jewish quarter, thin lines for the tram routes, small circles marking the streets you could enter by, and two cemeteries hatched on the left',
            'caption' => 'Plan dzielnicy zydowskiej, the plan of the Jewish quarter, printed in Polish on Tuesday 15 October 1940 in Nowy Kurjer Warszawski, a Warsaw daily that appeared under German occupation. The heavy line is the boundary and the circles are the entry streets.',
            'credit' => 'Nowy Kurjer Warszawski, 15 October 1940, in Polish, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The institutions behind this page are the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, the Warsaw Ghetto Museum, the Jewish Historical Institute in Warsaw, which holds the Ringelblum archive, the European Holocaust Research Infrastructure and the Polish state history portal dzieje.pl. That Jews numbered about three million in Poland in 1933, around 9.5 per cent of its citizens and the largest Jewish population in Europe, is the Holocaust Memorial Museum. Figures of about 3.3 million are often quoted for 1939 and are not printed here, because none of the institutions opened for this page publishes one. The eight hundred years are counted from the twelfth century settlement, which is a conventional starting point rather than a documented date. The charter granted at Kalisz on 16 August 1264 by Boleslaw the Pious, duke of Greater Poland, which took the Jews of his duchy under his own protection and forbade the accusation of ritual murder, is the Virtual Shtetl portal of POLIN, the Museum of the History of Polish Jews. The nearly 370,000 Jews in Warsaw in 1939, a little under 30 per cent of the city, are the Warsaw Ghetto Museum. The express letter Reinhard Heydrich sent on 21 September 1939, its order to concentrate Jews from the countryside in larger towns chosen for their railway junctions, and its instruction to set up Councils of Jewish Elders, are documented by the European Holocaust Research Infrastructure from the original in the German Federal Archives, file R58/954. The same document separates the stages from what it calls the final aim, and that wording is not glossed here. That the first ghetto in occupied Poland was created at Piotrkow Trybunalski on 8 October 1939 is the Warsaw Ghetto Museum, which calls it the first in Europe, and the Holocaust Memorial Museum names the same town and the same month without the day. The armband decree of 23 November 1939, signed by Hans Frank, covering every Jew over the age of ten and requiring a white armband with a blue star on the right upper sleeve, is the Holocaust Memorial Museum. The Warsaw Ghetto Museum dates the obligation from December 1939, which is when it came into force, and the two are not in conflict. Lodz sealed on 30 April 1940 with about 160,000 people, the renaming of the city after the German general Karl Litzmann, Chaim Rumkowski at the head of the Jewish council, the strategy of making the ghetto indispensable through work, the 74 workshops running by July 1942 and the deportation of about 67,000 people to Auschwitz in August 1944 are all the Holocaust Memorial Museum. The deportations from Lodz to the German killing centre at Chelmno, about 70,000 Jews and 4,300 Roma between January and September 1942, are the same source. That Lodz was the last ghetto left in German-occupied Poland is the Holocaust Memorial Museum, and that is the claim printed here rather than the last in Europe, because the ghetto the Arrow Cross regime shut Jews into in Budapest in November 1944 outlasted it and stood until Soviet troops took the city in February 1945, which is the same source. Whether Rumkowski bought time or handed the Germans their lists is left here as an argument, because it is one. His speech of September 1942 asking the ghetto to give up its children is not quoted, because a quotation of that weight needs a critical edition and none was opened. The Warsaw ghetto ordered in October 1940 and closed on 16 November 1940 is the Warsaw Ghetto Museum, which also lists the streets the boundary ran along. The Holocaust Memorial Museum gives the area as 1.3 square miles, which is the 3.4 square kilometres printed here, the density of 7.2 people to a room, the German ration of 181 calories a day, the actual 1941 average of 1,125 calories and the 83,000 dead of starvation and disease between 1940 and the middle of 1942. The same source describes the wall as over ten feet high, which is the three metres printed here. The footbridge over Chlodna street at the corner of Zelazna, opened on 26 January 1942 to join the large and the small ghetto, is the Warsaw Ghetto Museum, which notes that a symbolic reconstruction stands there now and that Chlodna street itself was left outside the ghetto. The frame says only that the Germans had cut the district in two, without the date, because the order that did it is dated to December 1941 by the texts on the city boundary markers and by no institution opened for this page. Adam Czerniakow, the engineer who chaired the Warsaw Jewish council and killed himself on 23 July 1942 after refusing to sign the deportation notice, is both museums. The Jewish Order Service is described here as unarmed and several thousand strong without a figure, because the figures published for it vary. The children who carried food through gaps in the wall, the illegal schools and concerts and the workshops working for German firms are general to the literature and no count is claimed for them. That Hans Frank made leaving the ghetto punishable by death from 15 October 1941, for the Jew who left and for anyone who knowingly hid one, belongs to the article on the occupation and the Holocaust, where this site already carries it, and it is not repeated in these frames. The Roma deported from Lodz to Chelmno appear here only in that figure, because the frames had no room for the rest of what was done to them, and it is a story this site still owes them. More than 1,300 ghettos in all, of which about 1,100 were set up by German authorities, almost all of them in occupied Poland, the occupied Baltic states and the occupied Soviet Union, and the distinction between enclosed ghettos, open ghettos and destruction ghettos, are the Holocaust Memorial Museum, which also dates the liquidations to 1941 to 1944, puts most of them in 1942 and 1943 and makes Lodz the last, in the summer of 1944. Operation Reinhard and the camps at Belzec, Sobibor and Treblinka belong to the article on the occupation and the Holocaust and are only named here. That the gassing had begun before the ghettos were emptied is the Holocaust Memorial Museum, which dates the first transport to the German killing centre at Chelmno to 7 December 1941, about 700 Jewish men, women and children from the nearby town of Kolo, murdered the next day. The Warsaw deportation of 22 July to 21 September 1942 and the figure of about 265,000 people taken to Treblinka are the Holocaust Memorial Museum and the Warsaw Ghetto Museum, which puts it at 75 per cent of the ghetto. Counts between 250,000 and 300,000 circulate, and the number both institutions publish is the one printed. The roughly 35,000 people killed inside the ghetto during the same operation are the Holocaust Memorial Museum and are left out of the frames for want of room. Janusz Korczak was born Henryk Goldszmit and ran the orphanage called Dom Sierot, and on 5 August 1942 he walked from Sienna 16 to the Umschlagplatz with the children and with Stefania Wilczynska, the head teacher, which is the Warsaw Ghetto Museum. The number of children is given as about 150 by dzieje.pl, other accounts say nearly two hundred, and no number is printed here. That Emanuel Ringelblum founded Oneg Shabbat in November 1940, that the archive holds about 35,000 documents including more than 300 drawings, that ten tin boxes were buried at 68 Nowolipki street in the summer of 1942 and two milk cans at the same address in February 1943, that a third part was hidden near 34 Swietojerska street on 19 April 1943 and has never been found, that the boxes were recovered in September 1946 and the cans by accident in December 1950, and that the archive has been on the UNESCO Memory of the World register since 1999, is the Jewish Historical Institute in two places on its own site. The Chronicle of the Lodz ghetto and the photographs taken in secret are named without figures, because the counts published for them vary and none was verified here. No photograph of a dead or dying person, of a starving child or of anyone being humiliated appears on this page. Most surviving photographs of ghetto streets were taken by German propaganda companies and by German police, of people who were in no position to refuse, and this page does not reprint them. The pictures here are objects, documents, buildings and memorials instead. The Warsaw ghetto uprising of April 1943 is named and not told, because it has an article of its own in this series. The markers of the ghetto boundary in Warsaw are given here as more than twenty rather than as a count. The city marked twenty-one places between April and November 2008 and unveiled a twenty-second at Sienna street on 27 January 2010, and no institution opened for this page publishes the present total, so no number is printed. The cast-iron strip in the pavement reads Mur getta 1940 and Ghetto wall 1943. POLIN, the Museum of the History of Polish Jews, stands on land the ghetto covered, and its core exhibition tells a thousand years of Jewish life in these lands in eight galleries, one of which, curated by the historians Barbara Engelking and Jacek Leociak, is the Holocaust. That is the museum\'s own description of the gallery and of the exhibition. The Radegast goods station in Lodz and the Ghetto Field in the Jewish cemetery there are named as places to go, with no opening hours, because opening hours change and none were checked. The plan of the quarter reproduced here was printed in Polish in Nowy Kurjer Warszawski on 15 October 1940, which the sheet itself states in its masthead. Library catalogues and the general literature describe that paper as a propaganda daily the German occupation authorities published in Polish, and no museum or institute page saying so was opened for this article, so the caption claims only that it came out in Warsaw under the occupation. Title picture: the Radegast station in Lodz, the siding from which the people of the ghetto were deported, photographed by Kuroczynski, Wikimedia Commons, CC0.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],
    'camps' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Death camps Nazi Germany built in Poland',
            'description' => 'The six German Nazi killing sites on occupied Polish soil, what was done in them, who told the world, and how to visit these places today.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · The death camps',
            'title' => 'Nazi Germany built the killing',
            'title_accent' => 'where the victims lived',
            'lede' => 'From the summer of 1942 the ghettos of occupied Poland were emptied, and this page is where the trains went. Nazi Germany had decided to murder the Jews of Europe and carried that decision out mostly on ground it had taken from Poland, because the largest Jewish population in Europe lived here and because it was a long way from anyone in Germany who might have looked. Nearly all of it happened in six places. Nine frames on how the decision was taken, what a transport actually was, who got out and told the world, who fought back, and how to stand at these places now.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'decision',
                'photo' => 'decision',
                'label' => 'The decision',
                'year' => '1941 - 1942',
                'title' => 'The killing moved indoors, and it moved here',
                'body' => 'Through 1941 Nazi Germany murdered Jews by shooting, mostly by SS and police units behind the eastern front. Then it moved indoors. At Chelmno, in the part of Poland Germany had annexed outright, the first transport arrived on 7 December 1941 and everyone on it was killed the next day in the backs of sealed vans, with the exhaust piped in. Six weeks later, on 20 January 1942, Reinhard Heydrich, head of the German Security Police, sat fifteen officials down at Wannsee in Berlin. The United States Holocaust Memorial Museum is clear that nothing was decided there. It coordinated a decision already taken, and the camps went up in occupied Poland because that is where the victims were.',
                'caption' => 'The monument at Chelmno, where the gas vans were first used in December 1941',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Kolanin, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'reinhard',
                'photo' => 'reinhard',
                'label' => 'Operation Reinhard',
                'year' => '1942 - 1943',
                'title' => 'Three camps built for nothing but killing',
                'body' => 'In 1942 the SS opened three more camps, all inside the General Government, the colony Nazi Germany had made of central Poland. Belzec began mass killing on 17 March, Sobibor followed in the spring and Treblinka on 23 July. The plan had a name, Operation Reinhard. An SS general, Odilo Globocnik, ran it from Lublin, and a police captain, Christian Wirth, built and commanded the camps. Nobody was meant to stay in them. Belzec had twenty to thirty German SS and police officials and about a hundred guards trained at Trawniki, and it killed some 434,500 people. Operation Reinhard murdered about 1.7 million Jews in under two years, on the figures of the Holocaust Memorial Museum.',
                'caption' => 'Belzec: stones over the graves, and the names of the towns people came from',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Dreamcatcher25, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'transport',
                'photo' => 'transport',
                'label' => 'What a transport was',
                'year' => '1942 - 1943',
                'title' => 'From the wagon door to the fire in two hours',
                'body' => 'Treblinka had a building on its platform dressed as a small railway station, with a wooden clock, invented timetables and signs for onward trains, so that several thousand frightened people kept walking forward. They were told this was a transit camp and that they would wash before travelling on. A fenced path the SS called the tube ran to a room labelled as showers, where a diesel engine pumped its exhaust inside. Almost everyone on a train was dead within about two hours. The exception was the Sonderkommando, prisoners kept alive to empty the chambers and burn the bodies, then shot and replaced every few months. One who got out, Samuel Willenberg, lived to record his testimony.',
                'caption' => 'Treblinka: the cast basalt slab over the ground where the bodies were burned',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'auschwitz',
                'photo' => 'auschwitz',
                'label' => 'Auschwitz-Birkenau',
                'year' => '1940 - 1945',
                'title' => 'A camp for Poles that became the largest grave',
                'body' => 'Auschwitz did not begin as a killing centre. The SS opened it in June 1940 in a former Polish army barracks outside the town of Oswiecim, for Polish political prisoners, and the first transport of them arrived on 14 June 1940. In October 1941 the SS started building Birkenau on the fields next door, at first for Soviet prisoners of war. From March 1942 trains ran to its ramp, where an SS doctor waved most of each trainload straight to the gas chambers and the rest into the camp. The Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum counts about 1.1 million dead: around a million Jews, some 70,000 Poles, about 21,000 Roma and Sinti, and around 15,000 Soviet prisoners of war.',
                'caption' => 'The gatehouse at Birkenau and the track that was laid in through it',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Ingo Mehling, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'majdanek',
                'photo' => 'majdanek',
                'label' => 'Majdanek and the rest',
                'year' => '1941 - 1944',
                'title' => 'A camp on the edge of a working city',
                'body' => 'Majdanek was not hidden in a forest. It stood on the edge of Lublin, in plain sight of the road, and it did several jobs at once: a labour camp, a place of detention, a warehouse for the clothes of the Reinhard dead, and from October 1942 a site with gas chambers. Polish civilians, Soviet prisoners of war, Roma and Jews were held there together. Then on 3 and 4 November 1943 the SS shot the Jewish labourers it still had in the Lublin district, at Majdanek and at the camps of Trawniki and Poniatowa. About 42,000 people died in two days, in what the Germans called Operation Harvest Festival and the Holocaust Memorial Museum calls the largest German massacre of the Holocaust.',
                'caption' => 'The mausoleum at Majdanek, a dome over the ashes, with a guard tower beside it',
                'credit' => 'Photo: MesserWoland, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'world',
                'photo' => 'world',
                'label' => 'Telling the world',
                'year' => '1942 - 1944',
                'title' => 'Men got out, and were believed too slowly',
                'body' => 'Witold Pilecki, a Polish officer, let a German round-up take him in September 1940 so as to reach Auschwitz, built a conspiracy there, sent reports to the Polish underground and escaped on the night of 26 April 1943. He gets his own article here. Jan Karski, a courier of that underground, was smuggled into the Warsaw ghetto in late 1942, watched Jews loaded into wagons for Belzec, and told the British foreign secretary Anthony Eden and President Franklin Roosevelt in July 1943. On 7 April 1944 Rudolf Vrba and Alfred Wetzler escaped from Auschwitz and wrote down how it worked. The report reached the West that June. The Allied answer was that winning the war came first.',
                'caption' => 'The Jan Karski bench in Warsaw, outside the museum of Polish Jewish history',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Cybularny, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'revolts',
                'photo' => 'revolts',
                'label' => 'The revolts',
                'year' => '1943 - 1944',
                'title' => 'Three times the work squads rose anyway',
                'body' => 'The prisoners kept alive to work knew they would be killed for knowing it, so they planned. At Treblinka on 2 August 1943 they took weapons from the armoury and over three hundred got through the fence, although two thirds were hunted down and shot. At Sobibor on 14 October 1943 Leon Feldhendler, one of the prisoners there, and Alexander Pechersky, a Soviet army officer who had reached the camp three weeks earlier, led a revolt that killed eleven of the German staff and put about three hundred prisoners into the woods. Fifty-eight of them lived to the end of the war. At Birkenau on 7 October 1944 the Sonderkommando blew up crematorium IV, and around 250 of them died.',
                'caption' => 'Sobibor: wreaths laid at the mound of ash on the site of the camp',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Hajog, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'evidence',
                'photo' => 'evidence',
                'label' => 'Erasing it',
                'year' => '1943 - 1945',
                'title' => 'What they could not bury under a field',
                'body' => 'When the Reinhard camps had done their work the SS pulled them down, dug the graves open, burned what was in them and sowed crops on top. It had a code name, Aktion 1005, and Heinrich Himmler, the head of the SS, gave it to an officer named Paul Blobel. Treblinka came down in the autumn of 1943 and its last prisoners were made to wipe it off the map, then shot. It failed. Much of the evidence went and by no means all of it, as the Holocaust Memorial Museum puts it. Nazi Germany had counted everything, British intelligence read its telegrams, and the Sonderkommando buried manuscripts at Birkenau. Soviet troops reached Auschwitz on 27 January 1945 and found about 7,000 people alive.',
                'caption' => 'A German coded message of January 1943 counting the dead of Operation Reinhard',
                'credit' => 'Telegram signed by Hermann Hoefle, The National Archives, Kew, HW 16/23, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'Where to stand',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'Five places to go, and one thing to get right',
                'body' => 'Auschwitz-Birkenau at Oswiecim costs nothing to enter, but you reserve an entry card in advance at visit.auschwitz.org, and the museum says it does not recommend the visit for children under fourteen. Majdanek stands inside Lublin and you can walk straight in. Treblinka, Belzec and Sobibor are memorials in open country a couple of hours by road from Warsaw or Lublin, and they are very quiet. All five are graves and cemeteries. Dress and behave as you would at a funeral, take no smiling photographs of yourself, and do not climb on anything. One more thing, and Poles will thank you for it. These were German Nazi camps in occupied Poland. They were never Polish camps.',
                'caption' => 'The international monument at Birkenau, between the ruins of the crematoria',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Jorge Lascar, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A black and white photograph of a wall chart headed CONCENTRATION CAMPS: a printed map of Germany and central Europe covered in dark dots, with pasted paper labels round the edge, among them Treblinka, Majdanek and Auschwitz, each joined to its dot by a ruled line',
            'caption' => 'Concentration Camps, the wall map the American prosecution put in evidence on 8 April 1947 at the Pohl trial in Nuremberg, lettered in English. Dots mark the camps across German-occupied Europe, and Treblinka, Majdanek and Auschwitz are labelled along the eastern edge.',
            'credit' => 'OMGUS Military Tribunal, Nuremberg, 8 April 1947, US National Archives, public domain',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The institutions behind this page are the Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum at Oswiecim, the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, The National Archives at Kew and the National Archives of the United States. That the murder of Jews by Nazi Germany began with shooting behind the eastern front and then moved to gassing, that the first transport reached the killing centre at Chelmno on 7 December 1941 and was murdered the next day in gas vans using engine exhaust, and that at least 152,000 Jews and 4,300 Roma died there, is the Holocaust Memorial Museum. That the Wannsee conference of 20 January 1942 was convened by Reinhard Heydrich, that fifteen officials attended and that they did not debate whether to murder the Jews of Europe but how to carry out a decision already taken, is the same museum in the plainest words it uses anywhere. This page does not put a date on the decision itself, because the museum does not either. Operation Reinhard under SS general Odilo Globocnik, with the police captain Christian Wirth building and running the camps, and about 1.7 million Jews murdered in it, is the Holocaust Memorial Museum. The same source gives 17 March 1942 for the start of mass killing at Belzec, about 434,500 Jews killed there, a staff of twenty to thirty German SS and police officials and between ninety and a hundred and twenty guards trained at Trawniki. Sobibor is given here as the spring of 1942 rather than a month, because the museum says May in one article and April in another. Treblinka opened on 23 July 1942 with the first transports from the Warsaw ghetto, and between 870,000 and 925,000 people were killed there, which is the same source. The building on the Treblinka platform disguised as a railway station, with a wooden clock and invented timetables and signs, the fenced path the SS called the tube, the room labelled as showers and the diesel engine that fed it, are the Holocaust Memorial Museum. The two hours are the order of magnitude the same descriptions give and are written here as about two hours rather than as a measured figure. Samuel Willenberg is described as a survivor of the Treblinka revolt by the Holocaust Memorial Museum, which holds his oral history and his memoir. Chil Rajchman left a memoir too and is not named here for want of room. That Auschwitz was founded in June 1940 for Polish political prisoners, and that the first transport of them arrived on 14 June 1940, is the Auschwitz Museum, and the Holocaust Memorial Museum dates the founding the same way. Birkenau begun in October 1941 as a camp for prisoners of war and opened in March 1942 is the Auschwitz Museum. About 1.1 million people murdered at Auschwitz, of whom about a million were Jews, some 70,000 Poles, about 21,000 Roma and Sinti and around 15,000 Soviet prisoners of war, are the Auschwitz Museum. The Holocaust Memorial Museum publishes 70,000 to 75,000 Poles and more than 14,000 Soviet prisoners, so the round figures are the ones printed. Majdanek as a labour camp, a place of detention, a store for the property of the Reinhard dead and a site with gas chambers from October 1942, holding Polish civilians, Soviet prisoners of war and Jews, is the Holocaust Memorial Museum. Operation Harvest Festival on 3 and 4 November 1943 at Majdanek, Trawniki and Poniatowa, about 42,000 Jews shot and the largest German massacre of the Holocaust, is the same museum, which also gives 18,000 shot at Majdanek in a single day. Witold Pilecki letting a Warsaw round-up take him in September 1940, his conspiracy inside the camp and his escape on the night of 26 April 1943 with Jan Redzej and Edward Ciesielski, are the Auschwitz Museum. His reports are named and not quoted, because he has an article of his own in this series. Jan Karski reaching the Warsaw ghetto and a transit camp in late 1942, seeing Jews loaded into freight wagons for Belzec, reporting to the British foreign secretary Anthony Eden and to President Franklin Roosevelt in July 1943, and being told that military victory came first, is the Holocaust Memorial Museum. Rudolf Vrba and Alfred Wetzler escaping on 7 April 1944 and their report circulating in the West between 18 and 22 June 1944 are the same museum, and the Auschwitz Museum describes the same escape and the reports that followed it. The Treblinka revolt of 2 August 1943, the weapons taken from the armoury, more than three hundred escapes and two thirds of those recaptured and killed, is the Holocaust Memorial Museum. The Sobibor revolt of 14 October 1943 led by Leon Feldhendler and Alexander Pechersky, a Soviet prisoner of war who had arrived at the end of September, eleven German staff killed, about three hundred out and fifty-eight alive at the end of the war, is the same museum in two places. The Sonderkommando revolt at Birkenau on 7 October 1944, the destruction of crematorium IV and about 250 dead, is the Holocaust Memorial Museum, which also describes what the Sonderkommando were made to do and how often they were replaced. Aktion 1005, the code name for destroying the forensic evidence, with Heinrich Himmler putting Paul Blobel in charge of it, is the Holocaust Memorial Museum, which adds that much but by no means all of the evidence was destroyed. The dismantling of Treblinka in the autumn of 1943 and the shooting of the prisoners who did it are the same source. The telegram reproduced here was sent under the name of Hermann Hoefle, the SS and police commander in Lublin, on 11 January 1943, and lists arrivals to 31 December 1942 at four places written as L, B, S and T, adding up to 1,274,166. It survives because British code breakers intercepted and decrypted it, and it is held by The National Archives at Kew under HW 16/23. The identification of the four letters with Lublin, Belzec, Sobibor and Treblinka is the reading published by historians and is described here only as a count of the Operation Reinhard dead. That Soviet forces liberated Auschwitz on 27 January 1945 and found about 7,000 survivors there is the Holocaust Memorial Museum. The manuscripts that members of the Sonderkommando buried at Birkenau are named without their authors, because no museum page listing them was opened for this article. The Ringelblum archive of the Warsaw ghetto belongs to the article on the ghettos, where this site tells it properly. No photograph of a corpse, of a person at the moment of death, of a naked or humiliated prisoner or of a selection on the ramp appears on this page. The album of photographs taken at Birkenau in 1944 and the German propaganda pictures were made by the perpetrators, of people who could not refuse, and they are not reprinted here. The pictures on this page are memorials, buildings, a document and a map instead. That entry to Auschwitz-Birkenau is free, that an entry card is reserved in advance at visit.auschwitz.org and that the museum does not recommend the visit for children under fourteen is the museum booking site. No opening hours or ticket times are printed, because they change. Title picture: the field of stones at Treblinka, photographed by Tajchman Maria, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],
    'ghettouprising' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The Warsaw Ghetto Uprising, 1943, not 1944',
            'description' => 'The Jewish rising inside the Warsaw ghetto, 19 April to 16 May 1943, told plainly and kept apart from the Warsaw Uprising of 1944.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · The ghetto rising',
            'title' => 'They knew what the trains were',
            'title_accent' => 'and fought anyway',
            'lede' => 'By the autumn of 1942 the Warsaw ghetto was a remnant. Most of the people shut inside it were already dead at Treblinka, and the survivors knew it, because a few had escaped from the camp and walked back to say so. On 19 April 1943 the Germans came to finish the district and were met with gunfire. POLIN calls it the largest armed Jewish revolt of the war and the first urban rising in occupied Europe. One thing to get straight before you read on: there are two Warsaw risings and they are not the same. This is the Jewish one, inside the ghetto, in April 1943. The Warsaw Uprising of August 1944 was the Polish Home Army across the whole city, and it has its own article here. Nine frames on who fought, with what, and what is left to see.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'rump',
                'photo' => 'rump',
                'label' => 'What was left',
                'year' => '1942 - 1943',
                'title' => 'A district of workshops, and nowhere to go',
                'body' => 'Between 22 July and 21 September 1942 the Germans took about 265,000 people out of the Warsaw ghetto and murdered them at the killing centre at Treblinka. What was left was a rump district of German workshops. How many were in it is genuinely disputed. The United States Holocaust Memorial Museum says about 60,000 in one article and 70,000 to 80,000 in another, POLIN in Warsaw says 45,000 to 50,000, Yad Vashem about 50,000, and the Jewish Historical Institute counts some 35,000 registered as workers. They were mostly young and mostly without families. Nobody believed the word resettlement any more.',
                'caption' => 'A surviving stretch of the ghetto wall in a courtyard at 55 Sienna street',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'fighters',
                'photo' => 'fighters',
                'label' => 'Two organisations',
                'year' => '1942 - 1943',
                'title' => 'Two armed groups and almost no weapons',
                'body' => 'The Jewish Combat Organization, the ZOB, was founded on 28 July 1942 by three Zionist youth movements: Ha-Szomer ha-Cair, Dror and Akiba. It nearly died at once. On 3 September the Germans killed two of its leaders, Jozef Kaplan and Szmul Breslaw, and the whole arsenal, five pistols, was lost. It was rebuilt that October with the socialist Bund and the communists in it, under a 24-year-old from Ha-Szomer ha-Cair, Mordechai Anielewicz. A second group formed too, the Jewish Military Union or ZZW, around Pawel Frenkel and Leon Rodal of the Zionist right. Neither had arms. The historian Krzysztof Persak counts ten pistols from the Polish Home Army before January 1943, twenty at most.',
                'caption' => 'A block of the Memorial Route of Jewish Martyrdom and Struggle, on Stawki street',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Cybularny, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'january',
                'photo' => 'january',
                'label' => 'January 1943',
                'year' => '18 - 21 January 1943',
                'title' => 'Four days in January that changed everything',
                'body' => 'At half past six on the morning of 18 January 1943 German units came in to take 8,000 people out, on the orders of the head of the SS, Heinrich Himmler. Anielewicz put his fighters into the column with pistols hidden, and at his signal they opened fire in the street. In another quarter the group led by Cywia Lubetkin fired down on the Germans from the windows of flats. The Germans pulled back, and after four days broke the operation off with 5,000 to 6,500 people taken instead of the rest. The Polish underground drew its own conclusion. Through Arie Wilner, the ZOB emissary to it, the Home Army now handed over fifty pistols, fifty grenades and explosives.',
                'caption' => 'The stone on Zamenhofa street to Arie Wilner of the ZOB, with daffodils on it',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Mateusz Opasinski, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'bunkers',
                'photo' => 'bunkers',
                'label' => 'Under the floors',
                'year' => 'February - April 1943',
                'title' => 'Several hundred bunkers under a doomed district',
                'body' => 'After January nobody reported for a transport. They dug instead. Several hundred shelters went in under cellars and yards, stocked with food and water, the better ones wired for light, plumbed, and joined to the sewers. The command bunker at 18 Mila street had six ways out. Air was the problem, and survivors describe a match that would not stay lit. POLIN makes two things plain. The fighters could not hope to win, and chose to die with weapons in their hands. And it was the choice of ordinary people to sit in the dark rather than walk to the Umschlagplatz, the loading yard the trains left from, that made the German operation take four weeks instead of three days.',
                'caption' => 'A post-war model of the bunker at 18 Mila street, at the Jewish Historical Institute',
                'credit' => 'Photographer unknown, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'april',
                'photo' => 'april',
                'label' => '19 April 1943',
                'year' => '19 - 23 April 1943',
                'title' => 'The Germans walked into empty streets',
                'body' => 'On 19 April 1943, the eve of Passover, more than a thousand Waffen-SS men and police came in to clear the ghetto for good. The streets were empty. The fighters hit them at Gesia and Nalewki and again at Mila and Zamenhofa, and after about two hours the Germans withdrew. The Jewish Historical Institute counts twelve German casualties that first day against one fighter killed. The officer in charge, Ferdinand von Sammern-Frankenegg, was sacked the same day and replaced by an SS general, Jurgen Stroop. On Muranowski square the ZZW under Pawel Frenkel raised two flags, the blue and white Zionist flag and the Polish red and white. Yad Vashem says they flew for almost four days.',
                'caption' => 'The Monument to the Ghetto Heroes on Zamenhofa street, unveiled in April 1948',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Fred Romero, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'burning',
                'photo' => 'burning',
                'label' => 'Burned out',
                'year' => 'Late April - May 1943',
                'title' => 'What he could not win, Stroop burned',
                'body' => 'Stroop would not fight underground, so he set the district alight house by house and drove people up into the open. Into the shelters his men found they threw smoke candles and explosives. POLIN describes what that did to people hiding on the upper floors of burning houses, and many of them jumped into the street. Others went down into the sewers and moved under the city. Almost everything the Germans left about these weeks is Stroop reporting on himself. A photographer went with his staff and took about 115 pictures, and some fifty of them were bound into an album made for Heinrich Himmler. This site does not reprint those photographs, for the reason given at the end.',
                'caption' => 'The ghetto after the fighting, with the church of St Augustine left standing',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Leonard Jabrzemski, 1945, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'mila',
                'photo' => 'mila',
                'label' => 'The last bunker',
                'year' => '8 - 10 May 1943',
                'title' => 'The command died at 18 Mila street',
                'body' => 'On 27 April the ZOB command moved into the largest bunker in the ghetto, at 18 Mila street, dug by smugglers. More than three hundred people were inside. On 8 May somebody gave it away and the Germans ringed the building. The civilians came out, the fighters did not, and the Germans threw in grenades and pumped in gas. Arie Wilner called on the others to take their own lives rather than be taken. About a hundred fighters died there, Anielewicz among them, and about fifteen got out by an exit the Germans had missed. Two days later Symcha Rotem, known as Kazik, brought eighty to a hundred out through the sewers. Marek Edelman, who had commanded five ZOB groups, came out with them.',
                'caption' => 'The mound over the bunker at 18 Mila street, raised from the rubble in 1946',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'after',
                'photo' => 'after',
                'label' => 'After the fire',
                'year' => '1943 - 1944',
                'title' => 'One sentence, a false total, a camp on the rubble',
                'body' => 'At half past eight in the evening of 16 May 1943 Stroop blew up the Great Synagogue on Tlomackie street and closed his report with a line: there is no Jewish residential district in Warsaw any more. He claimed 56,065 Jews caught or killed, and POLIN says that figure is certainly inflated and that nothing better exists. The Holocaust Memorial Museum counts at least 7,000 dead in the fighting or in hiding and about 42,000 deported to camps in the Lublin district, where most were shot that November. Nazi Germany then built a camp on the rubble and called it KL Warschau. Home Army soldiers freed 348 prisoners from it on 5 August 1944, during the Warsaw Uprising, the other rising.',
                'caption' => 'The monument on Anielewicza street to the 348 prisoners freed from Gesiowka',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Dreamcatcher25, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'Where to stand',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'A monument, a mound and a paper flower',
                'body' => 'POLIN, the Museum of the History of Polish Jews, opened on 19 April 2013 on ground the ghetto covered, and it faces the Monument to the Ghetto Heroes across the square. That monument was unveiled on 19 April 1948, sculpted by Nathan Rapoport, and it is faced with Swedish labradorite that the German armaments minister, Albert Speer, had ordered in 1942 for victory monuments. Behind it the Memorial Route runs to the Umschlagplatz, past the mound at 18 Mila street. Willy Brandt knelt on the monument steps on 7 December 1970. Every 19 April people in Warsaw wear a paper daffodil, because Marek Edelman brought yellow flowers here each year until he died in 2009.',
                'caption' => 'POLIN, the Museum of the History of Polish Jews, on ground the ghetto covered',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Gucio10, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'aerial' => [
            'alt' => 'A black and white aerial photograph looking straight down on a city district reduced to rubble: the street grid survives as pale lines, every block between the lines is a field of broken walls, and the round face of the camera clock sits in one corner',
            'caption' => 'The northern part of the ghetto from the air, photographed after 16 May 1943, when the fighting was over and the district had been levelled. The streets survive as lines between fields of rubble. The dial in the corner is the clock of the aircraft camera.',
            'credit' => 'Aerial reconnaissance photograph, after 16 May 1943, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The institutions behind this page are POLIN, the Museum of the History of Polish Jews in Warsaw, the Jewish Historical Institute in Warsaw, the Warsaw Ghetto Museum, the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum and Yad Vashem, together with a 2025 study by the historian Krzysztof Persak of the Institute of Political Studies of the Polish Academy of Sciences, published in Przeglad Historyczno-Wojskowy, on what the Polish Home Army did and did not do. How many Jews were left in the ghetto after the deportations of summer 1942 is not an agreed number, and this page prints the disagreement rather than choosing: the Holocaust Memorial Museum says about 60,000 in its article on Warsaw and 70,000 to 80,000 in its article on the uprising, POLIN gives 45,000 to 50,000 for April 1943, Yad Vashem about 50,000, and the Jewish Historical Institute counts some 35,000 registered in German workshops. That about 265,000 people were deported from Warsaw to Treblinka between 22 July and 21 September 1942 is the Holocaust Memorial Museum and the Warsaw Ghetto Museum, and this site tells that story in its article on the ghettos. The founding of the Jewish Combat Organization on 28 July 1942 by Ha-Szomer ha-Cair, Dror and Akiba, the killing of Jozef Kaplan and Szmul Breslaw on 3 September 1942, the loss of its first five pistols and the rebuilding of the organization that October with the Bund, Poalej Syjon and the communists inside it, are the Jewish Historical Institute, the Warsaw Ghetto Museum and Yad Vashem. No source opened for this page gives a date on which Mordechai Anielewicz became commander, so none is printed. The Jewish Military Union around Pawel Frenkel and Leon Rodal, out of the Revisionist Zionist movement Betar, is the Warsaw Ghetto Museum, the Jewish Historical Institute and Yad Vashem, which date its founding variously to the autumn of 1942, the turn of 1942 and 1943, and January or February 1943, so no month is printed here. Dawid Apfelbaum, named as a commander of that union on a memorial in Warsaw, is not named on this page, because the Jewish Historical Institute reports that historians now regard him as most probably a fictitious figure. The estimates of fighters are 300 to 500 in the Combat Organization according to POLIN and about 500 according to the Holocaust Memorial Museum, and 150 to 260 in the Military Union, and this page therefore gives no total. The weapons are Krzysztof Persak: ten pistols from the Home Army before January 1943, at most twenty, then fifty pistols, fifty grenades and explosives afterwards. Persak also shows that the Home Army chief of staff in Warsaw, Stanislaw Weber, claimed five hundred grenades and machine guns after the war, that Jewish sources and the wartime Home Army documents say fifty, and that Tadeusz Komorowski wrote in 1950 that the Home Army had offered weapons on its own initiative when in fact it did so with great reluctance. Persak concludes that more pistols could probably have been handed over, but that the underground army had few working rifles and little automatic weaponry of its own at the time, and that its early reserve towards the Jewish underground was in some measure antisemitism and in some measure disbelief that Jews would fight. The Warsaw Ghetto Museum separately says the Combat Organization received sixty or seventy pistols from the Home Army in all, which does not agree with Persak, and the disagreement is left standing here rather than averaged. The German operation of 18 January 1943, ordered by Heinrich Himmler and aimed at 8,000 people, Anielewicz opening fire from inside the column, the group led by Cywia Lubetkin firing from windows, the German withdrawal, and 5,000 to 6,500 people taken before the operation was called off, are the Jewish Historical Institute, Yad Vashem and the Holocaust Memorial Museum, which gives 21 January for the suspension. That the four days made the April rising possible is Yitzhak Zuckerman, quoted by Yad Vashem, and POLIN says the same in its own words. The bunkers are POLIN and the Warsaw Ghetto Museum, which report several hundred built and 631 claimed destroyed in German reporting, and Wirtualny Sztetl for the bunker at 18 Mila street with its six exits, water and electricity. That civilian refusal to report for deportation is what made the German operation last four weeks is POLIN, in those words. The German strength on 19 April, more than a thousand Waffen-SS and police in action daily, is POLIN, while the Holocaust Memorial Museum gives around 2,000 soldiers and police in all, and the Jewish Historical Institute prints the tallies Stroop himself kept of his units. The fighting at Gesia and Nalewki and at Mila and Zamenhofa, the German withdrawal after about two hours, twelve German casualties against one fighter killed on the first day, and the removal of Ferdinand von Sammern-Frankenegg and his replacement by Jurgen Stroop the same day, are the Jewish Historical Institute. The two flags on Muranowski square, the blue and white Zionist flag and the Polish red and white, raised by the Military Union, are POLIN, and that they flew for almost four days until 23 April is Yad Vashem. The burning of the district house by house, the smoke candles and explosives thrown into discovered shelters, and people jumping from the upper floors of burning houses, are POLIN. That about 115 photographs were taken by a photographer attached to Stroop, that roughly fifty of them went into a bound album, and that three copies were made, one for Heinrich Himmler, is Yad Vashem. The bunker at 18 Mila street, the ZOB command moving in on 27 April, more than three hundred people inside, the betrayal and encirclement on 8 May, the civilians leaving and the fighters staying, the grenades and gas, the call by Arie Wilner to take their own lives, about a hundred fighters dead including Anielewicz and about fifteen out through a missed exit, are Wirtualny Sztetl, which is published by POLIN, with POLIN and the Warsaw Ghetto Museum agreeing on about a hundred dead. The Jewish Historical Institute prints the claim in Stroop\'s own report of about 140 killed and 60 captured there, which is higher. The escape through the sewers, led by Symcha Rotem, known as Kazik, with Polish sewer workers and Wladyslaw Gaik of the communist Gwardia Ludowa, bringing eighty to a hundred fighters out to a manhole on Prosta street, is the Jewish Historical Institute, which dates it to 10 May, while POLIN dates it to 8 May. Marek Edelman commanding five fighting groups of the Combat Organization at the brushmakers\' works, coming out through the sewers, publishing his account in Lodz in 1945, joining the workers\' defence committee and then Solidarity, and dying on 2 October 2009, is POLIN. The destruction of the Great Synagogue on Tlomackie street at half past eight in the evening of 16 May 1943 and the closing line of Stroop\'s report are POLIN and the Jewish Historical Institute, with Yad Vashem giving the German original. The figure of 56,065 Jews caught or killed is Stroop\'s own, printed by the Jewish Historical Institute, while POLIN gives it as more than 56,000 and states in plain words that his figures are certainly inflated and that no others exist, which is why this page prints no death toll of its own for the fighting. A figure of about 13,000 killed, half of them burned alive, is repeated in many popular accounts and was not found on any of the institutional sites read for this page, so it does not appear here. At least 7,000 dead in the fighting or in hiding, another 7,000 sent to Treblinka and about 42,000 sent to camps in the Lublin district, where most were murdered in November 1943, are the Holocaust Memorial Museum. That the fighters could not hope to win and chose to die with weapons in their hands rather than passively is POLIN, in its own words, and no other claim about what they expected is made here. That shooting was still heard in the ruins in June 1943 and that people hid there into the end of that year is POLIN. The camp built on the rubble, KL Warschau along Gesia street, with more than 2,500 Greek Jews brought from Auschwitz between August and November 1943 to clean bricks, is the Warsaw Ghetto Museum, and that soldiers of the Home Army battalion Zoska freed 348 prisoners there on 5 August 1944 is both that museum and the Holocaust Memorial Museum. The Monument to the Ghetto Heroes unveiled on 19 April 1948, sculpted by Nathan Rapoport with the architecture by Leon Marek Suzin, eleven metres high and faced with Swedish labradorite ordered by Albert Speer in 1942 for German victory monuments, and the mound raised over the bunker at 18 Mila street in 1946 with the small monument added at its foot in 2006 by Hanna Szmalenberg and Marek Moderau, are Wirtualny Sztetl. The marking of the ghetto boundary at 21 points, begun on 19 November 2008 to a design by Eleonora Bergman and Tomasz Lec, is the City of Warsaw, and the wording cast into the pavement strips is not quoted here because no institutional page was found that gives it. POLIN opening to visitors on 19 April 2013, with its main exhibition from 28 October 2014, is POLIN. Willy Brandt kneeling at the monument on 7 December 1970, and the absence of any evidence that the gesture was planned, are Wirtualny Sztetl. The daffodils are POLIN, which explains that Marek Edelman brought yellow flowers to the monument every year and that its own paper daffodil campaign has run since 2013. No count of daffodils or volunteers is printed, because the figures change every year. No map is published on this page. The only plans of the ghetto at its final extent that could be found are the ones drawn inside the German report on its destruction, and the printed plan of the district from 1940 already appears in the article on the ghettos, so a map that would add nothing new was left out. No photograph from the Stroop report appears here, including the photograph of the boy with his hands raised. Those pictures were taken by the men doing the killing, of people who were about to be killed and could not refuse to be photographed, and reprinting them hands the photographer his audience. The pictures on this page are memorials, a surviving wall, a model, two photographs of the ruins and one aerial reconnaissance frame. Title picture: the ruins of the ghetto photographed by Leonard Jabrzemski in 1945, Wikimedia Commons, public domain.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],
    'pilecki' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Witold Pilecki: the volunteer for Auschwitz',
            'description' => 'The Polish officer who let himself be caught in 1940 to get inside Auschwitz, spent two and a half years reporting from it, and was shot in 1948.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · Witold Pilecki',
            'title' => 'He volunteered',
            'title_accent' => 'for Auschwitz',
            'lede' => 'Auschwitz as a killing site is another article here, and so is the underground state he worked for. This page is about one man who went into that camp on purpose, with forged papers, to find out what it was and tell somebody. He is not a saint on this page. He was a farmer and a cavalry officer who took on a job almost nobody would take, got some of it badly wrong, was not listened to, and was killed for something else entirely eight years later. Jan Karski, the courier who carried the same kind of news west, gets the next article. Nine frames.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'before',
                'photo' => 'before',
                'label' => 'Before the war',
                'year' => '1901 - 1939',
                'title' => 'Born in Russia because of a lost rising',
                'body' => 'Witold Pilecki was born on 13 May 1901 in Olonets in northern Russia, because his family had been sent there. The Pileckis were Polish gentry in the eastern borderlands, punished after the January Rising of 1863 against the tsar. In 1910 his mother took the children to Wilno, now Vilnius in Lithuania. He joined the scouts and in December 1918 fought Russia\'s new Bolshevik army at the Ostra Brama gate. He volunteered again when the Red Army came in 1920 and rode back into Wilno with the 211th Uhlan Regiment under Lucjan Zeligowski. Then he farmed: the family estate at Sukurcze from 1926, Maria Ostrowska as his wife from 1931, two children, a fire brigade, a riding club for boys.',
                'caption' => 'Scouts at Orel on the Oka in Russia in 1917, Pilecki seated first from the right',
                'credit' => 'Photo: unknown photographer, 1917, Warsaw Rising Museum collection, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'secret',
                'photo' => 'secret',
                'label' => 'War and conspiracy',
                'year' => '1939 - 1940',
                'title' => 'A division broken, then an army in flats',
                'body' => 'Nazi Germany invaded on 1 September 1939 and Pilecki went to war with the cavalry of the 19th Infantry Division. It was destroyed near Piotrkow Trybunalski on the night of 5 September. He crossed the Vistula and fought on in a squadron of the reserve 41st Infantry Division under Major Jan Wlodarkiewicz, until that division went too. Both reached occupied Warsaw, and on 9 November 1939 were in at the founding of an underground army. Pilecki gave it its name, the Secret Polish Army, in Polish Tajna Armia Polska, and was its chief of staff. From 1940 it worked with the Union of Armed Struggle, the main underground army, answering to the exile government, and in 1941 it went inside it.',
                'caption' => 'The American Julien Bryan filming a Polish officer in Warsaw in September 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Julien Bryan, Warsaw, September 1939, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'volunteer',
                'photo' => 'volunteer',
                'label' => 'Into the camp',
                'year' => 'September 1940',
                'title' => 'He walked into a round-up on purpose',
                'body' => 'The Germans had opened a camp that June at Oswiecim, which they called Auschwitz, in old Polish barracks, and nobody in Warsaw knew what it was for. The Secret Polish Army decided to put a man inside it. Whose idea that was is not settled, and Poland\'s Institute of National Remembrance says so plainly: some accounts have Pilecki volunteering, others have Wlodarkiewicz talking him into it. On 19 September 1940 he let a German round-up in the Zoliborz district of Warsaw take him, carrying papers in the name of a real man, Tomasz Serafinski. He arrived on the night of 21 September and became prisoner 4859. At registration a guard knocked two of his teeth out.',
                'caption' => 'A camp registration photograph of prisoner 4859, made by the SS at Auschwitz in 1940',
                'credit' => 'Photograph: SS camp administration, Auschwitz, 1940, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'inside',
                'photo' => 'inside',
                'label' => 'Inside the wire',
                'year' => '1940 - 1943',
                'title' => 'A conspiracy built in fives',
                'body' => 'He built an organisation in the barracks, the Union of Military Organisation, in Polish Zwiazek Organizacji Wojskowej, in cells of five, so that a prisoner under interrogation could give away no more than four others. It fed people, stole medicine, placed its men in the offices where the camp kept its records, and got word out. The first report reached Warsaw in October 1940, a month after he arrived. General Stefan Rowecki, who commanded the underground army inside Poland, read it and sent it on to London in March 1941. On 11 November 1941 he promoted Pilecki to second lieutenant, inside the camp.',
                'caption' => 'The wire and a warning post at the main camp at Auschwitz, photographed in 2013',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Draceane, 2013, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'escape',
                'photo' => 'escape',
                'label' => 'The escape',
                'year' => 'April 1943',
                'title' => 'Out of the camp bakery before dawn',
                'body' => 'Early in 1943 the Germans started arresting his men. He handed the organisation over to Major Zygmunt Boncza-Bohdanowski and got himself moved to the work detail at the camp bakery, about two kilometres outside the main camp. On the night of 26 April 1943 he and two other prisoners, Jan Redzej and Edward Ciesielski, walked out of it. They made for Bochnia to find the Home Army, as the Union of Armed Struggle was called by then, and were handed to the deputy commander of its post at Nowy Wisnicz. He turned out to be Tomasz Serafinski, the real one, whose name Pilecki had been carrying for three years.',
                'caption' => 'Nowy Wisnicz south of Bochnia, where the escape ended and the first report was written',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Kawior, 2007, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'report',
                'photo' => 'report',
                'label' => 'The report',
                'year' => '1943 - 1945',
                'title' => 'Two million, counted from the inside',
                'body' => 'He stayed in Serafinski\'s house for three months and wrote: a short report in the summer of 1943, then a much longer one in Italy in 1945, over a hundred typed pages. Gas chambers going up at Birkenau, the huge extension the SS built on the fields next door. Over a thousand people killed a day. Women and children walked into barracks they were told were bath houses. He counted about 97,000 dead among the registered prisoners and, from tallies kept by work squads, over two million killed without ever being registered. The museum at the site now puts the total at about 1.1 million. He was counting trains from inside a barrack, and he counted far too high.',
                'caption' => 'The rail spur running into Birkenau, past the block leaders\' guardhouse',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Winfried Laasch, 2019, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'warsaw',
                'photo' => 'warsaw',
                'label' => 'The rising',
                'year' => '1943 - 1945',
                'title' => 'Refused a rescue, he fought as a private',
                'body' => 'Back in Warsaw he asked the Home Army command to attack the camp and free the prisoners. It refused. The Institute of National Remembrance writes that the command saw no chance of such an operation succeeding, and that Pilecki understood the argument put to him. He joined NIE, a deep organisation meant to outlast a Soviet occupation, under Colonel August Emil Fieldorf. Its men were told to stay out of the rising the Home Army launched against the Germans in Warsaw on 1 August 1944. He went in anyway as an ordinary private, gave his rank away when the officers around him were killed, and held a building on Aleje Jerozolimskie that his soldiers called Witold\'s redoubt.',
                'caption' => 'A sentry on Aleje Jerozolimskie during the Warsaw Uprising, 4 August 1944',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Sylwester Braun, 1944, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'trial',
                'photo' => 'trial',
                'label' => 'The trial and the shot',
                'year' => '1945 - 1948',
                'title' => 'Shot in the back of the head at Mokotow',
                'body' => 'The rising lost, he went into German captivity at Oflag VII-A Murnau in Bavaria, and in 1945 to the Polish Second Corps in Italy, a Polish army under British command led by General Wladyslaw Anders. In October 1945 its staff sent him back into Poland under a false name, to report on the government Moscow was installing. He was arrested on 8 May 1947 and beaten through the investigation. His trial opened on 3 March 1948 before a military court in Warsaw under Lieutenant Colonel Jan Hryckowian, once a Home Army officer himself. On 15 March he was sentenced to death, and on 25 May 1948 a prison executioner, Piotr Smietanski, shot him in the back of the head.',
                'caption' => 'Witold Pilecki in the dock at his trial in Warsaw in March 1948',
                'credit' => 'Photo: unknown photographer, March 1948, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'Where to see it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'A cell you can stand in, and a late book',
                'body' => 'The body was never handed over and no grave was ever found. On 1 October 1990 the Supreme Military Court quashed the verdict, and on 5 September 2013 the minister of defence promoted him posthumously to colonel. The prison on Rakowiecka street in Warsaw is now the Museum of Cursed Soldiers and Political Prisoners of the Polish People\'s Republic, and the cell where he waited has been put back. The Institute of National Remembrance digs at a corner of Powazki Military Cemetery for the people killed there, and his remains have not been identified. A state institute in Warsaw set up in 2017 carries his name. His report first appeared in English in 2012.',
                'caption' => 'The reconstructed cell at Rakowiecka 37 in Warsaw where he waited to be shot',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Oeleau, 2025, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The institutions behind this page are the Institute of National Remembrance in Warsaw and its portal Przystanek Historia, the Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum at Oswiecim, the news service dzieje.pl of the Polish Press Agency, the Institute of Solidarity and Valour named after Witold Pilecki, the Home Army Museum in Krakow, the Museum of the Second World War in Gdansk and the Office for War Veterans. The spine of the page is a life by Pawel Sztama published in the Institute of National Remembrance bulletin 5 of 2019 and reprinted on Przystanek Historia, checked against the museum at Oswiecim wherever the two overlap. Birth on 13 May 1901 at Olonets, the family moved east after the January Rising of 1863, the move to Wilno in 1910, the Lelewel gymnasium, the defence of the Ostra Brama gate in December 1918, the volunteering of 1920 and the ride into Wilno with the 211th Volunteer Uhlan Regiment under Lucjan Zeligowski in October 1920, Sukurcze from 1926, the marriage to Maria Ostrowska in 1931 and the Krakus mounted training squadron taken into the 19th Infantry Division in 1937 are all Sztama, with the Office for War Veterans agreeing on the farm and the training squadron. The exact wedding day and the birthplaces of the two children, Andrzej born in 1932 and Zofia in 1933, are given differently in different places, so this page gives neither. The scouting at Orel in Russia in 1916 is not carried by any institutional page found for this article, and it appears here only as what the photograph is, a scout troop at Orel in 1917 from the collection of the Warsaw Rising Museum with Pilecki identified in it. The 19th Infantry Division broken near Piotrkow Trybunalski on the night of 5 and 6 September 1939, the retreat across the Vistula and the squadron of the reserve 41st Infantry Division under Jan Wlodarkiewicz are Sztama. The Secret Polish Army was founded on 9 November 1939, which three separate Institute of National Remembrance texts and dzieje.pl agree on, and the institute adds that it came out of a merger of Wlodarkiewicz\'s group with a group of about three hundred students around Jan Dangiel, which dzieje.pl leaves out, so this page says the two men were in at the founding rather than that they founded it alone. Pilecki invented the name. The institute describes the passage into the Union of Armed Struggle as cooperation from 1940 and absorption in 1941, with no single date, and that is how it is written here. On whose idea the camp mission was, the Institute of National Remembrance states openly that some accounts say Pilecki volunteered and others that Wlodarkiewicz pushed him, and no institutional page found for this article says the Union of Armed Struggle command approved the mission in advance, so this page does not say it either. The round-up in Zoliborz on 19 September 1940, the borrowed papers of Tomasz Serafinski, the arrival on the night of 21 and 22 September 1940 and the number 4859 are confirmed by both the Institute of National Remembrance and the Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, which dates his imprisonment to 22 September 1940. The two teeth knocked out at registration is Pilecki\'s own report as quoted by the institute. The Union of Military Organisation built in cells of five, the tasks it set itself, the first report carried to the Union of Armed Struggle in October 1940, Stefan Rowecki reading it and forwarding it to London in March 1941, and the promotion to second lieutenant on 11 November 1941 are Sztama. The museum at Oswiecim independently names Pilecki as a founder of the camp resistance and confirms that he had himself caught in a Warsaw round-up in September 1940 in order to reach the camp. Two things often told about the camp years are left out because no institutional page found for this article carries them: that Pilecki fell ill with typhus, and that his organisation built and ran a radio transmitter through 1942. The camp typhus epidemic of 1942 and 1943 is documented by the museum, his own case is not, and the radio is asserted only outside the institutions, so neither is printed here. The escape on the night of 26 and 27 April 1943 from the work detail at the camp bakery about two kilometres outside the main camp, with Jan Redzej and Edward Ciesielski, the walk towards Bochnia in search of the Home Army and the meeting at Nowy Wisnicz with the real Tomasz Serafinski, deputy commander of the local post, are the Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum and the Institute of National Remembrance together. The museum also holds the detail that the painter Jan Stasiniewicz painted the two men who had shared one name together in that house. The often repeated escape route through Alwernia and Tyniec is on no institutional page found for this article and is not printed. The report is quoted from the Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, which publishes the passages on gas chambers being built at Birkenau, on over a thousand victims a day and on women and children sent to barracks that were gas chambers, and which prints his own arithmetic: about 97,000 dead among the numbered prisoners and over two million killed without registration, counted, as he wrote, from the daily calculations of work squads working nearby. The museum\'s established figure for the dead at Auschwitz is about 1.1 million, so his estimate was much too high, and this page says so rather than repeating his number as a fact. The refusal of the Home Army command to attack the camp is the museum, which writes that he did not obtain consent for a fight to free the prisoners, and the Institute of National Remembrance, which adds that the command saw no chance of such an action succeeding and that Pilecki understood the reasons given him. Nobody found for this article documents a specific Allied decision taken or refused because of his reports, so this page makes no claim about what they changed in the West. His service in the NIE organisation under August Emil Fieldorf, the order that kept its men out of the Warsaw Uprising, his fighting as a private in the Chrobry II grouping, the redoubt on Aleje Jerozolimskie, Oflag VII-A Murnau, the Second Corps in Italy and the return to Poland in October 1945 are Sztama. The court\'s own wording, quoted by the institute, places his intelligence work in Italy from July 1945 to 8 December 1945 and in Poland to 8 May 1947, the day he was arrested. The trial opened on 3 March 1948 before the military district court in Warsaw under Jan Hryckowian, with Maria Szelagowska, Tadeusz Pluzanski, Makary Sieradzki and four others in the dock. The death sentence was passed on 15 March 1948, not in May, which five separate Institute of National Remembrance and government pages agree on, and the execution by Piotr Smietanski at Mokotow prison on Rakowiecka street followed on 25 May 1948. The Institute of National Remembrance states that he was buried in an unmarked grave and that nobody was ever tried for it. Whether the prime minister Jozef Cyrankiewicz, himself an Auschwitz survivor, intervened for him or against him is claimed both ways outside the institutions and confirmed neither way, so it is not on this page. The quashing of the verdict on 1 October 1990 was by the Supreme Military Court and not by the Supreme Court, and the promotion to colonel is Decision 2156 KADR of the Minister of National Defence of 5 September 2013, a document published in full. He also holds the Order of the White Eagle posthumously, but the year is given inconsistently outside the institutions and no state page carrying the date was reached for this article, so no year is printed. The Institute of Solidarity and Valour named after Witold Pilecki was created by the act of 9 November 2017, which was checked in the official gazette. The search by the Institute of National Remembrance at the Powazki Military Cemetery in Warsaw has not produced an identification of his remains: the archaeologist leading it, Krzysztof Szwagrzyk, has said he believes Pilecki lies there, and has also said the identification is still missing, so this page says only that the remains have not been identified. The English edition of the report, The Auschwitz Volunteer, translated by Jarek Garlinski, was published in Los Angeles in 2012, which is the publisher\'s own record and the library catalogue. No map is published on this page. The escape of April 1943 is the only part of the story that is spatial, no published map of it was found that could be captioned with a maker, a date and a language, and a hand-drawn line across southern Poland would tell the reader nothing the sentence does not, so rather than publish a map that could not be sourced properly, none is published. Title picture: the monument to Witold Pilecki in Warsaw, photographed by Haydn Blackey, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 2.0.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],
    'karski' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Jan Karski: the courier who carried the news out',
            'description' => 'The courier smuggled into the Warsaw ghetto in 1942, who crossed occupied Europe and told Franklin Roosevelt what he had seen with his own eyes.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · Jan Karski',
            'title' => 'He got the news out',
            'title_accent' => 'and was not believed',
            'lede' => 'Witold Pilecki, who had himself imprisoned in Auschwitz to report on it from inside, has the article before this one. Karski did the opposite job. He went into the Warsaw ghetto and into a camp as a witness, then carried what the Polish underground knew out through occupied Europe to London and Washington, where he was heard at the top and nothing was done. This page is nine frames on a courier who memorised his reports instead of writing them down, and on what the West did with the one he brought in 1942.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'before',
                'photo' => 'before',
                'label' => 'Before the war',
                'year' => '1914 - 1939',
                'title' => 'Born Kozielewski, trained for diplomacy',
                'body' => 'Jan Karski was born Jan Kozielewski in Lodz on 24 June 1914, the youngest of six children of a saddler, Stefan Kozielewski, and his wife Walentyna. Lodz was then a mill city where Poles, Jews and Germans lived alongside one another, and the Institute of National Remembrance records that he had Jewish friends at his state secondary school. He read law and then diplomacy at the Jan Kazimierz University in Lwow, came out top of the reserve artillery cadet school in 1936, and joined the foreign ministry, serving in Geneva and in London. On 23 August 1939 a policeman knocked and handed him his mobilisation order.',
                'caption' => 'Jan Kozielewski\'s own diplomatic curriculum vitae, written in Warsaw in 1936',
                'credit' => 'Photograph of a 1936 document: Mieciousa, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'captivity',
                'photo' => 'captivity',
                'label' => 'Taken east',
                'year' => '1939',
                'title' => 'He tore off his officer\'s badges',
                'body' => 'He went to war as a lieutenant of the 5th Horse Artillery Battalion, whose peacetime barracks stood at Oswiecim, the town the Germans would rename Auschwitz. His unit never got into the fighting. On 17 September 1939 the Red Army crossed the border and took him prisoner near Tarnopol. He was sent to a camp at Kozielszczyzna, which the Institute of National Remembrance counts among the eight the Soviets used for Polish prisoners, with Kozelsk, Starobelsk and Ostashkov. Officers from those camps were shot in the spring of 1940. Hearing of a German-Soviet exchange of prisoners, Karski pulled off his insignia and went over as a private.',
                'caption' => 'The cross of the September campaign on the Polish war cemetery at Kharkiv',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Aleksander Martyniak, 2007, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'courier',
                'photo' => 'courier',
                'label' => 'Carrying reports',
                'year' => '1939 - 1940',
                'title' => 'A courier who carried nothing on paper',
                'body' => 'In November 1939 he jumped from the German transport taking him west and reached Warsaw. His elder brother Marian Kozielewski, who commanded the Polish police in the city and was building a conspiracy inside it, brought him into the underground. The government in exile under General Wladyslaw Sikorski sat in France, at Angers, and the resistance at home needed men who could get to it. In January 1940 Karski went out through Krakow, Zakopane, Slovakia, Hungary, Yugoslavia and Italy. He took the alias Jan Karski and kept it for life, and he carried almost nothing written, because he could memorise a report and recite it.',
                'caption' => 'The summit of Kasprowy Wierch in 1939, on the border Poland shared with Slovakia',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Karoly Ofner, 1939, Fortepan collection, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'slovakia',
                'photo' => 'slovakia',
                'label' => 'Caught and cut out',
                'year' => '1940',
                'title' => 'Thirty-two people paid for his rescue',
                'body' => 'On the night of 14 June 1940, on his second run west, Slovak police seized him in the village of Demjata. The man whose cottage he slept in had informed on him. He dropped his microfilm into a bucket of water, but four frames survived and told the German secret police, the Gestapo, who he was. Interrogated at Presov and certain he would break, he cut his wrists. Saved, he was put under guard in the hospital at Nowy Sacz, and on 28 July 1940 the Union of Armed Struggle, the main underground army, took him out of it. The Museum of the History of Poland records that on 21 August 1941 the Germans shot 32 people connected with that rescue.',
                'caption' => 'Plaques in the hospital at Nowy Sacz, the upper one to the men who freed him',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Zalasem1, 2022, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'mission',
                'photo' => 'mission',
                'label' => 'What they asked',
                'year' => '1942',
                'title' => 'Two Jewish leaders gave him a message',
                'body' => 'He spent months hidden in the countryside. In 1942 the underground sent him west again, to London and then Washington. Before he left he met two leaders of the Jewish underground in Warsaw. One was Leon Feiner of the Bund, the Jewish socialist party. The other was a Zionist, named by the Jewish Historical Institute as Adolf Berman of Poale Zion Left and by the Polish foreign ministry as Menachem Kirszenbaum. They told him the Germans were not persecuting Polish Jews but killing all of them, and, in the words the Israeli Holocaust memorial Yad Vashem quotes, that their entire people would be destroyed. They wanted the Allied governments told, and told by a witness.',
                'caption' => 'The German notice of 18 October 1940 ordering a Jewish district in Warsaw',
                'credit' => 'Notice signed by Ludwig Leist, 1940, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'izbica',
                'photo' => 'izbica',
                'label' => 'What he saw',
                'year' => '1942',
                'title' => 'The camp he named was the wrong one',
                'body' => 'Feiner had him smuggled into the Warsaw ghetto twice, and accounts differ over whether that was in August or in October 1942. Then, in the uniform of a Ukrainian guard and with a bribed guard beside him, he walked into a transit camp and watched Jews beaten into goods wagons. Story of a Secret State, the book he wrote in 1944, calls the place Belzec. The Jewish Historical Institute in Warsaw states that this is an error and that the camp was Izbica, a town near Zamosc from which the Germans sent Jews on to Belzec and Sobibor. He got back to a shop nearby, asked for vodka and slept for a day and a half.',
                'caption' => 'A gravestone lying in the Jewish cemetery at Izbica near Zamosc',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Immortablemonster, 2012, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'reports',
                'photo' => 'reports',
                'label' => 'London, Washington',
                'year' => '1942 - 1943',
                'title' => 'Heard in two capitals, believed in neither',
                'body' => 'He crossed Germany, France and Spain and reached London in November 1942. He reported to Sikorski, by then the prime minister in London, and to the British foreign secretary Anthony Eden. On 10 December 1942 the Polish foreign minister Edward Raczynski sent the Allied governments a note on the killing, and on 17 December the Allies declared together that Nazi Germany was exterminating the Jews of Europe. Nothing followed that stopped it. In July 1943 Karski saw President Franklin Roosevelt. Felix Frankfurter, a justice of the Supreme Court, told him, by his own account, that he was unable to believe him.',
                'caption' => 'Felix Frankfurter, a justice of the United States Supreme Court, in 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Harris and Ewing, 1939, Library of Congress, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'after',
                'photo' => 'after',
                'label' => 'The long silence',
                'year' => '1944 - 2000',
                'title' => 'A bestseller, then forty quiet years',
                'body' => 'In November 1944 he published Story of a Secret State in the United States, an account of the Polish underground that sold in hundreds of thousands of copies. Then he stopped talking about the war. He stayed in America, took a doctorate at Georgetown University in Washington in 1952 and taught international relations there for forty years. The film-maker Claude Lanzmann filmed him in 1978 and the film Shoah put him in front of the world again in 1985. Yad Vashem named him Righteous Among the Nations, its title for people who risked their lives to save Jews, on 2 June 1982, Israel made him an honorary citizen in 1994, and he died in Washington on 13 July 2000.',
                'caption' => 'Healy Hall at Georgetown University in Washington, where he taught',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Warren LeMay, 2018, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'Where to see it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'A bench you can sit down on beside him',
                'body' => 'The sculptor Karol Badyna has seated Karski in bronze on benches in Lodz, Kielce, Warsaw, Krakow, Washington, New York and Tel Aviv. The Warsaw one was unveiled on 11 June 2013 beside POLIN, the museum of the history of Polish Jews, and a button on it plays his own voice. President Barack Obama gave him the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2012, twelve years after his death. The Jewish Historical Institute awards a prize in his name and in his wife\'s, the dancer Pola Nirenska. He was heard at the top in London and in Washington, and he was not acted on.',
                'caption' => 'The Karski bench in the Survivors Park in Lodz, the city he was born in',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Jerzy Strzelecki, 2014, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The institutions behind this page are the Institute of National Remembrance in Warsaw, the Emanuel Ringelblum Jewish Historical Institute in Warsaw, the Museum of the History of Poland and its Karski service, Yad Vashem in Jerusalem, the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum in Washington, the news service dzieje.pl of the Polish Press Agency, the Jan Karski Educational Foundation and the Polish foreign ministry. The birth date is a real disagreement and this page does not hide it. Dzieje.pl gives 24 June 1914 and calls 24 April an error, the Institute of National Remembrance prints both and notes that the April date is the one in his official documents, adding the family story that the priest registering the birth wrote VI where he meant IV. The picture on the first frame is his own curriculum vitae of December 1936, which gives 24 June, and that is the date printed here. The saddlery on Kilinskiego street in Lodz, the six children, the Jesuit primary school, the Jewish friends at the state secondary school named after Jozef Pilsudski, the law and diplomacy degrees at the Jan Kazimierz University in Lwow, the first place at the reserve artillery cadet school at Wlodzimierz Wolynski in 1936, the ministry postings in Geneva and London, the mobilisation order of 23 August 1939, the 5th Horse Artillery Battalion at Oswiecim, the capture near Tarnopol on 17 September 1939, the camp at Kozielszczyzna, the removal of his insignia on news of the German-Soviet exchange and the escape from the German transport in the middle of November 1939 are all the Institute of National Remembrance. That the officers held in those camps were shot in the spring of 1940 has its own article here, and this page does not claim that any source proves what would have happened to Karski, only that he went over as a private. His brother, Marian Kozielewski, commanded the Polish police of the General Government in Warsaw and built a conspiracy inside it, which is the institute again. The route of January 1940 through Krakow, Zakopane, Kosice, Budapest, Yugoslavia and Italy to Modane is the institute, and dzieje.pl adds that he was a courier to Paris and Angers from January 1940. The arrest on the night of 14 and 15 June 1940 in the Slovak village of Demjata, the guide Franciszek Musial, the microfilm dropped into water with four frames surviving and the interrogation at Presov are the Jan Karski Educational Foundation. The torture, the suicide attempt and the rescue from the hospital by the Union of Armed Struggle are the Museum of the History of Poland and dzieje.pl together. The date of the rescue, 28 July 1940, the doctor Jan Slowikowski who kept him listed as gravely ill, and the 32 people shot on 21 August 1941 in reprisal are the Museum of the History of Poland, and the local wartime history project Wojenny Nowy Sacz names four of them, Tadeusz Szafran, Jozef Jenet, Stanislaw Suski and Karol Glod. The 1942 meetings with Leon Feiner of the Bund are carried by the Jewish Historical Institute, Yad Vashem and the Polish foreign ministry. Who the second man was is not settled: the Jewish Historical Institute names Adolf Berman of Poale Zion Left, the foreign ministry names Menachem Kirszenbaum, and this page gives both rather than choosing. The sentence that their entire people would be destroyed is the wording Yad Vashem prints. The two entries into the Warsaw ghetto are in every source used here, and the month is not: the foreign ministry puts the meetings in August 1942 and the Jewish Historical Institute puts the whole sequence in the autumn, so this page says the accounts differ. On the transit camp, the correction is the standard one and it is stated plainly rather than buried. Karski wrote in Story of a Secret State that he had been at Belzec, the Jewish Historical Institute states that this is an error and that the place was the transit ghetto at Izbica, and the institute dates the visit from the deportations of 18 and 19 October and 1 November 1942, following Jakub Chmielewski in the Jewish History Quarterly. The Holocaust Encyclopedia of the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum also says Izbica, while a caption in the same museum collections still says Belzec, which is exactly why the correction is printed here. The Ukrainian guard uniform, the bribed guard, the wagons loaded with a hundred and forty people and the day and a half of sleep afterwards are the Jewish Historical Institute quoting Karski\'s own book. The arrival in London in November 1942, the report to Sikorski and the meeting with Anthony Eden are the Museum of the History of Poland and the foreign ministry. The note sent by Edward Raczynski on 10 December 1942 to the governments that had signed the United Nations declaration, and the joint Allied declaration of 17 December 1942 read out in the British parliament, are the Jewish Historical Institute, which also records that the declaration spoke of labour camps and not of extermination camps. Roosevelt received him in July 1943, which the foreign ministry and the Holocaust Encyclopedia both give, and this page prints no day, because the institutional pages reached for this article do not carry one. The Frankfurter exchange is given as what Karski said it was rather than as a transcript, because the wording changes from telling to telling. The print run of Story of a Secret State is given as 360,000 copies by dzieje.pl and as 400,000 by the foreign ministry, so this page says hundreds of thousands. Georgetown, the doctorate of 1952 and the forty years of teaching are the Holocaust Encyclopedia, Yad Vashem and the foreign ministry. The recognition as Righteous Among the Nations on 2 June 1982 and the honorary citizenship of Israel in 1994 are Yad Vashem. The Presidential Medal of Freedom of 2012 is the Jan Karski Educational Foundation, dzieje.pl and the Institute of National Remembrance. The death in Washington on 13 July 2000 is dzieje.pl. The benches are the work of Karol Badyna, and the Warsaw one, unveiled on 11 June 2013 on Irena Sendler avenue beside POLIN, with a button that plays Karski speaking, is documented by the Warsaw public art register Puszka, which also names the City of Warsaw as the funder and the Museum of the History of Poland as the initiator. The years of the other benches are given only outside the institutions, so they are not printed. The award named after Jan Karski and Pola Nirenska is run by the Jewish Historical Institute. No map is published on this page. Karski\'s routes are spatial and a museum teaching map of them exists, but it is not published under a licence this site can use, the drawing on Wikimedia Commons is the work of a contributor rather than of a cartographer or a historian, and rather than publish a map that cannot be captioned with a maker, a date and a language, none is published. Two things are left out for room and named here rather than dropped in silence: the suicide of Szmul Zygielbojm, the Bund representative on the Polish National Council in London, on 12 May 1943 in protest at the indifference of the world, which the Jewish Historical Institute records, and the speech Karski gave in 1981 in which he said he had failed his wartime mission, which Yad Vashem prints in full. Title picture: the Jan Karski mural on Lubelska street in Warsaw, which carries his sentence that whoever does not condemn, consents, photographed by Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],
    'enigma' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Enigma: the Poles who broke it first',
            'description' => 'Marian Rejewski broke the German cipher machine in Warsaw in 1932, and Poland handed the whole method to Britain and France five weeks before the war.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · Enigma',
            'title' => 'They read it first',
            'title_accent' => 'and nearly lost the credit',
            'lede' => 'Jan Karski, the courier who carried the news of the Holocaust out of occupied Poland, has the article before this one. This story starts earlier, in peacetime, with three students in a Poznan lecture room. It ends with a German cipher that the Allies read for most of the war, a Polish mathematician working as an office clerk in a provincial town, and a museum that opened in 2021 to say what had happened. Nine frames on a piece of work that was decisive, was given away for free, and was then left out of the story for forty years.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'machine',
                'photo' => 'machine',
                'label' => 'The machine',
                'year' => '1918 - 1930',
                'title' => 'A business gadget the German army took over',
                'body' => 'A German engineer, Arthur Scherbius, patented a cipher machine on 23 February 1918 and sold it through the 1920s to banks and firms that wanted their telegrams private. It looked like a typewriter in a wooden box. Press a key and a lamp lit under a different letter, because the current ran through three turning wheels, called rotors, and every keystroke moved one of them, so the same letter never came out the same way twice in a row. The German army took the idea over, added a plugboard of cables that swapped pairs of letters before and after the rotors, and issued its own version from 1930. The Germans were sure that nobody could ever work through that many settings.',
                'caption' => 'An Enigma K, the commercial model, at the Enigma Cipher Centre in Poznan',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Lukasz Gdak, 2024, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'bureau',
                'photo' => 'bureau',
                'label' => 'Why Poland first',
                'year' => '1919 - 1930',
                'title' => 'Poland hired mathematicians, not linguists',
                'body' => 'The Second Republic, the Polish state restored in 1918 and the subject of its own article here, lay between Germany and the Soviet Union and listened to both of them as a matter of survival. Its code-breaking office, the Cipher Bureau of the general staff, had attacked Enigma with the usual sort of staff, people good at languages and puzzles, and got nowhere. In January 1929 it tried what nobody else had thought of. It ran a secret cryptology course for mathematics students of Poznan University, twenty-three of them, in a city that had been under German rule until 1918 and whose students still spoke German.',
                'caption' => 'The Imperial Castle in Poznan, which housed the university mathematics institute',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Diego Delso, 2014, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'three',
                'photo' => 'three',
                'label' => 'Three students',
                'year' => '1905 - 1932',
                'title' => 'Rejewski, Rozycki and Zygalski',
                'body' => 'Three of the twenty-three are the reason this page exists. Marian Rejewski, born in Bydgoszcz on 16 August 1905, took his mathematics degree in Poznan on 1 March 1929 under Professor Zdzislaw Krygowski, who had taught the course alongside the officers sent from Warsaw. Jerzy Rozycki, born on 24 July 1909 in Olszana, and Henryk Zygalski, born in Poznan on 15 July 1908, sat through the same lectures. From 1929 the three worked part time in a Cipher Bureau branch in the basement of the city garrison headquarters. In the autumn of 1932 the branch closed and all three moved to Warsaw.',
                'caption' => 'Zygalski, Rozycki and Rejewski, photographed in 1940 at the Bruno centre in France',
                'credit' => 'Photo: unknown, 1940, Enigma Cipher Centre collection, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'break',
                'photo' => 'break',
                'label' => 'The first break',
                'year' => '1932 - 1933',
                'title' => 'He worked out the wiring on paper',
                'body' => 'In Warsaw, Rejewski was given Enigma as a task of his own, in secret from his colleagues, with a commercially bought machine, a daily pile of intercepted German signals and the theory of permutations. On 8 December 1932 he was given copies of the German key tables for September and October 1932, obtained by Captain Gustave Bertrand of French intelligence from Hans-Thilo Schmidt, a paid informant inside the German cipher office. Within weeks Rejewski had reconstructed the wiring of a machine he had never seen. The nickname that stuck to the theorem behind it, the theorem that won World War II, is credited to an American mathematician, Cipher A. Deavours.',
                'caption' => 'Enigma rotors off their spindle, the wiring Rejewski worked out without seeing it',
                'credit' => 'Photo: TedColes, 2011, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'machines',
                'photo' => 'machines',
                'label' => 'What they built',
                'year' => '1934 - 1938',
                'title' => 'A card catalogue, paper sheets and a bomba',
                'body' => 'In January 1933 Rejewski brought Rozycki and Zygalski in, and the three spent six years keeping pace with German changes. Rejewski built the cyclometer, a device that let them compile a catalogue of 105,456 card entries, after which a day of settings could be found in roughly a quarter of an hour. Zygalski cut sheets of paper perforated with about a thousand holes each, to be laid over one another on a lit table. In November 1938 came the bomba kryptologiczna, the cryptological bomb, six electrical machines that ground through 17,576 rotor positions in about two hours. In early 1938 the Poles were reading three quarters of what they intercepted.',
                'caption' => 'The memorial stone at Pyry in the Kabaty Woods, where the Cipher Bureau worked',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, 2018, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'harder',
                'photo' => 'harder',
                'label' => 'Germany moves',
                'year' => '1938 - 1939',
                'title' => 'Two more rotors, and the money ran out',
                'body' => 'On 15 December 1938 every German Enigma was issued with five rotors instead of three, of which three went into the machine on any given day. That took the number of rotor orders from six to sixty, so the Poles now needed sixty sets of Zygalski sheets rather than six, and sixty of those bombs rather than six. Within weeks the standing German procedure moved to ten plugboard cables, and the plugboard alone could then be wired in about 150 million million ways. The Cipher Bureau could still do the mathematics. It could not pay for the machinery, and everybody in Warsaw could see a war coming.',
                'caption' => 'An Enigma plugboard with six of its cables, in Hut 11 at Bletchley Park',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Michael Garlick, 2024, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 2.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'pyry',
                'photo' => 'pyry',
                'label' => 'The handover',
                'year' => '1939',
                'title' => 'What Poland gave away in July 1939',
                'body' => 'On 25 July 1939, at a Cipher Bureau centre at Pyry outside Warsaw, Polish officers showed the British and the French everything. Colonel Gwido Langer and Major Maksymilian Ciezki, who had taught on the Poznan course, were two of them. Alastair Denniston, head of the British Government Code and Cypher School, and its chief cryptanalyst Alfred Dillwyn Knox took home a Polish-built copy of the military Enigma, the French a second one. Britain could not read Enigma before that meeting, on the word of its own codebreakers. What came after was British. At Bletchley Park the mathematician Alan Turing designed a bombe of another kind, and naval Enigma was broken there in Hut 8.',
                'caption' => 'The rebuilt Turing-Welchman bombe on show at Bletchley Park in England',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Mike Peel, 2006, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'after',
                'photo' => 'after',
                'label' => 'What became of them',
                'year' => '1939 - 1980',
                'title' => 'France, a sinking ship, a clerk in Bydgoszcz',
                'body' => 'Nazi Germany invaded on 1 September 1939 and the Cipher Bureau went east. The three crossed into Romania on 17 September, the day the Soviet Union invaded too, and worked on in France, at Bruno near Paris and then at Cadix in the south, under false names. On 9 January 1942 the steamer Lamoriciere sank in the Mediterranean and Jerzy Rozycki drowned with it. Rejewski and Zygalski crossed into Spain in January 1943, spent three months in Spanish cells and reached England that July, where a Polish radio unit at Boxmoor took them in and Enigma was closed to them. Zygalski stayed and taught mathematics until he died in 1978. Rejewski went home in 1946 and spent twenty years as a clerk.',
                'caption' => 'Rejewski, Zygalski and Rozycki playing cards at Cadix in France, about 1941',
                'credit' => 'Photo: unknown, about 1941, collection of Maria and Gerard Bryschak, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'memory',
                'photo' => 'memory',
                'label' => 'Where to see it',
                'year' => '1974 - today',
                'title' => 'The credit turned up forty years late',
                'body' => 'The whole business stayed secret until 1974, when a British officer, Frederick Winterbotham, published The Ultra Secret and told it as a British story. A French intelligence officer, Gustave Bertrand, had written a year earlier that Poles solved Enigma, without naming them. Polish papers went looking, Rejewski came forward, and he died in 1980 with the record half corrected. Poznan unveiled a monument to the three in 2007 and opened the Enigma Cipher Centre on 25 September 2021, both of them on the same street. At Bletchley Park a rebuilt bombe runs for visitors, and Rejewski and Zygalski are now on its Roll of Honour, the British list of everyone who did signals intelligence.',
                'caption' => 'The 2007 monument to the three cryptologists in the middle of Poznan',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Pnapora, 2014, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The institutions and published work behind this page are the Institute of National Remembrance in Warsaw and its biography of Marian Rejewski by Krzysztof Osinski, the Enigma Cipher Centre in Poznan, Adam Mickiewicz University in Poznan, Bletchley Park Trust, the Bombe Rebuild Trust, the Crypto Museum of Paul Reuvers and Marc Simons, and the article on the Enigma machine written by Christopher Kasparek for the Encyclopedia of Modern Europe, which is also where the bibliography used here comes from, including Wladyslaw Kozaczuk, Gustave Bertrand, Gordon Welchman and Rejewski\'s own papers in the journal Cryptologia. Arthur Scherbius, the patent of 23 February 1918, the commercial machines of the 1920s, the army version of 1930, the plugboard, the five rotors issued from 15 December 1938 and the ten plugboard cables of the German procedure are the Crypto Museum, which also gives 150,738,274,937,250 as the number of ways ten cables can be plugged, rounded here to about 150 million million. The life of Rejewski is the Institute of National Remembrance throughout: the birth in Bydgoszcz on 16 August 1905, the mathematics degree in Poznan on 1 March 1929 under Zdzislaw Krygowski, the cryptology course of 15 January to 22 March 1929 with twenty-three participants taught by Krygowski with Major Franciszek Pokorny, Captain Maksymilian Ciezki and the engineer Antoni Palluth, the Poznan branch of the Cipher Bureau, the move to Warsaw in the autumn of 1932, the key tables for September and October 1932 handed over on 8 December 1932, the reconstruction of the machine by the end of December 1932, the bringing in of Rozycki and Zygalski in January 1933, the cyclometer and the bomba, the Zygalski sheets, the reading of 75 per cent of intercepts in early 1938, the two conferences with allied intelligence in Paris in January 1939 and in Warsaw in July 1939, the handing over of one replica each, the evacuation through Romania on 17 September 1939, the centre called Bruno from 20 October 1939, the centre called Cadix at Uzes, the death of Jerzy Rozycki in the sinking of the Lamoriciere on 9 January 1942, the crossing into Spain on 29 January 1943, the three months in Spanish cells, the arrival in England at the end of July 1943, the posting to the Polish radio intelligence company at Boxmoor, the exclusion from Enigma work, the return to Poland on 21 November 1946, the clerical jobs in Bydgoszcz from 1947 to 1967, the dismissal of 28 January 1950, the surveillance by the communist security service, the memoir typed in 1967 that nobody wanted, the Bertrand memoir that brought the Polish press looking, and the death on 13 February 1980. The birth dates of Rozycki, 24 July 1909 in Olszana, and of Zygalski, 15 July 1908 in Poznan, and the death of Zygalski at Liss in England on 30 August 1978, are carried by the Polish heritage portal Dziedzictwo Polonii, and the Zygalski dates are confirmed by National Geographic Polska, which adds that he defended his master\'s thesis in December 1931 and taught in England after the war. That all three were mathematics students of Poznan University is stated by the Bombe Rebuild Trust as well. The date of 25 July 1939 for the meeting at Pyry in the Kabaty Woods is the Encyclopedia of Modern Europe, and the conference ran on for two more days, which is why some accounts date it to 26 and 27 July. Alastair Denniston and Alfred Dillwyn Knox as the British participants are Bletchley Park Trust, which also states that the meeting significantly helped the subsequent British attack on Enigma, in the words of its research historian David Kenyon. The sentence that Britain could not read Enigma before the meeting rests on Gordon Welchman of Hut 6, who wrote that Ultra would never have got off the ground had the British not learned the details of the machine and of the German operating procedures from the Poles in the nick of time. The Polish contribution is not the whole story and this page does not pretend it is. The Turing-Welchman bombe was a different machine from the Polish bomba, which the Bombe Rebuild Trust states plainly, Welchman added the diagonal board that made it work at scale, and the breaking of naval Enigma in Hut 8 rested on key tables and machines seized from German submarines and weather ships, which is the Encyclopedia of Modern Europe again. The card catalogue of 105,456 entries, the roughly one thousand holes per Zygalski sheet and the 17,576 rotor positions a bomba tested are from the same encyclopedia article and from the Crypto Museum. Hans-Thilo Schmidt, who sold the German documents, was run by French intelligence under the cover name Asche, which is the Encyclopedia of Modern Europe, and what became of him is not printed here because the sources reached for this page do not settle it. The phrase the theorem that won World War II is printed by that same encyclopedia article without saying who coined it. The attribution to Cipher A. Deavours, an American mathematician and an editor of the journal Cryptologia, is the standard one in the literature and is given here as an attribution rather than as a quotation from an institution. The number of bombs built, six, one for each rotor order, and the six sets of Zygalski sheets are standard in the literature used here and are not attributed to a single institution. Frederick Winterbotham published The Ultra Secret in 1974 and Gustave Bertrand published his memoir in Paris in 1973, both of which are in the bibliography above, and the Institute of National Remembrance records that Bertrand wrote that Polish cryptologists had solved Enigma without giving their names. This page does not say that Winterbotham lied, because the sources reached for it do not support that. It says what is documented, which is that Bletchley Park itself now describes the Polish contribution as one that may have been overlooked in the past, and has been adding Polish names to its Roll of Honour. The monument to the three cryptologists, unveiled in 2007 in front of the Imperial Castle on Swiety Marcin street in Poznan, is the Institute of National Remembrance. The opening of the Enigma Cipher Centre in the Collegium Martineum on 25 September 2021 is Adam Mickiewicz University. No map is published on this page. Nothing on it turns on where a border ran, the one spatial fact that matters is that Poland lay between Germany and the Soviet Union, which the other articles here already carry, and no published map of the Enigma story exists under a licence this site can use that could be captioned with a maker, a date and a language. Four things are left out for room and named here rather than dropped in silence: the clock method invented by Jerzy Rozycki, the visit Alan Turing paid to the Polish team at Bruno in 1940, the arguments among historians about how much Ultra shortened the war, where a common estimate of two years is quoted by the Encyclopedia of Modern Europe and is exactly the kind of figure that cannot be verified, and the film Sekret Enigmy of 1979 and the television series made from it, which is how most Poles of that generation first heard the story. Title picture: Enigma machines and a box of rotors in the Polish Army Museum in Warsaw, photographed by Adrian Grycuk in 2025, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0 PL.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],
    'forces' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Poles abroad: 303 Squadron and Monte Cassino',
            'description' => 'How an occupied country still had soldiers on every Allied front: 303 Squadron, the army that walked out of the Soviet Union, and Monte Cassino.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · Polish forces abroad',
            'title' => 'They fought everywhere',
            'title_accent' => 'and most could not go home',
            'lede' => 'Enigma, the German cipher that three Polish mathematicians read first, has the article before this one. This one is about the men who did the fighting. Poland was occupied for the whole war and never stopped having an army: in France, in the air over England, in the Atlantic, in Italy, in Normandy, in the Netherlands, and from 1943 in a second army marching west under Soviet command. Nine frames on where they came from, what they did, and why so many of them could not go home afterwards.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'escape',
                'photo' => 'escape',
                'label' => 'Getting out',
                'year' => '1939 - 1940',
                'title' => 'How an army left a country it had lost',
                'body' => 'On 17 September 1939, with German armies across the country and the Soviet Union invading from the east, Poland\'s commander in chief, Marshal Edward Rydz-Smigly, ordered his troops over the border into Romania or Hungary. About 85,000 got across. Some 44,000 were moved on through Yugoslavia, Italy, Greece and Turkey to Marseille, and German agents in Salonika reported two extra carriages added to the Orient Express every evening with eighty to a hundred Poles aboard. By June 1940 the Polish army in France numbered 82,261 men. The Podhale Rifle Brigade under General Zygmunt Bohusz-Szyszko fought at Narvik in Norway. Then France fell in six weeks and they sailed again, to Britain.',
                'caption' => 'Podhale Rifle Brigade soldiers on a ship off Norway, April 1940',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Polish official photographer, 1940, Imperial War Museums via Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'britain',
                'photo' => 'britain',
                'label' => 'The pilots',
                'year' => '1940',
                'title' => 'A hundred and forty five Poles in one summer',
                'body' => 'Of the airmen who reached Britain in 1940, 145 flew in the Battle of Britain, by the Imperial War Museums count: 79 in British squadrons, the rest in two Polish ones, 302 and 303. Number 303, named after Tadeusz Kosciuszko, who led a rising in 1794, went operational on 31 August 1940 under a British squadron leader, Ronald Kellett. Its wartime tally of 126 German aircraft was the highest claimed in the battle, and later checks against German records cut every such claim, the RAF total from 2,692 to 1,733. Twenty-nine Polish pilots died. Hugh Dowding of Fighter Command, who had doubted them, wrote that without them he hesitated to say the outcome would have been the same.',
                'caption' => 'Pilots of 303 Squadron walking from a Hurricane, October 1940',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Stanley Devon, 1940, Imperial War Museums via Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'navy',
                'photo' => 'navy',
                'label' => 'The warships',
                'year' => '1939 - 1945',
                'title' => 'The navy sailed before the first shot',
                'body' => 'The navy had left before the war began. On 30 August 1939 Admiral Jozef Unrug signalled the codeword Peking, and three destroyers, Burza, Blyskawica and Grom, left Gdynia for Scotland, arriving on 1 September, the war a few hours old. The submarine Orzel was interned at Tallinn in Estonia and stripped of her charts. Her crew broke out on the night of 17 September under Lieutenant Jan Grudzinski and reached Britain with no maps. The rest was convoy escort work. One night of it became famous: on 26 May 1941 the destroyer Piorun found the battleship Bismarck in the Atlantic and, hopelessly outgunned, opened fire on the order of her captain Eugeniusz Plawski: three salvoes for Poland.',
                'caption' => 'The destroyer Blyskawica, kept as a museum ship in Gdynia',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Szymon Rutkowski, 2021, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'anders',
                'photo' => 'anders',
                'label' => 'Out of the east',
                'year' => '1941 - 1943',
                'title' => 'An army and its children walked out to Iran',
                'body' => 'A second Polish army was raised inside the Soviet Union out of Poles who had been deported there, and the article on the government in exile tells how Moscow came to let it go. In 1942 it left for Iran, more than 115,000 people, about 78,500 of them soldiers and the rest civilians, a great many of them children. The Institute of National Remembrance counts 2,600 of those children at Isfahan, 650 at Balachadi in India on land given by the Maharaja of Nawanagar, Jam Saheb Digvijaysinhji, about 8,000 across east Africa, some 730 at Pahiatua in New Zealand and about 800 at Santa Rosa in Mexico. The soldiers also brought out a bear cub, bought in Iran that August and named Wojtek.',
                'caption' => 'Wojtek the bear wrestling a soldier of his supply company',
                'credit' => 'Photo: unknown photographer, Imperial War Museums via Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'cassino',
                'photo' => 'cassino',
                'label' => 'Monte Cassino',
                'year' => 'May 1944',
                'title' => 'The fourth try at the monastery hill',
                'body' => 'In Italy that army became the Polish Second Corps under General Wladyslaw Anders, and in May 1944 it was handed Monte Cassino, a monastery on a mountain above the road to Rome that three Allied assaults had already failed to take. The fourth went in on 11 May. On 18 May a Polish patrol put a flag up in the ruins. The state history portal dzieje.pl puts the cost at 923 dead, 2,931 wounded and 345 missing, and 1,072 of them lie in the cemetery cut into the slope below. On the night of the last attack two soldiers of the corps, Feliks Konarski and Alfred Schutz, wrote the song about red poppies that every Pole still knows by heart.',
                'caption' => 'The Polish war cemetery terraced into the hill below the monastery',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Pilecka, 2007, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'maczek',
                'photo' => 'maczek',
                'label' => 'Falaise and Breda',
                'year' => '1944 - 1994',
                'title' => 'The general who ended up behind a bar',
                'body' => 'A different Polish army had been built in Scotland out of the men who escaped France. Its armoured division, under General Stanislaw Maczek, landed in Normandy in August 1944 and held Hill 262, which its crews called the Mace, until the Falaise pocket shut on the retreating German Seventh Army. On 29 October 1944 it took the Dutch city of Breda by working round behind it, with no artillery barrage and no bombing, and by the Polish and Dutch record the old town and its people came through it. Communist Poland then took Maczek\'s citizenship away. He lived in Edinburgh with no pension and worked as a barman, died in 1994 aged 102, and lies in Breda among his soldiers.',
                'caption' => 'Shermans of the 1st Polish Armoured Division, Normandy, 8 August 1944',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Sergeant Wilkes, 1944, Imperial War Museums via Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'arnhem',
                'photo' => 'arnhem',
                'label' => 'Arnhem 1944',
                'year' => '1944 - 2006',
                'title' => 'Blamed in 1944, decorated in 2006',
                'body' => 'That September, while Maczek was fighting his way north, the 1st Independent Polish Parachute Brigade was dropped at Driel on the south bank of the Rhine, days late because of the weather, to reach a British airborne division trapped across the water at Arnhem. The operation failed. Field Marshal Bernard Montgomery and Lieutenant General Frederick Browning laid the failure on the brigade commander, Major General Stanislaw Sosabowski, and he was removed from his command. The Netherlands answered that on 31 May 2006, when Queen Beatrix gave the brigade the Military Order of William and Sosabowski, long dead by then, the Bronze Lion.',
                'caption' => 'The parachute brigade in a museum case: two tunics and a beret',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Gary Todd, 2010, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'berling',
                'photo' => 'berling',
                'label' => 'The other army',
                'year' => '1943 - 1945',
                'title' => 'A second Polish army, marching the other way',
                'body' => 'Not every Pole in the Soviet Union got out with Anders. Tens of thousands were left behind, and from May 1943 Moscow raised a division from them at Sielce on the Oka river under General Zygmunt Berling. It went into its first battle at Lenino in what is now Belarus on 12 October 1943 with about 12,400 men, and the Museum of Polish History records that in two days it lost 510 dead, 1,776 wounded and 652 missing. It grew into the First Polish Army, which fought to the Vistula opposite Warsaw in 1944, into Warsaw itself in January 1945, and into Berlin that spring. After the war it was the army the new government in Warsaw had, and the only one it trusted.',
                'caption' => 'Infantry of the Kosciuszko division advancing at Lenino, October 1943',
                'credit' => 'Photo: unknown photographer, 1943, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'end',
                'photo' => 'end',
                'label' => 'No way home',
                'year' => '1945 - 1949',
                'title' => 'The parade that went by without them',
                'body' => 'Two Polish armies finished the war on the winning side and one of them had nowhere to go. The eastern half of the country these men came from was now Soviet, and the London government they had served had lost British and American recognition in July 1945. In May 1946 Britain set up the Polish Resettlement Corps, and the Polish state archives count about 114,000 former soldiers passing through it. At the Victory Parade in London on 8 June 1946 no Polish unit marched. Imperial War Museums records that Britain invited the Warsaw government instead and, after protests at home, offered places to 25 Polish pilots, who turned them down.',
                'caption' => 'Polish war graves in the cemetery at Newark in England',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Delta 51, 2025, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A colour military map sheet headed Bitwa o Monte Cassino, the town of Cassino on the right of the sheet, the monastery hill shaded in red contours on the left, a numbered grid ruled across the whole sheet, a legend of Polish symbols down the right-hand margin and a scale bar along the bottom',
            'caption' => 'Bitwa o Monte Cassino: Cassino-Piedimonte at 1 to 25,000, the artillery fire plan, drawn and printed in Polish by the 12th Geographic Company of the Polish Second Corps in Italy in 1945, from ground survey and aerial photographs taken in May 1944. The monastery hill is the red shading on the left.',
            'credit' => 'Map: 12th Geographic Company, Polish Second Corps, 1945, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
        ],
        'visit' => [
            'label' => 'Where to find them',
            'body' => 'The Polish war cemetery at Monte Cassino is cut into the slope below the monastery, and Anders is buried in it among his soldiers. The Polish military cemetery in Breda stands on the Ettensebaan, with the Maczek Memorial beside it telling the Dutch half of the story. In London the Polish Institute and Sikorski Museum at 20 Princes Gate keeps the archive of the whole thing, and the Polish War Memorial at RAF Northolt carries the 1,903 Polish air force dead. In Warsaw the Polish Army Museum moved into the Citadel in 2023.',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The institutions behind this page are the Institute of National Remembrance in Warsaw and its portal Przystanek Historia, the state history portal dzieje.pl run by the Polish Press Agency and the Ministry of Culture, the Museum of Polish History, Imperial War Museums in London, the Military Historical Bureau of the Polish armed forces, the Central Archives of Modern Records in Warsaw, the Office for Veterans at kombatanci.gov.pl, the magazine Polska Zbrojna, the Museum of the Second World War in Gdansk, the Adam Mickiewicz Institute portal culture.pl, the Museum of Warsaw, the Dutch foundation Stichting Driel-Polen and the Maczek Memorial in Breda. The evacuation of 1939 follows a study by Janusz Wrobel for the Institute of National Remembrance: the directive of 17 September 1939 by the commander in chief, Marshal Edward Rydz-Smigly, to withdraw into Romania or Hungary, over 40,000 servicemen in Hungary by early October and about 25,000 to 26,000 in Romania, about 85,000 who avoided captivity by crossing a border, about 44,000 evacuated onward, the routes through Yugoslavia, Italy, Greece and Turkey to Marseille, the German agents in Salonika reporting two carriages added to the Orient Express each evening with eighty to a hundred Poles aboard, and the figure of 82,261 men in the Polish army in France in June 1940. The round number of eighty to ninety thousand that circulates for the army in France is that last figure, and it is printed here as the source gives it rather than rounded. Narvik is the Institute of National Remembrance and the Office for Veterans, which both give the brigade of General Zygmunt Bohusz-Szyszko losses of 97 dead, 28 missing and 189 wounded, and date the evacuation from Norway between 3 and 8 June 1940. Everything about the Battle of Britain is Imperial War Museums, in an article by Mariusz Gasior: 145 Polish airmen in the battle, 79 of them in British squadrons, 32 in 302 Squadron and 34 in 303, the squadron declared fully operational on 31 August 1940 after Ludwik Paszkiewicz broke formation and shot down a German bomber the day before, the British squadron leader Ronald Kellett, the score of 126 enemy aircraft with 13 probables and 9 damaged, the twenty-nine Polish pilots killed, and the Dowding sentence, which that article gives as had it not been for the magnificent work of the Polish squadrons and their unsurpassed gallantry, I hesitate to say that the outcome of battle would have been the same. The same article is the source for the revision: it states plainly that figures for claimed kills vary and that the whole RAF score was lowered from 2,692 to 1,733 aircraft destroyed because British and German records did not agree, and that the Polish total of 203.5 is given by other sources as 131. This page prints the wartime claim and the revision together and picks neither, because no recount of one squadron could be verified here against an institution. Witold Urbanowicz, the Polish commander of 303 with fifteen kills, and Josef Frantisek, the Czech who flew with the Poles and was the top scorer of the battle with seventeen, are left out for room and named here instead. The navy is the Military Historical Bureau and Polish Radio for the Peking plan: the order of Admiral Jozef Unrug on 30 August 1939 and the three destroyers Burza, Blyskawica and Grom sailing from Gdynia and reaching Scotland on the afternoon of 1 September, after the invasion had started. Grom was sunk off Narvik on 4 May 1940 with 59 of her crew. The escape of the submarine Orzel, interned at Tallinn on 15 September 1939 and stripped of her charts, breaking out in the night of 17 to 18 September under Lieutenant Jan Grudzinski and reaching Britain without maps, is the Polish embassy in Estonia at gov.pl and Polska Zbrojna. The night of 26 to 27 May 1941 is the Institute of National Remembrance, in an article by Mateusz Kubicki: the sighting at 22.37 by signalman Edward Dolecki at 14,500 metres, the order three salvoes for Poland given at 22.50 by Commander Eugeniusz Plawski, about an hour of firing, and Piorun reaching Devonport on 28 May with thirty tonnes of fuel left. The signal that Piorun is often said to have sent is not printed here, because the Institute of National Remembrance does not carry it and the order it does carry is better attested. The evacuation out of the Soviet Union in 1942, more than 115,000 people with about 78,500 soldiers among them, is the Institute of National Remembrance and dzieje.pl, and the article on the government in exile on this site carries the agreement that made it possible. The children are the Institute of National Remembrance: about 2,600 in Isfahan, 650 at Balachadi in India on land given by the Maharaja of Nawanagar, Jam Saheb Digvijaysinhji, over 18,000 Polish citizens in 22 settlements in south-east Africa of whom about 8,000 were children, 838 refugees including about 730 children landing at Wellington on 1 November 1944 and going on to Pahiatua, and 1,432 Poles at Santa Rosa in Mexico in 1943 of whom about 800 were children. Wojtek was given to the 22nd Artillery Supply Company in August 1942 and fed condensed milk from a vodka bottle, which is the education service of the Institute of National Remembrance, and he died in Edinburgh on 2 December 1963. The story that he carried shells at Monte Cassino is told by his own unit and is repeated by Polish institutions, but historians have questioned it, so it is not printed here as a fact. Monte Cassino is dzieje.pl, which gives 923 Polish dead, 2,931 wounded and 345 missing, and states that the Polish war cemetery opened on the hill in 1945 with 1,072 Polish soldiers buried in it. The Museum of Polish History dates the decision to give the Polish Second Corps the assault to the end of March 1944, and the capture of the ruins to 18 May 1944. The song Czerwone maki na Monte Cassino, the red poppies on Monte Cassino, words by Feliks Konarski and music by Alfred Schutz, both of them soldiers of the corps and members of its Polish Soldier Theatre, was written in the night of 17 to 18 May 1944, which is culture.pl and the Museum of Warsaw. The 1st Polish Armoured Division, Hill 262 called the Mace at Mont Ormel, and the liberation of Breda between 28 and 30 October 1944 are the Institute of National Remembrance, the Museum of Polish History and the Central Military Library, which all record that the city was taken by a flanking move without an artillery barrage or bombing and that this spared both the old town and the inhabitants. Polska Zbrojna puts it as taken without bloody fighting, which saved the lives of its people. No figure for civilian dead at Breda is printed here because none was found in an institutional source, and the claim is given as those sources give it. That Stanislaw Maczek was stripped of Polish citizenship by the communist authorities, was left without a pension and worked as a barman in Edinburgh is stated by dzieje.pl, by the Museum of the Second World War in Gdansk and by Polska Zbrojna, the last of which adds that his citizenship was restored in 1971. He died on 11 December 1994 aged 102 and is buried in the Polish military cemetery in Breda. Arnhem: the drop at Driel on the south bank of the Rhine, the blame laid on Major General Stanislaw Sosabowski by Field Marshal Bernard Montgomery and Lieutenant General Frederick Browning, and his removal from command are Polska Zbrojna and the Dutch foundation Stichting Driel-Polen. The honours of 31 May 2006, when Queen Beatrix awarded the Military Order of William to the brigade and the Bronze Lion to Sosabowski posthumously, are the same Dutch foundation and the Polish daily Rzeczpospolita. The date of Sosabowski\'s death is not printed here because it was not checked against an institution. The army raised in the east is the Museum of Polish History and dzieje.pl: the Tadeusz Kosciuszko 1st Infantry Division formed from May 1943 at Sielce on the Oka under General Zygmunt Berling, about 12,400 men at Lenino on 12 and 13 October 1943, and losses there of 510 dead, 1,776 wounded and 652 missing, which is the Museum of Polish History, while the war diary of the division published by dzieje.pl from the Central Military Archive gives 502 dead, 1,776 wounded and 663 missing. A study by Przemyslaw Benken for the Institute of National Remembrance states that the division took no ground at Lenino and that the figure of 1,500 German dead sometimes quoted has nothing to do with reality, which is why no German figure is printed here. This page does not take a side on the political question, and it calls these men neither traitors nor liberators, because they were conscripts and deportees before they were anything else. The end is Imperial War Museums for the Victory Parade in London on 8 June 1946, which records that none of the Polish forces who had fought for Britain was invited at first, that Britain had recognised the Soviet-backed government in Warsaw and invited its representatives instead, that after protests including Winston Churchill last-minute invitations went to 25 Free Polish pilots, and that those pilots refused in protest because the rest of the Polish forces were not invited. The Polish Resettlement Corps was set up in May 1946 and about 114,000 former soldiers of the Polish Armed Forces passed through it, which is the Central Archives of Modern Records in Warsaw. The figure of 1,903 Polish air force dead commemorated on the Polish War Memorial at RAF Northolt is Imperial War Museums. One map is published on this page and it earns its place, because the ground at Monte Cassino is the whole of that story: it is the Polish artillery fire plan of Cassino-Piedimonte, made by the 12th Geographic Company of the Polish Second Corps, and it is captioned with its maker, its date and its language. No map of the other frames is published, because the movements they describe run across three continents and no published map of them exists under a licence this site can use. The Warsaw Uprising of 1944 is the article after this one and is not told here. The Polish Home Army, the government in exile and the breaking of the Enigma cipher have their own articles on this site and are only bridged. Four things are left out for room and named here rather than dropped in silence: the Polish bomber and transport squadrons in the RAF, the 2nd Corps at Ancona and Bologna after Monte Cassino, the Polish forces at Tobruk in 1941, and the Haren settlement in Germany that Maczek\'s soldiers called Maczkow. The aviation museum at Warsaw Okecie is not named on this page because nothing about it could be confirmed in an institutional source here, and the Polish Army Museum, which moved into the Warsaw Citadel in 2023, is named instead. No opening hours are printed, because they change and none were checked. Title picture: Polish soldiers of the Second Corps under fire at Monte Cassino in May 1944, photographer unknown, Wikimedia Commons, public domain.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
