    'rising' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The Warsaw Uprising of 1944: sixty-three days',
            'description' => 'On 1 August 1944 the Home Army rose against the German occupation of Warsaw for 63 days. This is not the ghetto uprising of 1943.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · The Warsaw Uprising',
            'title' => 'Sixty-three days',
            'title_accent' => 'and then no city left',
            'lede' => 'First, the thing visitors get wrong. There are two Warsaw risings. The one in April 1943 was fought by Jews inside the ghetto and has its own article here. This one is the other: August 1944, the whole city, the Polish Home Army, the underground army that the article before this one described. It began with the Soviet front a river away and ended with Warsaw emptied and burned on orders. Ten frames on why it was launched, how it was fought, what it cost, and why Poland still argues about it.',
            'meta_line' => 'Ten frames · about thirteen minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'why',
                'photo' => 'why',
                'label' => 'Why August',
                'year' => '1943 - 1944',
                'title' => 'The Red Army came to the far bank',
                'body' => 'The article before this one left the Home Army holding an order. On 20 November 1943 its commander, General Tadeusz Komorowski, code name Bor, set out Operation Tempest: rise region by region as the Soviet front moved west, and meet the Red Army as the host rather than the beaten. By late July 1944 it stood on the Vistula, the river through Warsaw. The Institute of National Remembrance records that Komorowski hesitated, that his Commander-in-Chief in London, General Kazimierz Sosnkowski, called a rising an impulse with no political sense, and that Moscow radio was heard calling the city to arms. On the evening of 31 July, with Soviet tanks reported across it, he fixed the hour.',
                'caption' => 'General Tadeusz Bor-Komorowski in a British press photograph of 1945',
                'credit' => 'Photo: unknown photographer, 1945, National Digital Archives via Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'whour',
                'photo' => 'whour',
                'label' => 'Hour W, 17.00',
                'year' => '1 August 1944',
                'title' => 'Tens of thousands rose, one gun in ten',
                'body' => 'The order set five in the afternoon of Tuesday 1 August 1944, and Poles still call that moment godzina W, hour W. The Warsaw Rising Museum counts about 25,000 Home Army soldiers on the streets that first day and some 55,000 through the whole rising, among them 12,000 women and 5,500 people under eighteen, and states that roughly one soldier in ten had a weapon. The first days won the centre, the Old Town, Wola and Zoliborz. They did not win the bridges, the airport or the strongest German positions, which is exactly what the plan had needed. The command had expected days. Within a week the German counter-attack had cut the city into separate islands.',
                'caption' => 'Insurgents of the Koszta company on Moniuszki street, August 1944',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Eugeniusz Lokajski, 1944, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'wola',
                'photo' => 'wola',
                'label' => 'The Wola massacre',
                'year' => '5 - 7 August 1944',
                'title' => 'A whole district killed in three days',
                'body' => 'On 5 August German units entered Wola, the working district west of the centre, and began killing its people house by house on the authority of Heinrich Himmler, who commanded the SS. The Museum of Warsaw names the SS regiment of Oskar Dirlewanger, raised from convicts, as the worst, SS-Gruppenfuhrer Heinz Reinefarth as the commander on the ground, and Erich von dem Bach, the German general commanding the suppression, as the man who stopped it. The counts differ and none is firm. The Museum of Warsaw gives about 30,000 dead in Wola, other estimates run to 65,000, and the Institute of National Remembrance puts Wola with the neighbouring district of Ochota at 50,000 to 60,000.',
                'caption' => 'Civilians building a barricade in Wola in the first days of August 1944',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, 1944, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'fighting',
                'photo' => 'fighting',
                'label' => 'Barricades and sewers',
                'year' => 'August - September 1944',
                'title' => 'Two thousand barricades and a way beneath',
                'body' => 'The fighting was street by street. The Warsaw Rising Museum counts about 2,000 barricades built from paving stones, tram rails and furniture, and about a hundred Blyskawica submachine guns turned out in cellar workshops. Once German troops held the wide avenues, the sewers were the only link left between districts, and the Old Town garrison was evacuated through them at the start of September. Scouts ran a field post across the city, which the Museum of Warsaw records carrying three to six thousand letters a day and 10,000 on 13 August. From 8 August a station called Blyskawica broadcast in Polish and English. One armoured car was built on a lorry chassis and named Kubus.',
                'caption' => 'Kubus, the armoured car built by insurgents on a lorry chassis',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, 1944, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'civilians',
                'photo' => 'civilians',
                'label' => 'A million people',
                'year' => 'August - October 1944',
                'title' => 'Barley from a brewery, a wedding in a cellar',
                'body' => 'About a million people were living in Warsaw when it started, and they had nowhere to go. Water came from wells dug in courtyards once the mains were cut. Most of the food came from one address: the Haberbusch i Schiele brewery on Krochmalna street, taken in the first days, whose stores of barley were carried across the city in sacks and boiled into a thin soup. People slept in cellars, children carried messages, couples married in basement chapels. Songs were written while it was happening. The best known, Palacyk Michla, was written on 4 August by Jozef Szczepanski, aged twenty-one, of the Parasol battalion. He was dead within a month.',
                'caption' => 'The insurgent kitchen in the Adria building on Moniuszki street',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Eugeniusz Lokajski, 1944, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'help',
                'photo' => 'help',
                'label' => 'The airdrops',
                'year' => 'August - September 1944',
                'title' => 'Help flew 1,300 kilometres each way',
                'body' => 'Supplies came by air from Brindisi in southern Italy, about 1,300 kilometres away, flown by Polish, British, South African, Australian and American crews. Polish Radio puts the cost at 19 British aircraft with 133 airmen aboard and 15 Polish aircraft with 105. The Soviet Union, whose front line stood on the far bank of the Vistula, refused Allied aircraft permission to land behind it until 10 September, and on 18 September over a hundred American B-17s flew the one daylight mission allowed. From 15 September the First Polish Army under General Zygmunt Berling, raised in the Soviet Union and under Soviet command, put men across the river at Czerniakow. About 3,500 were lost.',
                'caption' => 'Insurgents gathering an airdrop at Bracka and Chmielna streets',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, 1944, Central Archives of Historical Records via Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'end',
                'photo' => 'end',
                'label' => 'The capitulation',
                'year' => '2 October 1944',
                'title' => 'Signed in a manor house outside the city',
                'body' => 'On 2 October 1944, in a manor house at Ozarow west of Warsaw, two Home Army officers, Colonel Kazimierz Iranek-Osmecki and Lieutenant Colonel Zygmunt Dobrowolski, signed the capitulation with Erich von dem Bach. The history portal dzieje.pl gives the terms: the insurgents were to be held as prisoners of war under the Geneva Convention of 1929 and not prosecuted for the fighting. Over 15,000 went into German camps. The Warsaw Rising Museum counts about 16,000 insurgents and 130,000 to 150,000 civilians dead, dzieje.pl about 18,000 and at least 150,000. Then the city was emptied through a transit camp at Pruszkow, at least 400,000 people by the Institute of National Remembrance.',
                'caption' => 'A transport of expelled civilians leaving the camp at Pruszkow, 1944',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, 1944, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'razing',
                'photo' => 'razing',
                'label' => 'The city erased',
                'year' => 'October 1944 - January 1945',
                'title' => 'An order that the city disappear',
                'body' => 'On 9 October 1944 Himmler ordered that Warsaw vanish from the face of the earth, with only the railway spared. Burning and demolition detachments, Brandkommando and Sprengkommando, worked the empty streets with police engineers under SS-Gruppenfuhrer Willy Schmelcher until January 1945. Libraries and archives were burned on purpose. The Institute of National Remembrance records the Krasinski Library losing 100,000 old prints, 50,000 manuscripts and some 2,000 incunabula, and the Central Archives of Modern Records left with five per cent. It puts the destruction at 60 to 80 per cent. The survey map printed in 1949 gives 84 per cent, and the article on rebuilding Warsaw starts there.',
                'caption' => 'The Old Town Market Place in Warsaw in 1945, burnt out and under snow',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, 1945, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'argument',
                'photo' => 'argument',
                'label' => 'Was it worth it',
                'year' => '1945 - today',
                'title' => 'An argument that was never settled',
                'body' => 'Poland has never agreed about it, and this page does not take a side. Sosnkowski, the Commander-in-Chief in London, was against it before it began. Under communist rule the Home Army was persecuted and the subject was political, which is the setting for the study General Jerzy Kirchmayer published in 1959. In London in 1971 the historian Jan M. Ciechanowski argued that the rising had been a political and military mistake. Others answered that a city with the Red Army at the gate and the NKVD, the Soviet secret police, behind it had no third option. Jan Nowak-Jezioranski, the courier who carried the arguments to London and back, defended it for the rest of his life.',
                'caption' => 'The Warsaw Uprising Monument on Krasinski Square, unveiled in 1989',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Dennis G. Jarvis, 2016, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 2.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'Where to stand',
                'year' => 'Today',
                'title' => 'At five in the afternoon Warsaw stops',
                'body' => 'Every 1 August at five in the afternoon the sirens sound across Warsaw and the city stops where it is. Drivers pull over and get out, people stand still on the pavement, and it lasts about a minute. The main ceremony is at the Gloria Victis monument in the insurgent quarters of the Powazki Military Cemetery. The Warsaw Rising Museum sits in the old tram power station on Grzybowska street. You will start seeing the kotwica, the anchor of Polska Walczaca, Fighting Poland, on walls, and plain sandstone plaques designed by the sculptor Karol Tchorek marking where people were shot. One of them stands on Kilinski street.',
                'caption' => 'The anchor of Fighting Poland on the tower of the Warsaw Rising Museum',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Ivanevian, 2014, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A printed map of Warsaw and its suburbs at a scale of 1 to 50,000, with district names in capitals, the Vistula running down the middle, patches of red marking ground held by the insurgents in August and September 1944, olive patches marking ground the Germans did not firmly hold, and an English legend down the right-hand side',
            'caption' => 'Warsaw at 1 to 50,000 with the ground held by the Home Army, in English, dated 1 October 1944 and based on a 1 to 25,000 sheet drawn by the Geographical Section of the Polish Army General Staff. Red is held ground, olive is ground the Germans never firmly held.',
            'credit' => 'Map: Geographical Section, General Staff, 1944, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
        ],
        'visit' => [
            'label' => 'If you go',
            'body' => 'The Warsaw Rising Museum on Grzybowska street is the place to start, and it is loud and crowded rather than solemn, which was a deliberate choice. Walk the Old Town and look down: some manhole covers are marked for the sewer routes. If you happen to be in Warsaw on 1 August, the only thing expected of you at five in the afternoon is to stop walking and stand still until the sirens finish. Nobody will mind that you are a foreigner. They will mind if you keep strolling through it with a coffee.',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The institutions behind this page are the Warsaw Rising Museum and its database at 1944.pl, the Institute of National Remembrance and its portal Przystanek Historia, the state history portal dzieje.pl run by the Polish Press Agency and the Ministry of Culture, the Museum of Warsaw, the Museum of Polish History, the Dulag 121 Museum in Pruszkow, Polish Radio and the Adam Mickiewicz Institute portal culture.pl. The decision follows an article for Przystanek Historia on hour W: the instruction of 20 November 1943 that set out Operation Tempest, the staff meeting with Generals Tadeusz Pelczynski and Leopold Okulicki, the agreement with the Government Delegate Jan Stanislaw Jankowski on 22 July 1944, the reports of Soviet tanks near Praga on the evening of 31 July, and the signal to London that the fight for the capital would begin on 1 August at 17.00. The same article carries the opposition of the Commander-in-Chief, General Kazimierz Sosnkowski, who wrote on 25 July 1944 that any thought of an armed rising was an impulse without political sense which could cause tragic and needless losses. It also states that broadcasts from Moscow radio calling the population to arms were clearly heard in the city. No script of those broadcasts is quoted here, because none could be verified against an institution, and the page says only what the Institute of National Remembrance says. The numbers of insurgents are the Warsaw Rising Museum, in a compilation by Michal Tomasz Wojciuk: about 25,000 Home Army soldiers at the start and some 55,000 in all, 12,000 women, 5,500 people under eighteen, 300 foreigners from over twenty countries, about one soldier in ten armed, about a hundred Blyskawica submachine guns and about 2,000 barricades. The same compilation gives about 16,000 Polish soldiers dead, about 130,000 to 150,000 civilians dead, about 3,000 German dead and 15,378 Poles taken prisoner, while dzieje.pl gives about 18,000 insurgents dead, at least 150,000 civilians and German losses of 15,000 to 25,000 killed and wounded. Those German figures differ by an order of magnitude between two institutions, so both are named and neither is averaged. Wola is the Museum of Warsaw, which names Himmler as the author of the order, Erich von dem Bach as commander of the pacification, SS-Gruppenfuhrer Heinz Reinefarth as the man directing it on the ground and the convict SS regiment of Oskar Dirlewanger as the worst of the units, gives about 30,000 dead and calls it the largest single massacre of civilians carried out during the Second World War. Estimates published elsewhere run as high as 65,000, and a bulletin of the Institute of National Remembrance gives Wola and the neighbouring district of Ochota together at 50,000 to 60,000 murdered. A range is printed here rather than a single figure, because the institutions do not agree and the killing was never counted. The RONA brigade of Bronislav Kaminski is not named in this page, because the institutional source opened here places Dirlewanger in Wola and does not put Kaminski there, and an accusation of this weight is not printed on an unverified attribution. The field post is the Museum of Warsaw: letters of at most twenty-five words, three to six thousand a day, 10,000 on 13 August, about 200,000 items in all, carried free by scouts. The Institute of National Remembrance dates the founding of that post to 6 August 1944 and the Museum of Warsaw to 2 August, so no founding date is printed. The armoured car Kubus is the Institute of National Remembrance: built in the workshop of Stanislaw Kwiatkowski on the corner of Tamka and Topiel streets, the work led by Jozef Fernik, on the chassis of a Chevrolet lorry, used against Warsaw University on 23 August and 2 September 1944 and disabled by its own crew on 6 September. The radio station Blyskawica first broadcast on 8 August 1944 from the Post Office Savings Bank building on Jasna street, in Polish and English, which is the Museum of Polish History and the Warsaw Rising Museum. The barley of the Haberbusch i Schiele brewery on Krochmalna street is dzieje.pl and the oral history archive of the Warsaw Rising Museum, in which survivors describe carrying it out in sacks. The song Palacyk Michla, the anthem of the Parasol battalion, was written on 4 August 1944 by Jozef Szczepanski, code name Ziutek, who is in the Warsaw Rising Museum biographical database and who died of wounds the following month. The airdrops are Polish Radio: first Polish sorties on 4 and 5 August 1944, about 1,300 kilometres each way from Brindisi, crews from Poland, Britain, South Africa, Australia and the United States, 19 British aircraft lost with 133 airmen and 15 Polish aircraft with 105, Stalin refusing landing rights until 10 September and the shuttle mission of 18 September 1944 flying over a hundred B-17 Flying Fortresses with nearly 1,300 containers. The figure of exactly 110 aircraft that circulates for that mission is not printed here, because the source opened gives only over a hundred. The crossings to Czerniakow are the Institute of National Remembrance: about 2,000 soldiers of the First Polish Army reached the left bank from 15 September 1944, about 3,500 were lost and about 400 came back. Whether General Zygmunt Berling was removed from command over it is not stated here, because it could not be confirmed. The capitulation is dzieje.pl: signed on 2 October 1944 at Ozarow by Colonel Kazimierz Iranek-Osmecki and Lieutenant Colonel Zygmunt Dobrowolski for the Home Army and by Erich von dem Bach for Germany, with prisoner of war status under the 1929 Geneva Convention and no prosecution for wartime or political activity. Pruszkow is the Institute of National Remembrance and the Dulag 121 Museum: the camp ran from 6 August 1944 to 16 January 1945 and at least 400,000 people passed through it, some resettled in the General Government, some sent to forced labour in Germany and about 60,000 to concentration camps, among them roughly 13,500 to Auschwitz. The destruction is the Institute of National Remembrance: Himmler ordering on 9 October 1944 that the city disappear completely from the face of the earth, police engineers of the Technische Nothilfe under SS-Gruppenfuhrer Willy Schmelcher with Brandkommando and Sprengkommando detachments, the Krasinski Library losing 100,000 old prints, 50,000 manuscripts and about 2,000 incunabula, the city archive destroyed entirely, the Central Archives of Modern Records left with five per cent, and a total of 60 to 80 per cent of the city. The article on rebuilding Warsaw on this site gives 84 per cent from the survey map printed in 1949, and the Warsaw Rising Museum arrives at 65 per cent by adding 25 per cent lost in the fighting to 30 per cent destroyed afterwards. All three are printed on this site as their sources give them. The map on this page is a real one: Warsaw at 1 to 50,000 showing the ground held by the Home Army, in English, dated 1 October 1944, based on a 1 to 25,000 sheet drawn up by the Geographical Section of the Polish Army General Staff. The memory is dzieje.pl for the Warsaw Rising Museum, opened on 31 July 2004 in the former tram power station after Lech Kaczynski, then mayor of Warsaw, decided in 2003 to build it, and for the sandstone plaques designed by the sculptor Karol Tchorek, which read a place sanctified by the blood of Poles who died for the freedom of the homeland and which the conservator entered into the register of monuments in 2023. The monument on Krasinski Square by Wincenty Kucma and Jacek Budyn was unveiled on 1 August 1989, which is the Museum of Warsaw and culture.pl. The sirens at seventeen hundred on 1 August and the ceremony at the Gloria Victis monument in the insurgent quarters of the Powazki Military Cemetery are Polish Radio and the city of Warsaw. No number of graves in those quarters is printed, because the only figure found came from a page that would not open. The explosion on Kilinski street on 13 August 1944, which killed over 300 people, is named here rather than in the frames: the Warsaw Rising Museum states plainly that the vehicle was a Borgward demolition carrier taken from the Germans and that the story of a deliberate tank trap is not true, so that story is not repeated on this page. The Warsaw Ghetto Uprising of April 1943 is a different event with its own article here and is not retold. Operation Tempest, the Home Army and the government in London have their own articles and are bridged rather than repeated, and the rebuilding of Warsaw after 1945 is told in the article on rebuilding. Things left out for room and named here rather than dropped in silence: the rising in Mokotow and Zoliborz and their separate capitulations, the Gesiowka camp freed on 5 August 1944 and the Jews liberated from it, the Ochota massacre, the Zoska and Parasol battalions in detail, and the Karl mortar that shelled the city. Title picture: the Prudential building in central Warsaw hit by a shell from a Karl mortar on 28 August 1944, photographed by Sylwester Braun from a roof on Kopernika street, Wikimedia Commons, public domain.',
        ],
