    ],
    'underground' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The Polish Underground State, 1939-1945',
            'description' => 'Occupied Poland ran a state in secret: courts, schools for hundreds of thousands of children, and a Home Army of about 350,000 sworn soldiers.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · The Second World War · The Underground State',
            'title' => 'They took the country',
            'title_accent' => 'not the state',
            'lede' => 'Most of occupied Europe produced resistance movements. Poland produced a government. From September 1939 it kept courts, schools, a welfare office, a press of hundreds of titles and an army of several hundred thousand sworn soldiers, all of it illegal, all of it reporting to the Polish government that had escaped to London and which has its own article here. This page is about the half that was not shooting: who ran it, who paid for it, what it taught, what it sent out, and what happened to the people at the top of it after the Germans left. Nine frames.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'meaning',
                'photo' => 'meaning',
                'label' => 'Not just an army',
                'year' => '1939 - 1945',
                'title' => 'It was an administration, not only an army',
                'body' => 'That phrase, underground state, is worth slowing down on. Poland kept a civil administration through the occupation, the Government Delegate\'s Office, in Polish Delegatura Rzadu na Kraj, with eighteen departments shadowing the ministries of a country nobody was allowed to govern openly. It taught, it paid welfare, it registered German crimes, and it held courts that sentenced in the name of the Polish Republic. The armed underground, from 1942 called the Home Army, Armia Krajowa, was one arm of that and not the whole of it. Waldemar Grabowski of the Institute of National Remembrance in Warsaw calls the whole structure a phenomenon on a world scale.',
                'caption' => 'An underground poster of September 1943 listing ten death sentences',
                'credit' => 'Poster: Directorate of Underground Resistance, 1943, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'beginning',
                'photo' => 'beginning',
                'label' => 'How it started',
                'year' => '1939 - 1942',
                'title' => 'From a besieged city to 350,000 soldiers',
                'body' => 'It starts on 27 September 1939, the day before Warsaw signed its surrender, when General Michal Tokarzewski-Karaszewicz founded the Service for Poland\'s Victory on the written authority of the capital\'s defence commander, General Juliusz Rommel. In Paris on 13 November 1939 the exile government replaced it with the Union of Armed Struggle, and from 30 June 1940 that union was run inside the country by General Stefan Rowecki, code name Grot. On 14 February 1942 the prime minister in London, General Wladyslaw Sikorski, ordered the name changed for external use to Armia Krajowa. By 1944 the Institute of National Remembrance counts over 350,000 sworn soldiers.',
                'caption' => 'Home Army soldiers of the 27th Volhynian Division at rest in 1944',
                'credit' => 'Photo: unknown photographer, 1944, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'delegatura',
                'photo' => 'delegatura',
                'label' => 'The delegate',
                'year' => '1940 - 1945',
                'title' => 'Eighteen departments and courts that shot',
                'body' => 'The civil side had a head. Cyryl Ratajski, a former mayor of Poznan, became Government Delegate on 3 December 1940. Jan Piekalkiewicz followed him in August 1942, was arrested in Warsaw on 19 February 1943 and died in German hands on 19 June. Jan Stanislaw Jankowski took over in April 1943 and was made deputy prime minister inside the country in the spring of 1944. Under him sat special courts, military and civilian, which from late 1942 tried Poles who informed for the Germans or blackmailed hidden Jews for money. Nobody has a national total of the sentences. One name: Wlodzimierz Les, whose denunciation got the Ulma family shot with the Jews they hid, killed on 10 September 1944.',
                'caption' => 'A forged German identity card made in 1942 for Jan Bytnar of the Home Army',
                'credit' => 'Document: General Government form, 1942, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'schools',
                'photo' => 'schools',
                'label' => 'Schools and print',
                'year' => '1939 - 1945',
                'title' => 'Ninety copies, a thousand secret students',
                'body' => 'Nazi Germany closed Polish secondary schools and universities, so they went on in flats. How many children sat in secret classes is not settled: the Institute of National Remembrance has printed one and a half million, and Grabowski treats the round million as a claim that some people make. Krakow is easier to picture. The clandestine Jagiellonian University began in May 1942 with one teacher and six students, and about a thousand passed through it. The press was the other half. Biuletyn Informacyjny, the underground weekly edited by Aleksander Kaminski, started on 5 November 1939 in ninety copies and reached 43,000 by 1943.',
                'caption' => 'The underground weekly Biuletyn Informacyjny of 24 February 1944',
                'credit' => 'Newspaper: Biuletyn Informacyjny, 1944, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'supply',
                'photo' => 'supply',
                'label' => 'Paid by parachute',
                'year' => '1941 - 1944',
                'title' => 'The money and the guns came by parachute',
                'body' => 'None of this was free, and the budget arrived from the sky. From the night of 15 February 1941, British aircraft flew in 316 specially trained officers, the cichociemni, the silent and unseen, out of 579 who qualified. A hundred and twelve of them died, nine of those executed in communist Poland after the war. The same flights carried in over 35 million dollars from London, in notes and gold. Of roughly 670 tons of supplies dispatched, 443 tons were actually picked up off the ground. What the weapons did is in the Home Army\'s own returns to London: 732 trains derailed and 930 locomotives damaged by mid-1944.',
                'caption' => 'A Blyskawica submachine gun, built in secret workshops, in Warsaw',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Przemek P, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'intelligence',
                'photo' => 'intelligence',
                'label' => 'What it sent out',
                'year' => '1942 - 1944',
                'title' => 'A rocket in pieces and a council for Jews',
                'body' => 'Information went the other way. Home Army intelligence worked out what the German rocket site at Peenemunde was, followed the tests to Blizna in the south, and on the night of 25 July 1944 put the parts of a V-2 rocket and an engineer\'s report onto a British Dakota on a field near Wal-Ruda, north of Tarnow. This was old ground. Polish mathematicians had broken the German Enigma cipher in 1932, and that has its own article here, as does Jan Karski, the courier who took an eyewitness account of the Holocaust to London and Washington. The same office funded Zegota, the Council to Aid Jews, from 4 December 1942, which POLIN calls the only state body in Europe set up to help Jews.',
                'caption' => 'The Zegota memorial stone in Warsaw, with daffodils laid on it',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Rafal Zambrzycki, Chancellery of the Sejm, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'politics',
                'photo' => 'politics',
                'label' => 'Four parties',
                'year' => '1942 - 1944',
                'title' => 'Four parties, and two armies outside them',
                'body' => 'It had a parliament substitute too. The Council of National Unity met on 9 January 1944 under the socialist Kazimierz Puzak, fourteen people from four parties, and it answered the communists, whose own National Council of the Homeland had met eight days earlier. Those communists had their own army, the People\'s Army, about six thousand strong and taking its orders from Moscow. On the other flank the National Armed Forces, founded on 20 September 1942, signed a merger with the Home Army in March 1944 and then split over it. Some of their units murdered communist partisans and Jews in hiding, and that is documented in the Home Army\'s own reporting.',
                'caption' => 'The first page of the Fighting Poland programme, July 1943',
                'credit' => 'Typescript: National Political Representation, 1943, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'end',
                'photo' => 'end',
                'label' => 'How it ended',
                'year' => '1943 - 1953',
                'title' => 'Dissolved, then tried in Moscow',
                'body' => 'On 20 November 1943 the Home Army commander, General Tadeusz Komorowski, code name Bor, ordered Operation Tempest: rise region by region as the Red Army came, and meet it as the host. The Warsaw Uprising of August 1944 was its largest and costliest action and has its own article. On 19 January 1945 the last commander, General Leopold Okulicki, released his soldiers from their oath. In March a Soviet security general, Ivan Serov, invited the leadership to a villa at Pruszkow outside Warsaw under a written guarantee of safety and arrested sixteen of them. Moscow tried them that June: ten years for Okulicki, eight for Jankowski, three acquitted.',
                'caption' => 'A corridor of the former Mokotow prison on Rakowiecka street, Warsaw',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'Where to see it',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'An anchor you will start seeing everywhere',
                'body' => 'The persecution went on for years. Over 200,000 people went through the postwar underground, and two men from this story died in Warsaw prisons: Witold Pilecki, the officer who had himself imprisoned in Auschwitz to report from inside, and who has his own article here, shot on 25 May 1948, and Emil Fieldorf, who ran the Home Army sabotage command, hanged on 24 February 1953. Fieldorf is the patron of the Home Army Museum in Krakow, whose rebuilt exhibition opened on 27 September 2012, the day the Polish parliament had in 1998 named for the Underground State. The Warsaw Rising Museum opened on 31 July 2004. And then there is the anchor, on the wall beside you.',
                'caption' => 'The Home Army Museum in Krakow, in an old Austrian barracks building',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Pawel Swiegoda, 2009, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 2.5',
