        'anchor' => [
            'alt' => 'A large anchor painted in red and white on grey tarmac: the shank and crossbar form the letter P, and the curved arms below form the letter W, with the digits 1 and 9 in the left arm and 4 and 4 in the right',
            'caption' => 'The kotwica, the anchor of Polska Walczaca, Fighting Poland: a letter P standing on a letter W. It was picked in a clandestine competition early in 1942 out of twenty seven entries, and by 16 April that year Biuletyn Informacyjny was reporting that the sign had taken over the capital. This one is painted on tarmac with the year 1944 worked into the arms.',
            'credit' => 'Photo: Silar, 2014, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The institutions behind this page are the Institute of National Remembrance in Warsaw and its portal Przystanek Historia, the news service dzieje.pl of the Polish Press Agency, the Home Army Museum in Krakow, the Polish History Museum, POLIN, the Museum of the History of Polish Jews, Yad Vashem and the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum. The founding of the Service for Poland\'s Victory on 27 September 1939 by Michal Tokarzewski-Karaszewicz, on written authority given the day before by Juliusz Rommel, and on the eve rather than the day of the capitulation of Warsaw, is the Institute of National Remembrance and Przystanek Historia. The Union of Armed Struggle dates from the decision of 13 November 1939 and not from the transmitting order of 4 December, which is dzieje.pl and Przystanek Historia, and Stefan Rowecki commanded the whole of it inside the country from 30 June 1940, which is dzieje.pl and the Institute of National Remembrance. The renaming order of 14 February 1942 is quoted verbatim by the Institute of National Remembrance: Sikorski abolished the name Union of Armed Struggle for external use and ruled that all soldiers in active service in the country form the Home Army. The strength figure is deliberately a range in the sources and not a single number: the Institute of National Remembrance prints over 350,000 sworn soldiers including about 10,000 officers, Przystanek Historia prints close to 380,000 for early 1944 and about 360,000 for the spring, and dzieje.pl prints about 380,000 for the summer of 1944 from the commander\'s own return. The figure of 400,000, which circulates widely, was not found on any institutional page and is not printed here. Roughly 450,000 people passed through the organisation across the war, and over 35,000 of them were killed or murdered by the middle of 1944. That the Home Army was the largest resistance movement in occupied Europe is asserted by the Institute of National Remembrance and hedged by Norman Davies, and it is therefore not stated flatly on this page, because Soviet partisan formations were larger. The eighteen departments of the Government Delegate\'s Office are dzieje.pl, which names Internal Affairs, Information and Press, Labour and Social Welfare, and Education and Culture among them. Cyryl Ratajski was appointed on 3 December 1940 and was the first chief delegate rather than the only one, because Adolf Bninski ran the lands annexed to the Reich at the same time. Jan Piekalkiewicz was appointed on 5 August 1942, arrested on 19 February 1943 and died on 19 June 1943 in German custody in Warsaw, and the sources differ on the building, so none is named here. Jan Stanislaw Jankowski took office in April 1943, with no exact day on any institutional page, and became deputy prime minister in the country under the presidential decree of 26 April 1944 and the Council of Ministers at Home formed on 3 May 1944. He was arrested by the Soviet security service on 28 March 1945, sentenced to eight years and not ten, and died on 13 March 1953 at Vladimir prison two weeks before his sentence ended, which is the Institute of National Remembrance. Stefan Korbonski was acting delegate from 10 April 1945. The special courts, military from late 1942 under the Home Army and civilian under the Delegate\'s Office, and the public warning of the Directorate of Civil Resistance of 18 March 1943 that anyone helping the Germans murder Jews would be punished, are Przystanek Historia and the State Archive in Lublin. No institution gives a national total of death sentences passed or carried out, so this page gives none and names one case instead, Wlodzimierz Les, the man who informed on the Ulma family in Markowa, shot on 10 September 1944. The secret teaching figures are genuinely in conflict inside one institution: a text by Wieslaw Jan Wysocki reprinted on three Institute of National Remembrance addresses gives about 1.5 million children and about 100,000 young people at secondary and higher level, while Waldemar Grabowski of the same institute, speaking to dzieje.pl, writes of many hundreds of thousands of young people and adds that some claim it was around a million. This page prints the disagreement rather than a number. The clandestine Jagiellonian University in Krakow beginning in May 1942 with one teacher and six students, and about a thousand students in all, is Przystanek Historia. The University of Warsaw gives about 3,500 clandestine students in 1944 and dzieje.pl gives 4,500, and dzieje.pl counts 6,500 diplomas from clandestine study nationally. Over 1,700 school leaving certificates were granted in the Warsaw district in 1942, which is the Polish History Museum, and no national total exists. Biuletyn Informacyjny, its first issue on 5 November 1939, its last on 19 January 1945, the same day the Home Army was dissolved, its editor Aleksander Kaminski, its first print run of 90 copies and its run of 43,000 from the middle of 1943, are Przystanek Historia, dzieje.pl and the Home Army Museum. A peak of 50,000 copies is often repeated and stands on no institutional source, so it does not appear here. On the size of the underground press, dzieje.pl gives about thirty illegal titles in October 1939 rising to about six hundred in 1944, which is a count of titles in circulation rather than a cumulative total, and the commonly quoted totals of 1,400 or 2,000 titles were not found on any institutional page. For scale, the Archive of Modern Records has digitised 1,282 underground and insurgent titles from its own holdings. The cichociemni are the Institute of National Remembrance: 316 parachuted in between 1941 and 1944 out of 579 who qualified, the first drop on the night of 15 and 16 February 1941, and 112 dead, of whom nine were murdered in judicial killings in communist Poland, which is why the commonly quoted 103 is incomplete. The money flown in, over 35 million American dollars in banknotes and gold, over 20 million German marks and 41 million occupation zloty, is Przystanek Historia. Two Institute of National Remembrance pages agree on about 670 tons of supplies dispatched and 443 tons collected on the ground, while a third gives about 600 tons, and the two pages also give different numbers of sorties, so this page gives the tonnage and not the flights. The sabotage returns, 732 trains derailed, 38 bridges blown, 930 locomotives and 19,058 wagons damaged between 1941 and the middle of 1944, come from the reports the Home Army commander sent to London and are Przystanek Historia. The number 6,930 for locomotives, which circulates, is not what the institution prints. The sabotage command, Kedyw, was created by Rowecki\'s order of 22 January 1943 and first led by Emil Fieldorf. The killing of Franz Kutschera on 1 February 1944, and the roughly 5,000 Poles murdered in Warsaw in the three and a half months before it, are the Institute of National Remembrance and are not retold on this page for want of room. On the rockets, the Institute of National Remembrance says a V-2 fell unexploded near Mezenin on the Bug and the Polish Aviation Museum says near Klimczyce by Sarnaki, which is why this page says near Sarnaki on the Bug, and both agree on the flight of the night of 25 and 26 July 1944 from the strip code named Motyl at Wal-Ruda north of Tarnow. Zabno, which is often named, is not named by either. The engineer who wrote the report was Antoni Kocjan of the Home Army Bureau of Industrial Studies. The claim that 48 per cent of the intelligence reaching British intelligence came from Polish sources traces to a 1945 British summary as rendered by the Polish Heritage Society in Britain, is stated with inconsistent denominators, competes with a figure of 43 per cent, and is not demonstrably the conclusion of the Anglo-Polish Historical Committee volume of 2005 to which it is usually attributed, so it does not appear on this page. Zegota, founded on 4 December 1942 out of a committee set up on 27 September 1942 by Zofia Kossak-Szczucka and Wanda Krahelska-Filipowiczowa, is the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, Yad Vashem, POLIN and dzieje.pl. It was a joint Polish and Jewish body, with Leon Feiner of the Bund and Adolf Berman of the Jewish National Committee in its leadership under the chairman Julian Grobelny, and Irena Sendler ran its children\'s section from 1943. The wording that it was the only state organisation in Europe whose aim was to help Jews is POLIN\'s own. POLIN states that the scale of its help cannot be determined, and gives about 300 people receiving money at the start of 1943, some 2,000 by the end of that year and about 4,000 by the summer of 1944, together with more than 50,000 forged documents. Yad Vashem publishes a study by Josef Kermish arguing that Polish historiography has greatly overestimated the Council\'s achievements, and quoting Adolf Berman writing to the Delegate on 28 October 1943 that it was helping no more than 1,000 to 1,500 people, a drop in the ocean. POLIN also quotes Marcin Urynowicz to the effect that in Warsaw roughly seventy per cent of the organised rescue effort was the work of Jewish organisations, which is why this page says the Delegate\'s Office funded Zegota rather than that it paid for the rescue. The Council of National Unity of 9 January 1944, fourteen members under Kazimierz Puzak, drawn from the Polish Socialist Party, the Peasant Party, the National Party and the Labour Party, and formed as an answer to the communist National Council of the Homeland constituted eight days earlier, is the Institute of National Remembrance and the Home Army Museum. Its programme declaration, on what the Polish nation is fighting for, is dated 15 March 1944 by two Institute of National Remembrance texts and by a scan of the original, and the date of March 1945 given on one Home Army Museum page is an error. The Polish Workers Party was founded on 5 January 1942, and the Institute of National Remembrance states that its People\'s Guard was in fact created on 28 March 1942 and the founding backdated to 6 January, and that it was renamed the People\'s Army on the night of 31 December 1943. Its strength of about six thousand in the summer of 1944 is Przystanek Historia. The National Armed Forces were founded on 20 September 1942, signed a merger agreement with the Home Army on 7 March 1944 at a strength of about 100,000, and more than half of their leadership rejected that agreement in April 1944, which is the Institute of National Remembrance. On what some of their units did, this page follows the evidence rather than either camp. The killing of a People\'s Guard detachment and local peasants at Borow on 9 August 1943 is called a murder by the Institute of National Remembrance\'s own portal, which counts 25 dead. That units of the National Armed Forces hunted and killed Jews in hiding is in the Home Army\'s own situational report to London of 21 June 1944, quoted by the historian Joshua Zimmerman, and in studies by Dariusz Libionka, while the Institute of National Remembrance\'s popular publications still describe such accusations as communist propaganda, and this page does not repeat that description. The murder of over 190 Orthodox Ukrainian villagers at Wierzchowiny on 6 June 1945 by a National Armed Forces group under Mieczyslaw Pazderski is confirmed by Grzegorz Motyka of the Institute of National Remembrance, who says the research leaves no doubt and that the counter-version of a Soviet provocation offers no evidence at all. The Home Army\'s own record is not airbrushed either. Joshua Zimmerman, writing in East European Politics and Societies and Cultures in 2020, concludes from survivor testimony and underground documents that the record ranged from atrocious acts of murder to extraordinary acts of aid, that about thirty per cent of the testimonies he read describe a Home Army that rescued and protected Jews, and that the view of an endemically antisemitic Home Army is only partially supported. Operation Tempest was ordered on 20 November 1943, and the Institute of National Remembrance\'s Polish pages date its start to January 1944 in Volhynia, with mobilisation from 12 January and the first clash on 19 January, while its English pages say March 1944, so this page gives the order and the Volhynian start. The dissolution order of 19 January 1945 is quoted verbatim by the Institute of National Remembrance, and both it and Przystanek Historia stress that it was not an order to stop: Leopold Okulicki released his soldiers from their oath and told them to go on working for full independence. Czestochowa, often named as the place, appears on no institutional page. The arrests at Pruszkow on 27 and 28 March 1945, run by Ivan Serov of the Soviet security service under the false name Ivanov and preceded by a written guarantee of safety, are the Polish History Museum and Przystanek Historia. The trial in Moscow ran from 18 to 21 June 1945: Okulicki received ten years, Jankowski eight, Adam Bien and Stanislaw Jasiukowicz five each, Puzak eighteen months, and three of the sixteen were acquitted, so this page does not say all sixteen were convicted and does not mention death sentences, because there were none. Over 200,000 people passed through the postwar underground between 1944 and 1956 and about 20,000 were under arms in 1945, which is the Institute of National Remembrance, while the figure of about 9,000 killed between 1945 and 1955 is flagged as uncertain by the agency that prints it and is therefore not used here. Over 53,000 people came out of hiding in the amnesty of February to April 1947. Witold Pilecki was shot on 25 May 1948 and Emil Fieldorf hanged on 24 February 1953, both at Mokotow prison in Warsaw, and both have their own articles or will have. The Day of the Polish Underground State on 27 September was established by a resolution of the Sejm of 11 September 1998, which this page checked against the official gazette, and not by an act. The Home Army Museum in Krakow has been on its site since 16 May 1992 and its rebuilt permanent exhibition opened on 27 September 2012, deliberately on that day, which is the museum\'s own history page. The Warsaw Rising Museum admitted visitors from 31 July 2004. The anchor was chosen in a clandestine competition held early in 1942 from twenty seven entries, and Biuletyn Informacyjny reported on 16 April 1942 that the sign had taken over the capital, which is Przystanek Historia. Its author is not settled: the Institute of National Remembrance names both Anna Smolenska, a scout and art history student at the clandestine University of Warsaw who died at Auschwitz in 1943, and the painter Jan Sokolowski, so this page names neither as the author. No map is published on this page. The Home Army district boundaries changed repeatedly and no published map of them was found that could be captioned with a maker, a date and a language, and a map of Operation Tempest would belong to an article about 1944 rather than to this one, so rather than publish a map that could not be sourced properly, none is published. Title picture: the Monument to the Polish Underground State and the Home Army in Warsaw, opposite the Sejm, photographed by Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the Second World War',
    ],
    'occupation' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The occupation and the Holocaust: Poland 1939-1945',
            'description' => 'What happened in Poland between the end of the September campaign and 1945: the two occupations, the ghettos, the camps, and how to visit them.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · Independence and war · Topic 04',
            'title' => 'Occupied:',
            'title_accent' => 'six years',
            'lede' => 'The fighting stopped in October 1939 and the worst of it had not started. Two occupiers divided the country, both went first for the people who might lead it, and Nazi Germany built the machinery of the Holocaust on ground it had taken. Ten frames on what was done, who did it, what is known and what is still argued over, and how to stand at the places today. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Ten frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'zones',
                'photo' => 'zones',
                'label' => 'Two occupations',
                'year' => '1939 - 1941',
                'title' => 'One country, cut in two and run two ways',
                'body' => 'Poland never surrendered and never had a collaborationist government, but by October 1939 it held no ground. Nazi Germany annexed the western lands outright: Poznan, Pomerania, Upper Silesia and the Free City of Danzig became provinces of the Reich, and Poles and Jews were expelled from them to make room for German settlers. The rest of the German zone, with Warsaw, Krakow, Radom and Lublin, became the General Government, a colony run from Wawel Castle in Krakow by Hans Frank, a Nazi Party lawyer. The Soviet Union took everything east of it and, after elections its authorities staged on 22 October 1939, annexed the territory on 1 and 2 November.',
                'caption' => 'The arcaded courtyard of Wawel Castle in Krakow, which Hans Frank took as his seat',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Zygmunt Put, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'elites',
                'photo' => 'elites',
                'label' => 'The elites first',
                'year' => '1939 - 1940',
                'title' => 'Both occupiers went for the same people',
                'body' => 'Neither occupier wanted a Polish leadership left standing. Through the autumn of 1939 German police and SS units shot teachers, priests, officials and landowners in the annexed lands, in an operation they called the Intelligenzaktion. The AB-Aktion repeated it in the General Government from 30 March 1940, and the Institute of National Remembrance puts the two together at roughly 50,000 dead. Warsaw lost its share in secret at Palmiry in the Kampinos forest, 21 executions and more than 1,700 people. On 6 November 1939 the Gestapo called the professors of Krakow to a lecture and sent 183 of them to the Sachsenhausen concentration camp. Moscow worked by decision instead.',
                'caption' => 'Collegium Novum of the Jagiellonian University in Krakow, photographed about 1900',
                'credit' => 'Photograph from the Polona digital library, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'east',
                'photo' => 'east',
                'label' => 'Taken east',
                'year' => '1940 - 1943',
                'title' => 'Four nights that emptied the eastern towns',
                'body' => 'Moscow decided on 5 March 1940: the Politburo, the Soviet party leadership, ordered nearly 22,000 Polish officers and officials shot, the crime called Katyn. Around it went the deportations. In four waves, in February, April and June 1940 and in May and June 1941, 330,000 to 340,000 people were loaded into goods wagons for Siberia and Kazakhstan, by the records of the NKVD, the Soviet secret police. When Nazi Germany turned on the Soviet Union in June 1941, the Soviet leader Joseph Stalin needed Poles: General Wladyslaw Anders, freed from the Lubyanka prison, led an army of them out to Iran in 1942. Germany found the graves in April 1943. Moscow blamed Germany until 1990.',
                'caption' => 'The Sybir Memorial Museum in Bialystok, built around a siding and a goods wagon',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Jan Szewczyk, Sybir Memorial Museum, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'daily',
                'photo' => 'daily',
                'label' => 'Under German rule',
                'year' => '1940 - 1944',
                'title' => 'Round-ups, ration cards and a state underground',
                'body' => 'Back in the German zone, a Pole lived with constant shortage and constant risk. Ration cards covered a fraction of what a person eats, so food came from the black market. The street round-up, the lapanka, could take anyone who was out, and at least 1.5 million Polish citizens were sent to work in the Reich. Universities and secondary schools were closed. From 15 October 1941 Hans Frank ordered death for any Jew leaving the ghetto and for anyone who knowingly hid one. Against that the country ran a state of its own under the Polish government now in London: its own courts, an underground press, and classes teaching about 90,000 pupils and students in 1943 and 1944.',
                'caption' => 'The bronze memory tree at the Pawiak prison museum in Warsaw, hung with plaques',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Jolanta Dyr, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'ghettos',
                'photo' => 'ghettos',
                'label' => 'Behind the walls',
                'year' => '1939 - 1942',
                'title' => 'Four hundred thousand people, one district',
                'body' => 'Poland had been home to the largest Jewish community in Europe, more than three million people, and the Germans shut them in before they killed them. The Lodz ghetto was closed in the spring of 1940. Warsaw was ordered in October 1940 and sealed in November: more than 400,000 people in about three and a half square kilometres, an average of 7.2 to a room. There was almost no work and almost no food. The United States Holocaust Memorial Museum puts the average ration inside the Warsaw ghetto in 1941 at 1,125 calories a day, and counts 83,000 people who died there of hunger and disease between 1940 and the middle of 1942.',
                'caption' => 'A surviving stretch of the Warsaw ghetto wall in the courtyard at 55 Sienna Street',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Cezary Piwowarski, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'camps',
                'photo' => 'camps',
                'label' => 'The killing centres',
                'year' => '1941 - 1944',
                'title' => 'Nazi Germany built the machinery on occupied ground',
                'body' => 'When Germany attacked the Soviet Union on 22 June 1941, SS and police units shot Jewish communities in the lands it took, eastern Poland among them. Then the killing moved indoors. Chelmno, in the annexed lands, gassed its first transport in vans on 8 December 1941. In 1942 the SS opened three camps built for nothing else - Belzec, Sobibor, Treblinka - under Operation Reinhard, run by SS general Odilo Globocnik. About 1.7 million Jews were murdered in it. Auschwitz-Birkenau near Krakow and Majdanek near Lublin had killing centres too. Jan Karski, a Polish underground courier, saw the Warsaw ghetto and reported it in London and Washington. The Allies put military victory first.',
                'caption' => 'The memorial at Belzec: a cut through the ground leading to the wall of names',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Dreamcatcher25, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'revolt',
                'photo' => 'revolt',
                'label' => 'Fighting back',
                'year' => '1942 - 1943',
                'title' => 'A rising in the ghetto and a buried archive',
                'body' => 'Between July and September 1942 about 265,000 people were taken from the Warsaw ghetto to Treblinka. When the Germans came back on 19 April 1943 for the rest, they were shot at. The Jewish Combat Organisation, in Polish Zydowska Organizacja Bojowa, some 500 fighters under the 24-year-old Mordechai Anielewicz, and the Jewish Military Union, about 250 more, held streets until 16 May while the SS burned the district block by block. Prisoners revolted at Treblinka on 2 August 1943 and at Sobibor in October. And the historian Emanuel Ringelblum had already begun burying the ghetto\'s own record of itself in tin boxes and milk cans.',
                'caption' => 'One of the milk churns in which the Ringelblum archive was buried in the Warsaw ghetto',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Brigade Piron, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'neighbours',
                'photo' => 'neighbours',
                'label' => 'Poles and Jews',
                'year' => '1941 - 1944',
                'title' => 'Rescuers, blackmailers and a burned barn',
                'body' => 'Both of these are true and neither cancels the other. Zegota, the Council to Aid Jews, was set up on 4 December 1942 and paid for by the government in London, the only rescue body in occupied Europe funded by a state. Irena Sendlerowa and her co-workers took several hundred children out of the ghetto. Jozef and Wiktoria Ulma and their six children were shot at Markowa on 24 March 1944 for hiding eight Jews. Other Poles blackmailed Jews in hiding, and the German-run Polish police helped hunt them. At Jedwabne on 10 July 1941 at least 340 Jewish neighbours were murdered, and the Institute of National Remembrance found the killers were local Polish men acting at German instigation.',
                'caption' => 'The monument at Markowa to Jozef and Wiktoria Ulma and their six children',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Dreamcatcher25, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'totals',
                'photo' => 'totals',
                'label' => 'The arithmetic',
                'year' => '1939 - 1945',
                'title' => 'Between five and six million, still argued over',
                'body' => 'The figure usually given is 5.6 to nearly 6 million Polish citizens killed, about three million of them Jews. The United States Holocaust Memorial Museum puts the non-Jewish Polish civilian dead at 1.8 to 1.9 million and the Jewish dead at at least three million, and the Institute of National Remembrance has revised its own totals more than once. None of it is settled: the pre-war population is uncertain, the records were made by the people doing the killing, and the borders moved under the survivors afterwards. The two columns are one country. What came out in 1945 was not what went in.',
                'caption' => 'The Umschlagplatz monument in Warsaw, on the siding where the deportations were loaded',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'visiting',
                'photo' => 'visiting',
                'label' => 'Going there',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'How to behave where the worst of it happened',
                'body' => 'Auschwitz-Birkenau is a burial ground, not an attraction. Entry costs nothing, but you need a card booked ahead at visit.auschwitz.org, the guided route takes about three and a half hours, and anything bigger than a small rucksack is turned away at the gate. Dress as you would for a funeral and do not photograph yourself there. Start at POLIN in Warsaw: it is about the thousand years that came before, not about the murder. The ghetto boundary is marked in the pavement at twenty-one points. Treblinka is a field of stones. Palmiry keeps its cemetery in the forest. The Katyn Museum is in the Warsaw Citadel.',
                'caption' => 'POLIN, the Museum of the History of Polish Jews, on ground the Warsaw ghetto covered',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Steven Lek, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A printed administrative map of central and southern Poland headed GENERALGOUVERNEMENT along the foot, with Warsaw, Krakow, Radom and Lublin marked as seats, county boundaries drawn as broken lines, railways as heavy lines, and a legend in Polish across the bottom of the sheet',
            'caption' => 'An administrative map of the General Government at a scale of 1:1,000,000, drawn in 1943. The title and the seats are German, the legend is Polish, and the map covers only the central zone. The lands annexed to the Reich lie off its western edge.',
            'credit' => 'Archiwum Glowne Akt Dawnych, the Central Archives of Historical Records in Warsaw, public domain',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The division of the country follows the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, which names the annexed provinces and the General Government under Hans Frank, and the Institute of National Remembrance and the Polish Press Agency history service dzieje.pl for the Soviet side: the elections staged on 22 October 1939 and the annexation by decrees of 1 and 2 November. The figure of roughly 50,000 killed in the Intelligenzaktion and the AB-Aktion together is the institute\'s, from its exhibition on the destruction of the Polish elites. Dzieje.pl gives about 40,000 for 1939 alone, so the two accountings are not the same and a round figure is used. Palmiry follows dzieje.pl and the Museum of Independence in Warsaw: 21 executions between December 1939 and July 1941, more than 1,700 people, the largest on 20 and 21 June 1940. That 183 people were arrested in Krakow on 6 November 1939, 155 of them from the Jagiellonian University, is the university\'s own figure and the institute\'s. Around twenty of them died. Katyn, the deportation waves and the figure of 330,000 to 340,000 deported follow the institute and dzieje.pl, which take the number from NKVD transport records. The Union of Siberian Deportees, an association of survivors, puts it far higher, at about 1.3 million. The ghettos, the ration of 1,125 calories a day, the 83,000 dead of hunger and disease, the deportation of about 265,000 people to Treblinka, the uprising of 19 April to 16 May 1943 and the figures for Operation Reinhard, Chelmno, Auschwitz and Majdanek all follow the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, as does the figure of at least 1.5 million Polish citizens sent to forced labour in the Reich. The decree of 15 October 1941 is quoted from the POLIN Museum\'s Polish Righteous portal, which also states plainly that the death penalty for helping Jews was applied outside Poland too, in Belarus, Ukraine and Serbia, so this page does not call it unique. That Zegota was the only state-funded rescue body in occupied Europe is POLIN\'s wording and the institute\'s. The number of children Irena Sendlerowa and her co-workers saved is not known: POLIN and the museum in Washington both write several hundred, and the figure of about 2,500 that is often quoted comes from a statement she and three colleagues made in 1979 about everything the network did. Jedwabne follows the decision closing the institute\'s own investigation, signed by the prosecutor Radoslaw Ignatiew on 30 June 2003, which establishes at least 340 victims, names local Polish men as the direct perpetrators, holds the crime to have been committed at German instigation, and rejects the older figure of 1,600 as highly improbable. The exhumation was stopped at the request of Jewish religious authorities, so no exact count exists. The Ringelblum archive follows the museum in Washington and the Jewish Historical Institute in Warsaw, which holds it: ten tin boxes, two milk churns and a metal cylinder, buried in three places, of which two were found, on 18 September 1946 and 1 December 1950. The visiting advice is taken from the Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial\'s own rules and from the city of Warsaw, which marked the ghetto boundary at twenty-one points in 2008. Title picture: the war cemetery at Palmiry in the Kampinos forest, photographed by Przemyslaw Jahr, public domain.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the history section',
    ],

    'borders' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The borders moved: Poland after 1945',
            'description' => 'What Tehran, Yalta and Potsdam decided with no Pole in the room, which cities were lost in the east, and how many people were moved west.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · Communist Poland · Topic 01',
            'title' => 'The borders',
            'title_accent' => 'moved',
            'lede' => 'Poland came out of the war on the winning side and several hundred kilometres west of where it went in. Ten frames on who decided that, what was lost, what was taken, and how many millions of people had to move so that the new map would fit. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Ten frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'tehran',
                'photo' => 'tehran',
                'label' => 'Tehran, 1943',
                'year' => 'November - December 1943',
                'title' => 'Three men move a country west',
                'body' => 'The last topic ended with the Red Army in Warsaw and the borders still open. They had been settled a year earlier. From 28 November to 1 December 1943 the three Allied leaders met at Tehran: Joseph Stalin of the Soviet Union, Winston Churchill for Britain and Franklin Roosevelt for the United States. They agreed that Poland\'s eastern border would follow the Curzon line, proposed in 1920 by the British foreign secretary Lord Curzon, and that Poland would be paid in German land in the west. Churchill later described moving three matchsticks west to show it. No Pole was in the room, and the Polish government in exile in London was not told.',
                'caption' => 'Stalin, Roosevelt and Churchill at the Soviet legation in Tehran, 1943',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, US Signal Corps, Library of Congress, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'potsdam',
                'photo' => 'potsdam',
                'label' => 'Yalta and Potsdam',
                'year' => 'February - August 1945',
                'title' => 'The line is drawn, and Poland is not asked',
                'body' => 'At Yalta, from 4 to 11 February 1945, the three put it on paper. Poland\'s eastern frontier was to follow the Curzon line, with departures of five to eight kilometres in its favour, and Poland was to receive substantial additions in the north and west, the western line left to a peace conference that never met. The Polish authority they dealt with was Moscow\'s own, the Polish Committee of National Liberation, proclaimed on 22 July 1944 and seated at Lublin, its State National Council chaired by Boleslaw Bierut. At Potsdam, from 17 July to 2 August 1945, the land east of the rivers Oder and western Neisse passed to Polish administration. Bierut was heard there. He did not sign.',
                'caption' => 'Churchill, Roosevelt and Stalin on the patio at Yalta, February 1945',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, United States government, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'kresy',
                'photo' => 'kresy',
                'label' => 'What was lost',
                'year' => '1939 - 1945',
                'title' => 'Wilno, Lwow and Grodno stop being Polish',
                'body' => 'What Poland gave up was the Kresy, the eastern borderlands of the republic between the wars. Wilno is now Vilnius, the capital of Lithuania. Lwow is now Lviv in Ukraine. Grodno is in Belarus. In the census of 1931, which asked people for their mother tongue rather than their nationality, Polish was given by 65.9 per cent of the inhabitants of Wilno and 63.5 per cent of those of Lwow. The Jan Karski Institute for War Losses puts the country at 389,700 square kilometres before the war and 311,700 after it, a net loss of 78,000, a fifth of what there had been. Roughly 180,000 went in the east and roughly 100,000 came in the west.',
                'caption' => 'Akademicka street in Lwow, photographed between 1925 and 1939',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Marek Munz, Polish State Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'recovered',
                'photo' => 'recovered',
                'label' => 'Recovered Territories',
                'year' => '1945 - 1948',
                'title' => 'A name that made a conquest sound like a homecoming',
                'body' => 'The new western lands were given a name: Ziemie Odzyskane, the Recovered Territories. On 13 November 1945 the government set up a Ministry of the Recovered Territories under Wladyslaw Gomulka, first secretary of the communist party. Recovered pointed back to the medieval Piast dukes who had once ruled some of this ground, and it hid two things: the German centuries in between, and the eastern loss these lands were payment for. Breslau became Wroclaw, Stettin became Szczecin, Allenstein became Olsztyn, and Danzig became Gdansk, though Danzig had not been part of Germany since 1920, when it was made a Free City under the League of Nations.',
                'caption' => 'The guide to the Exhibition of the Recovered Territories, Wroclaw, 1948',
                'credit' => 'Print, National Library of Poland, Polona, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'repatriation',
                'photo' => 'repatriation',
                'label' => 'Moved west',
                'year' => '1944 - 1959',
                'title' => 'The word for it was repatriation',
                'body' => 'On 9 September 1944 Edward Osobka-Morawski, who chaired the Polish Committee of National Liberation, signed agreements in Lublin with Nikita Khrushchev for Soviet Ukraine and Panteleimon Ponomarenko for Soviet Belarus. Soviet Lithuania followed on 22 September. Poles and Jews were to move west, Ukrainians and Belarusians east. Repatriation means going home, and almost nobody it was applied to was: they were leaving houses their families had lived in for generations. The Institute of National Remembrance calls the word propaganda and the thing expulsion. Official figures count 1.2 million. The historian Pawel Nalezniak puts the real number at about two million.',
                'caption' => 'A settlers\' wagon at the Depot History Centre in Wroclaw: We are going home',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Tom Ja, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'expulsion',
                'photo' => 'expulsion',
                'label' => 'The Germans go',
                'year' => '1944 - 1949',
                'title' => 'Twenty kilograms, and four hours to be gone',
                'body' => 'Close to ten million Germans lived in these provinces in 1939. In the winter of 1944 and 1945 millions fled or were evacuated ahead of the Red Army. In the summer of 1945, before Potsdam had agreed anything, the Polish Second Army cleared the border belt: at Bad Salzbrunn in Lower Silesia, now Szczawno-Zdroj, an order posted at six on the morning of 14 July gave the town until ten, twenty kilograms a head and no carts or animals. Organised transports followed, 2,189,286 people by the end of 1947. How many died is disputed: the West German statistical office said 2.25 million in 1958, a Federal Archives study about 600,000, and German and Polish historians now settle near 420,000.',
                'caption' => 'The order to the German inhabitants of Bad Salzbrunn, 14 July 1945',
                'credit' => 'Polish sector commander\'s order, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'vistula',
                'photo' => 'vistula',
                'label' => 'Operation Vistula',
                'year' => 'April - July 1947',
                'title' => 'Ukrainians and Lemkos moved inside Poland',
                'body' => 'In the south-east the Ukrainian Insurgent Army, the UPA, fought the Polish state. By 1945 it had killed about a hundred thousand Polish civilians in Volhynia, now western Ukraine, and in Eastern Galicia, on Polish estimates, and Polish units killed Ukrainians in return. By the end of 1946 some 480,000 Ukrainians had gone east. On 28 March 1947 General Karol Swierczewski, deputy defence minister, was killed in a UPA ambush in the Bieszczady. The deportation plan had gone to the security commission the day before. From 28 April Operation Vistula moved 140,575 Ukrainians and Lemkos, a Carpathian people, across the former German lands. Party members and Red Army veterans went too.',
                'caption' => 'Soldiers clearing a village in the Beskid mountains, spring 1947',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, Institute of National Remembrance, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'kielce',
                'photo' => 'kielce',
                'label' => 'The survivors',
                'year' => '1945 - 1947',
                'title' => 'Kielce, 4 July 1946, and the last exodus',
                'body' => 'Of some 3.35 million Polish Jews about 420,000 were still alive, most of them because they had spent the war in the Soviet Union, and they came back to houses other people were living in. On 4 July 1946 in Kielce, after a boy called Henryk Blaszczyk told a story about being held in a cellar by Jews, a crowd with soldiers and policemen in it attacked the Jewish committee building at Planty 7. The building had no cellar. The Institute of National Remembrance counted 37 Jews and three Poles killed. The museums print 42 and two. Some 60,000 people left the country in the three months that followed.',
                'caption' => 'Planty 7 in Kielce, where the pogrom of July 1946 took place',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Grzegorz Pietrzak, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.5',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'strangers',
                'photo' => 'strangers',
                'label' => 'A foreign city',
                'year' => '1945 - 1985',
                'title' => 'The library of Lwow moves into Breslau',
                'body' => 'Wroclaw filled with people from Lwow, and part of Lwow came with them. The Ossolineum, the library founded there in 1817 by Count Jozef Maksymilian Ossolinski, sent about thirty per cent of its pre-war collections west, the last transport in 1947. With them travelled the Panorama of Raclawice, fifteen metres high and a hundred and fourteen metres round, showing Polish peasants beating a Russian army. The communist authorities kept it rolled up until a rotunda opened on 14 June 1985. Stanislaw Kulczynski, last rector in Lwow, became the first in Wroclaw. Everyone else moved into German flats and used what they found in them. Polish has a word for that: poniemieckie, post-German.',
                'caption' => 'The Ossolineum in Wroclaw, where the library from Lwow settled',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Michal460, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'What is left',
                'year' => '1950 - today',
                'title' => 'One language, and German writing on the walls',
                'body' => 'In the census of 1931 about a third of the country gave a mother tongue other than Polish, and historians suspect the count flattered the Polish share. By 1950, in the words of the Institute of National Remembrance, a state of many nations had become a uniform one, which is why a visitor hears so little but Polish. The border took longer. East Germany accepted it at Zgorzelec on 6 July 1950, West Germany in the Warsaw Treaty of 7 December 1970 while reserving the question for a peace treaty, and a united Germany confirmed it on 14 November 1990. Go and look: the Depot History Centre in Wroclaw, the Dialogue Centre Upheavals in Szczecin, any wall where the paint is coming off.',
                'caption' => 'A pre-war barber\'s sign on Ksiecia Witolda street in Wroclaw, 2023',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Robert Niedzwiedzki, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A schematic map of the change of borders: Poland between the wars drawn in dashes, Poland after 1945 drawn solid and shifted west, with Vilnius, Grodno and Lviv outside the new state and Szczecin, Gdansk, Olsztyn and Wroclaw inside it',
            'caption' => 'Schematic: the republic of 1921 to 1939 in dashes, the Poland of 1945 solid. The frontiers are simplified and the arrows are approximate.',
            'labels' => [
