            ],
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'Tehran, Yalta and Potsdam follow the Institute of National Remembrance, the Polish Press Agency history service dzieje.pl, the Museum of the Second World War in Gdansk and the Museum of Polish History. The wording of the agreements is read from the Yalta communique and the Potsdam protocol in the Avalon Project at Yale Law School. The areas before and after the war come from the Jan Karski Institute for War Losses and the 1931 mother-tongue figures from the Central Statistical Library. The Ministry of the Recovered Territories follows Irena Siwinska of the Institute of National Remembrance, which calls the name itself propaganda. The Free City of Danzig follows niepodlegla.dzieje.pl. The transfers of Poles follow Pawel Nalezniak and Grzegorz Chajko in the institute\'s bulletin and the Sybir Memorial Museum. The expulsion of the Germans follows Stanislaw Jankowiak and the historian Maren Roeger for the Bavarian centre for political education, with the death toll given as the open dispute it is. Operation Vistula follows the institute and Grzegorz Motyka before the Sejm commission for national minorities in 2007, and the Senate resolution of 3 August 1990. Kielce follows the institute\'s prosecutors, POLIN and the Jewish Historical Institute. The Ossolineum and the Panorama of Raclawice follow the Ossolineum and the National Museum in Wroclaw. The treaties of 1950, 1970 and 1990 are checked against the institute and dzieje.pl. Title picture: a leaflet of the Polish Committee of National Liberation of 1944 calling for Polish border posts on the Oder, National Library of Poland, Polona, public domain.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the history section',
