    'rebuilding' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Rebuilding Warsaw from paintings and drawings',
            'description' => 'How Warsaw was put back after 1945, what the old town was copied from, what is genuinely old, and why UNESCO listed a reconstruction in 1980.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · Communist Poland · Topic 02',
            'title' => 'Rebuilding',
            'title_accent' => 'Warsaw',
            'lede' => 'Warsaw came out of the war as rubble, and the old town standing there today was put back from paintings, measured drawings and photographs. Ten frames on who decided to rebuild, what they copied, what is genuinely old, what was quietly invented, and what the same years built next door. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Ten frames · about twelve minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'rubble',
                'photo' => 'rubble',
                'label' => 'What was left',
                'year' => 'October 1944 - January 1945',
                'title' => 'A capital with almost nobody left in it',
                'body' => 'The last topic left millions of people moving into somebody else\'s cities. The capital was a different problem: it was barely there. Warsaw held about 1.5 million people before the war. The Museum of Warsaw counts some 160,000 afterwards. The map beside this frame, printed in 1949 from aerial photographs flown in 1945, shades every building by its damage and gives the total: 84 per cent. Much of that was deliberate. After the Warsaw Uprising of 1944, a 63-day revolt of the Polish underground against the German occupation, Heinrich Himmler, who commanded the SS, ordered on 9 October that the city disappear, and German squads burned the empty streets until January 1945.',
                'caption' => 'The Old Town Market Square in Warsaw in ruins, 1945',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Leonard Sempolinski, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'lodz',
                'photo' => 'decision',
                'label' => 'Or move to Lodz',
                'year' => 'January 1945',
                'title' => 'Bierut wanted the capital moved to Lodz',
                'body' => 'Rebuilding was not the obvious answer. Boleslaw Bierut, the communist who chaired the State National Council that Moscow had installed as Poland\'s provisional parliament, thought the damage too complete, and wanted the capital moved to Lodz, a textile city about a hundred and thirty kilometres away taken almost intact on 19 January 1945. Ministries were already working there. The Polish Press Agency history service dzieje.pl and the historian Przemyslaw Waingertner both put the decision with Joseph Stalin, the Soviet leader, who told the Poles on 22 January 1945 that Warsaw would be the capital. A government nobody had elected also needed something to point at.',
                'caption' => 'Polish Army officers in the ruins of central Warsaw, January 1945',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'bos',
                'photo' => 'bos',
                'label' => 'The office',
                'year' => '1945 - 1951',
                'title' => 'An office of 1,500 people to put a city back',
                'body' => 'Four weeks after the Soviet Red Army walked into the ruins, on 14 February 1945, the new communist authorities set up the Biuro Odbudowy Stolicy, the Warsaw Reconstruction Office, under the architect Roman Piotrowski. By July it had about 1,500 architects, engineers, surveyors and lawyers on its books. A decree Bierut signed on 26 October 1945 took every plot of land in Warsaw into the city\'s hands, which is what made planning on that scale possible and what Polish courts are still unpicking. The office worked until 1951, and UNESCO put its archive on the Memory of the World register in 2011.',
                'caption' => 'Architects of the Warsaw Reconstruction Office over a city plan',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Edward Falkowski, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'doctrine',
                'photo' => 'doctrine',
                'label' => 'The doctrine',
                'year' => '1945 - 1964',
                'title' => 'The conservator who broke his own rule',
                'body' => 'Conservation has a rule: look after what survives, do not build a copy, because a copy proves nothing about the past. Jan Zachwatowicz, who ran the historic architecture department at the office and was Poland\'s chief conservator from 1945, wrote in the journal Biuletyn Historii Sztuki i Kultury in 1946 that a nation and the monuments of its culture are one thing, and that since Poles could not accept having theirs torn away, they would rebuild them. He knew what he was breaking. The profession wrote the rule down in the Venice Charter of 1964: all reconstruction work should be ruled out a priori. Zachwatowicz signed that charter too.',
                'caption' => 'Jan Zachwatowicz, who set the rules the rebuilding followed',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Wladyslaw Miernicki, National Digital Archives, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'sources',
                'photo' => 'bellotto',
                'label' => 'What they copied',
                'year' => '1767 - 1939',
                'title' => 'A Venetian painter and 35,000 drawings',
                'body' => 'Rebuild from what, though. Bernardo Bellotto, a Venetian painter Poles call Canaletto, arrived in 1767 as court painter to Stanislaw August Poniatowski, the last king of Poland, and left a gallery of views of the city. Twenty-four survive, and the castle he painted has a room of them. He tidied up what he saw, so the office read them against the rest: inventories made before 1939, photographs, and the 35,000 drawings of the Polish Architecture Department, founded at the Warsaw University of Technology in 1922 by Professor Oskar Sosnowski. One of the people drawing there was the architect Maria Zachwatowicz, and the Sacramentine church in the New Town was rebuilt from her sheets.',
                'caption' => 'Warsaw from the terrace of the Royal Castle, painted in 1773',
                'credit' => 'Bernardo Bellotto, National Museum in Warsaw, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'bricks',
                'photo' => 'bricks',
                'label' => 'The brick campaign',
                'year' => '1945 - 1953',
                'title' => 'The whole nation builds its capital',
                'body' => 'From May 1945 the rebuilding had a slogan, The Whole Nation Builds Its Capital, and people gave money and Sundays to it. The bricks came from the German towns in the west that the last topic called the Recovered Territories. Demolition brigades took Wroclaw, the former Breslau, apart street by street and sent the bricks east by the hundred million. Szczecin and the smaller Silesian towns went the same way. Wroclaw paid for that: its own centre stood in ruins for years afterwards, and the brick trade ended in a fraud trial. Warsaw was rebuilt partly out of other Polish cities.',
                'caption' => 'Volunteers clearing rubble by the Jablonowski Palace, Warsaw 1945',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'honest',
                'photo' => 'honest',
                'label' => 'What is genuine',
                'year' => '1949 - 1960s',
                'title' => 'Old fronts, new flats, one century chosen',
                'body' => 'Be honest about what the old town is. The office rebuilt the look of the late eighteenth century, because that is the period the paintings and the inventories documented, so the nineteenth-century storeys and shopfronts were left out. Behind the painted fronts the flats are post-war flats, with post-war plumbing and rooms made bigger. What is genuinely old is mostly low down: cellars, ground floors, stretches of wall. Some of it is not a copy of anything. The Barbican, the brick gate in the walls, had gone long before the war and was raised again, in UNESCO\'s words, from historical and conservation studies rather than from records of the building itself.',
                'caption' => 'The Barbican of Warsaw going up in new brick, about 1953',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, Warsaw 1945-1970, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'castle',
                'photo' => 'castle',
                'label' => 'The castle',
                'year' => '1939 - 1984',
                'title' => 'The castle waited forty years for its turn',
                'body' => 'One building stayed a hole in the ground. The Royal Castle, where the Polish constitution of 3 May 1791 was voted through, burned under German shelling in September 1939, and German engineers drilled it for explosives and blew it up in September 1944. The communist leadership left the site empty for a quarter of a century. Only in 1971 did Edward Gierek, the first secretary of the communist party, agree to rebuild it, and then a committee under Stanislaw Lorentz, director of the National Museum in Warsaw, collected the money from the public. The shell was handed over in 1974 and the castle opened to visitors in 1984.',
                'caption' => 'The Royal Castle in Warsaw under reconstruction, 1972 or 1973',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'palace',
                'photo' => 'palace',
                'label' => 'The other Warsaw',
                'year' => '1950 - 1956',
                'title' => 'A socialist city goes up beside the old one',
                'body' => 'While the old town went back up, a different city was rising beside it. The Marszalkowska housing district, known as MDM, was built between 1950 and 1952 around Plac Konstytucji, Constitution Square, by a team under the architect Jozef Sigalin: a widened avenue, stone fronts, stone workers along the parapets. Then came the Palace of Culture and Science, agreed with the Soviet Union in April 1952, put up by Soviet builders at the Soviet government\'s expense and handed over on 21 July 1955. It carried Stalin\'s name until 17 November 1956. Warsaw still jokes that its terrace has the best view in the city, because it is the one place you cannot see the palace from.',
                'caption' => 'The Palace of Culture and Science going up in the early 1950s',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'How to read it',
                'year' => '1980 - today',
                'title' => 'UNESCO put a rebuilt town on the list',
                'body' => 'In 1980 UNESCO added the historic centre of Warsaw to the World Heritage List, calling it an outstanding example of a near-total reconstruction of a span of history covering the 13th to the 20th century. Its own file says the interiors were modernised and some houses were left unbuilt on purpose, so that the walls and the view from the river would show. Read the city that way: brick at knee height tends to be old, everything above it is the 1950s. 1980 was also the year the strike started in the Gdansk shipyard that the next topic is about.',
                'caption' => 'The Old Town Market Square in Warsaw, photographed in 2018',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Maksym Kozlenko, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'map' => [
            'alt' => 'A printed map of Warsaw from 1949 at a scale of 1 to 20,000, with the built-up areas shaded by how badly they were destroyed and the words 84 per cent of destruction printed across the bottom',
            'caption' => 'Warsaw at 1:20,000 with an inventory of the destruction, in Polish and English, 1949',
            'credit' => 'Main Office of Land Surveying, drawn up by L. Kowalski and F. Piatkowski, National Library of Poland, public domain',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The scale of the destruction follows the Museum of Warsaw and the city\'s own report on its war losses, drawn up in 2004 by a team under the historian Wojciech Falkowski from 62,000 questionnaires filled in by inhabitants in 1945 and 1946. The order of 9 October 1944 and the demolition squads follow Marek Getter in the bulletin of the Institute of National Remembrance. Lodz as the alternative capital follows the Polish Press Agency history service dzieje.pl and Przemyslaw Waingertner, who wrote the book on the city in those years. The Warsaw Reconstruction Office, its dates, its method and its archive follow UNESCO\'s own file on the Historic Centre of Warsaw and the Museum of Warsaw. The wording of the conservation rule is quoted from article 15 of the Venice Charter of 1964, published by ICOMOS. Bellotto\'s role follows UNESCO\'s file, which says the late-18th-century look was chosen because that period was the best documented. The measured drawings follow the Faculty of Architecture at the Warsaw University of Technology, which dates its Polish Architecture Department to 1922, and the piece on Maria Zachwatowicz published by the city of Warsaw. The bricks follow the historians of the demolition campaign, above all Gregor Thum on post-war Wroclaw and Adam Przywara for the Museum of Warsaw. The counts per city vary between them and are given here as an order of magnitude. The Palace of Culture and Science follows the palace\'s own history pages. The map is the 1949 destruction inventory published by the Main Office of Land Surveying and digitised by the National Library of Poland, and the figure of 84 per cent is read off the map itself. Title picture: Castle Square in Warsaw in 1945, with the toppled Sigismund\'s Column in the rubble, photographed by Leonard Sempolinski, Wikimedia Commons, public domain.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the history section',
    ],
    'solidarity' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Solidarity, 1980: ten million members',
            'description' => 'How a strike in the Gdansk shipyard in August 1980 produced a legal union of ten million people, and how martial law put it down in December 1981.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · Communist Poland · Topic 03',
            'title' => 'Solidarity',
            'title_accent' => '1980',
            'lede' => 'In August 1980 a sacked crane driver in Gdansk set off a strike that ended with a legal trade union of about ten million people. Sixteen months later the army put it down. Eleven frames on the revolts that failed first, the Polish pope, the boards at the shipyard gate, martial law, and the years spent underground. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Eleven frames · about thirteen minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'queues',
                'photo' => 'queues',
                'label' => 'Living with it',
                'year' => 'the 1970s',
                'title' => 'A country where the shop was the news',
                'body' => 'The last topic left Warsaw rebuilt and a government nobody had elected pointing at it. This frame is what that government felt like from inside. Edward Gierek, who ran the communist party through the 1970s, borrowed heavily in the West to fill the shops. When the loans came due the shops emptied again, and sugar went on ration cards in 1976. Every newspaper, poster and school magazine passed the censors on Mysia Street in Warsaw before it could be printed. One institution stayed outside the party, the Roman Catholic Church, which is why so much of what follows happens in a church or next to one.',
                'caption' => 'An empty shop counter of the 1970s, in the European Solidarity Centre',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Wojciech Pedzich, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'poznan',
                'photo' => 'poznan',
                'label' => 'Poznan, 1956',
                'year' => 'June 1956',
                'title' => 'Workers ask for bread, the army answers',
                'body' => 'It had been tried before. On 28 June 1956 the morning siren went at the Stalin Works in Poznan, the locomotive and machine factory that had been Cegielski\'s and is Cegielski\'s again, and the crew walked into town behind banners reading We demand bread. The government sent almost ten thousand soldiers and 359 tanks. Investigators at the Institute of National Remembrance, the state body that examines the communist period, put the dead at not fewer than 58 and say the count is still argued over. The youngest confirmed victim, Romek Strzalkowski, was thirteen. Wladyslaw Gomulka took the party over that October, promising a Polish road.',
                'caption' => 'Tanks on Stalin Square in Poznan during the rising of June 1956',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'march',
                'photo' => 'march',
                'label' => 'March 1968',
                'year' => '1968',
                'title' => 'A banned play, and then a purge',
                'body' => 'Twelve years on the trouble started with students. Dziady, or Forefathers\' Eve, a verse drama by the national poet Adam Mickiewicz, was staged by Kazimierz Dejmek at the National Theatre in Warsaw, drew anti-Russian applause, and was taken off after the performance of 30 January 1968. Two students who protested, Adam Michnik and Henryk Szlajfer, were struck off the rolls of Warsaw University, and the rally held for them on 8 March was broken up by police. Gomulka answered with an anti-Zionist campaign pushed by Mieczyslaw Moczar, a deputy interior minister. Polish Jews lost their jobs. About 15,000 left in 1968 and 1969, on a document that took their citizenship with it.',
                'caption' => 'The one-way travel document issued to Anna Trachtenherc, who left in 1968',
                'credit' => 'Polish People\'s Republic, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'coast',
                'photo' => 'coast',
                'label' => 'December 1970',
                'year' => 'December 1970',
                'title' => 'Price rises, and shooting on the coast',
                'body' => 'Two years later the quarrel was about money again. On 12 December 1970, a fortnight before Christmas, Gomulka went on radio and television to raise the price of 45 groups of goods. Shipyard workers in Gdansk struck on 14 December and the protests spread to Gdynia, Szczecin and Elblag. On the morning of Thursday 17 December soldiers opened fire on men stepping off the commuter train at Gdynia Shipyard station on their way to work. The list the authorities finally let be printed in January 1971 carried 44 names. The Polish Press Agency gives at least 45 dead and more than 1,100 wounded. On 20 December the party dropped Gomulka and made Edward Gierek first secretary.',
                'caption' => 'The Monument to the Fallen Shipyard Workers of 1970, raised in Gdansk in 1980',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Jan Mehlich, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'kor',
                'photo' => 'kor',
                'label' => 'Radom and Ursus',
                'year' => '1976',
                'title' => 'Two strikes, and a committee that stayed',
                'body' => 'Gierek\'s borrowed prosperity ran out. On 24 June 1976 the prime minister, Piotr Jaroszewicz, announced steep food price rises in the Sejm, the Polish parliament. Next day some 70,000 to 80,000 people stopped work, above all at the Ursus tractor plant outside Warsaw, in Radom and in Plock, and the rises were withdrawn within a day. Then came the arrests and the beatings Poles learned to call health paths. On 23 September fourteen writers, lawyers and academics, among them Jacek Kuron, founded the Workers\' Defence Committee, KOR, to pay the fines and find the lawyers. Michnik, expelled in 1968, argued their case from abroad.',
                'caption' => 'The memorial stone to June 1976 at Ursus, on the edge of Warsaw',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Rafal Klisowski, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'pope',
                'photo' => 'pope',
                'label' => 'A Polish pope',
                'year' => '1978 - 1979',
                'title' => 'Let your Spirit descend, and renew this land',
                'body' => 'Then came something no committee had planned. On 16 October 1978 the cardinals in Rome elected Karol Wojtyla, the archbishop of Krakow, as pope. He took the name John Paul II and was the first bishop of Rome from outside Italy since 1523. He came home from 2 to 10 June 1979. On the first day, saying mass on Victory Square in Warsaw, he ended his homily: Let your Spirit descend. Let your Spirit descend and renew the face of the earth, the face of this land. The Institute of National Remembrance puts that crowd at close to 200,000, the secret police at 150,000 to 170,000. People kept order themselves, and saw how many of them there were.',
                'caption' => 'John Paul II among the crowds on his first pilgrimage home, June 1979',
                'credit' => 'Photograph, www.gov.pl via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'august',
                'photo' => 'august',
                'label' => 'August 1980',
                'year' => 'August 1980',
                'title' => 'A sacked crane driver, and a shipyard',
                'body' => 'A year later the push came from the yards again. Anna Walentynowicz had driven a crane at the Lenin Shipyard in Gdansk for decades and handed out free-union leaflets. On 7 August 1980, five months short of her pension, she was dismissed. Her workmates struck on 14 August to get her back, wavered after two days, and stayed in when Lech Walesa, an electrician the yard had sacked in 1976, took over the committee. On 17 August two young activists, Arkadiusz Rybicki and Maciej Grzywaczewski, wrote 21 demands on sheets of shipyard plywood and hung them on Gate No 2. The first asked for free trade unions. Delegates from about 800 workplaces joined them.',
                'caption' => 'The 21 demands, written on shipyard plywood, shown at the European Solidarity Centre',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Wojciech Pedzich, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'carnival',
                'photo' => 'carnival',
                'label' => 'Sixteen months',
                'year' => '1980 - 1981',
                'title' => 'Ten million members, and a legal union',
                'body' => 'On 31 August 1980 the deputy prime minister, Mieczyslaw Jagielski, signed for the government in the shipyard, and three more agreements followed, at Szczecin, Jastrzebie-Zdroj and Huta Katowice. On 10 November the Supreme Court in Warsaw registered Solidarnosc, Solidarity, as an independent union. About ten million people joined, a figure that was always an estimate. Poles call the sixteen months that followed the carnival. They ended at Bydgoszcz: on 19 March 1981 police beat three union men, Jan Rulewski among them, at a session of the provincial council, and it was the primate, Cardinal Stefan Wyszynski, who talked both sides down.',
                'caption' => 'The Solidarity news service of 20 March 1981, reporting from Bydgoszcz',
                'credit' => 'Serwis Informacyjny Biura Krajowego, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'martial',
                'photo' => 'martial',
                'label' => '13 December',
                'year' => 'December 1981',
                'title' => 'The state declares war on its own union',
                'body' => 'A four-hour warning strike on 27 March 1981 stopped most of the country, but the general strike behind it never came. At six on Sunday morning, 13 December 1981, the radio carried General Wojciech Jaruzelski, who was prime minister, defence minister and party leader at once, announcing a state of war. A Military Council of National Salvation took over. Poles turned its initials, WRON, into wrona, a crow. Telephones went dead, travel needed a permit, telegrams came back stamped Censored. By the interior ministry\'s own count 3,173 people were interned in the first twenty-four hours. On 16 December riot police stormed the Wujek coal mine in Katowice and shot nine miners dead.',
                'caption' => 'A telegram of March 1982 about a family funeral, stamped by the censor',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Stiopa, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'underground',
                'photo' => 'underground',
                'label' => 'Underground',
                'year' => '1982 - 1988',
                'title' => 'Printed in cellars, honoured in Oslo',
                'body' => 'Solidarity was outlawed in October 1982 and carried on without permission: presses in flats, books brought in from Paris, a radio that broadcast for minutes at a time. Martial law was suspended on 31 December 1982 and lifted on 22 July 1983. In 1983 the Nobel committee gave Walesa the peace prize. He stayed at home rather than risk not being let back in, and his wife Danuta read his speech in Oslo. On 19 October 1984 three officers of the secret police took Father Jerzy Popieluszko, whose Warsaw sermons filled the street, from his car at Gorsk near Torun and killed him. His body came out of the Wloclawek reservoir on 30 October, and about 600,000 people came to the funeral.',
                'caption' => 'Books and journals printed in Poland without permission in the 1980s',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Julo, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'Still arguing',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'Where to stand, and what is still argued',
                'body' => 'Neither general nor electrician is settled history here. Jaruzelski said until his death in 2014 that martial law was the lesser evil that kept Soviet tanks out. The minutes of the Soviet leadership on 10 December 1981 have the KGB chief, Yuri Andropov, and the foreign minister, Andrei Gromyko, ruling any out. In 2017 forensic experts in Krakow found that files naming Walesa as an informer in the 1970s were genuine. He says they were forged. Go and look: the European Solidarity Centre at Gate No 2, the three crosses outside it, the cross at Wujek, the Popieluszko museum in Warsaw. In 1988 the yards struck again, and the government asked the banned union to sit down with it.',
                'caption' => 'The European Solidarity Centre beside the 1970 monument, at the Gdansk shipyard',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Gerda Arendt, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'Poznan 1956 follows the Institute of National Remembrance, whose Poznan investigators put the dead at not fewer than 58 while noting that historians still differ, and the Polish Press Agency history service dzieje.pl for the troops and the 359 tanks. March 1968, including the last performance of Dziady on 30 January and the figure of about 15,000 emigrants in 1968 and 1969, follows dzieje.pl and the POLIN Museum of the History of Polish Jews. December 1970 follows dzieje.pl and the Institute of National Remembrance, which explains the gap between the official list of 44 names published in January 1971 and the higher counts. June 1976, the health paths and the founding of KOR on 23 September 1976 with its fourteen members follow the Institute of National Remembrance. Adam Michnik was not among the fourteen. The election of 1978 and the wording of the homily of 2 June 1979 are taken from the Vatican\'s own text, which prints the appeal twice. The crowd figures are the Institute of National Remembrance and the interior ministry files it quotes. The dismissal of Anna Walentynowicz on 7 August 1980, the course of the strike and the four August agreements follow the Institute of National Remembrance. The boards, their plywood and the date of 17 August follow the National Maritime Museum, which owns them and lends them to the European Solidarity Centre, and UNESCO, which entered them on the Memory of the World register in 2003. The registration of 10 November 1980 was by the Supreme Court, not the provincial court that had altered the statute. The Bydgoszcz crisis and the strike of 27 March 1981 follow the Institute of National Remembrance. For martial law the figure of 3,173 internments in the first day is Grzegorz Majchrzak on ministry records, and the nine dead at Wujek are the Institute of National Remembrance. The Nobel Prize of 1983 and Danuta Walesa reading the speech are from the Nobel Foundation. The abduction at Gorsk, the recovery of the body on 30 October 1984 and the funeral of about 600,000 people follow the Institute of National Remembrance. The dispute over martial law is set out with the Soviet record of 10 December 1981 published by dzieje.pl and the Polish History Museum. The handwriting opinion of 2017 is the Institute of National Remembrance communique, and Lech Walesa\'s denial is recorded in it. The signature of Mieczyslaw Jagielski rests on a single source here and is flagged as such. The Roads to Freedom exhibition is often still listed by guidebooks: it ran from 2001 to 2014 and closed when the European Solidarity Centre opened on 30 August 2014. Title picture: Gate No 2 of the Gdansk Shipyard in 2024, photographed by Adrian Tync, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the history section',
    ],
    'june1989' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'June 1989: the election that ended communism',
            'description' => 'How a bankrupt government sat down with the union it had banned, what the Round Table agreed, and what happened when Poland voted on 4 June 1989.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · Communist Poland · Topic 04',
            'title' => 'June',
            'title_accent' => '1989',
            'lede' => 'On 4 June 1989 Poles were allowed to vote against their government in a third of the seats in one house of parliament. They took all but one of those seats, and within seven months the People\'s Republic had changed its name. Nine frames on why the state came to the table, what the deal actually was, and what Poles still argue about. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about eleven minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'broke',
                'photo' => 'broke',
                'label' => 'Out of money',
                'year' => '1987 - 1988',
                'title' => 'A state that could not pay for itself',
                'body' => 'The last topic ended in 1988, with the shipyards striking again and the government offering to talk to the union it had outlawed. Why would it? Because it was broke. General Wojciech Jaruzelski, the officer who had declared martial law in 1981 and still led the communist party, asked the country for permission to raise prices in a referendum on 29 November 1987. His own rules required more than half of everyone on the register to vote yes. Turnout was 67.32 per cent, the lowest the People\'s Republic had recorded up to then, and neither question cleared that bar. Prices went up in February 1988 anyway, by about a hundred per cent.',
                'caption' => 'A Polish meat ration card for August 1989, printed in grams per month',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Kgbo, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'talks',
                'photo' => 'talks',
                'label' => 'A villa, quietly',
                'year' => '1988 - 1989',
                'title' => 'A general and an electrician, in private',
                'body' => 'Strikes followed that spring and again in August. On 31 August 1988 the interior minister, General Czeslaw Kiszczak, who had run the police through martial law, met Lech Walesa, the Gdansk electrician who led the banned union Solidarity, in a ministry villa. From 16 September they talked at Magdalenka, a village south of Warsaw, with Catholic bishops in the room as witnesses. Moscow was not going to rescue anyone. Mikhail Gorbachev, the Soviet leader since 1985, had quietly dropped the rule that Soviet tanks would keep the neighbours communist.',
                'caption' => 'General Czeslaw Kiszczak, interior minister, in an official portrait of 1988',
                'credit' => 'Wojskowa Agencja Fotograficzna, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'table',
                'photo' => 'table',
                'label' => 'The Round Table',
                'year' => 'February - April 1989',
                'title' => 'Two months of talks at a circular table',
                'body' => 'The talks opened on 6 February 1989 in the Namiestnikowski Palace in Warsaw, then the seat of the government and today the Presidential Palace. More than fifty people sat at a table built as a circle so that nobody had the head of it. Kiszczak led for the government, Walesa for the opposition, and the Church sent observers. They signed on 5 April. Solidarity would be a legal union again, and there would be an election in June: 65 per cent of the seats in the Sejm, the lower house, reserved for the party and its two allies, 35 per cent open to anyone, a new upper house of 100 freely elected senators, and a restored presidency that everyone understood was meant for Jaruzelski.',
                'caption' => 'The plenary session of the Round Table in Warsaw, February or April 1989',
                'credit' => 'Institute of National Remembrance, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'campaign',
                'photo' => 'campaign',
                'label' => 'Eight weeks',
                'year' => 'April - May 1989',
                'title' => 'A newspaper, and a photograph with Walesa',
                'body' => 'Solidarity had eight weeks and no party machine. It built Citizens\' Committees, local groups that picked the candidates and ran the campaign out of borrowed rooms. The Warsaw one worked from a cafe called Niespodzianka, the Surprise. Every candidate was photographed beside Walesa, so that a voter who had never heard of the man on the poster could see who vouched for him. On 8 May the first issue of Gazeta Wyborcza appeared, eight pages, 150,000 copies, edited by Adam Michnik, a writer the police had been arresting since 1968. Its masthead carried the union\'s lettering, the thick red letters Jerzy Janiszewski drew in the Gdansk shipyard in 1980.',
                'caption' => 'The plaque on plac Konstytucji in Warsaw marking the Niespodzianka cafe',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'vote',
                'photo' => 'vote',
                'label' => 'The fourth of June',
                'year' => '4 June 1989',
                'title' => 'The Sunday the country voted them out',
                'body' => 'At dawn on Sunday 4 June ten thousand copies of a poster went up on Warsaw walls: Gary Cooper walking out of the western High Noon with a ballot paper in his hand instead of a revolver and a Solidarity badge above the sheriff\'s star. Tomasz Sarnecki, a 23-year-old art student, had drawn it, and every committee he showed it to called it too confrontational, so it was printed abroad and flown in the night before. Sixty-two per cent of voters turned out. Solidarity took 160 of the 161 Sejm seats it was allowed to contest, and 92 of the 100 in the Senate. The government coalition won no Senate seat at all.',
                'caption' => 'A 1989 election notice still in a gateway on Stalowa Street in Warsaw',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'list',
                'photo' => 'list',
                'label' => 'The national list',
                'year' => 'June 1989',
                'title' => 'Thirty-five names, and two survivors',
                'body' => 'One ballot paper was different. A national list carried 35 senior figures of the coalition, unopposed, needing only half the votes cast. Voters crossed them out. Two got through: Mikolaj Kozakiewicz, who went on to chair the new Sejm, and Adam Zielinski. That left 33 seats empty, with nothing in the agreement to fill them. The parties amended the election law between the rounds and moved those seats into the districts. The second round on 18 June drew a quarter of the electorate. Solidarity ended with 99 of the 100 senators. It lost only in Pila province.',
                'caption' => 'An observer\'s pass for a Wroclaw polling district, issued on 29 May 1989',
                'credit' => 'Julo, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'handover',
                'photo' => 'handover',
                'label' => 'Your president',
                'year' => 'July - August 1989',
                'title' => 'Your president, our prime minister',
                'body' => 'None of this had been a free election: two thirds of the Sejm was never in play, and in Beijing that same Sunday the Chinese army was clearing Tiananmen Square. The presidency was Jaruzelski\'s by agreement, and the new parliament could now refuse it. On 3 July Michnik printed a way through in Gazeta Wyborcza: your president, our prime minister. On 19 July the National Assembly, the two houses sitting together, elected Jaruzelski by 270 votes to 233, one more than he needed, because Solidarity members abstained or stayed away. Jaruzelski made Kiszczak prime minister on 2 August, and Kiszczak could not form a government.',
                'caption' => 'The Sejm letter appointing Tadeusz Mazowiecki prime minister, 24 August 1989',
                'credit' => 'Sejm of the Republic of Poland, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'government',
                'photo' => 'government',
                'label' => 'A thick line',
                'year' => 'August - December 1989',
                'title' => 'A government the party did not run',
                'body' => 'The two parties that had always voted with the communists, the peasants\' ZSL and the democrats\' SD, crossed the floor to Solidarity on 17 August. A week later the Sejm made Tadeusz Mazowiecki prime minister by 378 votes to 4: a Catholic editor who had advised the shipyard strikers in 1980, and the first head of government in the Soviet bloc who was not a communist. Kiszczak stayed on as interior minister. Mazowiecki said his government would draw a thick line under the past and answer only for what it did next, and Poles have argued about that sentence ever since. In December it passed ten laws drafted by his finance minister, Leszek Balcerowicz. What they cost is the next story.',
                'caption' => 'Mazowiecki and chancellor Helmut Kohl at a mass of reconciliation, November 1989',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Andrzej Slusarczyk, Krzyzowa Foundation, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'argued',
                'photo' => 'argued',
                'label' => 'Still arguing',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'What Poles have not agreed on since',
                'body' => 'The rest of the bloc went in months: the Berlin Wall was opened on 9 November, Czechoslovakia\'s government fell, and Nicolae Ceausescu, the Romanian dictator, was shot at Christmas. Poland had gone first. From 31 December the People\'s Republic was the Republic of Poland again, with the crown back on its eagle. None of it is settled. One side calls the Round Table a bloodless handover, the other a deal that shielded the men who had run the old state, and the files of the secret police are still fought over in court. The table itself left the Presidential Palace in December 2025 for the Museum of Polish History.',
                'caption' => 'The Museum of Polish History at the Warsaw Citadel, which now holds the table',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Rafal Chmielewski, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The referendum of 29 November 1987 follows the Institute of National Remembrance, which prints the two questions and the rule that a yes needed more than half of everyone on the register, and the official result published in Monitor Polski: 26,201,169 people entitled to vote, 17,638,483 votes cast, 67.32 per cent. The strikes of 1988, the first Kiszczak and Walesa meeting of 31 August 1988 and the Magdalenka talks from 16 September follow the Polish Press Agency history service dzieje.pl. The Round Table dates, the 65 per cent reserved for the coalition and the freely elected Senate follow dzieje.pl. The photograph of the plenary session is held by the Institute of National Remembrance under reference IPN BU 3333/89 and its own catalogue dates it 6 February or 5 April 1989, which is why the caption says February or April. The first issue of Gazeta Wyborcza on 8 May 1989, its eight pages and its 150,000 copies follow dzieje.pl and the weekly Polityka. The masthead line, there is no freedom without Solidarity, is Lech Walesa\'s. The poster is described rather than shown, because it is still in copyright: the print run of ten thousand, the printing abroad and its appearance at dawn on 4 June follow the Institute of National Remembrance and an interview Tomasz Sarnecki gave dzieje.pl. The results of both rounds, the collapse of the national list, the two candidates who survived it and the change to the election law between the rounds follow dzieje.pl. The election of Wojciech Jaruzelski on 19 July 1989 by 270 votes to 233 with 34 abstentions follows the Institute of National Remembrance and the resolution of the National Assembly printed in Monitor Polski. The formula your president, our prime minister is Adam Michnik\'s, in Gazeta Wyborcza of 3 July 1989. The coalition of 17 August 1989 follows the Polish History Museum. Tadeusz Mazowiecki was appointed prime minister on 24 August 1989 by 378 votes to 4 and his government was confirmed on 12 September by 402 votes of 415. The thick line sentence is quoted from the full text of his speech published by dzieje.pl. That prices in 1989 ended at three and a half times their 1988 level is the annual consumer price index of the Central Statistical Office, which gives 351.1 against 1988 as 100. The Balcerowicz laws were passed on 28 December 1989 and took effect on 1 January 1990. The constitutional amendment was passed on 29 December and came into force on 31 December. The lettering of the Solidarity mark, thick red letters leaning on one another like a crowd, was drawn by Jerzy Janiszewski in the Gdansk shipyard in August 1980, and the union publishes his account of it. The Round Table stood in the Presidential Palace from 2004 until December 2025, when President Karol Nawrocki lent it to the Museum of Polish History at the Warsaw Citadel. The Chancellery remains its owner, the museum showed it on its own from 13 February to 17 May 2026, and it enters the permanent exhibition in 2027. Title picture: the Round Table photographed in the Presidential Palace in 2019 by Adrian Grycuk, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the history section',
    ],
    'transition' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'The transition: what 1990 cost Poland',
            'description' => 'What the Balcerowicz plan did from 1 January 1990, how unemployment appeared out of nothing, and why Poles still argue about that decade.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · After 1989 · Topic 01',
            'title' => 'Nineteen',
            'title_accent' => 'ninety',
            'lede' => 'On 1 January 1990 Poland changed the rules of its economy overnight, and the whole country found out what that felt like on the same morning. The shops filled, the prices tripled, and a word nobody here had needed for forty years, unemployment, turned up within months. Nine frames on what changed, who paid for it, and why Poles still cannot agree whether it was a rescue or a robbery. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about ten minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'plan',
                'photo' => 'plan',
                'label' => 'The first of January',
                'year' => '1989 - 1990',
                'title' => 'The ten laws that changed the money',
                'body' => 'The last topic ended with those ten laws. The Sejm, the lower house of parliament, passed them on 28 December 1989 and they took effect three days later. They were drafted by Leszek Balcerowicz, deputy prime minister and finance minister under Tadeusz Mazowiecki, the first prime minister in the Soviet bloc who was not a communist. Most shop prices had already been freed during 1989. These laws let the rest go and pushed up the ones the state still fixed: coal, gas, electricity, rail fares. Subsidies ended. The zloty became exchangeable for hard currency inside Poland at a rate fixed to the dollar, and a tax called the popiwek punished any firm that raised wages above a set norm.',
                'caption' => 'A one-cent PeKaO voucher, the dollar money Poles shopped with before 1990',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Aw58, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'shops',
                'photo' => 'shops',
                'label' => 'Full shelves',
                'year' => '1990',
                'title' => 'The shops filled and the prices tripled',
                'body' => 'Poles woke up to shops that had things in them and to prices they could not pay. The Central Statistical Office, the government\'s own statisticians, measured a rise of 79.6 per cent in January 1990 alone. Over the whole year it put consumer prices 585.8 per cent above 1989, after a rise of 251.1 per cent during 1989 itself, and another 70.3 per cent in 1991. Savings kept in zlotys came out worth a fraction of what went in. Real wages fell by roughly a quarter. The queues that had defined the 1980s simply stopped, because what rationed the goods now was the price.',
                'caption' => 'Polish tram and railway tickets of 1989 and 1991, priced in old zlotys',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Felix O, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 2.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'jobs',
                'photo' => 'jobs',
                'label' => 'A new word',
                'year' => '1990 - 1994',
                'title' => 'Unemployment, which had not officially existed',
                'body' => 'Communist Poland had guaranteed everyone a job and reported no unemployment at all, so the word arrived with no habits attached to it. The Central Statistical Office counted 1,126,100 people registered as unemployed at the end of 1990, a rate of 6.5 per cent. A year later it was 2,155,600 and 12.2 per cent, and by the end of 1993 it was 2,889,600 and 16.4 per cent. It peaked at 16.9 per cent in July 1994. The Ursus tractor works outside Warsaw, which had turned out about fifty thousand tractors in 1983, shed most of its people. The Gdansk Shipyard, where the strike of August 1980 had begun, was declared bankrupt by a court on 8 August 1996.',
                'caption' => 'New Ursus tractors lined up outside the works near Warsaw in the 1970s',
                'credit' => 'Photo: E. Moscicki, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'land',
                'photo' => 'land',
                'label' => 'The state farms',
                'year' => '1991 - 1995',
                'title' => 'When the state farms simply stopped',
                'body' => 'Factories were half of it. The other half were the panstwowe gospodarstwa rolne, the state farms: enormous holdings worked for wages rather than by families, most of them on land in the north and the west that Poland took over in 1945. A law of 19 October 1991 handed them to a new body, the Agricultural Property Agency, which by the end of 1995 had absorbed 1,667 wound-up farms. A study published by the University of Warsaw counts about 475,000 people working them in the 1980s and 123,000 left in December 1995. The agency also inherited 328,000 flats, because a state farm was usually a whole village with one employer.',
                'caption' => 'The ruins of a state farm at Kozia Gora, photographed in July 2010',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Keller OE, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'bazaar',
                'photo' => 'bazaar',
                'label' => 'Camp beds and stalls',
                'year' => '1989 - 2007',
                'title' => 'A country that traded out of car boots',
                'body' => 'What a great many people did instead was trade. Goods came back in car boots from Berlin and Istanbul and went out again from camp beds unfolded on the pavement. The largest version of this was the Stadion Dziesieciolecia in Warsaw, the Tenth Anniversary Stadium, raised in 1955 out of the rubble of the destroyed city and by now falling apart. Traders spread across its terraces from 1989. From 1996 the market was called Jarmark Europa, the Europe Fair, and ran to some five thousand stalls. Polish newspapers called it the biggest open-air market in Europe. The last day of trading was 30 September 2007.',
                'caption' => 'The last day of trading at the Stadion Dziesieciolecia, September 2007',
                'credit' => 'Photo: masti, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'shares',
                'photo' => 'shares',
                'label' => 'Fifteen funds',
                'year' => '1993 - 1998',
                'title' => 'A share certificate for every adult',
                'body' => 'Selling off the state\'s companies was the other half of the plan, and the part most Poles held in their hands. A law of 30 April 1993 put 512 state enterprises into fifteen National Investment Funds. From November 1995 any adult could pay 20 zlotys and collect a universal share certificate, a bearer paper standing for a slice of all fifteen funds at once. Nearly 25.9 million were issued, about 96 per cent of the people entitled to one. The certificate traded at around 100 zlotys at its best, and most people sold theirs quickly. Nobody now describes the scheme as having made Poland a nation of shareholders.',
                'caption' => 'A universal share certificate of the investment funds, dated 10 April 1995',
                'credit' => 'Polish State Treasury, Wikimedia Commons, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'politics',
                'photo' => 'politics',
                'label' => 'The camp splits',
                'year' => '1990 - 1997',
                'title' => 'The side that won 1989 turned on itself',
                'body' => 'Politics came apart while this was happening. In the summer of 1990 Lech Walesa, the Gdansk electrician who had led the Solidarity union, declared what Poles still call the war at the top and went after the government his own movement had installed. Tadeusz Mazowiecki stood against him for president and finished third, behind Stan Tyminski, a businessman nobody had heard of who had flown in from Canada. Walesa won the run-off on 9 December 1990 with 74.25 per cent. The first fully free parliamentary election came on 27 October 1991: 43.2 per cent of voters turned out, and candidates from 29 separate committees reached the Sejm.',
                'caption' => 'President Lech Walesa in his Warsaw office, photographed on 3 March 1992',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Witold Krassowski, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'ledger',
                'photo' => 'ledger',
                'label' => 'The argument',
                'year' => '1993 - 1999',
                'title' => 'Poles voted the communists\' heirs back in',
                'body' => 'On 19 September 1993, in a wholly free election, the Democratic Left Alliance, built out of the communist party that had run the country, took 171 of the 460 seats in the Sejm and made Waldemar Pawlak of the peasants\' party prime minister. In 1995 its own leader, Aleksander Kwasniewski, beat Walesa for the presidency. That was a verdict on the cost, delivered by people who could now vote either way. Balcerowicz argues that only a shock could have stopped the inflation and that the growth after 1992 rests on it. Grzegorz Kolodko, finance minister from 1994, argues the January jump was far larger than the government\'s own forecast and that the poorest carried it.',
                'caption' => 'The Julia coal mine in Walbrzych, closed in the 1990s, now a museum',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Marek Mroz, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'today',
                'photo' => 'today',
                'label' => 'What you can see',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'How the 1990s look from a pavement now',
                'body' => 'Output climbed back above its 1989 level somewhere around the middle of the decade, and the rules were finally settled by a new constitution, voted through in a referendum on 25 May 1997. Some towns never caught up. Nowa Huta in Krakow and Ursus in Warsaw are districts built around one works that is now a fraction of its old size. In Warmia or western Pomerania the long empty cowsheds beside a block of flats were a state farm. The towers in central Warsaw, none of them standing in 1989, are what the decade eventually paid for. Ask a Pole about the 1990s and you get the rescue or the robbery, rarely both. What came next was NATO in 1999 and the European Union in 2004.',
                'caption' => 'The towers of central Warsaw in 2024, with cranes still at work on more',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Jakub Zerdzicki, Unsplash',
            ],
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The ten laws of the stabilisation package, passed by the Sejm on 28 December 1989 and in force from 1 January 1990, and the rises in the prices the state still fixed, follow the history service of the Polish Press Agency, dzieje.pl. That most consumer prices had already been freed before the package, 86 per cent of consumer sales by December 1989, comes from Grzegorz Kolodko\'s book Polityka finansowa, stabilizacja, transformacja, published by the TIGER centre at Kozminski University, which is also where his criticism is quoted from, including the government\'s forecast of a 45 per cent price rise for January 1990. The actual rise of 79.6 per cent that month, and the annual consumer price indices of 351.1 for 1989, 685.8 for 1990 and 170.3 for 1991 against the previous year as 100, are the published series of the Central Statistical Office. So are the registered unemployment figures: 1,126,100 people and 6.5 per cent in December 1990, 2,155,600 and 12.2 per cent in December 1991, 2,889,600 and 16.4 per cent in December 1993, and the peak of 16.9 per cent in July 1994. The fall in real wages during 1990 is given as anything between 24 and 27 per cent depending on whether net wages or real incomes are measured, which is why this page says roughly a quarter. The law of 19 October 1991 on the management of agricultural property of the State Treasury is Dziennik Ustaw 1991 number 107 item 464 in the Sejm\'s legal database. The 1,667 wound-up state farms, the employment of about 475,000 people in the 1980s and 123,000 in December 1995, and the 328,000 flats, come from E. Gorzelak\'s chapter on state farming and regional development in the University of Warsaw regional studies volume Przemiany polskiej przestrzeni. The same chapter names the provinces where the unemployment that followed was worst: Koszalin, Slupsk, Olsztyn and Suwalki, and the eastern part of Szczecin province. Poland redrew its provinces on 1 January 1999, so those names do not map onto the present ones, and this page describes the areas instead. The law on national investment funds is Dziennik Ustaw 1993 number 44 item 202, of 30 April 1993. The fifteen funds, the 512 companies, the fee of 20 zlotys, the almost 25.9 million certificates issued and the market price of around 100 zlotys come from Polish press reporting of the scheme and from dzieje.pl, which also records that it ran from December 1994 to 1998 and did not deliver the mass ownership it promised. The bankruptcy of the Gdansk Shipyard on 8 August 1996 is recorded in a report of the Supreme Audit Office and in a European Commission state aid decision. Trading at the Stadion Dziesieciolecia began in 1989, the name Jarmark Europa dates only from 1996, and the lease ended on 30 September 2007. The stadium was pulled down afterwards for the National Stadium. The presidential results of 1990, Lech Walesa\'s 74.25 per cent in the run-off of 9 December and Stanislaw Tyminski finishing ahead of Tadeusz Mazowiecki in the first round, follow dzieje.pl and the State Electoral Commission. The turnout of 43.20 per cent on 27 October 1991 is from the commission\'s own announcement, and the 1993 seat counts of 171 and 132 follow Polish Radio. The constitution was adopted by the National Assembly on 2 April 1997, approved in the referendum of 25 May 1997 by a little over half of those who voted on a turnout of under 43 per cent, and came into force on 17 October 1997. The year national output passed its 1989 level again depends on which estimate of the 1990 fall is used, 7.2 or 11.6 per cent, which puts it in 1995 or in 1996. This page says the middle of the decade rather than pick one. Title picture: the terraces of the Stadion Dziesieciolecia in Warsaw in August 2008, a trader\'s stencil still on the concrete, photographed by Piotr Waglowski, Wikimedia Commons, public domain.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the history section',
    ],
    'euro' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'NATO in 1999, the EU in 2004',
            'description' => 'Why Poland wanted into NATO and the European Union, how it got in, and what the two dates paid for: motorways, open borders and emigration.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · After 1989 · Topic 02',
            'title' => 'Nineteen ninety-nine,',
            'title_accent' => 'two thousand and four',
            'lede' => 'Almost everything a visitor notices about Poland today dates from the two mornings when it got into two clubs. NATO took it in on 12 March 1999, the European Union on 1 May 2004, and between those dates the country rebuilt its army, rewrote its law, took the barriers off its western border and watched a million people, then two million, go to work abroad. Nine frames on how it happened and what it cost. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about ten minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'grey',
                'photo' => 'grey',
                'label' => 'The empty middle',
                'year' => '1989 - 1993',
                'title' => 'A country between two systems, alone',
                'body' => 'The last topic ended with the constitution of 1997 and with two dates still ahead. Both grew out of one worry. The Warsaw Pact, the Soviet-led military alliance Poland had belonged to since 1955, dissolved itself on 1 July 1991, and the Soviet Union ended that December. Poland was left in what its own diplomats called a grey zone: no alliance, no guarantee, and Soviet troops still on its soil, where they had been without a break since 1944. A treaty signed in Moscow on 22 May 1992, during a visit by President Lech Walesa, set the terms for their leaving. The last combat unit drove out on 28 October 1992, the last soldiers took a train out of Warsaw on 18 September 1993.',
                'caption' => 'Russian Su-27 fighters leaving the base at Stargard, 10 July 1992',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Rob Schleiffert, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 2.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'door',
                'photo' => 'door',
                'label' => 'The door opens',
                'year' => '1994 - 1997',
                'title' => 'Poland knocks, and the alliance opens slowly',
                'body' => 'NATO, the North Atlantic Treaty Organisation, was the alliance western Europe had built with the United States in 1949, and it had never taken in a former opponent. Its first offer was smaller than membership. The Partnership for Peace, launched at a NATO summit in Brussels in January 1994, let outsiders train and plan with the alliance while promising them no defence at all. Poland signed its framework document on 2 February 1994, the first country to do so, and the prime minister, Waldemar Pawlak, signed it himself. Three and a half years later the allies met in Madrid and, in a declaration of 8 July 1997, invited the Czech Republic, Hungary and Poland to begin accession talks.',
                'caption' => 'The flags of the member states at NATO headquarters in Brussels, 2016',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Adrian Cadiz, US Department of Defense, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'missouri',
                'photo' => 'missouri',
                'label' => 'A town in Missouri',
                'year' => '1997 - 1999',
                'title' => 'Poland joins NATO in small-town Missouri',
                'body' => 'The accession protocols were signed in Brussels on 16 December 1997, and every member had to ratify them. The Sejm, the lower house of the Polish parliament, authorised ratification on 11 February 1999 by 409 votes to 7. The ceremony was held on 12 March 1999 at the Harry S. Truman Presidential Library in Independence, Missouri, because the United States keeps the founding treaty, which Truman signed in 1949. Bronislaw Geremek, Poland\'s foreign minister and by training a medieval historian, handed the accession documents to Madeleine Albright, the American secretary of state. Jan Kavan of the Czech Republic and Janos Martonyi of Hungary handed over theirs.',
                'caption' => 'The Harry S. Truman Presidential Library in Independence, Missouri',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Jim Bowen, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'army',
                'photo' => 'army',
                'label' => 'What it changed',
                'year' => '1999 - today',
                'title' => 'The conscript army was wound up',
                'body' => 'Membership meant rebuilding the army to NATO\'s standards rather than Soviet ones, and that was expensive. Poland signed a contract for 48 American F-16 fighters on 18 April 2003. It sent soldiers to Afghanistan from 2002, where the defence ministry\'s own magazine, Polska Zbrojna, counts about 28,000 of them over the years, and to Iraq from 2003, where Poland was given one of the occupation sectors to run. The conscript army went too: the last compulsory call-up was in 2008, none were issued from January 2009, and conscription was suspended in law from 1 January 2010. In June 2022 President Joe Biden announced a permanent American army command post in Poznan.',
                'caption' => 'A Polish F-16 taxiing past an American one at Lask air base, March 2014',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Ryan Conroy, US Air Force, public domain',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'road',
                'photo' => 'road',
                'label' => 'The long list',
                'year' => '1991 - 2002',
                'title' => 'Nine years spent rewriting Polish law',
                'body' => 'The European Union wanted more than an army. Poland signed an association agreement with what was then the European Communities on 16 December 1991, in force from February 1994, and the foreign minister, Andrzej Olechowski, handed in the application in Athens on 8 April 1994. What counted was the list the European Council set in Copenhagen in June 1993: a stable democracy under the rule of law, a working market economy, and all of Union law taken on. The law on courts, food and state aid was rewritten, and the eastern border became the Union\'s outer edge: visas for Belarusians, Russians and Ukrainians began on 1 October 2003. Talks ran from 31 March 1998 to 13 December 2002.',
                'caption' => 'Polish border guards checking the Moscow train at Terespol, May 2008',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Foma, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'treaty',
                'photo' => 'treaty',
                'label' => 'Athens and home',
                'year' => '2003',
                'title' => 'Two days of voting, and a yes',
                'body' => 'The accession treaty was signed on 16 April 2003 in Athens, in the Stoa of Attalos, a rebuilt ancient colonnade on the edge of the old market place. Then Poles had to agree to it. The referendum ran over two days, 7 and 8 June 2003, because the result only bound the country if more than half the electorate voted, and nobody was sure one day would manage it. It did. The State Electoral Commission recorded a turnout of 58.85 per cent, with 77.45 per cent voting yes and 22.55 per cent voting no. About 17.6 million people took part.',
                'caption' => 'The Stoa of Attalos in Athens, where the accession treaty was signed',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Julian Lupyan, Wikimedia Commons, CC0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'firstmay',
                'photo' => 'firstmay',
                'label' => 'The first of May',
                'year' => '2004 - 2017',
                'title' => 'Ten states join, and the young leave',
                'body' => 'Poland entered the European Union on 1 May 2004 with nine other countries: Cyprus, the Czech Republic, Estonia, Hungary, Latvia, Lithuania, Malta, Slovakia and Slovenia. Most older members kept their labour markets shut to the newcomers for years. Ireland, Sweden and the United Kingdom opened theirs at once, and that decided where people went. The Central Statistical Office, the government\'s own statisticians, estimates about a million Poles living temporarily abroad at the end of 2004 and about 2,540,000 at the end of 2017, its highest figure. In Britain alone the estimate rose from 150,000 to 793,000. It calls these approximations rather than hard counts.',
                'caption' => 'A Polish shop on the high street in West Ealing, London, in 2015',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Philafrenzy, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'money',
                'photo' => 'money',
                'label' => 'What it paid for',
                'year' => '2004 - today',
                'title' => 'The money you are driving on',
                'body' => 'Money moved the other way. The Polish finance ministry counts 245.5 billion euros received from the Union budget between 1 May 2004 and the end of 2023, against 83.7 billion paid in, and roughly two thirds of what came in went on building things. The roads authority, GDDKiA, had about 720 kilometres of motorway and expressway on the day Poland joined and 5,593 kilometres in August 2026. Border checks on land ended on 21 December 2007, when Poland entered the Schengen area. Poland has chaired the Council of the Union twice, in 2011 and in 2025. You can see where the rest went without trying: a blue board with twelve yellow stars stands beside half the building sites in the country.',
                'caption' => 'The A4 motorway where it runs through Krakow, June 2024',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Jakub Halun, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'argument',
                'photo' => 'argument',
                'label' => 'The argument',
                'year' => '2015 - today',
                'title' => 'A hard row with Brussels, and what it left',
                'body' => 'None of this was quiet. From 2015 Polish governments changed the way judges are appointed and disciplined. The European Commission, which runs the Union day to day, held that this broke the rule of law, opened the procedure under Article 7 of the Union treaty in December 2017, and held money back. On 29 February 2024 it released about 137 billion euros to Poland, and on 29 May 2024 it closed the procedure. Poland also kept its own currency. It is bound by treaty to take the euro one day, but no date is fixed and no government is in a hurry. Support held: the polling institute CBOS found 82 per cent in favour of membership in February 2026, and 89 per cent for NATO in April 2025.',
                'caption' => 'Caryatids at the back of the Supreme Court building in Warsaw',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Wojciech Mula, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The dissolution of the Warsaw Pact on 1 July 1991, the treaty signed in Moscow on 22 May 1992 during Lech Walesa\'s visit, the departure of the last combat unit on 28 October 1992 and of the last soldiers from Warsaw on 18 September 1993, with the farewell at the Belweder palace the day before, follow the history service of the Polish Press Agency, dzieje.pl, and the Institute of National Remembrance. The same service gives the strength of the Soviet garrison, set at 62,000 to 66,000 men by the agreement of 1956 and down to about 48,000 by the end of 1990. The launch of the Partnership for Peace in January 1994 and Poland\'s signature of its framework document on 2 February 1994 are on NATO\'s own site, which also publishes the Madrid Declaration of 8 July 1997 with the sentence inviting the Czech Republic, Hungary and Poland to begin accession talks. The accession protocols of 16 December 1997, the Sejm vote of 11 February 1999 and the ceremony of 12 March 1999 come from dzieje.pl. That Jan Kavan of the Czech Republic and Janos Martonyi of Hungary stood beside Bronislaw Geremek and Madeleine Albright is recorded in Historic Documents of 1999, published by CQ Press. The end of conscription is dated from an answer given in the Sejm, which states that no call-ups were to be made from January 2009. The last compulsory call-up was in 2008 and the suspension took legal effect on 1 January 2010. The figure of about 28,000 Polish soldiers in Afghanistan since 2002 is from Polska Zbrojna, the defence ministry\'s magazine, in October 2014. The American army command post in Poznan, Camp Kosciuszko, was announced in June 2022 and is described by the United States Army. The dates on the road to the European Union, the association agreement of 16 December 1991 in force from 1 February 1994, the application handed in at Athens on 8 April 1994, the talks opened on 31 March 1998 and closed on 13 December 2002, and the treaty signed on 16 April 2003, follow the chronology published by the Akademia Wymiaru Sprawiedliwosci, a Polish state academy. The agreement itself is on EUR-Lex. The visa requirement for citizens of Belarus, Russia and Ukraine from 1 October 2003 was announced by the Polish foreign ministry in June 2003. The referendum figures, a turnout of 58.85 per cent and 77.45 per cent voting yes, are the State Electoral Commission\'s own published results. Accession on 1 May 2004, entry to the Schengen area on 21 December 2007, the presidencies of the Council of the European Union in 2011 and 2025 and the fact that Poland still uses the zloty are on the European Union\'s own country page for Poland. The emigration estimates are the Central Statistical Office\'s report on temporary emigration in 2004 to 2020, published on 25 October 2021, which says plainly that they show the trend rather than hard numbers. The budget figures, 245.5 billion euros received and 83.7 billion paid in between 1 May 2004 and the end of 2023, are the finance ministry\'s, reported by the Polish Press Agency in April 2024. The road lengths, about 720 kilometres of motorway and expressway on 1 May 2004 and 5,593 kilometres in August 2026, are GDDKiA\'s, the national roads authority. The procedure under Article 7, opened in December 2017 and closed on 29 May 2024, and the release of about 137 billion euros on 29 February 2024, are described by the Polish ministry of justice. The support figures are CBOS reports of February 2026 and April 2025. Title picture: the terminal at Krakow Airport in August 2024, photographed by Bahnfrend, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the history section',
    ],
    'today' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Poland since 2004',
            'description' => 'What happened to Poland after 2004: two million people leaving and many coming back, a million Ukrainians arriving, and the war next door.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'History · After 1989 · Topic 03',
            'title' => 'Two thousand and four,',
            'title_accent' => 'and everything after',
            'lede' => 'This is the last stop in the history section, and it is the part you will actually walk into. Since 2004 Poland has lost people to the west and gained them from the east, grown rich enough to be expensive, stopped using cash, and argued with itself about coal, courts and rights at the top of its voice. Nine frames on the country as it is in 2026, and where all of it came from. Scroll and the frames move sideways.',
            'meta_line' => 'Nine frames · about ten minutes · sources at the end',
        ],
        'frames' => [
            [
                'key' => 'leaving',
                'photo' => 'leaving',
                'label' => 'The leaving',
                'year' => '2004 - today',
                'title' => 'Two million went west, and then it turned',
                'body' => 'The last topic counted the people who left after 1 May 2004 and the money that came the other way. This one is about the country they left, came back to and changed. The Central Statistical Office, the government statisticians every Pole calls GUS, watched the number of Poles in Britain fall three years running and then drop by a quarter in 2020. Its report of 12 August 2026 projects about 1.51 million Poles abroad at the end of 2025, counted on a stricter rule than the old series. The regions they left are still thinner on people: Lodzkie, around the old textile city of Lodz, held 2.63 million people in 2000 and 2.35 million in 2024.',
                'caption' => 'Dworcowa Street in Bytom, Upper Silesia, on a June morning in 2024',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Abraham, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'fewer',
                'photo' => 'fewer',
                'label' => 'Fewer children',
                'year' => '2013 - today',
                'title' => 'The other half of the shrinking',
                'body' => 'Emigration was only half of it. Since 2013 more people have died in Poland every year than have been born, 859,000 more in total on the Central Statistical Office\'s count, and in 2024 alone 251,800 births against 408,500 deaths. Eurostat, the statistical office of the European Union, put the Polish birth rate at 1.14 children per woman in 2024, third lowest in the Union. Governments answered with cash: the child benefit called 500 plus began on 1 April 2016 and went up to 800 zloty a month on 1 January 2024. Extreme poverty among under-eighteens roughly halved by 2017, helped as much by rising wages. Births rose for two years and then fell below where they started.',
                'caption' => 'A new playground on the Bienczyce estate in Krakow, August 2023',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Mach240390, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'arrivals',
                'photo' => 'arrivals',
                'label' => 'The other way',
                'year' => '2014 - 2021',
                'title' => 'A country people leave starts taking people in',
                'body' => 'While Poles were going west, other people were arriving, and nearly all of them from one country. Ukrainians came for work after 2014, the year Russia annexed Crimea and fighting began in the Donbas, in eastern Ukraine, and they came easily, because a Polish employer could take them on with a form filed at a labour office rather than a full work permit. In 2021 alone employers filed 1.64 million such declarations for Ukrainian citizens. At the end of that year the social insurance institution, ZUS, had 627,000 Ukrainians on its books out of 875,100 foreigners in all. This is why the adverts at Katowice station were already in Ukrainian in 2018.',
                'caption' => 'An advert for money transfers to Ukraine at Katowice station, 2018',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Kamil Czainski, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'refuge',
                'photo' => 'refuge',
                'label' => 'February 2022',
                'year' => '2022 - today',
                'title' => 'A million people arrive, and are taken in',
                'body' => 'On 24 February 2022 Russia invaded Ukraine, and the crossings in south-eastern Poland became the busiest border in Europe. Poland built no refugee camps. The state paid households 40 zloty a person a day to take people in, at first for up to sixty days, and when the United Nations refugee agency, UNHCR, interviewed 4,567 refugees here in June 2022, 35 per cent were staying with hosts and 34 per cent renting, against 15 per cent in collective shelters run in schools and hostels. Most of them stayed: Eurostat counted 969,250 people under temporary protection in Poland at the end of 2025, and ZUS had 857,100 Ukrainians insured.',
                'caption' => 'Refugees from Ukraine at the reception point at Przemysl station, March 2022',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Bartosz Siedlik, European Commission audiovisual service, CC BY 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'frontline',
                'photo' => 'frontline',
                'label' => 'The war next door',
                'year' => '2022 - today',
                'title' => 'The country became the back door to a war',
                'body' => 'The war did not stay on the far side of the border. Almost everything the West sends Ukraine goes out through one airport in the south-east, Rzeszow-Jasionka. Polish officials put the share above ninety per cent, which is their claim rather than a measurement. Poland rearmed as well. NATO\'s own accounts put Polish core defence spending at 3.75 per cent of national output in 2024 and an estimated 4.25 per cent in 2025, the highest in the alliance in both years, against about 2.3 for its European members. A visitor sees the small end of it: Ukrainian flags on town halls, collection boxes inside supermarkets.',
                'caption' => 'Ukrainian patients being flown on from Rzeszow-Jasionka airport, May 2022',
                'credit' => 'Photo: EU Civil Protection and Humanitarian Aid, CC BY 2.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'money',
                'photo' => 'money',
                'label' => 'Money and prices',
                'year' => '2004 - today',
                'title' => 'Richer, cashless, and short of flats',
                'body' => 'Behind all of it is a long run of growth. Measured by Eurostat against local prices, Polish income per head was 52 per cent of the European Union average in 2004 and 81 per cent in 2025, and the World Bank calls it over thirty years of growth broken only by a shallow dip in 2020. Wages have not caught up: the Central Statistical Office put average gross pay in firms at 9,509 zloty a month in July 2026, around 2,180 euros. You will notice that nobody carries cash. BLIK, the Polish phone payment standard launched in 2015, handled 2.9 billion payments in 2025. Poles notice the flats: a square metre in a new Warsaw flat cost 7,510 zloty in 2015 and 16,783 in mid-2026.',
                'caption' => 'New flats and offices on Przyokopowa Street in Warsaw, October 2021',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Panek, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'made',
                'photo' => 'made',
                'label' => 'What got made',
                'year' => '2004 - today',
                'title' => 'Two museums, two Nobels and a monster hunter',
                'body' => 'Some new confidence went into buildings. The Warsaw Rising Museum, on the 1944 revolt against German occupation, opened to the public in October 2004, and POLIN, the Museum of the History of Polish Jews, opened its main exhibition on 28 October 2014. Some went into writing: Wislawa Szymborska, a poet in Krakow, won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1996, Olga Tokarczuk, a novelist in Wroclaw, the 2018 prize. Pawel Pawlikowski\'s film Ida took the foreign film Oscar in 2015. The Witcher games, made in Warsaw by CD Projekt, had sold over 85 million copies by March 2026. And in July the country moves into a field, at the Open\'er and Pol\'and\'Rock music festivals.',
                'caption' => 'The glass wall of the POLIN museum in Warsaw, October 2022',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Kamil Kulawik, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'argues',
                'photo' => 'argues',
                'label' => 'The argument',
                'year' => '2015 - today',
                'title' => 'A country that argues with itself, loudly',
                'body' => 'Poles argue about it all loudly, and they vote. Law and Justice, a conservative party, governed from 2015. Turnout on 15 October 2023 reached 74.38 per cent, the highest since 1989, and Donald Tusk took over as prime minister that December. The subjects stayed. Coal still made over half of Polish electricity in 2025 on Forum Energii\'s count, and the winter smog comes with it. Over a hundred councils passed resolutions against what they called LGBT ideology from 2019, the last withdrawn in April 2025. On 22 October 2020 the Constitutional Tribunal, the court that reads the constitution, cut the commonest legal ground for abortion. The city put the march that followed at 100,000.',
                'caption' => 'The Belchatow power station, seen across the Slok reservoir, May 2024',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Stephan van Helden, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
            [
                'key' => 'now',
                'photo' => 'now',
                'label' => 'Landing today',
                'year' => 'today',
                'title' => 'What all of it means when you get off the plane',
                'body' => 'So you get off the plane in a country much richer than it was and aware of it, emptier than it was and worried about it, and more mixed than anyone here remembers. You will pay for coffee with your phone, ride a Warsaw metro line that did not exist in 2014, hear Ukrainian on the tram, see a concrete block from the 1970s next to a glass tower, and meet people who went to Britain, came home, and have views on both. None of it makes sense without the thousand years underneath, which is where this section started: a tribe on a river, a duke called Mieszko, and a decision about which church to join. Go back and read it from there.',
                'caption' => 'The Main Square in Krakow on a March morning, 2023',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Minderbinder, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            ],
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The emigration figures are the Central Statistical Office\'s: the fall in the number of Poles in Britain, down by 164,000 or almost a quarter during 2020 after two earlier falls, is in its report on temporary emigration in 2004 to 2020, published on 25 October 2021, and the figure of about 1.51 million Poles abroad at the end of 2025 is in the successor report of 12 August 2026, in which the 2025 value is a projection and which counts only stays of a year or more, so it cannot be lined up against the older series. The provincial populations, the excess of 859,000 deaths over births since 2013 and the 251,800 births against 408,500 deaths in 2024 are from the same office\'s study of Poland\'s demographic situation to 2024, published on 29 September 2025. The birth rate of 1.14 children per woman in 2024, third lowest in the European Union after Malta and Spain, is Eurostat\'s. Eurostat and the Central Statistical Office use different population bases and so publish slightly different rates for the same year. The child benefit began under the act of 11 February 2016 and rose to 800 zloty under the act of 7 July 2023, both in the Journal of Laws. The fall in extreme poverty among under-eighteens from 9 per cent in 2015 to 4.7 per cent in 2017 is the statistical office\'s, and it credits rising wages and falling unemployment alongside the benefit rather than the benefit alone. The 1.64 million declarations filed for Ukrainian workers in 2021 are the labour ministry\'s, published through the public employment service. The insurance figures, 627,000 Ukrainians out of 875,100 foreigners at the end of 2021 and 857,100 out of 1,288,800 at the end of 2025, are from the annual studies of foreigners in the Polish social insurance system published by ZUS, the Social Insurance Institution. The 40 zloty a day paid to households comes from the regulation of 15 March 2022 made under the special act of 12 March 2022 on help for citizens of Ukraine, and was initially limited to 60 days. The accommodation shares, 35 per cent with hosts, 34 per cent renting and 15 per cent in collective sites, are from the profiling update UNHCR published in June 2022 after 4,567 interviews in Poland. The 969,250 people under temporary protection at the end of 2025 are Eurostat\'s count. That more than ninety per cent of Western aid to Ukraine passes through Rzeszow-Jasionka was stated by President Karol Nawrocki at the logistics centre there on 2 December 2025 and published by the National Security Bureau. It is an official statement rather than a measurement, and earlier Polish figures were lower. The defence shares, 3.75 per cent of output in 2024 and an estimated 4.25 per cent in 2025, are from NATO\'s own table of defence investment, published in July 2026. The income comparison, 52 per cent of the Union average in 2004 and 81 per cent in 2025, is Eurostat\'s, the growth record is the World Bank\'s country overview, the wage of 9,509 zloty gross in July 2026 is the statistical office\'s, the 2.9 billion BLIK payments in 2025 are the operator\'s, Polski Standard Platnosci, and the Warsaw prices per square metre are from the National Bank of Poland\'s database of housing transaction prices. The museums give their own dates: the Warsaw Rising Museum opened to visitors in October 2004 and POLIN opened its main exhibition on 28 October 2014. The Nobel prizes are on the Nobel Foundation\'s site, Szymborska\'s for 1996 and Tokarczuk\'s for 2018, presented in 2019. The sales of over 85 million copies of the Witcher games are CD Projekt\'s own figure, given in March 2026. Ida won the Academy Award for the best foreign language film at the ceremony of 22 February 2015, and the two music festivals count their own crowds with nobody checking, which is why no attendance is quoted here. The turnout of 74.38 per cent on 15 October 2023 is the State Electoral Commission\'s, and the government took office on 13 December 2023. The coal share of 52.2 per cent in 2025 is from the yearbook of Forum Energii. The count of local resolutions against what their authors called LGBT ideology, and the withdrawal of the last of them on 24 April 2025 under pressure over European funding, follow Kampania Przeciw Homofobii and the Atlas of Hate. The judgment of the Constitutional Tribunal of 22 October 2020, case K 1/20, is on the Tribunal\'s own site and was published in the Journal of Laws on 27 January 2021. The figure of about 100,000 marchers in Warsaw on 30 October 2020 is Warsaw city hall\'s estimate, and other counts that day were lower. Title picture: central Warsaw from the roof garden of the Forest building, September 2022, photographed by Cybularny, Wikimedia Commons, CC0.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the history section',
        'restart' => 'That is the end of the story. Start it again',
    ],
