            'body_1' => 'Most capitals grew. This one was taken apart. After the rising of 1944 German demolition squads worked through the empty city street by street, and over 85 per cent of the historic centre came down. The decision to rebuild it exactly, rather than clear the rubble and start again, was made while people were still living in cellars.',
            'body_2' => 'That is why UNESCO put the old town on its list in 1980, and the citation says so plainly: it is there as an example of reconstruction, not as a surviving medieval town. Knowing that changes the walk. You are not looking at what was built in the 17th century. You are looking at what a country decided was worth building twice.',
