            'body_1' => 'Krakow was the capital for over five hundred years, and the kings went on being crowned and buried at Wawel long after the court had moved to Warsaw in 1596. It kept the university, founded in 1364, and under Austrian rule in the 19th century it kept the Polish language and the Polish theatre when the other two partitions did not allow them.',
            'body_2' => 'Its buildings survived the Second World War nearly intact. Its people did not. The professors of the university were arrested in November 1939 and sent to camps, and the Jewish community of the city, about a quarter of its inhabitants, was shut into the ghetto in Podgorze in 1941 and then killed. Walking here means walking through a city that kept its face and lost a third of its face.',
        ],

