        'history' => [
            'label' => 'History',
            'title' => 'Nine hundred years in eight dates',
            'items' => [
                ['year' => '1124', 'note' => 'Otto, bishop of Bamberg, baptises the town on the instructions of the Polish duke Boleslaw the Wrymouth. Pomerania becomes Christian and settles into its long position on the edge of Poland rather than inside it.'],
                ['year' => '1630', 'note' => 'Sweden takes the city during the Thirty Years War and holds it for ninety years. The last duke of the Pomeranian house had died in 1637 without an heir, which is how the region stopped governing itself.'],
                ['year' => '1720', 'note' => 'Prussia buys the city from Sweden. Stettin becomes Prussian, then German, and the port grows into the sea outlet for Berlin.'],
                ['year' => '1729', 'note' => 'Sophie of Anhalt-Zerbst is born here, the daughter of the Prussian commander of the town. At fifteen she is sent to Russia, and in 1762 she becomes the empress Catherine the Great, who does more than anyone else to take Poland off the map.'],
                ['year' => '1873', 'note' => 'The fortress walls come down and the city is rebuilt with wide avenues and star-shaped junctions copied from the Paris of Napoleon III. That plan is why the centre looks nothing like a Polish old town.'],
                ['year' => '1945', 'note' => 'The new border is drawn west of the city and Polish administration takes over on 5 July. The German population is expelled and Poles are settled in, most of them with nothing to do with this coast.'],
                ['year' => '1970', 'note' => 'In December, shipyard workers march on the party headquarters and set it alight, and the army opens fire. Sixteen people are killed in Szczecin, a number the government spent years not saying out loud.'],
                ['year' => '1980', 'note' => 'On 30 August, in a clubroom at the shipyard, the government signs an agreement with striking workers here. It is the first of the August agreements, signed one day before the more famous one in Gdansk.'],
            ],
        ],

        'myths' => [
            'label' => 'What is true',
            'title' => 'Three things people repeat',
            'items' => [
                ['tag' => 'True', 'title' => 'It does not look Polish', 'note' => 'Correct, and there is no mystery in it. The centre was laid out by Prussian planners copying Paris, the brick churches are Pomeranian and the terraces above the river are German municipal pride from 1907. Everything built before 1945 was built by somebody else. What is Polish here is the people walking through it.'],
                ['tag' => 'True, and awkward', 'title' => 'Catherine the Great was born here', 'note' => 'She was, in 1729, as Sophie of Anhalt-Zerbst. She left at fifteen, married the heir to the Russian throne, took the throne herself in 1762 and then did more than any other single person to erase Poland from the map in the partitions. The city states the fact and does not celebrate it.'],
                ['tag' => 'Not only Gdansk', 'title' => 'It all started in Gdansk in 1980', 'note' => 'The union did start there, and the name Solidarnosc with it. But the strike here ran at the same time, and the government signed in Szczecin first, on 30 August, a day before it signed in Gdansk. The Szczecin agreement is the one nobody abroad has heard of, which is roughly how this city experiences most things.'],
            ],
        ],

