    'bydgoszcz' => [

        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Bydgoszcz: the city a canal built',
            'description' => 'A city that exists because of a canal dug in 1774 to join two river systems, with water running through the middle of it.',
        ],

        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'Places · Kuyavia and Pomerania',
            'title' => 'Bydgoszcz',
            'title_accent' => '',
            'lede' => 'Most people planning a trip to Poland have never heard the name and could not say it. It is a city of water, granaries and one canal that changed the map of northern Europe, and it is the quietest big city in the country.',
            'meta_line' => 'Six parts · about eight minutes · one day on foot',
        ],

        'opening' => [
            'label' => 'What this place is',
            'title' => 'The city a canal built',
            'body_1' => 'In 1772 Prussia took this part of Poland in the first partition. Two years later it had dug a canal here, about twenty-five kilometres long, joining the Brda and the Notec and so linking the Vistula to the Oder and on to Berlin. Grain from Polish fields could now float west without paying anyone at the mouth of the Vistula. That is the reason there is a city this size here, and it cost, by the usual estimate, around two thousand lives to dig.',
            'body_2' => 'What grew on top of that is a river town of granaries, red brick and water, German-run for a hundred and forty-eight years and Polish again from January 1920, when it was handed over under the Versailles treaty without a shot. It is worth knowing for the contrast with the rest of this list: no royal capital, no famous square, no uprising in the schoolbooks. A working city that is much more pleasant than its reputation, largely because nobody built a reputation for it.',
        ],

        'map' => [
