            'title' => 'Six hundred years in eight dates',
            'items' => [
                ['year' => '1346', 'note' => 'King Casimir the Great charters a town on the Brda and builds a castle, to hold the river crossing against the Teutonic Knights just downstream.'],
                ['year' => '1772', 'note' => 'Prussia takes the region in the first partition of Poland. The town is renamed Bromberg and has fewer than a thousand people left in it after decades of war and plague.'],
                ['year' => '1774', 'note' => 'The canal opens after about a year and a half of digging, joining the Brda to the Notec and so the Vistula to the Oder. The usual estimate is that around two thousand of the workers died building it.'],
                ['year' => '1851', 'note' => 'The railway from Berlin arrives, and the city becomes a river and rail junction with sawmills, granaries and a port. Most of the centre you walk through was built in the sixty years after this.'],
                ['year' => '1920', 'note' => 'On 20 January, after a hundred and forty-eight years, the city is handed back to Poland under the Treaty of Versailles. No fighting is needed, and the German mayor hands over his office the day before.'],
                ['year' => '1939', 'note' => 'German forces enter on 5 September. Between October and November they shoot Poles from the city in a ravine at Fordon, on the edge of town, that has been called the Valley of Death ever since. Exhumations found several hundred bodies and the estimates run into thousands.'],
                ['year' => '1945', 'note' => 'The city comes through the war with its buildings largely intact, which is why the granaries, the tenements and the river front are original rather than rebuilt.'],
