        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The canal opened in 1774, after roughly eighteen months of digging, and the figure of about two thousand dead among the workers is the one the Bydgoszcz Canal Museum gives. The city returned to Poland on 20 January 1920 under the Treaty of Versailles. For the executions at Fordon in the autumn of 1939, the exhumation of 1947 found several hundred bodies and the Institute of National Remembrance puts the likely total in the thousands, which is as precise as the evidence allows. Opening hours and prices are left out on purpose, because they age faster than this page is updated.',
