        'history' => [
            'label' => 'History',
            'title' => 'Seven hundred years in eight dates',
            'items' => [
                ['year' => '1317', 'note' => 'The town gets its charter. It sits on the road from Krakow to Vilnius and Kiev, which for the next four centuries is the most profitable place in the region to stand.'],
                ['year' => '1418', 'note' => 'Orthodox painters from the east finish the frescoes inside the Gothic castle chapel, on the orders of King Wladyslaw Jagiello, who had been born a pagan Lithuanian. The chapel is still there and still astonishing.'],
                ['year' => '1569', 'note' => 'On 1 July the nobles of Poland and Lithuania sign the Union of Lublin, turning a shared king into a single state with one parliament, one currency and one foreign policy. It survives, in one form or another, until 1795.'],
                ['year' => '1578', 'note' => 'The Crown Tribunal, the highest court for the Polish half of the commonwealth, is seated in Lublin. Lawyers, litigants and their money come with it.'],
                ['year' => '1930', 'note' => 'The Chachmei Lublin yeshiva opens, at the time the largest Jewish religious college in the world. Before the war about a third of the city is Jewish.'],
                ['year' => '1941', 'note' => 'The Germans build a camp at Majdanek on the edge of the city. About eighty thousand people are killed there, most of them Jews, and the Soviet army reaches it in July 1944 almost intact, which is why it is the best preserved camp in Europe.'],
                ['year' => '1944', 'note' => 'From 1 August the Polish Committee of National Liberation sits in Lublin, installed by Moscow while Warsaw is still fighting. For a few months this is where the new Poland is being assembled.'],
                ['year' => '1980', 'note' => 'In July, two months before the coast, railway and factory workers in the Lublin region strike over prices and win concessions. It is the rehearsal for August, and it is usually left out of the story.'],
            ],
        ],

        'myths' => [
            'label' => 'What is true',
            'title' => 'Three things people repeat',
            'items' => [
                ['tag' => 'True', 'title' => 'Poland and Lithuania were one country', 'note' => 'They were, for over two hundred years, and it was signed here. The Commonwealth had two armies and two legal systems but one parliament and one king, chosen by election. At its largest it was the biggest state in Europe, and it is the thing Polish history most wants you to know about.'],
                ['tag' => 'Not a ruin, a court', 'title' => 'The castle is medieval', 'note' => 'Only the round tower and the chapel are. The big white building around them was put up in the 1820s as a prison, and it stayed a prison under the Russians, the Germans and then the communists until 1954. It is a museum now, and the two ages sit awkwardly side by side, which is honest.'],
                ['tag' => 'Please read this one', 'title' => 'Majdanek is a day trip', 'note' => 'It is four kilometres from the centre, a bus ride, free to enter and open most days, so it is easy to reach. It is also a place where about eighty thousand people were killed, with the barracks, the gas chambers and the crematorium standing as they were. Go if you mean to, give it half a day, and do not treat it as a stop between lunch and the old town.'],
            ],
