        'opening' => [
            'label' => 'What this place is',
            'title' => 'The mountains Poland decided to be sentimental about',
            'body_1' => 'The Tatras are the only alpine range in Poland, about fifty kilometres end to end, and Zakopane sits at the foot of them. Until the 1870s it was a poor village of shepherds and blacksmiths. Then a doctor named Tytus Chalubinski started sending patients here for the air, writers and painters followed, and within thirty years the highlanders and their way of life had been adopted by the Polish intelligentsia as the most authentic thing in the country.',
            'body_2' => 'That adoption is still visible. Stanislaw Witkiewicz took the local timber building and turned it into a design style he called Zakopane style, meant as a Polish national architecture. The highlanders, the gorale, kept their own speech, music and a firm sense of not being quite the same as lowland Poles. What you get today is a genuine mountain culture, a large amount of fried cheese sold to visitors, and above both of them a national park where the rules are strict for good reason.',
        ],

