        'opening' => [
            'label' => 'What this place is',
            'title' => 'The old town that is actually old',
            'body_1' => 'The Teutonic Knights, a German crusading order, founded Torun in 1233 as a base on the Vistula while they conquered the pagan Prussians to the north, a Baltic people with nothing to do with the German Prussia that later took the name. The town grew rich on the river trade, joined the Hanseatic league, and in 1454 the townspeople rose against the Order and pulled its castle down themselves. They left the ruins exactly as they fell, which tells you how they felt about it.',
            'body_2' => 'Then comes the part that matters to a visitor. Torun came through the second world war without serious damage, which almost nothing else in this country did. In Warsaw, Gdansk and Wroclaw you are looking at careful reconstructions of what was destroyed. Here you are looking at the buildings themselves, about three hundred of them from the 13th to the 16th century, which is why the place is on the UNESCO list.',
        ],

