        'history' => [
            'label' => 'History',
            'title' => 'Eight hundred years in nine dates',
            'items' => [
                ['year' => '1190', 'note' => 'The Teutonic Order begins as a hospital for German crusaders at the siege of Acre, in what is now Israel. Within a decade it is a military order, a brotherhood of monks who fight.'],
                ['year' => '1226', 'note' => 'Konrad of Mazovia, a Polish duke whose lands are raided by the pagan Prussians to the north, invites the Order to come and deal with them. It is the invitation that decides the next three centuries.'],
                ['year' => '1274', 'note' => 'Work starts on a castle above the Nogat. It is called Marienburg after the Virgin Mary, and it goes up in brick because there is no building stone in this landscape.'],
                ['year' => '1309', 'note' => 'The grand master Siegfried von Feuchtwangen moves the seat of the Order here from Venice. Malbork stops being a frontier post and becomes the capital of a monastic state with its own coinage, courts and foreign policy.'],
                ['year' => '1410', 'note' => 'The Order loses the battle of Grunwald in July to a Polish and Lithuanian army, and the victors march on the castle. Heinrich von Plauen holds it through a siege of almost two months, and the walls that were never taken are the ones you walk along.'],
                ['year' => '1457', 'note' => 'Casimir IV Jagiellon, king of Poland, buys the castle for 190,000 florins from the Bohemian mercenaries the Order had hired and then failed to pay. It becomes a royal residence, and the kings of Poland keep it for three hundred years.'],
                ['year' => '1772', 'note' => 'Prussia takes the region in the first partition of Poland. The castle is turned into barracks and a weaving works, the church is used as a store, and parts of it are pulled down for the bricks.'],
                ['year' => '1882', 'note' => 'The architect Conrad Steinbrecht begins forty years of restoration, the first attempt anywhere to put a Gothic castle back as it had been rather than as somebody imagined it. Much of what a visitor sees is his work.'],
                ['year' => '1945', 'note' => 'The castle is wrecked in the fighting of the last winter of the war, more than half of it burnt out. Poland rebuilds it again over the following decades, and UNESCO adds it to the World Heritage list in 1997, citing the rebuilding as much as the castle.'],
            ],
        ],

        'myths' => [
            'label' => 'What is true',
            'title' => 'Three things people repeat',
            'items' => [
                ['tag' => 'True, and awkward', 'title' => 'The Teutonic Knights invaded Poland', 'note' => 'They were invited. Konrad of Mazovia, a Polish duke, asked them in during 1226 because he could not hold his northern border against the pagan Prussians. What went wrong is that the Order was never anybody subject: it built a state of its own on the land it conquered, took Gdansk from Poland in 1308, and spent the next two centuries at war with the people who had called it in.'],
                ['tag' => 'Medieval, and rebuilt twice', 'title' => 'You are looking at a 14th-century castle', 'note' => 'The plan, the walls and much of the brick are genuinely of the 13th to 15th centuries. But the Prussian army used the place as barracks, Conrad Steinbrecht restored it between 1882 and 1922, and more than half of it burnt in 1945 and was rebuilt after the war. UNESCO listed it in 1997 for both things, the medieval castle and the long argument about how to put it back.'],
                ['tag' => 'Not in 1410', 'title' => 'Grunwald finished the Teutonic Order', 'note' => 'It broke the Order in the field and it never recovered its strength, but the castle itself held out that summer under Heinrich von Plauen and the state lasted another century. The Order gave up Malbork in 1457, signed away the lower Vistula in 1466, and its last grand master turned the remains into a Protestant duchy in 1525. There is a separate page here about the battle itself.'],
