        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Wieliczka: the salt mine under Krakow',
            'description' => 'A salt mine worked from the Middle Ages until 1996, with chapels, lakes and statues cut out of the rock salt, on the first UNESCO list of 1978.',
        ],

        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'Places · Lesser Poland',
            'title' => 'Wieliczka',
            'title_accent' => '',
            'lede' => 'Ten kilometres from Krakow, men dug for salt in the 13th century and kept digging without a break until the end of the 20th. Along the way they cut chapels, staircases and a hall the size of a cathedral out of the rock itself.',
            'meta_line' => 'Six parts · about eight minutes · half a day underground',
        ],

        'opening' => [
            'label' => 'What this place is',
            'title' => 'Why a hole in the ground is on the UNESCO list',
            'body_1' => 'Salt seems dull now because it costs nothing. For most of the last thousand years it was the only way to keep meat and fish through a winter, which made it a necessity rather than a seasoning, and whoever owned salt owned an income. The mines at Wieliczka and at Bochnia thirty kilometres away were run together as a royal monopoly, the Krakow Saltworks, and under Casimir the Great, the king who reigned from 1333 to 1370, that monopoly is reckoned to have produced something close to a third of the crown revenue. Castles, town walls and the university in Krakow were paid for out of this.',
            'body_2' => 'That is why the mine is not a tunnel. Nine levels go down to 327 metres, the galleries add up to around 245 kilometres, and the men who worked here spent their lives underground and decorated the place. They cut chapels so they could hear mass without climbing out, carved altars, saints and chandeliers from the rock salt, and left chambers the size of churches behind them. Wieliczka went on to the very first UNESCO World Heritage list in 1978, as one of only twelve places in the world on it, and salt was mined here commercially until 1996.',
        ],

        'map' => [
            'label' => 'Where it is',
            'title' => 'The south, on the edge of Krakow',
            'note' => 'A small town ten kilometres south-east of the centre of Krakow, reached in about half an hour by local train or bus. It is the easiest day trip in the country and the one most visitors to Krakow make.',
            'caption' => 'Schematic locator drawn on modern borders. The pin is placed from the real coordinates of the mine.',
            'alt' => 'Map of Poland with Wieliczka marked in the south',
