        'myths' => [
            'label' => 'What is true',
            'title' => 'Three things people repeat',
            'items' => [
                ['tag' => 'True', 'title' => 'The walls really are salt', 'note' => 'The grey rock is not stone and it is not painted. It is rock salt, laid down when a sea dried out here about fourteen million years ago, and it is grey rather than white because of the clay mixed through it. The guides are used to people touching it. They would rather you did not touch the carvings, because sweat and breath are what is slowly eating them.'],
                ['tag' => 'A legend, and labelled as one', 'title' => 'A princess ring led them to the salt', 'note' => 'The story says that Kinga, a Hungarian princess who married a Polish duke in the 13th century, threw her engagement ring into a Hungarian salt mine, and that when her miners dug the first shaft at Wieliczka they brought up a lump of salt with the ring inside it. It is a lovely story and it is told here as a story. The salt was already being worked from brine springs long before she arrived, and Kinga became the patron saint of Polish miners because the tale was useful, not because it happened.'],
                ['tag' => 'Take it seriously', 'title' => 'You can wander in and have a look', 'note' => 'You cannot. You go down in a group with a guide and you stay with that guide, because this is a mine with shafts, water and moving air, and the marked route is the only safe line through it. The descent is about 380 wooden steps, the whole tourist route is around 800 steps and three kilometres of walking, and it sits at 14 to 17 degrees whatever the weather is doing above. You come back up in a lift.'],
