        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'Wladyslaw Jagiello hunted here in the winter of 1409 to provision the campaign that ended at Grunwald, and Sigismund the Old made the killing of a bison here a capital offence in 1538. The last wild bison in the forest was killed in February 1919, the species survived in fifty-four animals held in zoos and parks, the first were brought back to Bialowieza in 1929, and free-living animals were released from 1952. The national park dates from 1932, the UNESCO listing from 1979, its extension to Belarus from 1992 and the enlargement from 2014. The Court of Justice of the European Union ruled against the logging in April 2018. Bison numbers and the protected areas are taken from Bialowieza National Park and were read in September 2026, and the size of the herd changes every year. Opening hours, guide fees and booking rules are left out on purpose, because they age faster than this page is updated.',
