        'reading' => [
            'label' => 'The rest of the story',
            'title' => 'Where this is told in full',
            'items' => [
                ['title' => 'The borders moved', 'note' => 'Why Poland was lifted westwards in 1945, and what that did to the people in it.', 'href' => '/history/the-borders-moved'],
            ],
        ],

        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The lakes are glacial, left by the retreat of the last ice sheet about twelve thousand years ago, and Sniardwy at 114 square kilometres is the largest in Poland. The Teutonic Knights were invited in by Konrad of Mazovia in 1226, Albrecht of Hohenzollern turned their state into a Protestant duchy in 1525, and the treaties of Wehlau and Bydgoszcz released it from Polish overlordship in 1657. The battle near Olsztynek that Germany named Tannenberg was fought in August 1914, and the plebiscite of July 1920 went heavily for Germany. Hitler used the Gierloz complex from June 1941, the attempt on his life there was made on 20 July 1944, and the bunkers were blown up in January 1945. The flight and expulsion of the German population and the settlement of Poles, many of them expelled from the east, followed the arrival of the Red Army in January 1945. Prices, opening hours and charter rates are left out on purpose, because they age faster than this page is updated.',
        ],

        'next' => 'Back to the places',
    ],

    'everyday' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Everyday life and language',
            'description' => 'How to behave and be understood here: say hello on the way in, pan and pani instead of you, twenty phrases, and shoes off at the door.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'Everyday life · Language',
            'title' => 'The rules nobody',
            'title_accent' => 'writes down',
            'lede' => 'Poles are less effusive than Americans and less formal than Germans, and the small customs matter here more than the big ones. None of it is difficult. It is simply that nobody explains it to visitors.',
            'meta_line' => 'Ten parts · about fifteen minutes · twenty phrases worth learning',
        ],
        'greet' => [
            'label' => 'The first five minutes',
            'title' => 'You say dzień dobry on the way in, to the room',
            'body_1' => 'This is the one thing that makes the biggest difference and the one visitors miss most often. Going into a shop, a lift, a doctors waiting room, a train compartment or a small bar, you say dzień dobry, which means good day. Not to a particular person, to the room. On the way out you say do widzenia, goodbye. Silence is not read as shyness here, it is read as rudeness, and it is the commonest reason a foreigner leaves with the impression that people here are cold.',
            'body_2' => 'The rest is simpler than it looks. Nobody talks to strangers without a reason, and asking someone in a queue how they are doing will get you a careful look, because that question is for people you know. It is not coldness. It only means a conversation starts from the matter at hand rather than from pleasantries. Once it has started people are surprisingly helpful, and somebody who speaks no Polish is more likely to be walked to the right address than waved away.',
        ],
        'forms' => [
            'label' => 'Pan and pani',
            'title' => 'You do not say you to a stranger',
            'body_1' => 'Polish has two levels of addressing people and mixing them up is heard at once. To somebody you do not know you say pan for a man or pani for a woman, and you build the sentence in the third person, literally as though you were talking about them rather than to them. The word ty, meaning you, is for family, friends and people roughly your own age who have agreed to it. Moving to ty is a moment in itself here, and it is usually offered by the older or more senior person.',
            'body_2' => 'There is good news for a visitor, though. In a shop, a restaurant or a station you do not need any of these words, because you can start with poproszę. Nobody expects a foreigner to get the endings right either, and simply trying to speak Polish is received very warmly.',
            'items' => [
                ['k' => 'pan', 'v' => 'To a man, said pahn. At a table, proszę pana on its own is how you get a waiters attention.'],
                ['k' => 'pani', 'v' => 'To a woman, said PAH-nee. It is used whatever her age and whether or not she is married.'],
                ['k' => 'poproszę', 'v' => 'Said poh-PROH-sheh, it means I would like. With a pointed finger this one word orders anything.'],
                ['k' => 'przejść na ty', 'v' => 'The offer to move to first names. It comes from the older person or the host, and is rarely refused.'],
            ],
        ],
        'english' => [
            'label' => 'Will you be understood',
            'title' => 'Under thirty nearly always, over sixty rarely',
            'body_1' => 'In a large city somebody under thirty-five almost certainly speaks English and often speaks it well, because English is taught here from the first year of school and nothing on television is dubbed. Over sixty it is the other way round, because that generation learned Russian at school. In a restaurant in any tourist city an English menu is standard, and so is being served in English at a bank or a phone shop. At a government office it varies, and it is worth having somebody with you.',
            'body_2' => 'A separate word about Russian. The whole older generation learned it at school, so many people understand it, but do not assume it, because younger people were never taught it. The simplest opener is English, and if that fails, hold up a translation app. You will hear Ukrainian here often in any case, because close to a million people from Ukraine settled in Poland after 2022 and you notice it in shops, on building sites and in schools.',
        ],
        'speak' => [
            'label' => 'Pronunciation',
            'title' => 'Eight rules and Polish names stop being frightening',
            'note' => 'Polish is written in the Latin alphabet and read consistently, so once you know a few pairs of letters you can read any name on the map. The stress falls almost always on the second to last syllable, and that one fact fixes the most.',
            'letters' => [
                ['k' => 'ł', 'v' => 'like the w in water. Łódź does not start with an l.'],
                ['k' => 'w', 'v' => 'like an English v. Warszawa is vahr-SHAH-vah.'],
                ['k' => 'sz and cz', 'v' => 'sh and ch. Szczecin starts with both at once.'],
                ['k' => 'rz and ż', 'v' => 'the same sound, like the s in measure.'],
                ['k' => 'c', 'v' => 'always ts. The word co is read tso.'],
                ['k' => 'j', 'v' => 'like an English y. Jadę is YAH-deh.'],
                ['k' => 'ą and ę', 'v' => 'nasal vowels, roughly on and en. Wąsy is VON-si.'],
                ['k' => 'ch and h', 'v' => 'one sound, rasping, like the ch in Scottish loch.'],
            ],
            'names_label' => 'Twelve names you will have to say anyway',
            'names' => [
                ['k' => 'Łódź', 'v' => 'wooj'],
                ['k' => 'Kraków', 'v' => 'KRAH-koof'],
                ['k' => 'Wrocław', 'v' => 'VROTS-wahf'],
                ['k' => 'Szczecin', 'v' => 'SHCHEH-cheen'],
                ['k' => 'Gdańsk', 'v' => 'g-DAHNSK'],
                ['k' => 'Poznań', 'v' => 'POZ-nahn'],
                ['k' => 'Bydgoszcz', 'v' => 'bid-GOSHCH'],
                ['k' => 'Toruń', 'v' => 'TOH-roon'],
                ['k' => 'Przemyśl', 'v' => 'PSHEH-mishl'],
                ['k' => 'Zakopane', 'v' => 'zah-koh-PAH-neh'],
                ['k' => 'Katowice', 'v' => 'kah-toh-VEE-tseh'],
                ['k' => 'Warszawa', 'v' => 'vahr-SHAH-vah'],
            ],
        ],
        'phrases' => [
            'label' => 'Phrases',
            'title' => 'Sixteen things worth being able to say',
            'note' => 'The middle column is how it sounds for a reader of English. The stressed syllable is in capitals.',
            'items' => [
                ['pl' => 'Dzień dobry', 'say' => 'jen DOH-bri', 'en' => 'Good day, used from morning until dusk. This is what you walk in with, everywhere.'],
                ['pl' => 'Dobry wieczór', 'say' => 'DOH-bri VYEH-choor', 'en' => 'Good evening, once it is dark.'],
                ['pl' => 'Do widzenia', 'say' => 'doh vee-DZEH-nyah', 'en' => 'Goodbye. Said on the way out of a shop even if you bought nothing.'],
                ['pl' => 'Cześć', 'say' => 'cheshch', 'en' => 'Hi and bye. Only to somebody you are on first name terms with.'],
                ['pl' => 'Proszę', 'say' => 'PROH-sheh', 'en' => 'Please, here you are, go ahead and come in, all at once. It does the most work of any word here.'],
                ['pl' => 'Poproszę', 'say' => 'poh-PROH-sheh', 'en' => 'I would like that. Point and say it, and you have ordered.'],
                ['pl' => 'Dziękuję', 'say' => 'jen-KOO-yeh', 'en' => 'Thank you.'],
                ['pl' => 'Przepraszam', 'say' => 'psheh-PRAH-sham', 'en' => 'Sorry, and the same word is how you stop somebody in the street.'],
                ['pl' => 'Tak, nie', 'say' => 'tahk, nyeh', 'en' => 'Yes and no. Careful: nie sounds close to the English no and means the same thing.'],
                ['pl' => 'Nie mówię po polsku', 'say' => 'nyeh MOO-vyeh poh POL-skoo', 'en' => 'I do not speak Polish. The sentence that usually switches a conversation into English.'],
                ['pl' => 'Czy mówi pan po angielsku?', 'say' => 'chi MOO-vee pahn poh ahn-GYEL-skoo', 'en' => 'Do you speak English, to a man. To a woman, say pani instead of pan.'],
                ['pl' => 'Ile to kosztuje?', 'say' => 'EE-leh toh kosh-TOO-yeh', 'en' => 'How much is it.'],
                ['pl' => 'Rachunek proszę', 'say' => 'rah-HOO-nek PROH-sheh', 'en' => 'The bill please. A waiter will not bring it until you ask.'],
                ['pl' => 'Gdzie jest toaleta?', 'say' => 'gjeh yest toh-ah-LEH-tah', 'en' => 'Where is the toilet. The doors are often marked with a triangle for men and a circle for women.'],
                ['pl' => 'Smacznego', 'say' => 'smahch-NEH-go', 'en' => 'Enjoy your meal. Said before anybody takes the first bite.'],
                ['pl' => 'Na zdrowie', 'say' => 'nah ZDROH-vyeh', 'en' => 'Cheers, and also what you say when somebody sneezes.'],
            ],
        ],
        'visit' => [
            'label' => 'Visiting',
            'title' => 'Shoes come off at the door and you will eat more than you planned',
            'body_1' => 'An invitation into somebody\'s home means more here than it does in much of western Europe, because people meet at home more often than out. Shoes come off at the door, always, and the host will often hand you slippers. Bringing something is the done thing on a first visit or when the invitation is a formal one, and flowers, a cake or a bottle of wine all do the job. At friends you see every week you simply turn up, and nobody is counting.',
            'body_2' => 'Then there will be food, and there will be too much of it. Refusing a second helping is sometimes taken as politeness, so if you genuinely cannot, say so a second time and laugh while you do. There is a saying here about going out for one beer, and it practically never ends at one. A visit can go the same way, so leave yourself some room that evening.',
            'items' => [
                ['k' => 'Shoes', 'v' => 'Taken off in the hall without being asked. Many homes have slippers waiting for guests.'],
                ['k' => 'The table', 'v' => 'You will be sat at it straight away and something will be put in front of you, even on a fifteen minute visit. Refusing gets you nowhere, it is easier to take a little.'],
                ['k' => 'The toast', 'v' => 'Na zdrowie, the glass emptied in one, and something to eat straight after. Vodka is not sipped through an evening.'],
                ['k' => 'Leaving', 'v' => 'Plan the evening looser than the invitation suggests, because it rarely ends at the hour it was meant to.'],
            ],
        ],
        'calendar' => [
            'label' => 'The calendar',
            'title' => 'When the country slows down or stops',
            'note' => 'A few dates and habits that can wreck the plans of somebody who does not know about them. What gets eaten on these days is on the food page instead.',
            'items' => [
                ['k' => 'Shopping Sundays', 'v' => 'On most Sundays the shops are shut, because Sunday trading has been restricted by law since 2018. There are a handful of trading Sundays a year, mostly before the holidays. Petrol stations, duty pharmacies, bakeries, stations, small local shops and every restaurant stay open. Do your shopping on Saturday.'],
                ['k' => 'Name days', 'v' => 'The feast day of the saint you are named after. For the older generation it counts for more than a birthday, and everybody knows the date. The person whose day it is brings cake to work rather than receiving presents.'],
                ['k' => 'The May weekend', 'v' => 'The first long weekend of May, because 1 and 3 May are both public holidays. Half the country travels, room prices go up, and from that weekend until September people grill on any patch of grass.'],
                ['k' => 'All Saints', 'v' => 'On 1 November, the day the dead are remembered, and a public holiday. Families drive to cemeteries, often across half the country, the roads jam, and by evening the cemeteries glow with candles. It is not the local Halloween, but it is not a day of mourning either. Relatives meet at the graves, tidy them and talk about the dead out loud, often laughing while they do. It is warm rather than sad.'],
                ['k' => 'Christmas and Easter', 'v' => 'On these days the country simply stops. Shops, museums and most restaurants are closed on 25 and 26 December and on Easter Sunday and Monday. Plan your food ahead, because by eight in the evening on 24 December you will not buy anything.'],
            ],
        ],
        'travel' => [
            'label' => 'Getting about',
            'title' => 'How to cross the country',
            'note' => 'Poland is smaller than the map suggests: Warsaw to Krakow, Gdansk or Wroclaw is two and a half to three hours by train. A domestic flight almost never makes sense, because getting to the airport and through it eats the whole difference.',
            'items' => [
                ['k' => 'Trains between cities', 'v' => 'The fastest and most comfortable way. The big cities are linked by PKP Intercity, and the main routes are run by Pendolino trains. You buy the ticket in the app of whichever operator runs the route, or on its website, both of which have an English version, and the earlier you buy the less it costs. On long routes the seat is tied to the ticket, so you board a particular carriage.'],
                ['k' => 'Local trains', 'v' => 'Shorter routes are run by Polregio and the regional railways. Cheaper, slower, and with no seat reservation. This is also where you find that a smaller town simply has no service, because the line was closed in the 1990s.'],
                ['k' => 'Coaches', 'v' => 'FlixBus and regional operators reach the places the railway no longer does, and can undercut the train. They also sit in the same traffic as everybody else, so over a long distance the train usually wins.'],
                ['k' => 'Driving', 'v' => 'A driving licence issued in the European Union is enough, one from outside it usually needs an international permit. The motorway network was built in fifteen years, largely with European money, and it drives well. Some concession sections are tolled at barriers, while the state-run sections have been free for cars since July 2024. Parking in city centres is paid on working days.'],
                ['k' => 'Around a city', 'v' => 'Tram, bus, and in Warsaw a metro. Buy the ticket in the jakdojade app or from the machine at the stop, by card. Larger cities also have public bicycles and scooters by the minute, and Bolt and Uber work out cheaper than hailing a taxi.'],
                ['k' => 'What to expect', 'v' => 'Stations in the big cities have been rebuilt, and are clean and signed in English. A train can run a quarter of an hour late and nobody makes a scene about it. Carriages are quiet, phone calls happen in the corridor, and you say dzien dobry when you step into a compartment.'],
            ],
        ],
        'practical' => [
            'label' => 'Practical',
            'title' => 'Six things you were going to ask anyway',
            'items' => [
                ['k' => 'Emergency number', 'v' => '112, as everywhere in the European Union, and the operator will answer in English. The old numbers still work too: 999 ambulance, 998 fire, 997 police.'],
                ['k' => 'Tap water', 'v' => 'In cities it is tested and safe to drink. It can taste distinctly of chlorine, so not everybody likes it, but buying bottled water is not necessary here.'],
                ['k' => 'Paying', 'v' => 'Card and phone work virtually everywhere, including market stalls and buses. Cash is worth having for small milk bars and for some stallholders.'],
                ['k' => 'Tipping', 'v' => 'Nothing like the obligation it is in the United States. If you want to leave something, ten per cent is plenty. Say the amount as you pay by card, because the terminal will not ask.'],
                ['k' => 'The pharmacy', 'v' => 'A pharmacist will advise you and can sell more without a prescription than you might expect. Larger cities have round the clock pharmacies, and in the evening one nominated pharmacy is on duty.'],
                ['k' => 'Getting around', 'v' => 'Bolt, Uber and Free Now run in every larger city and work out cheaper than hailing a taxi. For public transport, install jakdojade. You type in where you are and where you want to go, and it plans the tram and bus route, shows live departures and sells you the ticket inside the app, so you never have to find a machine or carry coins. It covers most Polish cities and has an English version.'],
            ],
        ],
        'careful' => [
            'label' => 'Careful',
            'title' => 'Six sentences that change the conversation',
            'note' => 'This is not about touchiness. Each of these is either wrong or far too simple, and each of them lands on something that still hurts. Nobody will shout at you, but the conversation will go somewhere else.',
            'items' => [
                ['k' => 'Polish concentration camps', 'v' => 'The camps were built and run by Nazi Germany, in a country it had occupied since 1939 and whose state had been abolished. Poland was the victim of that occupation, not its host. The wording is German Nazi camps in occupied Poland, which is also the form UNESCO uses.'],
                ['k' => 'You were a Soviet republic', 'v' => 'It was not. After 1945 Poland had its own state, its own army and its own government, imposed and dependent on Moscow but its own. That is a satellite state rather than part of the Soviet Union, and the difference is felt sharply here.'],
                ['k' => 'It is almost like Russia', 'v' => 'Polish and Russian cannot be confused on the page, because Polish is written in the Latin alphabet. The history of the two countries is in large part the history of one taking the others independence away. The comparison is meant kindly and still lands badly.'],
                ['k' => 'Everybody here is Catholic', 'v' => 'The country has that reputation and still has thousands of churches, but the share of young people going to mass has fallen very steeply in a decade and a half and is now among the lowest in Europe. Asking somebody about their faith can be as private here as asking what they earn.'],
                ['k' => 'Poles do not like Ukrainians', 'v' => 'Turn the sentence round, because the other way is nearer the truth: we like Ukrainians, and a Ukrainian is welcome here. After 24 February 2022, the day Russia attacked Ukraine, millions of refugees crossed this border, and no camps were waiting for them and none were built, because people took them into their own flats instead. In March that year, 94 per cent of Poles told the state research centre CBOS that the country should be doing it. Close to a million people from Ukraine live here now, in the same blocks of flats, on the same building sites, behind the same shop counters and on the same hospital wards, and their children sit in the same classrooms as ours. If you are Ukrainian, you will be treated as a neighbour here rather than as a guest who has stayed too long. The honest part is that this has got harder rather than easier. In a survey CBOS ran between 8 and 20 January 2026, on a sample of 938 people, 29 per cent of Poles declared liking for Ukrainians and 43 per cent declared dislike, the third year running that the figures have slipped. The argument is about money, meaning which benefits go to whom, and about history. The history is this. Between 1943 and 1945 units of the Ukrainian Insurgent Army, the armed wing of the Ukrainian nationalist movement, killed, on Polish estimates, about a hundred thousand Polish civilians in Volhynia and Eastern Galicia, and Ukrainian civilians died in Polish reprisals. The name that comes up is Stepan Bandera, who had led that movement before the war and whose name Polish turned into the word banderowcy. Anybody who lived there carried it for the rest of their life. The grandmother of the author of this site, born before the war, would still glance over her shoulder at the word Ukraine long after 2000. None of that is aimed at the Ukrainian standing in front of you, and you will not feel it in a shop, at work or at somebody\'s table.', 'link' => 'The whole story: Volhynia and Eastern Galicia, 1943'],
                ['k' => 'Poles hate Russians', 'v' => 'Take this one just as plainly: if you are Russian and you come here, you will be treated like anybody else and nobody will ask you to answer for the war. The quarrel is with a state and its policy rather than with people. Russians living in Poland work and study here normally, Russian literature and music have an audience here, and some of the people who left Russia because they refuse this war have settled here. Poles do not want a war with anybody and would rather live at peace with every neighbour, including that one. What I will not pretend is that the feeling is warm. In the same CBOS survey of January 2026, 7 per cent of Poles declared liking for Russians and 74 per cent declared dislike, the worst result of the twenty-one nations on the list. After the Soviet Union attacked Poland from the east on 17 September 1939, after Katyn, where the Soviet secret police shot nearly 22,000 Polish officers and officials in the spring of 1940, and after the forty-five years of dependence on Moscow that followed the war, that is not hard to understand. The decision to attack Ukraine belongs to the government that took it, not to citizens nobody asked, and in person Poles make that distinction even where a survey cannot show it.'],
            ],
            'close' => 'The rule people actually live by here sits in an old proverb, jak Kuba Bogu, tak Bóg Kubie, which is the Polish way of saying you get back what you give. Somebody who respects the people around them and does nobody any harm is treated the same way here, wherever they came from.',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The restriction on Sunday trading comes from a law in force since 1 March 2018, and both the number of trading Sundays and the list of exceptions have been amended since, so check the particular date before you travel. 112 works across the European Union. Tap water quality is confirmed by the testing the sanitary inspectorate publishes for each city. The pronunciation column is an approximation for a reader of English rather than a phonetic transcription. The German Nazi camps in occupied Poland are named here in the form UNESCO adopted in 2007. The figure of about a hundred thousand Polish victims in Volhynia and Eastern Galicia comes from the Institute of National Remembrance, and Ukrainian victims of Polish reprisals are put at 10,000 to 12,000 by Polish historians and at 17,000 to 24,000 by Ukrainian ones. The Polish parliament called those events a genocide in a resolution of 22 July 2016. The figures for how Poles feel about other nations are from the CBOS survey of 8 to 20 January 2026, carried out on a sample of 938 people, and the 94 per cent who backed taking in refugees is CBOS from March 2022. Both are declarations made to a pollster, which is a different thing from how people behave towards each other, and the page says so. Opening hours and prices are left out on purpose, because they age faster than this page is updated.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the home page',
    ],
    'food' => [

        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Food in Poland',
            'description' => 'What is actually on the table: sour soups, fermented cucumbers, a chop bigger than the plate, and a midday meal that is not lunch. With recipes.',
        ],

        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'Food and the table',
            'title' => 'Sour, fermented,',
            'title_accent' => 'made to last',
            'lede' => 'Polish cooking comes from a cold place with a short growing season, so almost everything good about it began as a way of keeping food until spring. Learn a dozen names and you can order well in any town.',
            'meta_line' => 'Seventeen dishes · about fifteen minutes · one very large lunch',
        ],

        'table' => [
            'label' => 'At the table',
            'title' => 'Six things worth knowing',
            'items' => [
                ['k' => 'Obiad is not lunch', 'v' => 'The biggest meal of the day is eaten in the afternoon, between one and five. On Sunday it can be soup and then a main course. On a working day hardly anybody has time for both, so it is just the main.'],
                ['k' => 'Soup', 'v' => 'Soup is not a starter here, it is the first course of that meal. You can also order nothing but soup and no one will think it odd, and it costs very little.'],
                ['k' => 'Smacznego', 'v' => 'Before anyone starts eating, people say smacznego, roughly smahch-NEH-go. That is the whole ceremony.'],
                ['k' => 'Second helpings', 'v' => 'In a house you are invited to, somebody will ask if you want more. A first no is sometimes taken as politeness, and you may get more anyway.'],
                ['k' => 'Tipping', 'v' => 'Nothing like the obligation it is in the United States, and nobody makes an issue of it. If you want to leave something, ten per cent is the usual figure, or simply whatever you can.'],
                ['k' => 'Paying by card', 'v' => 'Cards work almost everywhere, even at a market stall. The exception is a small milk bar, where you sometimes still need cash.'],
            ],
        ],

        'dishes' => [
            'label' => 'The list',
            'title' => 'Seventeen things to eat',
            'note' => 'In the order you would meet them across a day rather than by importance. Click any dish to open its recipe.',
            'items' => [
                [
                    'photo' => 'pierogi',
                    'name' => 'Pierogi',
                    'kind' => 'Main',
                    'said' => 'pye-ROH-ghee',
                    'w' => 1280, 'h' => 960,
                    'note' => 'Dough parcels, boiled and often fried afterwards. The ruskie kind is potato and curd cheese, and the name points at Ruthenia in the east, not at Russia.',
                    'credit' => 'Photo: MOs810, CC BY-SA 4.0',
                    'recipe' => [
                        'time' => '1 h 30',
                        'serves' => 'serves 4',
                        'ingredients' => [
                            '500 g plain flour',
                            '1 egg',
                            '250 ml hot water',
                            '1 tbsp oil',
                            '1 kg potatoes',
                            '500 g curd cheese',
                            '2 large onions',
                            'butter, to serve',
                            'salt and pepper',
                        ],
                        'steps' => [
                            'Boil the potatoes in salted water, drain, press them through a ricer and leave to cool.',
                            'Chop one onion and soften it in butter. Mix it with the potato and the curd cheese and season well. The filling should taste clearly salty.',
                            'Heap the flour on a board, make a well, add the egg, the oil and a pinch of salt. Pour in the hot water a little at a time and knead until the dough is smooth and springy. Cover with a warm bowl and rest for 20 minutes.',
                            'Roll the dough thin, cut circles with a glass, put a teaspoon of filling on each and seal the edges firmly, or they will open in the pot.',
                            'Cook in batches in salted boiling water. Give them two to three minutes from the moment they float.',
                            'Serve with melted butter, or fry them in a pan with the second onion, diced.',
                        ],
                    ],
                ],
                [
                    'photo' => 'zurek',
                    'name' => 'Zurek',
                    'kind' => 'Soup',
                    'said' => 'ZHOO-rek',
                    'w' => 1280, 'h' => 960,
