            ],
            'notes' => [
                ['name' => 'The market', 'note' => 'Every town has one most days of the week, and it is the cheapest lesson in what the season is: tomatoes in late summer, forest mushrooms in autumn, apples and cabbage in winter. Nobody expects you to haggle.'],
                ['name' => 'Pierogarnia', 'note' => 'A place that does almost nothing but pierogi, boiled or fried, by the dozen. The fastest way to try four fillings in one sitting.'],
                ['name' => 'Restauracja', 'note' => 'An ordinary restaurant will do the classics properly and put a soup, a main and a compote on the menu as a set at midday.'],
                ['name' => 'The new Polish cooking', 'note' => 'Since about 2010 a generation of cooks has gone back to regional recipes, forgotten grains and fermentation, and some of those kitchens are now among the best in central Europe.'],
            ],
        ],

        'wigilia' => [
            'label' => 'One meal above all',
            'title' => 'Wigilia, on Christmas Eve',
            'body_1' => 'Christmas Eve is one of the most important days of the year in Poland, and the whole of Christmas turns on that evening rather than on the day after. Together with Easter it is one of the two days when almost everybody has time off at once, so the family drives in from all over the country and finally sits down together after months of work. The whole of 24 December goes on cooking, and people sit down in the evening, traditionally once the first star appears in the sky. There are twelve dishes and none of them has meat in it.',
            'body_2' => 'Before anyone eats, everyone shares the oplatek, a thin white wafer: each person goes to each other person, breaks off a piece of theirs and wishes them well in their own words. In a large family that takes a quarter of an hour, and it is the part of the evening that gets to people. Then everybody tries a little of every dish and the table gets loud, because everyone has something to tell. One place is left empty for an unexpected guest, there is a wisp of hay under the tablecloth, and after supper come presents, carols, and at midnight the pasterka, the shepherds mass. Vodka does not usually appear on the table on the Eve itself, but from Christmas Day onwards it does.',
            'photo_caption' => 'A Christmas Eve table',
            'photo_credit' => 'Photo: Klarqa, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            'wafer_caption' => 'Oplatek, the wafer broken and shared before anyone eats',
            'wafer_credit' => 'Photo: Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 3.0',
            'items' => [
                ['title' => 'Barszcz with uszka', 'note' => 'Clear beetroot soup with tiny mushroom dumplings floating in it. Almost always the first thing.'],
                ['title' => 'Carp', 'note' => 'Fried in pieces. The custom of buying it alive is older than the refrigerator and is now argued about every December.'],
                ['title' => 'Cabbage with mushrooms', 'note' => 'Sauerkraut stewed with dried forest mushrooms, and the smell of the whole evening.'],
                ['title' => 'Pierogi with cabbage', 'note' => 'The Christmas filling, no meat, often served in the soup rather than beside it.'],
                ['title' => 'Kutia', 'note' => 'Wheat, poppy seed, honey and nuts, eaten mostly in the east of the country and older than Christianity here.'],
                ['title' => 'Kompot z suszu', 'note' => 'A dark drink of dried fruit boiled with spices, served warm. It is not sweet and it is the only thing that survives twelve dishes.'],
            ],
        ],

        'numbers' => [
            'label' => 'In numbers',
            'title' => 'Six figures that explain a lot',
            'items' => [
                ['value' => '89.4 l', 'unit' => 'of beer a head', 'note' => 'What the average person in Poland drank in 2024, down from 93 litres the year before. Beer is more than half of all the alcohol drunk here.'],
                ['value' => '88 m', 'unit' => 'doughnuts in one day', 'note' => 'Eaten by adults on Fat Thursday, the last Thursday before the fast of Lent. The queues outside bakeries start in the morning.'],
                ['value' => '45 kg', 'unit' => 'of bread a head a year', 'note' => 'About half what people ate in the 1980s. There is still a bakery on almost every corner.'],
                ['value' => 'Number 1', 'unit' => 'in the EU for apples', 'note' => 'Poland is the largest producer and exporter of apples in the European Union, which is part of why there is so much apple cake.'],
                ['value' => '30 per cent', 'unit' => 'of the world mushroom trade', 'note' => 'Poland is the biggest exporter of cultivated mushrooms in Europe. Forest mushrooms people still pick themselves.'],
                ['value' => '90 per cent', 'unit' => 'of carp sold in December', 'note' => 'Poland farms more carp than any other EU country, and almost the entire year of it goes on that one evening.'],
            ],
            'note' => 'Alcohol and bread from the Polish statistical office, the doughnut figure from a market survey for Fat Thursday, apples, mushrooms and carp from the National Support Centre for Agriculture. The numbers are from 2024 and 2025.',
        ],

        'easter' => [
            'label' => 'The other holiday',
            'title' => 'Easter, a breakfast worth waiting forty days for',
            'body_1' => 'Easter is the other day of the year that brings the whole family in, and it goes differently from Christmas Eve. It starts the day before: on Holy Saturday people carry a basket of food to church to be blessed. On Sunday at dawn there is the rezurekcja, the resurrection mass, and straight afterwards the family sits down to breakfast. That breakfast, not lunch and not dinner, is the meal that matters over Easter, because it ends forty days of fasting.',
            'body_2' => 'It starts with an egg from the blessed basket. It is shared exactly the way the wafer is shared on Christmas Eve: everyone with everyone, with good wishes. Then the food goes on the table all at once and stays there for hours, because nobody leaves it. The zurek and the white sausage come out hot, while the ham, the stuffed eggs, the horseradish and the cakes wait on platters and people keep going back to them for half the day. In the basket blessed the day before there is an egg, bread, salt, a piece of sausage, horseradish and a sugar lamb with a red flag, and every one of those things stands for something.',
            'photo_caption' => 'Easter breakfast, the whole table at once',
            'photo_credit' => 'Photo: Katarzyna Javaheri, CC0',
            'basket_caption' => 'The blessed basket: egg, bread, salt, a lamb and a sprig of box',
            'basket_credit' => 'Photo: LLEW, CC BY-SA 4.0',
            'items' => [
                ['title' => 'Zurek or white barszcz', 'note' => 'The Easter version, with white sausage and egg in it, often served inside a hollowed loaf.'],
                ['title' => 'White sausage', 'note' => 'Sold uncooked, then boiled or baked rather than smoked. It is really only eaten at this time of year.'],
                ['title' => 'Stuffed eggs', 'note' => 'Halved eggs filled with the yolk mixed with mushrooms or chives, and served cold.'],
                ['title' => 'Ham and horseradish', 'note' => 'Smoked ham with horseradish, or with cwikla, which is horseradish grated with beetroot. The sharpness is there to cut the fat.'],
                ['title' => 'Mazurek', 'note' => 'A flat, very sweet cake under icing and dried fruit, usually with the word Alleluia written across it.'],
                ['title' => 'Babka', 'note' => 'A tall yeast cake with a hole in the middle. Nobody was allowed to talk loudly near a rising babka in case it sank.'],
            ],
            'monday' => 'Easter Monday, the day after Easter Sunday and also a public holiday, is smigus-dyngus, the wet Monday. Until noon you can be soaked with a bucket of water in the street, and it is not a show put on for visitors but a custom several centuries old. Dress that day in something you do not mind losing.',
        ],

        'year' => [
            'label' => 'The rest of the year',
            'title' => 'When else everybody sits down together',
            'items' => [
                ['name' => 'Sunday lunch', 'note' => 'The commonest reason a family ends up in one place. Broth, a main course, cake, and three hours at the table.'],
                ['name' => 'Fat Thursday', 'note' => 'The last Thursday before Lent, the forty days of fasting that lead up to Easter. It falls in February or early March, on a different date every year because Easter moves. The whole country eats doughnuts that day, and queues form outside the bakeries.'],
                ['name' => 'Name days', 'note' => 'The feast day of the saint you are named after, kept instead of a birthday and, for the older generation, counting for more than one. The person whose day it is brings cake to work rather than receiving presents.'],
                ['name' => 'The May weekend', 'note' => 'The first long weekend of May, because both 1 and 3 May are public holidays and most people take the days between them off. From that weekend until September people grill on any patch of grass: sausage, pork neck, beer.'],
                ['name' => 'All Saints', 'note' => 'On 1 November, the day the dead are remembered and one of the biggest in the year. Families drive to cemeteries, often across half the country, and light candles on the graves, so that by evening the cemeteries are glowing.'],
            ],
        ],

        'drinks' => [
            'label' => 'What is in the glass',
            'title' => 'Drinks, in the order you will meet them',
            'items' => [
                ['name' => 'Kompot', 'note' => 'Fruit boiled in water and cooled, barely sweet. What children and half the adults drink with the midday meal.'],
                ['name' => 'Herbata', 'note' => 'Tea, black, often with lemon and drunk from a glass. Ordering it with milk will be understood but noticed.'],
                ['name' => 'Piwo', 'note' => 'Beer, and the everyday drink. Poland has a large craft scene, and the standard lagers are cheap and cold.'],
                ['name' => 'Wodka', 'note' => 'Cold, in small glasses, swallowed in one go and always with food. It belongs to toasts and celebrations, not to an ordinary dinner.'],
                ['name' => 'Kefir and maslanka', 'note' => 'Fermented milk drinks, sold in every shop, drunk cold in summer and offered to you as a cure for most things.'],
                ['name' => 'Woda gazowana', 'note' => 'Sparkling water. If you want it still, ask for niegazowana, or you will get bubbles by default.'],
            ],
        ],

        'myths' => [
            'label' => 'What is true',
            'title' => 'Six things people repeat',
            'items' => [
                ['tag' => 'Wrong country', 'title' => 'Pierogi ruskie are Russian', 'note' => 'They are not. The name comes from Rus, the old Ruthenian lands that are now split between south-eastern Poland and western Ukraine, and it describes where the recipe travelled from. The filling is potato and curd cheese. Nothing about it is Russian, and saying so in a restaurant will start a longer conversation than you wanted.'],
                ['tag' => 'Half true', 'title' => 'It is all meat and potatoes', 'note' => 'There is a lot of pork, and a lot of potato. There is also more fermenting, souring and pickling than almost anywhere else in Europe, whole meals built on cabbage, beetroot, buckwheat and mushrooms, and a Christmas Eve dinner with no meat in it at all. Vegetarians have had it steadily easier here since about 2015, and milk bars have always been half meat-free because the subsidy only pays for those dishes.'],
                ['tag' => 'Not with dinner', 'title' => 'Poles drink vodka with everything', 'note' => 'Vodka belongs to weddings, name days, holidays, funerals and long evenings, where it is poured cold into small glasses, drunk in one go and always chased with food. Nobody sips it through a meal. What is actually on the table on an ordinary day is kompot, tea, water or beer.'],
                ['tag' => 'Unsettled', 'title' => 'The Poles invented vodka', 'note' => 'Both sides of that argument quote documents and none of them settles it. The Polish side cites a court record from Sandomierz of 1405, except that the word wodka there means a small body of water, a pond, rather than a drink. It turns up in something like its present sense only in 1534, in a herbal by Stefan Falimirz, and there it is a medicine. Which country distilled first is not decided by any of that paperwork.'],
                ['tag' => 'Older than you think', 'title' => 'The bagel is from New York', 'note' => 'The oldest known mention of a bagel is from 1610, in the rules of the Jewish community of Krakow, which record that they were given to women after childbirth. It reached New York with Polish Jews at the end of the 19th century. Its older relative, the Krakow obwarzanek, is written down in 1394 in the accounts of the court of Wladyslaw Jagiello and Queen Jadwiga, and is still sold off carts in the street there.'],
                ['tag' => 'The name misleads', 'title' => 'A milk bar serves milk', 'note' => 'It does not. The name comes from what used to fill the board in those places, dishes made with milk and eggs, which were the cheap meat-free ones. Today there is both, and nobody orders milk as such. It is simply a cheap canteen with soup and a main course.'],
            ],
        ],

        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The milk bar subsidy is a real line in the Polish state budget: about 61 million zloty in 2025, down from about 71 million in 2024, and it is what makes the meat-free dishes the cheapest things on the board. Prices were checked in 2025 and will move, which is why they are given as ranges. Pierogi ruskie take their name from Rus, the Ruthenian lands, not from Russia. The 1405 record from the Sandomierz court books that circulates online as the first mention of vodka is about a small body of water rather than a drink, and the word turns up in its present sense only in Stefan Falimirz in 1534. The oldest known mention of a bagel is in the rules of the Krakow Jewish community of 1610, and of the obwarzanek in the royal court accounts of 1394. The twelve dishes of Christmas Eve, the wafer and the extra place setting are described here as households actually keep them, and the explanations offered for the number twelve differ from region to region, so none is presented as the reason. Opening hours are left out on purpose, because they age faster than this page is updated.',
        ],

        'next' => 'Back to the home page',
    ],

    'life_now' => [

        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Poland today',
            'description' => 'The country you will actually land in: safe, paying into a public health service, with documents in a phone and an economy that is growing.',
        ],

        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'Poland today · Everyday life',
            'title' => 'The country you',
            'title_accent' => 'will actually land in',
            'lede' => 'The other pages on this site are about what happened here. This part is about what living here is like now: what works, what does not, and why somebody who could leave stays.',
            'meta_line' => 'Eleven parts · about fifteen minutes · every figure dated',
        ],

        'author' => [
            'label' => 'From the author',
            'title' => 'Why I do not want to move abroad',
            'body_1' => 'I write this site myself and I am not a travel agency, so I can say it plainly: I could work from anywhere in Europe and I do not want to. What follows is my list of reasons, set out honestly, with the things that do not work here included, because without those it would be a leaflet rather than an opinion.',
            'body_2' => 'I am not claiming this is the best country in the world. I am claiming that a few things people elsewhere have to worry about every day simply do not take up room in my head here. And that those happen to be the things you notice most once you have a child and a mortgage.',
        ],

        'safety' => [
            'label' => 'The first reason',
            'title' => 'You walk home at night the way you walk home by day',
            'stat_value' => '0.7',
            'stat_unit' => 'homicides per 100,000 people',
            'stat_note' => 'One of the lowest rates in Europe, from United Nations data for 2024. Assaults run at about 17 per 100,000, also among the lowest in the European Union.',
            'body_1' => 'Statistics are dry, so let me put it another way. At two in the morning I walk across the main square with a small child and I do not check who is behind me. I walk home in the dark without planning a route that avoids a particular district. We are not afraid of walking at night, and nobody in my house has to send a message to say they got home.',
            'body_2' => 'This does not mean there is no crime, because there is, or that no city has streets you would rather not cut through late at night, because every large city has them. It means that fear is not the background of the day. For somebody arriving from a place where it is otherwise, this is often the first thing they notice.',
        ],

        'health' => [
            'label' => 'The second reason',
            'title' => 'A year for a specialist, but nobody goes bankrupt',
            'body_1' => 'Start with what the letters mean, because NFZ comes up constantly here. The Narodowy Fundusz Zdrowia, the National Health Fund, is a single public pot: everybody who works has a health contribution taken from their income every month, and the fund pays out of it for the treatment of everybody insured. There is no policy to buy, no package to pick and nobody can be turned down. Now the part that does not work, because we all complain about it: waiting for a specialist on the NFZ can take a year or two. Some clinics book so far ahead that anyone with money goes private and pays a second time for something they already contribute to every month.',
            'body_2' => 'Now the other side. I get an appointment with my family doctor in one to three days. An ambulance costs nothing and you call one without doing sums first. A week in hospital does not end with a bill that changes a family for ten years. I also do not have a policy that I lose along with a job. When I read what an ambulance ride costs in the United States, I stop complaining about the queue for an orthopaedist. If you are coming from the European Union, a European Health Insurance Card gets you urgent treatment here on the same terms as a local.',
        ],

        'tech' => [
            'label' => 'The third reason',
            'title' => 'I have not carried a wallet for two years',
            'body_1' => 'That is not a figure of speech. The last time I had a wallet on me was about two years ago. A card or a phone pays for everything: the bus, the barber, the market stall, the kebab window, the village shop. A place with a cash only sign is rare enough that you remember it.',
            'body_2' => 'My identity card and driving licence are in the phone as well, in the government app mObywatel, and that is not a gimmick. The act of 26 May 2023, in force from 14 July that year, made the mDowod equal to an ordinary identity card inside Poland. Offices, courts, clinics, the post office and notaries have to accept it, and banks have had to since 1 September 2023. The driving licence has been in the app since 2020, and the police, the border guard and the road transport inspectorate are obliged to honour it, because they check your entitlement in the central register anyway. You cannot cross a border on it, which is worth remembering, but inside the country the plastic is redundant.',
        ],

        'together' => [
            'label' => 'The fifth reason',
            'title' => 'We will not chat without a reason, but in a crisis we stand together',
            'body_1' => 'We do not start conversations without a reason. Nobody says good morning in the street and nobody asks how you are doing in a queue, because this is not America, where the question is a reflex. Day to day we are also a divided country, and one conversation about politics is enough to hear it. At first glance it reads as coldness, and as a nation that does not hold together.',
            'body_2' => 'And then something like this happens. In April 2026 a twenty-three-year-old internet creator called Piotr Hancke, known as Latwogang, started a live stream on YouTube and did not stop it for nine days. He was raising money for Cancer Fighters, a foundation that helps children with cancer. It started with a song the rapper Bedoes recorded with Maja, one of the children the foundation supports. One after another they came, some to the flat he was streaming from, some down a camera link: Robert Lewandowski, Iga Swiatek, Adam Malysz, the singer Doda, and towards the end Chris Martin of Coldplay.',
            'body_3' => 'We raised more than 280 million zloty, comfortably over seventy million dollars, and set a Guinness world record for a charity fundraiser run live on the internet. A country of just under forty million did that in nine days, mostly in transfers of a few tens of zloty each. And it was not a one-off: since 1993 the Great Orchestra of Christmas Charity has spent one Sunday in January collecting for equipment for hospitals that treat children, and on that Sunday there are queues at the buckets in every town.',
            'body_4' => 'Let me put it another way. For those nine days the stream ran in our house without a break. On the second monitor at work, on the television all weekend from morning until night, and when we were helping friends move flat one earphone was still playing it. My girlfriend and I checked the total the way people check a football score. We were not unusual in that, and it is the part the figure of 280 million does not show.',
            'body_5' => 'And that is the thing we know about ourselves. Day to day we argue, but when we have to stand together we do, and history has tested us on it more than once: through the hundred and twenty years when Poland was not on the map at all, in September 1939, and in Solidarnosc, the movement of millions that brought communism down here in the 1980s. None of that has gone out of us. We will not start a conversation with you in the street, but when it counts we all put in.',
        ],

        'favour' => [
            'label' => 'The sixth reason',
            'title' => 'Two currencies: the favour and the zloty',
            'body_1' => 'People help each other here and you can count on it. A neighbour will help you carry a washing machine up the stairs, a friend from school will connect your boiler, an uncle will turn up with a trailer and move your furniture. Nobody issues an invoice and nobody names a price, because it is not a service, it is a favour. It still has to be repaid, and that is where something other than money starts to count.',
            'body_2' => 'Most often it is a favour for a favour. You help with the move today, he helps with your kitchen in six months, and nobody writes it down because both of them remember. Sometimes it is money pressed into a pocket over protests. And sometimes it is a flaszka, a bottle of vodka put on the table without a word of explanation. It is not a tip and it is not a bribe, and it will not get you anything at an office counter. It is a way of levelling the account so that nobody is left owing anybody. The habit is left over from the communist years before 1989, when the shops had nothing in them and who you knew, and what you could repay them with, counted for more than cash.',
            'money_title' => 'And the second currency is the zloty',
            'body_3' => 'You do not pay in euro here. The currency is the zloty, written zl or PLN, and it divides into a hundred grosz. Plenty of Poles complain that the country never joined the euro, and it is a standing argument. (Adopting the euro is not simply swapping one set of notes for another. It would mean handing the decisions about interest rates and the exchange rate to Frankfurt, and for an economy still growing faster than the EU average that is a real cost rather than a formality.)',
            'body_4' => 'A currency of your own works as a shock absorber. When things go badly in Europe the zloty can weaken, Polish exports get cheaper as a result, and part of the blow is taken by the exchange rate instead of by redundancies. That showed in the crisis of 2008 and 2009, when Poland was the only country in the European Union that did not fall into recession. In recent years the zloty has tended to strengthen, and you can see what it is worth today further down, in the section of figures, because we pull it straight from the National Bank of Poland.',
        ],

        'work' => [
            'label' => 'The seventh reason',
            'title' => 'We stopped being the cheap option and the work kept coming',
            'body_1' => 'Poland has stopped being a country you move work to because it is cheap. Work comes here because there are people who can do it. The largest technology companies in the world keep research and development centres, not call centres, in Warsaw, Krakow, Wroclaw and Gdansk, and Polish student teams reach the finals of the world university programming championship year after year.',
            'body_2' => 'The same shows up outside technology: in medicine, in construction, in video games, and in the good tradespeople you have to hunt for but can actually find. For me that has a simple consequence. I can do ambitious work here and I do not have to emigrate to get it.',
        ],

        'school' => [
            'label' => 'The eighth reason',
            'title' => 'School and university without tuition',
            'body_1' => 'School is compulsory and free, and full-time courses at public universities charge no tuition. Nobody takes out a loan for a degree and nobody spends the next twenty years paying it off. What comes out of that is a country where a degree is ordinary rather than a privilege, and you notice it in conversation with whoever you happen to meet.',
            'body_2' => 'To be fair about it: free study does not mean free living. The university is usually in another city, so there is a hall of residence or a rented room and five years of keeping yourself. I took two degrees at private universities and paid for both. I did not get a place for my engineering degree in my own city, although my brother did get into a public one, and I did my masters at weekends because I was already working. The opportunities are real, but they do not fall the same way for everybody, and branch campuses in smaller towns are opening every year.',
        ],

        'broken' => [
            'label' => 'The other side',
            'title' => 'Six things that do not work here',
            'note' => 'Above is the list of reasons I stay. This is the list of things I would leave out if this page were a leaflet. None of them will spoil a trip, but all of them shape what living here is like.',
            'items' => [
                [
                    'name' => 'The air in winter',
                    'note' => 'From October to March the air in smaller towns and villages can be genuinely bad, because houses are heated with coal and wood. On the worst days the limits are exceeded several times over. The big cities breathe far better than they did ten years ago and old stoves are being replaced with public money, but this one has years left to run.',
                ],
                [
                    'name' => 'Housing',
                    'note' => 'Flat prices have risen faster than wages for a decade, and in the big cities a young person without help from family will not buy one on a salary alone. Rent in Warsaw and Krakow eats a serious part of a pay packet. This is the thing everybody here under forty complains about.',
                ],
                [
                    'name' => 'The divide',
                    'note' => 'Politics splits this country into two camps that watch different television and do not agree on the same facts. It runs through families and through Christmas tables. A visitor will not see it, but if somebody near you starts talking politics you will work out within a minute that it is not a light subject.',
                ],
                [
                    'name' => 'Trains off the main lines',
                    'note' => 'Between the big cities the train is fast, cheap and on time. Away from them it varies, and some smaller towns you simply cannot reach, because the lines were closed in the 1990s and never came back. There is a phrase for it here, transport exclusion, and it affects a few million people.',
                ],
                [
                    'name' => 'English at a government counter',
                    'note' => 'In a bank, at a phone operator or in any larger company, being served in English is normal. At a government office it depends entirely on who is behind the window that day. Forms are often only in Polish, and arranging a translation is your problem.',
                ],
                [
                    'name' => 'The queue for a specialist',
                    'note' => 'I said this higher up, but it is the single most common complaint in this country, so it goes in the list. A family doctor takes a few days. A specialist on the public fund can take a year or two, and anyone with the money goes private and pays a second time.',
                ],
            ],
        ],
        'prices' => [
            'label' => 'What it costs',
            'title' => 'What a day here costs',
            'note' => 'Ranges rather than single numbers, because the gap between a small town and the middle of Warsaw is the real answer. Every price carries the date it was checked, because they move faster than this page is updated. The current value of the zloty is above.',
            'items' => [
                'coffee' => ['name' => 'Coffee in a cafe', 'note' => 'A cup in a city. More in a chain, less in a small cafe out in the country.'],
                'beer' => ['name' => 'Beer in a bar', 'note' => 'Half a litre. The top of the range is central Warsaw and Krakow, the bottom is an ordinary bar in a smaller town.'],
                'lunch' => ['name' => 'The midday meal', 'note' => 'The bottom of the range is a milk bar, the cheap state-subsidised canteen. The top is an ordinary restaurant with a soup and a main course.'],
                'transit' => ['name' => 'A city transport ticket', 'note' => 'One journey. A day ticket usually costs about three or four singles, and some cities have free days.'],
                'taxi' => ['name' => 'Across town', 'note' => 'Bolt or Uber, a few kilometres. The apps are everywhere here and work out cheaper than hailing a taxi.'],
                'train' => ['name' => 'Train between big cities', 'note' => 'Warsaw to Krakow, for example, two and a half hours. Booked ahead it can be much less, booked on the day rather more.'],
                'hotel' => ['name' => 'A night', 'note' => 'A double in a decent hotel out of season. Krakow in summer and Zakopane in winter cost more.'],
                'rent' => ['name' => 'Renting a studio flat', 'note' => 'Per month in a big city, before electricity and heating. Outside Warsaw and Krakow, considerably less.'],
            ],
        ],
        'figures' => [
            'label' => 'The country in numbers',
            'title' => 'The economy, prices, and what is moving right now',
            'note' => 'Some of these the page fetches for itself: the exchange rates from the National Bank of Poland and unemployment from the Local Data Bank of the Central Statistical Office. Those are marked as updated automatically, so you do not have to take our word for them. The rest have no open source, so somebody looked them up by hand and left the date on them.',
            'live_label' => 'Updated automatically',
            'checked_label' => 'Checked',
            'reading_label' => 'reading for',
            'items' => [
                'gdp' => ['name' => 'GDP growth', 'note' => 'The forecast for 2026, the analyst consensus. The Polish economy has grown faster than the EU average almost without a break since the 1990s, with one pause in the pandemic.'],
                'inflation' => ['name' => 'Inflation', 'note' => 'The forecast annual average for 2026. At the peak of the energy crisis in 2023 it passed eighteen per cent, so the present level is read here as calm.'],
                'unemployment' => ['name' => 'Unemployment', 'note' => 'The registered unemployment rate, one of the lowest in the European Union. For a visitor it means there is more work here than there are people to do it.'],
                'petrol' => ['name' => 'Petrol', 'note' => 'A litre of 95 octane at the pump. This one moves from week to week, so treat it as a photograph taken on the date given.'],
                'minimum' => ['name' => 'Minimum wage', 'note' => 'Gross per month, set by government regulation and usually raised once or twice a year.'],
            ],
            'rates_label' => 'What a zloty is worth',
            'rates_note' => 'The mid rate from the National Bank of Poland table of the date shown. The bank publishes on working days, so at a weekend you are seeing Friday.',
            'rates_missing' => 'The National Bank of Poland could not be reached just now, so no rates are shown rather than out of date ones.',
        ],

        'digital' => [
            'label' => 'The fourth reason',
            'title' => 'The state fits in a phone',
            'body_1' => 'Things that mean an afternoon off and a queue elsewhere are an app here. The annual tax return arrives already filled in and you approve it with one tap. A prescription is a four-digit code in a text message that you read out at the pharmacy with your national identity number. A sick note goes to the employer electronically and nobody carries a slip of paper. A nursery place is applied for on a website.',
            'body_2' => 'On top of that there is BLIK, the way of paying from a banking app: money sent to another person by phone number arrives in seconds and costs nothing. If you come from a country where a transfer takes three days and cheques still exist, that will strike you as stranger than the pierogi.',
            'items' => [
                ['name' => 'mObywatel', 'note' => 'Identity card and driving licence in the phone, accepted by offices and police inside the country.'],
                ['name' => 'e-PIT', 'note' => 'The annual tax return, filled in for you by the tax office. You only have to accept it.'],
                ['name' => 'e-prescription', 'note' => 'A code by text message instead of a piece of paper, and a doctor can issue one after a phone consultation.'],
                ['name' => 'BLIK', 'note' => 'Paying and sending money to a phone number from a banking app, instantly and free.'],
            ],
        ],

        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'The homicide rate comes from United Nations data for 2024, and the comparison with the rest of the Union from Eurostat. The mDowod is equal to an ordinary identity card under the act on the mObywatel application of 26 May 2023, in force from 14 July 2023, and the obligation on the police and the road transport inspectorate to accept the licence in the app follows rules in force since 2020. The Latwogang fundraiser for the Cancer Fighters foundation ran for nine days in April 2026 and raised more than 280 million zloty, which Guinness World Records recognised as a record for a charity fundraiser held live on the internet. The growth and inflation numbers are the analyst consensus quoted in mid 2026, not final figures. Fuel prices are given with a date because they move from week to week. The euro rate is taken directly from the National Bank of Poland. The prices in the table were checked in September 2026 and are given as ranges, because they depend on the city and the season. The winter breaches of air quality limits are recorded by the Chief Inspectorate of Environmental Protection, and most of them come from heating houses with coal and wood.',
        ],

        'next' => 'Back to the home page',
    ],

    'start' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Start here',
            'description' => 'Six texts in order for somebody who knows nothing about Poland: where it is, what happened here, how people live now and how to behave. About forty minutes.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'A reading path',
            'title' => 'I know nothing',
            'title_accent' => 'about Poland',
            'lede' => 'This is the order worth reading the site in. Six texts, each of which stands on its own, but together they answer the question of where you are actually going.',
            'meta_line' => 'Six texts · about forty minutes · come back whenever',
        ],
        'note' => 'Nothing here has to be read in order and nothing is locked. This list is for somebody who has landed on the site for the first time and does not know where to start.',
        'steps' => [
            ['n' => '01', 'title' => 'Where this actually is', 'note' => 'The middle of Europe, a flat country with no natural border to the east or the west, and why that one sentence explains most of the history here.', 'link' => 'Read it'],
            ['n' => '02', 'title' => 'A thousand years, briefly', 'note' => 'From the baptism of 966 to joining the European Union: the largest state in Europe, a hundred and twenty-three years with no country at all, two occupations and 1989.', 'link' => 'Read it'],
            ['n' => '03', 'title' => 'What living here is like', 'note' => 'Safety, health care, documents in a phone, the economy, and an honest list of what does not work. Plus what a day costs.', 'link' => 'Read it'],
            ['n' => '04', 'title' => 'What people eat', 'note' => 'Seventeen dishes with their recipes, the midday meal that is not lunch, and the two days a year when everybody sits down together.', 'link' => 'Read it'],
            ['n' => '05', 'title' => 'How to behave', 'note' => 'Saying hello on the way in, pan and pani instead of you, how to pronounce Polish names, sixteen phrases, and the sentences best left unsaid.', 'link' => 'Read it'],
            ['n' => '06', 'title' => 'Where to go', 'note' => 'Cities and places, each with its own history, a walk that fits in a day, and the things people repeat about them that are not true.', 'link' => 'Read it'],
        ],
        'after' => [
            'title' => 'And after that',
            'body' => 'Beyond this there are several dozen history articles, each about one thing and each with its sources. If you are after something specific, start from the history index. If your family came from here, there is a separate page about how to look.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the home page',
    ],
    'method' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'How this site is made',
            'description' => 'Where the facts on this site come from, what is deliberately left out and why, a dated log of corrections, and how to report an error.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'About this site',
            'title' => 'How this gets written',
            'title_accent' => 'and what I got wrong',
            'lede' => 'The home page says everything here is free, sourced, and that corrections are visible. This is the page where you can check that.',
            'meta_line' => 'Method · Corrections · Contact',
        ],
        'promise' => [
            'label' => 'The deal',
            'title' => 'What this site is and what it is not',
            'body_1' => 'I write it myself, as a private person. It is not a travel agency and it is not an institutional project either. There are no advertisements, no sponsored entries, no affiliate links and nothing to buy. There is no newsletter waiting at the bottom of the screen. Nobody pays me to make one place come out better than another.',
            'body_2' => 'What I do not promise: that everything is here, or that I am never wrong. The site is written piece by piece, plenty of subjects are still waiting their turn, and some of the text certainly contains mistakes I do not know about yet. That is what the list below is for, along with the address for telling me about the next one.',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Method',
            'title' => 'Where the facts here come from',
            'note' => 'Every longer article ends with its own sources section saying what came from where. What follows applies everywhere.',
            'items' => [
                ['k' => 'Institutions before the internet', 'v' => 'Museums, archives and research institutes come first, ahead of news portals and online encyclopedias. For history that mostly means the Institute of National Remembrance, the Museum of the History of Polish Jews, the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum and the museums of the individual sites. For figures about Poland today it means the Central Statistical Office, the National Bank of Poland and Eurostat.'],
                ['k' => 'No number without a name', 'v' => 'If there is a figure on this site, whoever publishes it is named next to it. Where historians give different figures I print them all and say whose they are, rather than picking one and pretending to a certainty nobody has.'],
                ['k' => 'What scholarship rejects is not here', 'v' => 'Theories historians have rejected are not described, not even in order to argue with them, because printing them spreads them and a foreign reader has no way to weigh my rebuttal. Legends stay, but they are labelled as legends, with the archaeology next to them.'],
                ['k' => 'Nothing that goes off quickly', 'v' => 'There are no opening hours and no ticket prices here, because they age faster than this page is updated. Where a price is needed I give a range with the date it was checked. The exchange rates and the unemployment rate the page fetches for itself, from the National Bank of Poland and the Central Statistical Office, and marks as updated automatically.'],
                ['k' => 'Pictures and maps are captioned', 'v' => 'Every photograph carries its author and licence, and a map carries its publisher, year and language as well. The maps are real ones, drawn by a cartographer or taken from an atlas, not drawn by me in an editor. On the rare occasion I do draw a diagram, the caption says plainly that it is a diagram.'],
                ['k' => 'Difficult subjects', 'v' => 'The occupation, the Holocaust, communism and relations with the neighbours come up here constantly. I write about them factually, without nationalist framing and without turning sites of murder into attractions. Where an argument between countries is still running, I say that it is running instead of settling it.'],
            ],
        ],
        'corrections' => [
            'label' => 'Corrections',
            'title' => 'What was wrong here and when I fixed it',
            'note' => 'A list of things that went onto the site in the wrong form and were changed. I record what it said before, because a correction that hides what was corrected proves nothing. The list starts in September 2026, along with this page.',
            'items' => [
                ['date' => '2026-09-20', 'where' => 'Volhynia, the borders, everyday life', 'was' => 'Two pages gave different scales for the same crime: one said tens of thousands of victims and only 1943, the other about a hundred thousand across 1943 to 1945.', 'now' => 'Every place says the same thing and attributes it, with the Polish and Ukrainian estimates given separately, because they differ.'],
                ['date' => '2026-09-20', 'where' => 'Poland today', 'was' => 'I said the electronic prescription is a six-digit code in a text message.', 'now' => 'The code has four digits and is read out at the pharmacy along with your national identity number.'],
                ['date' => '2026-09-20', 'where' => 'Everyday life and language', 'was' => 'The sentence about the camps said only Germany.', 'now' => 'It says Nazi Germany, as the rest of the site does.'],
                ['date' => '2026-09-20', 'where' => 'Everyday life and language', 'was' => 'I gave it as a rule that flowers are handed over in odd numbers, because even numbers belong to funerals.', 'now' => 'The sentence is gone. It came from etiquette guides rather than from life, and could not be stood up.'],
                ['date' => '2026-09-20', 'where' => 'Lodz', 'was' => 'The city was renamed Litzmannstadt after a general, without saying which one.', 'now' => 'The general has a name: Karl Litzmann, who commanded here in the winter of 1914.'],
                ['date' => '2026-09-20', 'where' => 'Everyday life and language', 'was' => 'I described All Saints as not a cheerful day.', 'now' => 'It is not a day of mourning either. Relatives meet at the graves and talk about the dead, often laughing while they do.'],
            ],
            'columns' => ['date' => 'When', 'where' => 'Where', 'was' => 'It said', 'now' => 'It says'],
        ],
        'contact' => [
            'label' => 'Contact',
            'title' => 'You found a mistake?',
            'body' => 'Write. The most useful report says which page, which sentence, and what it should say instead, ideally with a source. You do not have to be a historian and you do not have to write in Polish. Corrections that land go into the list above, together with what it said before.',
            'note' => 'I answer myself, and not always straight away, because this is done after hours.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the home page',
    ],

    'roots' => [
        'meta' => [
            'title' => 'Tracing Polish roots: where the records are',
            'description' => 'Why the town is in another country now, why the surname looks wrong, where the records actually are, and what citizenship by descent is and is not.',
        ],
        'hero' => [
            'kicker' => 'Polish roots · Archives and records',
            'title' => 'Your family left',
            'title_accent' => 'and the paper stayed',
            'lede' => 'If a great-grandfather sailed from Hamburg in 1907 and nobody in the family speaks Polish any more, you are probably holding three things: a surname spelt in a way no Pole would spell it, a half remembered village name, and a year. That is enough to start with, and this page is about what to do with it.',
            'meta_line' => 'Eight parts · about fifteen minutes · sources at the end',
            'photo_alt' => 'A narrow archive room with cardboard boxes of files stacked from floor to ceiling along both walls, each box carrying a printed label',
            'photo_caption' => 'A storeroom of the State Archive in Szczecin. Polish records sit in boxes like these, each one labelled with the collection it belongs to and its reference number.',
            'photo_credit' => 'Photo: Kapitel, 2019, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0',
        ],
        'start' => [
            'label' => 'Before you start',
            'title' => 'Three things decide whether this works',
            'body_1' => 'A search for a Polish ancestor almost never fails because the records were destroyed. It fails because nobody knows which parish to ask. Poland had no single central register of people before 1946, so every birth, marriage and death was written down in one particular parish or one particular office, and without the name of the place there is no entry to find. Everything below really comes down to getting from what your family remembers to the name of one village.',
            'body_2' => 'This is slow work and it can end without an answer, so it is better said at the start. A surname in four spellings, a village renamed twice and a family that told the story differently every time are normal here rather than exceptional. Most people who get there do it over months, one document at a time, and the first useful document is usually found abroad rather than in Poland.',
            'items' => [
                ['k' => 'The name', 'v' => 'Every version of it you can find, not the one you settled on. Gravestones, obituaries, army papers and the passenger list of the ship all spell it differently, and one of those spellings is the Polish one.'],
                ['k' => 'The place', 'v' => 'A village, not a region. Galicia or Poland written on a form is the name of a whole province rather than an address. What you need is a parish, and it is the village that tells you which parish.'],
                ['k' => 'The year', 'v' => 'Roughly when they were born and roughly when they left. The year decides which empire held the village at the time, and therefore what language the record is in.'],
                ['k' => 'The religion', 'v' => 'Roman Catholic, Greek Catholic, Jewish, Orthodox or Protestant. Each community kept its own books, so this decides which book the entry is in.'],
            ],
        ],
        'borders' => [
            'label' => 'The map moved',
            'title' => 'The village may be in Ukraine, Belarus or Lithuania now',
            'body_1' => 'Poland is not where Poland was when your family left. After the Second World War the country was moved several hundred kilometres west. The eastern half of pre-war Poland, with cities such as Lwow and Wilno, was taken by the Soviet Union and lies today in Ukraine, Belarus and Lithuania, and in exchange Poland was given German land in the west, where Breslau became Wroclaw and Stettin became Szczecin. Nobody asked the people living in either place, and millions of them were moved.',
            'body_2' => 'That has two consequences for a search. The first is that the record you want may be held in Vilnius, Minsk, Grodno or Lviv, while part of it is in Warsaw all the same, because books were carried west with the people. The second is that the village now carries a Ukrainian, Belarusian or Lithuanian name, so searching for the Polish one returns nothing. Find the pre-war name and the county it sat in, then work out what the place is called today.',
            'link' => 'The whole story: how the borders moved',
        ],
        'name' => [
            'label' => 'The surname',
            'title' => 'Why it is not spelt the way a Pole would spell it',
            'body_1' => 'Polish uses letters English does not have and sounds that English writes another way, so a Polish surname almost never crosses an ocean unchanged. The accents go first, because no American form and no American typewriter had them: Wojcik for Wójcik, Zukowski for Żukowski, Dombrowski for Dąbrowski. Then the sounds get respelt by ear, because English has no single letter for them. The pair sz becomes sh or s, cz becomes ch, w becomes v or f, because Polish w is the English v, and j becomes y, because Polish j is the English y.',
            'body_2' => 'Then there are the bigger jumps. Some families cut the name down and gave it an English ending, so Adamczyk became Adams and Łukaszewicz became Lukas. Some translated it, because a great many Polish surnames are ordinary words: Kowal, a blacksmith, became Smith, Młynarz, a miller, became Miller, Król, a king, became King, Bednarz, a man who makes barrels, became Cooper. And some names were simply swapped for an American one that sounded like nothing in particular. The Polish Genealogical Society of Connecticut and the Northeast, an American society that collects these from gravestones and obituaries, has Mościcki turning into Marson and Zieziulewicz into Fox.',
            'myth_title' => 'Nobody changed your name at Ellis Island',
            'myth_body_1' => 'This is the story almost every family tells and it is not true. The inspectors at Ellis Island, the immigration station in New York harbour that about twelve million people passed through between 1892 and 1954, did not write names down at all. The only list of names they had was the ship manifest, and that was drawn up in Europe, at the port of departure and often earlier still, at the desk of the agent who sold the ticket back home. The inspector found the numbered line the passenger had been written on and asked the questions again to check the answers already there. Marian L. Smith, the senior historian of the United States immigration service, quotes the rule they worked under plainly: the inspector was not to change an immigrant’s details unless the immigrant asked him to and unless the inspection showed the original entry was wrong.',
            'myth_body_2' => 'The language excuse does not hold either. Philip Sutton of the New York Public Library, who wrote the best known account of this, notes that inspectors spoke three languages each on average and were sent to the arrivals whose languages they spoke, with staff interpreters and immigrant aid societies on hand as well. What did happen is less dramatic and more interesting. Names were altered at the ticket agent’s desk in Europe, when one clerk copied them off a form onto the manifest. They were distorted in the old country, when a Polish name had to be written in Russian letters and somebody later turned it back into Latin ones. Above all they were changed in America, by the family itself, over years, on payrolls, in parish books and in census returns filled in by English speakers. From 1906 a court could also change the name formally when somebody was naturalised, and the naturalisation file is where that moment is recorded.',
            'items' => [
                ['k' => 'Accents', 'v' => 'Polish has nine letters English does not, and a form with no room for them flattens every one. Żukowski loses its dot and becomes Zukowski, Dąbrowski loses its tail and is often respelt by sound as Dombrowski.'],
                ['k' => 'Sounds', 'v' => 'The pair sz is the English sh, cz is ch, w is v, and j is y. A clerk writing by ear gives forms like Shimanski for Szymański and Yankowski for Jankowski.'],
                ['k' => 'Cut short', 'v' => 'A long name is trimmed and given an English ending. Adamczyk turns into Adams and Łukaszewicz into Lukas, and both are on the list the Polish Genealogical Society of Connecticut and the Northeast keeps.'],
                ['k' => 'Translated', 'v' => 'A great many Polish surnames are the names of trades. Kowal is a blacksmith and became Smith, Młynarz is a miller and became Miller, Bednarz makes barrels and became Cooper.'],
                ['k' => 'The ski ending', 'v' => 'In America it is written sky as often as ski, under the pull of the Russian, Yiddish and German names already spelt that way there.'],
                ['k' => 'The women', 'v' => 'In Poland the daughter of Mr Kowalski is Kowalska rather than Kowalski, and older records also write Kowalówna for an unmarried woman and Kowalowa for a married one. American clerks flattened all of it to the masculine form, which is why a grandmother’s maiden name abroad often does not match her Polish birth record.'],
            ],
        ],
        'records' => [
            'label' => 'Where the paper is',
            'title' => 'Nine addresses that hold almost all of it',
            'note' => 'None of the indexes below charges for a search. They differ in what they actually hold, so it is worth knowing which one answers which question. The web addresses are written out in full so that you can type them yourself.',
            'items' => [
                ['k' => 'The State Archives', 'v' => 'Archiwa Państwowe, the Polish state archive network: three central archives in Warsaw and thirty regional ones with their own branches. Records older than the retention period described below end up here, and a great deal of it is scanned and free to read.', 'web' => 'szukajwarchiwach.gov.pl'],
                ['k' => 'Geneteka', 'v' => 'An index of names built by volunteers of the Polskie Towarzystwo Genealogiczne, the Polish Genealogical Society, out of parish and civil registers, searchable by surname and by province. In September 2026 it held over 67 million entries from more than six thousand parishes, and it grows every week. It gives you the entry and the parish rather than the scan.', 'web' => 'geneteka.genealodzy.pl'],
                ['k' => 'FamilySearch', 'v' => 'The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints microfilmed Polish parish and civil registers for decades and has digitised most of that film. A free account opens the catalogue. Watch the icon beside a collection: a plain camera means you can read it anywhere, a camera with a key means the Polish archive that owns it allows viewing only inside a family history centre.', 'web' => 'familysearch.org'],
                ['k' => 'The registry office', 'v' => 'Urząd stanu cywilnego, the civil registry office of the town where the record was written. Under the act of 28 November 2014 a birth record stays there for a hundred years and a marriage or death record for eighty, counted from the end of the year it was made, and then goes to the state archive. So a great-grandfather born in 1890 is in an archive, while a grandmother born in 1935 is still at the office, and to get her record you have to show that you descend from her.', 'web' => null],
                ['k' => 'The parish', 'v' => 'Many registers, in the south above all, are still kept at the parish or in a diocesan archive rather than by the state. Church archives sit outside the state archive system and there is no legal right of access to them, so it depends on the parish priest. A letter in Polish, sent well before you travel, works far better than turning up at the door.', 'web' => null],
                ['k' => 'AGAD in Warsaw', 'v' => 'Archiwum Główne Akt Dawnych, the Central Archives of Historical Records, holds the parish books rescued from the lands Poland lost in the east: Roman Catholic registers of the Lviv archdiocese from 1604, Greek Catholic ones from 1728, Jewish ones from 1789. The catalogues are online, the page images for the most part are not, so this is a place you write to or visit.', 'web' => 'agad.gov.pl'],
                ['k' => 'The eastern archives', 'v' => 'For a village now in Lithuania, the Lithuanian State Historical Archive in Vilnius, whose Catholic registers are partly scanned at epaveldas.lt. For Belarus, the National Historical Archives in Minsk and its branch in Grodno. For western Ukraine, the Central State Historical Archive in Lviv. Some books never stayed there: the civil registers carried west when those provinces were annexed in 1945 are held by the registry office of Warszawa Śródmieście and pass to AGAD as they age past the retention period.', 'web' => 'epaveldas.lt'],
                ['k' => 'Jewish records', 'v' => 'JRI-Poland indexes Jewish vital records from present and former Polish territory and tells you which archive holds the original. JewishGen hosts that index alongside its own All Poland Database and a great deal else. Both are free and both ask you to register.', 'web' => 'jri-poland.org'],
                ['k' => 'The ship', 'v' => 'The passenger manifest is the last document that writes the name the way it was written in Poland, and it usually names the village as well and a relative left behind. Arrivals in New York are searchable free through the Statue of Liberty and Ellis Island Foundation, whose database runs from 1820 to 1957. It covers Ellis Island and, before that, Castle Garden, the landing station that received immigrants at the tip of Manhattan until Ellis Island opened in 1892.', 'web' => 'statueofliberty.org'],
            ],
        ],
        'language' => [
            'label' => 'The language of the record',
            'title' => 'Latin, German or Russian, and the partitions decide which',
            'body_1' => 'Poland vanished from the map for a hundred and twenty three years. Between 1772 and 1795 its neighbours, Russia, Prussia and Austria, divided the country between them in three steps, and it did not come back as a state until 1918. That is why the birth record of a Polish great-grandfather is very rarely in Polish. Whichever empire held the village kept the register in its own official language, and the year and the place together tell you which one you are looking at.',
            'body_2' => 'The good news is that these records follow a formula. A Russian era entry from the Polish lands is one long paragraph of fixed legal wording, always in the same order, and that wording is a direct translation of the Polish formula used before it. A Galician record is a ruled table with the same columns on every page. Once you recognise twenty words you can pull the names, the dates and the village out of a document in a language you cannot read.',
            'items' => [
                ['k' => 'The Russian partition, 1808 to 1868', 'v' => 'In the part of Poland that ended up under Russia, civil registration began in 1808 in the Duchy of Warsaw, the short lived state created by Napoleon Bonaparte, and the records were kept in Polish by the parish priest acting for the state. Until 1825 a single register covered everybody. From 1826, under the Civil Code of the Kingdom of Poland published on 13 June 1825, each religious community kept its own.'],
                ['k' => 'The Russian partition, 1868 to 1918', 'v' => 'After the January Uprising of 1863, a Polish rising against Russian rule that was put down within a year and a half, Russia set about erasing what was left of Polish self government. From 1868 the same records had to be written in Russian, in Cyrillic letters, and they stayed that way until Poland became a state again in 1918. The language changed, the wording did not.'],
                ['k' => 'Austrian Galicia, from 1784', 'v' => 'Galicia was the name Austria gave the southern share it took, around Krakow, Rzeszow, Przemysl and Lviv. A patent issued by the Emperor Joseph II on 20 February 1784 turned parish books into legal state documents in a fixed columnar layout, kept in Latin. Polish and German turn up in later entries and in notes in the margin. Jewish communities kept their own books from a patent of 7 May 1789, and from 1875 a clerk appointed by the government wrote them rather than the rabbi.'],
                ['k' => 'The Prussian partition, from 1874', 'v' => 'In the north and west, taken by Prussia, church registers were kept in Latin by Catholic parishes and in German by Protestant ones. Then the state took the job over. A Prussian law of 9 March 1874 created the Standesamt, the civil registry office, from 1 October 1874, and from 1 January 1876 the same system covered the whole German Empire. Those registers are in German from beginning to end.'],
                ['k' => 'After 1918', 'v' => 'With independence the records went back into Polish, but the three systems did not merge. In the former Russian part a priest still registered births, in the former Austrian part the parish book still did the job, in the former Prussian part a secular office did. One secular registry office for the whole country arrived only with two decrees of 25 September 1945, in force from 1 January 1946.'],
            ],
        ],
        'words' => [
            'label' => 'A record in three languages',
            'note' => 'Fourteen words that carry most of what a birth, marriage or death entry says. The Russian column is given in the spelling used today. Before the reform of 1918 the same words carried letters that have since gone, above all a hard sign at the end of a masculine word and the letter yat where an e now stands, so a word on your document may have a character or two more than it has here.',
            'col_en' => 'What it means',
            'col_la' => 'Latin',
            'col_de' => 'German',
            'col_ru' => 'Russian',
            'items' => [
                ['en' => 'born', 'la' => 'natus, nata, ortus', 'de' => 'geboren, Geburt', 'ru' => 'родился (rodilsia), родилась (rodilas)'],
                ['en' => 'baptised', 'la' => 'baptizatus, baptismus', 'de' => 'getauft, Taufe', 'ru' => 'крещение (kreshchenie)'],
                ['en' => 'marriage', 'la' => 'matrimonium, copulati', 'de' => 'Heirat, Trauung', 'ru' => 'брак (brak), венчание (venchanie)'],
                ['en' => 'died', 'la' => 'mortuus, obiit, defunctus', 'de' => 'gestorben, Tod', 'ru' => 'умер (umer), умерла (umerla)'],
                ['en' => 'son', 'la' => 'filius', 'de' => 'Sohn', 'ru' => 'сын (syn)'],
                ['en' => 'daughter', 'la' => 'filia', 'de' => 'Tochter', 'ru' => 'дочь (doch)'],
