        'citizenship' => [
            'label' => 'Citizenship by descent',
            'title' => 'What the law gives and what it does not',
            'body_1' => 'Poland passes citizenship by blood rather than by place of birth, and it sets no limit on how many generations back the line may run. What it does require is that the line was never broken. The starting point is the act of 20 January 1920 on the citizenship of the Polish State, the first citizenship law of a country that had only just come back into existence. It made citizens of the people settled on Polish territory at that moment, taken from the registers the Russian, Austrian and Prussian administrations had kept, provided they had not already taken another country’s citizenship.',
            'body_2' => 'From there the question is whether anybody in the chain lost it. Under the 1920 act citizenship went when somebody took a foreign citizenship or entered foreign service without permission, and it went with him for his wife and for children under eighteen. That one rule breaks most American, Brazilian and Argentine cases, because a grandfather naturalised in Chicago or Curitiba before 1951 usually took his whole household out with him. A Polish woman who married a foreigner lost it too, until the act of 8 January 1951 ended that on 19 January 1951. After the act of 15 February 1962 nobody could shed Polish citizenship simply by taking another one, because the state had to consent, so the later your ancestor was naturalised the better the chances.',
            'items' => [
                ['k' => 'It is confirmation, not a grant', 'v' => 'The procedure is called potwierdzenie posiadania obywatelstwa polskiego, confirmation that you hold Polish citizenship. The state is not giving you anything. It reads your documents and rules on whether the chain held or broke.'],
                ['k' => 'Who decides', 'v' => 'A wojewoda, the governor of a province. For somebody who has never lived in Poland, which is almost everybody reading this, it is the Mazowiecki wojewoda in Warsaw. The application can be filed through a Polish consulate, which passes it on.'],
                ['k' => 'What it rests on', 'v' => 'Documents, not family stories. A Polish birth, marriage or death record for the ancestor, proof of every link down to you, and whatever shows what happened to their citizenship abroad, the naturalisation file and its date above all.'],
                ['k' => 'Where it usually breaks', 'v' => 'The date an ancestor was naturalised abroad, a marriage to a foreigner before 19 January 1951, and unauthorised service in a foreign army. Each of them turns on one particular document and one particular date.'],
                ['k' => 'What comes afterwards', 'v' => 'A positive decision is followed by separate steps, each governed by its own law: a PESEL number, the entry of your foreign birth and marriage records into the Polish registry, and only then a passport.'],
                ['k' => 'Karta Polaka', 'v' => 'The Polish Card, from the act of 7 September 2007, is not citizenship. It recognises that you belong to the Polish nation. Since 14 July 2019 anybody in the world may apply, where before that it was open only to citizens of the states that came out of the Soviet Union. It asks for a declaration made in person in front of an official, basic Polish and some knowledge of Polish customs, and proof of a parent or a grandparent, or two great-grandparents, who were Polish. It brings a free long stay visa, the right to work without a permit and the right to study, and it does not let you cross the border without that visa.'],
            ],
            'disclaimer' => 'This page is not legal advice and cannot be. Citizenship cases turn on dates and documents that look different in every family, and on foreign law as much as on Polish law, so two cousins can get opposite answers. Nobody can promise you a passport, and anybody who promises one before reading your papers is selling something. If the case matters to you, take the documents to a lawyer who does this work, or ask the Polish consulate that covers the place where you live.',
        ],
        'ground' => [
            'label' => 'On the ground',
            'title' => 'What you will find when you go',
            'body_1' => 'People expect a house and find a field. Wooden villages burned, the war moved whole populations, and in the lands that lie today in Ukraine, Belarus or Lithuania the Polish families were moved out between 1944 and 1946, so the people living in your great-grandmother’s village are very unlikely to be related to her. What is usually still standing is the church, the cemetery and the line of the road.',
            'body_2' => 'It is worth saying too that almost nobody in a village speaks English. A parish priest may have some German or some Latin, a clerk in a small office will have neither. Anybody who arrives with a Polish speaker beside them, or with somebody on the other end of a telephone, gets several times as far in a day.',
            'items' => [
                ['k' => 'Write first', 'v' => 'An archive reading room works by appointment and a file has to be fetched up from the store. A letter or an email a few weeks ahead, in Polish, naming the parish, the surname and the years, can save the whole trip.'],
                ['k' => 'The parish', 'v' => 'If the books are still at the parish there is no right to see them, so this is a request rather than a claim. Arrange it beforehand, avoid Sunday and the hours around a mass or a funeral, and a donation to the parish is the normal thing.'],
                ['k' => 'The cemetery', 'v' => 'Village cemeteries were reused, so a grave from 1910 is rarely still standing. Where the stone survives, the surname is often spelt in a way you have never seen. Candles are the ordinary way of marking a visit and they are sold at the gate.'],
                ['k' => 'The village', 'v' => 'Knocking on a door and saying the surname works better than it sounds, because in a small place somebody always remembers who lived where. The oldest people know the most and speak the least English.'],
                ['k' => 'The names', 'v' => 'A place can have three names: the Polish one your family used, the German or Russian one on an old map, and the official one it carries today. Villages were renamed inside Poland after 1945 as well. Check all of them before deciding that a place does not exist.'],
                ['k' => 'Take copies', 'v' => 'Carry what you already have, printed out. A clerk, a priest or somebody met in a village can often place a family from a photograph, a surname and a date in a way no search box will.'],
            ],
            'close' => 'Very little of this gives a straight answer at the first attempt, and some of it gives none at all. What it does give, often enough, is a village you can stand in and a line in a book with your own surname in it, spelt the way it was spelt before anybody left.',
        ],
        'sources' => [
            'label' => 'Where this comes from',
            'body' => 'That names were not changed by inspectors at Ellis Island is the position of the historians who have worked on those records. Philip Sutton of the New York Public Library set out in 2013 how the manifests were drawn up in Europe and how inspectors were assigned by the languages they spoke, and Marian L. Smith, senior historian of the United States immigration service, quotes the rule the inspectors worked under and argues that a change of name was usually an act of self invention after arrival. The surname examples come from the changed surname list kept by the Polish Genealogical Society of Connecticut and the Northeast and from the study of Polish surnames in America published by the Polish Genealogical Society of America. Shimanski for Szymański and Yankowski for Jankowski are printed here as illustrations of the sound rules rather than as documented cases. The dates for the language of records follow the outline of Polish metrical and civil registration published by JRI-Poland and the Poland pages of FamilySearch: civil registration in Polish in the Duchy of Warsaw from 1808, the Civil Code of the Kingdom of Poland published on 13 June 1825 and separate registers by denomination from 1826, Russian required from 1868 after the January Uprising, the patent of the Emperor Joseph II of 20 February 1784 for Galicia and the patent of 7 May 1789 for Jewish communities there, the Prussian law of 9 March 1874 in force from 1 October 1874 and the whole German Empire from 1 January 1876. Sources disagree on whether the Russian requirement began in January or in April 1868, so only the year is printed. The word table is taken from the Latin, German and Russian genealogical word lists published by FamilySearch, and the Russian is given in modern spelling with a note about the letters the reform of 1918 removed. The retention periods are article 28 of the act of 28 November 2014 on civil status records: a hundred years for a birth record, eighty for a marriage or a death, and up to two more years for the transfer itself. The merging of the three systems into one registry office is the two decrees of 25 September 1945, in force from 1 January 1946. Citizenship follows the act of 20 January 1920, the act of 8 January 1951 in force from 19 January 1951, the act of 15 February 1962 and the act of 2 April 2009, and the procedure before a wojewoda is described by the Ministry of the Interior and Administration. The Karta Polaka is the act of 7 September 2007, opened to applicants anywhere in the world by the amendment of 16 May 2019, in force from 14 July 2019. The holdings named for the Central Archives of Historical Records come from its own published inventories, whose catalogues are online while the page images for the most part are not. The size of Geneteka was read off its own front page in September 2026 and grows constantly, so treat it as a floor rather than a figure. No prices and no opening hours are printed here, because they change faster than this page does. Title picture: a storeroom of the State Archive in Szczecin, photographed by Kapitel in 2019, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0.',
        ],
        'next' => 'Back to the home page',
    ],
